HUT! Airlines Cancel Hundreds Of Flights After Houthis BLOW UP Saudia Airbus JetPresley Gerber’s Drug Battle Blamed For Tragic Final Chapter As Years-Long Addiction Struggle EmergeTrump-Vance Clash? POTUS Honors Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella As VP Targets Tech Giant Over H-1B VisasMen Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​ : Jessie Buckley and Rory Kinnear Starrer Men Official Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​JD Vance Exposes H-1B Scam!: 6,000 Americans Duped; Microsoft, Wipro & TCS On Visa HitlistAndrew Mountbatten-Windsor’s Warrant Win; Why His Legal Fight Is Far From OverWATCH: Trump Heckler Assaulted At Texas Rally; POTUS Immediately Reacts‘Europe Gets New F-35’: Marco Rubio Flexes NATO Power With Massive Ammo Boost | WatchDolly Parton's Family Eyes BIOPIC Amid $650 Million Estate Feud; Who Will Play The Music Icon?Germany, France Push Tougher EU China Trade Policy As Europe’s Deficit Tops €1 Billion A DayLatest NewsJharkhand should 'revisit' DGP appointment rules, says Supreme CourtIPS quota in CAPF posts comes into forceFile doesn't reach judge in time, Supreme Court adjourns hearing on Punjab appealAI use ups homework scores, but slashes exam marks: StudyTaylor Swift and Travis Kelce branded ‘absentee’ owners over $5.35 million Ohio mansion; neighbours raise sewage, wildlife and security fears while couple’s team defends scaled-back fence and preserved accessLibra Shani Horoscope Today, 9 October 2026: Saturn Says Pay the Hidden Cost of Rest; Uttara Phalguni Moon Draws You Inward'Nana Patekar's final film 'Shakti Shalini' will be a tribute to the actor': Director Aditya SarpotdarGroup of 42 ex-judges defends SIR, slams opposition attack on Election CommissionJeff Cannonball cause of death: What happened to the wrestler who died at 39?Jason Kelce donates $8,000 to leukemia-stricken Eagles fan as fundraiser nears $150,000 goalProof of 21 'compromised transfers', 11 policy changes part of CBI's Punjab fileVirgo Shani Horoscope Today, 9 October 2026: Saturn Says Put the Partner's Share in Writing; Uttara Phalguni Moon Puts You in FrontWill those whose earnings have stopped love me: PM Modi amid SIR rowA CEO who was not a cyclist trained for months, then rode about 2,000 miles from Mexico to Canada in 12 days; along the route, his team raised and distributed more than $766,000 to over 70 people and organisationsStarlink entry row: Is Ambani the real boss of India, asks Elon MuskThis tiny Egyptian object was mistaken for a copper awl for a century; researchers now say its six surviving leather coils reveal a 5,300-year-old bow-powered rotary drillGST Council scraps tax officers' arrest powers, eases refund rulesNana Patekar’s death case: Goa Police registers 'unnatural death' case; to record statements of family and conduct post-mortem to establish cause of deathCopyright © 2026 Bennett, Coleman & Co. Ltd. All rights reserved. For reprint rights: Times Syndication ServiceFollow Us On","title":"Capricorn Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: Smooth progress brings success in studies, work and finances","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/astrology/horoscope/capricorn-horoscope-today-october-09-2026-smooth-progress-brings-success-in-studies-work-and-finances/articleshow/134793490.cms","id":413},{"content_hash":"28ca2144f86985232a5245594fadd8add05993fdc6d84984e773ba7551917658","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:35.340987+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/le-roman-dur_2072724_1819218.html","language":"","text":"La bataille autour des prix de fin d’année s’annonce bien. Il y a beaucoup de livres intéressants, et sans être à jour, loin de là, submergé par la marée toujours montante des romans, j’en ai déjà lu de très bons. Les meilleurs d’entre eux jusqu’ici relèvent du naturalisme le plus cru. Il y a une mode du roman dur, assez méprisable en tant que mode, car il est donné à n’importe quel petit débutant de faire le glorieux à peu de frais à force de jurons, de blasphèmes et de grossièretés. Les excès de la psychanalyse continuent aussi à faire long feu. J’ai vu dans un de ces romans un bon jeune homme se mettre à détester son père et se réjouir de sa mort parce qu’il l’avait entendu aimer sa mère. Ce que c’est que d’écouter aux portes et de s’être pris soi-même pour œdipe ! Cette protestation irritée contre la nature des choses me paraît dépourvue de toute espèce de valeur générale et de vérité. Joint à cela qu’il n’est pas certain qu’en abordant et en traitant ce sujet-là l’auteur n’ait assez sottement spéculé sur l’attrait qu’il y a à déplaire et sur le bénéfice du scandale. Quant aux durs et aux malabars, nous sommes aussi assez renseignés, et si l’on a le goût de ces choses un bon film de gangsters, une séance de catch où les adversaires ne s’épargnent pas, en apprennent très suffisamment sur le plaisir que peut procurer le spectacle de la violence. Il a d’ailleurs dans sa réalité, sous nos yeux, une grande supériorité sur sa description dans un roman où il faut faire l’effort d’imaginer. L’intérêt de ces romans forcenés ne consiste donc pas dans la brutalité de leurs personnages, mais dans la vérité possible de la vie où ils sont mêlés, et bien plus encore, comme toujours, dans le seul mérite littéraire de l’écrivain. Je ne me souviendrai peut-être pas exactement dans six mois des quelques douzaines d’histoires qu’il m’aura fallu avaler en cet octobre et ce novembre, et qui ne m’auront rien appris sur l’homme, son destin, sa misère, ses divertissements ; mais je saurai très bien que dans cette confusion tel et tel se seront révélés, confirmés, avec qui il faudra compter. Ce n’est pas le talent qui manque. Voici en première ligne M. Georges Arnaud. Il a fait l’an dernier un beau début avec le Salaire de la peur, où il racontait d’une manière dramatique et sans fioritures la vie d’une équipe de camionneurs transportant de la dynamite sur de mauvaises routes d’Amérique centrale. Sauteront ? Sauteront pas ? Il n’y avait pas d’autre question à se poser sur le sort de ces misérables. On était pendant tout le voyage sur leur menaçante guimbarde avec eux, et l’on avait l’impression qu’on risquait de faire tout sauter en tournant un peu brusquement les feuillets du livre. C’est que l’auteur avait déjà ce don extraordinaire de présence qui lui permet de figurer lui-même dans ce qu’il décrit, comme d’y rendre pareillement présent son lecteur au point de croire qu’on y est. Avec le Voyage du mauvais larron (1) vous voilà dans le bain. Vous avez été embarqué malgré vous à fond de cale d’un cargo faisant son chemin de port en port, sous les tropiques, en compagnie d’un passager clandestin, André Plessis, le héros du livre. Ce n’est pas un malchanceux, un désespéré ; c’est au contraire un jeune bourgeois, un intellectuel, licencié, qui a délibérément choisi cette expérience d’affranchissement. Il a eu un malheur dont on ne nous dit pas la cause ni les conditions (\" Mon adolescence a eu des nuits tristes. \"), mais le drame traversé a suffi pour faire de ce garçon un révolté qui va se jeter dans l’aventure pour le plaisir de se démontrer à lui-même qu’il est capable de mener sa vie à son gré, dangereusement, librement, hors de la facilité et de la routine. Il a déjà, peut-être injustement, connu la prison, et il \" en est sorti méchant \". Le voilà donc essayant de revenir gratis en France au bout des deux années qu’il s’est fixées avant de retrouver une femme qu’il aime, Elisabeth, exprès quittée pour cette épreuve et dont il espère qu’elle l’attend. Dans le fond du navire qui le bringuebale Plessis rêve et ressasse ses aventures, sortant la nuit de sa cachette pour aller voler quelque nourriture dans les cuisines endormies. On y est, et là aussi en participe à ses dangers : qu’on le découvre, il est mis à terre à la première escale, et tout serait à recommencer. Ou bien si le passage qui permet au clandestin de monter de nuit prendre une goulée d’air sur le pont était obstrué par une caisse de 600 kilos, s’il allait crever, pris au piège ? En fait on ne sait pas si André Plessis arrivera vivant à bon port, ou si ses dernières rêveries hallucinées ne sont pas déjà d’un agonisant. Elisabeth elle-même l’attend-elle vraiment, ou n’est-elle, entre beaucoup d’autres, que le symbole de l’éternelle créature désirée, sans cesse en fuite devant nous ? Tout cela en soi-même est déjà assez intrigant, et je dois dire que je me suis pris de sympathie pour ce \" mauvais larron \" malgré sa jactance et son goût un peu agaçant de s’installer dans son personnage. Mais tout de même c’est un homme, et je me demande quelle expérience de ces choses possède l’auteur, M. Georges Arnaud, pour avoir peint d’une façon si directe et si vraisemblable les avatars de son héros, tantôt chercheur d’or, camionneur, chauffeur de taxi, passeur d’armes, contrebandier et voleur même, prisonnier, tantôt faisant le coup de feu avec la police et les douaniers aux frontières de la Colombie, du Venezuela, du Chili ou de l’Équateur. Ses rencontres sont de toutes sortes : évadés, filles, assassins, mabouls, pauvres diables, jusqu’à un saint même, le Père Roger, avec lequel Plessis soignera les malades d’une léproserie où le choléra s’est mis de surcroît. Et il aime sa mère, ce mauvais garçon ! Un vagabond sentimental, fier de sa force et grand amateur de bagarre. \" Être seul, debout, agissant. \" Malgré la chère Elisabeth il y a plus d’une figure féminine assez alliciante dans ce livre remuant, aventureux et sensuel. M. Georges Arnaud a l’air bien renseigné aussi dans ce domaine. Bref, on le lit et on le croit ; et il est même capable d’émouvoir quand il parle de la solidarité des hommes durs, \" les paumés du sort \", comme il dit. On sera curieux de le suivre et de voir ce qu’il nous racontera une autre fois, maintenant qu’il semble revenu de ses Amériques et qu’il doit nous donner la preuve par neuf de son grand talent en l’exerçant avec la même force sur d’autres sujets. Il vous reste 50.46% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"Le roman dur","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/07/le-roman-dur_2072724_1819218.html","id":414},{"content_hash":"9540533022178c338998b08515d3ee4008039d859b2f4079a0b8d1ecc49c3a33","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:35.283406+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/31/military-rescues-abducted/","language":"","text":"Military Rescues 10 Abducted NYSC Members in Kogi Forest By Prnigeria - August 31, 2026 Military troops Military Rescues 10 Abducted NYSC Members in Kogi Forest The military high command has rescued 10 of the 15 National Youth Service Corps (NYSC) members abducted by suspected kidnappers in Kogi State. Director of Defence Information, Major General Michael Onoja, disclosed this on Monday during the monthly press briefing of the Defence Headquarters in Abuja. Read Also: The Humble Giant: A Tribute to Late Idris Ibrahim IBRO By Abdullahi Sani Maiunguwa Tinubu declares three-day mourning for 32 NAF personnel killed in air crash FRSC Divests Safeline Microfinance Bank, Hands Over to ROBOPAY Onoja said the 10 corps members were rescued from Egume Forest in Ofu Local Government Area of the state, adding that the operation was still ongoing to rescue the remaining victims and apprehend the perpetrators. The victims were among 15 NYSC members kidnapped last week while travelling from Abuja to Bayelsa State. The kidnappers had reportedly demanded a ransom of N20 million from the families of some of the victims. The Defence Headquarters urged members of the public to remain calm and vigilant, while encouraging them to promptly report suspicious movements or information that could assist security forces in rescuing the remaining victims and arresting the suspects. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSDefence HeadquartersMajor General Michael OnojaNYSC Previous articleFG Launches ‘Hire from Nigeria’ Campaign, Targets One Million JobsNext articleTroops Rescue 21 Kidnap Victims in Kogi Forests Prnigeria Xing","title":"Military Rescues 10 Abducted NYSC Members in Kogi Forest","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/08/31/military-rescues-abducted/","id":415},{"content_hash":"796e03881b4ef725ef5bcc95f7e949471b589151e689380af307c69e227fbe86","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:35.115241+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-governments-barnacles-34798","language":"","text":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on the government’s ‘barnacles’ Published: November 27, 2014 7:27pm EST https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-governments-barnacles-34798 https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-governments-barnacles-34798 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article University of Canberra Vice-Chancellor Stephen Parker and Michelle Grattan discuss the week in politics including Bronwyn Bishop’s record day, Julie Bishop, the future leadership of the Liberal Party and the government’s “barnacles”. Video Julie Bishop Bronwyn Bishop Stephen Parker, Michelle Grattan, University of Canberra Authors Stephen Parker Vice-Chancellor, University of Canberra Michelle Grattan Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra Disclosure statement The authors do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Canberra provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.urkstgfuw","title":"VIDEO: Michelle Grattan on the government’s ‘barnacles’","url":"https://theconversation.com/video-michelle-grattan-on-the-governments-barnacles-34798","id":416},{"content_hash":"a522b538c2d1b7fb172f4efdf525f86a73309bf99fc5f8d7b8ec9ff2fb3bed1a","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:34.833281+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-01/broken-hill-pub-junction-hotel-closes-amid-dwindling-population/106854376","language":"","text":"Closure of Broken Hill's Junction Hotel part of growing trend for country NSW pubsBy Kristina Rosengren and Andrew SchmidtABC Broken HillTopic:Pubs, Taverns and BarsPosted Wed 1 Jul 2026 at 1:00pmWed 1 Jul 2026 at 1:00pmWed 1 Jul 2026 at 1:00pm, updated Wed 1 Jul 2026 at 1:46pmWed 1 Jul 2026 at 1:46pmWed 1 Jul 2026 at 1:46pmHarry Plane says there were a few tears shed as he and his friends farewelled their favourite pub. (ABC Broken Hill: Kristina Rosengren)In short:The Junction Hotel has become the latest historical pub in Broken Hill to close its doors after being sold to a local business syndicate. The hotel's manager says the pub was no longer sustainable after losing its poker machines. What's next?A delegate of the Australian Hotels Association has warned hotels in remote locations need to diversify their business model in order to survive.abc.net.au/news/broken-hill-pub-junction-hotel-closes-amid-dwindling-population/106854376Link copiedShareShare articleFor more than 60 years, Harry Plane has had a designated seat at his favourite local pub.The Junction Hotel in the outback mining city of Broken Hill has served as a gathering place for miners, footy players, and railway workers for more than 130 years. As he sips on a beer, Mr Plane reminisces about the good old days of the pub.\"We used to have the mine boys in here every night, afternoon shift, or day shift,\" Mr Plane said.\"[It] used to be pretty wild.\"The Junction Hotel, pictured in the 1960s, opened in 1891 and was named after the nearby Junction Mine. (Supplied: Jenny Camilleri)Opened in 1891, the pub called last drinks earlier this month for the final time.The hotel's new owners, a local business syndicate, are unlikely to reopen it as a pub.Mr Plane said he would miss the camaraderie with fellow patrons the most.\"[It is] a very sad day,\" he said.\"I reckon there will be a few tears.\"Pub regulars John Kinnear and Michael Baldwin enjoy their final beers at the Junction Hotel. (ABC Broken Hill: Andrew Schmidt)In the mining city's heyday in the late 1800s and early 1900s, when its population peaked at more than 35,000, it boasted more than 70 pubs.The city's pubs have been declining along with its population, with the Junction Hotel the latest casualty.Since 2000-2001, the population in Broken Hill and far west NSW has declined by 18 per cent, to about 17,500 people.The decline is driven in part by the modernisation of mining operations, which require fewer workers.Diversify or dieDean Trengove is the Australian Hotels Association state council delegate and owns his own pub in the city.His venue includes poker machines, a restaurant and a drive-through bottle-o.Mr Trengove said he understood times were tough for the city's pubs due to evolving drinking habits, cost-of-living pressures and strict compliance.\"Society's changed and people are moving away from everyday drinking culture that sustained a lot of pubs,\" Mr Trengove said.\"There's a lot of things added into the price of a product that we're selling in pubs.\"Harry Plane says the Junction Hotel was a popular watering hole for miners coming off shift. (ABC Broken Hill: Kristina Rosengren)Mr Trengove said if pubs were to survive, they needed to consider diversifying their offerings.\"In Broken Hill, it's survival of the fittest and it's about the facilities that you offer and a lot of times it's how you run your business,\" he said.\"You've got to try and offer as many services as you can to maintain and give people a reason to come to the pub.\"Gone with the pokiesGrant Williams will likely hold the title of the last publican of the Junction Hotel.He was the pub's manager when it was previously sold in 2022 to a Sydney investor, who moved the hotel's nine poker machines to a different city.It was this decision that Mr Williams believes spelled the end of the pub.Grant Williams, likely the last publican of the Junction Hotel, locks the doors of the pub for the final time. (ABC Broken Hill: Andrew Schmidt)\"Once the pokies went, we didn't make any money,\" Mr Williams said.\"[The hotel] was losing a lot of money every week because I lost 50 regulars because they like to play the pokies.\"If you haven't got pokies, you haven't got a chance.\"Across Broken Hill and Wentworth, there are 469 poker machines in clubs and 51 in hotels, which collectively earned more than $11 million from December to March, according to Liquor & Gaming NSW data.Loss of culturePubs are woven into the rich social history of this remote mining city in far west New South Wales.Emily McInerney, who has published a book on the history, said the city was well-known for having a pub on every corner.Emily McInerney has written a book about the unique history of pubs in Broken Hill and far west NSW. (ABC Broken Hill: Kristina Rosengren)She said the names of pubs were closely tied to the region's mining history, such as the Silver King, the Bonanza and the Exchange, and they became meeting places for trade unions, social clubs and political rallies.Ms McInerney said even now, pubs were more than just a place to go and drink.More news from Broken HillHeritage listing for 'first of its kind' outback solar experimentTopic:Monument and Heritage SiteHeritage listing for 'first of its kind' outback solar experimentTopic:Monument and Heritage SiteCattle worth over half-a-million dollars reported missing at outback stationTopic:CrimeCouncil says there's 'no land' for charity's request for rough sleepersTopic:HomelessnessBest wet season in decades gives life to living medicine cabinetTopic:Nature\"Even if people aren't using it as a pub, it's still a function centre,\" she said.\"People have gotten married there, had 21sts and 30ths and 40ths and 50ths there.\"The Junction Hotel closed its doors for the final time on June 6. (ABC Broken Hill: Kristina Rosengren)The Junction now joins a long list of the city's other iconic hotels that have shut their doors, including the South Australian, the Old Royal, the Excelsior and the Southern Cross.Some establishments closed during the COVID-19 pandemic and never reopened, while others have been unable to weather the changing tides.\"It is a loss of culture, \" Ms McInerney said.\"But we are changing how we live and it's hard to probably be able to afford to sit at the pub on a Friday and Saturday night in this climate.\"Harry Plane has been a regular at the Junction Hotel for more than 60 years. (ABC Broken Hill: Andrew Schmidt)Mr Plane, however, will find a new watering hole.\"All the mates will go somewhere else, so I'll probably go with them,\" he said. \"I'll find somewhere to drink.\"At another local hotel, not too far from the Junction, Mr Plane and his friends have named a part of the bar 'the Junction refugees'.ABC Broken Hill — local news in your inboxGet our local newsletter, delivered free each FridayEmail addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerRelated storiesThis 'lovely little outback town basic pub' has just reopened and the locals couldn't be happierTopic:HistoryCity couple quit careers and take over outback pub Topic:Feel GoodPub owner says removing pokies made him feel like a criminalTopic:GamblingRelated topicsBroken HillHospitality IndustryPubs, Taverns and BarsRegional CommunitiesRural and Remote CommunitiesTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Closure of Broken Hill's Junction Hotel part of growing trend for country NSW pubs","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-01/broken-hill-pub-junction-hotel-closes-amid-dwindling-population/106854376","id":417},{"content_hash":"52ddf77d3caf0e52d77f87691a1ecff5a826da111bbf9524ae67f7613411ef4a","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:34.800703+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/make-your-money-last-in-retirement.html","language":"","text":"3 steps to help your money last in retirement Taking spending rates, healthcare costs and inflation into account can make a big difference in how long your savings will support you ONE QUESTION LOOMS LARGE for retirees: “Will I have enough money to last the rest of my life?” For 45% of Americans, the answer is no.1 But with thoughtful planning, “there are a number of things retirees can do to control how long their money might last,” says Merrill Financial Advisor Eric Breemen. “As long as you’re prepared to stay somewhat flexible, you and your advisor can work together to create a customized plan that accounts for your priorities and adjusts for hurdles you may encounter along the way.” Here are three things to consider that can make a big difference in making your money last. Step 1: Develop a sustainable spending rate While conventional wisdom often pegs the ideal annual spending rate for retirees at 4%, you should view that only as a starting point. After taking increasing longevity and other factors into account, your actual drawdown rate could range from 3% to 5%, depending on your retirement age, savings, income sources and health status. Assuming a 4% spending rate, someone with $1,000,000 in savings, for example, would have $40,000 in the first year of retirement to cover their living and leisure costs. “Your optimal spending rate is one of the first questions you’ll want to address,” Breemen adds. Your advisor can show you how different spending rates could affect how long your money might last and help you adjust your strategy as needed to account for things like inflation or market downturns. TIP TO HELP YOUR MONEY LAST If you have a family history of longevity, you may want to start with a sub-4% withdrawal rate to stretch your dollars. Women may want to stick closer to the lower end of the 3% to 5% withdrawal range to maximize their income because they tend to live longer than men, says Nevenka Vrdoljak, Chief Investment Office, Merrill and Bank of America Private Bank. If it’s looking like your income won’t be quite enough to support the retirement you’ve envisioned, says Vrdoljak, consider delaying claiming Social Security until age 70, adjusting your spending rate or working longer to bring in more income. Step 2: Let your investments work harder for you “Some investors tend to play it too safe as they begin retirement,” says Vrdoljak. Taking the cautious path often means putting money in lower-risk — but also lower-yielding — investments such as bonds, CDs, money market funds and Treasury bills. “But over a retirement of 30 or more years, a portfolio that's all cash and bonds can barely keep up with inflation or the likelihood of rising health costs,” says Anil Suri, a managing director for the Chief Investment Office, Merrill and Bank of America Private Bank. TIP TO HELP YOUR MONEY LAST Consider investing in Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities, which increase in value when inflation rises. If the stock market’s potential for volatility is what’s causing you to invest defensively, Breemen sometimes advises putting aside a “liquidity bucket” of several years’ worth of desired annual spending into cash, high-interest savings accounts, money market accounts and other liquid investments. The rest can be invested in a mix of stocks and bonds that could offer the potential for greater growth. “A liquidity bucket is a psychological safety net,” he notes. “You know you have that money to fall back on no matter what happens.” Inflation poses another risk to making your money last. For example, a 2% rise in inflation could whittle $1 million in cash down to $603,465 over 25 years.2 To hedge against inflation, consider adding assets that often keep pace with it, such as commodities like gold and real estate investment trusts, suggests Breemen. Owning real estate as an investment is another option. “Rental property is a very natural hedge against inflation. As inflation goes up, so does the rent.” Step 3: Plan for long-term care costs. As we live longer, healthcare tends to take up more of our retirement budgets. About 70%3 of Americans 65 and older will need some form of long-term care, which, for a semi-private room in a nursing facility, averages $111,325 per year.4 If you don’t already have traditional long-term care insurance — or, alternately, hybrid or permanent life insurance with a long-term care rider — you’ll want to earmark a certain percentage of your savings to help you cover future healthcare costs, says Breemen. “You could place those assets in a separate investment account designated for your future care,” he adds. Your advisor can help you estimate potential future costs, explain what Medicare does and doesn’t cover, share information about long-term care insurance options and talk you through various strategies to help close the gap. TIP TO HELP YOUR MONEY LAST When you fund a health savings account, you can use tax-free funds to pay for certain long-term care expenses. Stick with your plan and review it regularly. “This may be the most important advice of all,” says Suri. It’s always tempting to blow up your spending plan for something you’ve suddenly decided you really want. Similarly, it’s sometimes easy to get spooked by turbulent markets and make spur-of-the-moment investment decisions that might not support your long-term investment goals. “The key is to have a plan you’re comfortable with. That way, you’re more likely to stay the course.” Check in regularly with your advisor to adjust for any changes — both in the markets and in your life — so your investments can continue working toward your financial well-being, now and in the future. “These are evergreen principles,” says Suri. “They have stood the test of time.” Connect with Merrill on your terms Have an advisor contact you Match with an advisor Already a Merrill client Invest on your own, online Ready to work with a dedicated advisor in your area? Fill out the form below. There are items below that require your attention before submitting. Something went wrong! Your form couldn’t be submitted correctly. Please try again. Would you like us to contact you? Yes No First name* Last name* Email address* Confirm email address* Primary phone* Zip code* Investment minimum $250,000 By providing your contact information above, you agree that a representative of Merrill, the Brokerage affiliate of Bank of America Corporation, may contact you via telephone and/or email to discuss and/or offer investment products and services that may be appropriate for you. You agree that you are providing to us your consent for us to contact you regardless of any Do Not Call or Do Not Email privacy choices you may have previously expressed until you revoke this consent, or up to 90 days. You may revoke your consent at any time by notifying the Merrill representative. Submit Thank you. You’ll receive an automated email confirming receipt of your request shortly. A Merrill financial advisor will be in contact with you in the coming days. Meet the right advisor for you Answer a couple of questions about yourself to see advisors who match your preferences. Then, choose who you’d like to connect with. Find your match Are you currently working with a Merrill advisor? Log in to MyMerrill or Search by your advisor name 9am-9pm EST, M-F 1.800.637.7455 Are you looking for your 401(k)/retirement or equity awards account with Merrill? Log in to Benefits OnLine 9am-9pm EST, M-F 1.866.820.1492 If you’re interested in building your own portfolio with a wide range of investment choices, Merrill Edge® Self Directed may work for you. Learn more 1 Morningstar Center for Retirement & Policy Studies, “Beyond the Retirement Crisis Headlines: Why Employer-Sponsored Plans Are the Key to Retirement Adequacy for Today’s Workers,” July 2024. 2 Calculated using moneychimp.com Compound Interest Calculator, accessed February 2025. 3 LongTermCare.gov, “How Much Care Will You Need?,” accessed February 2025. 4 Genworth, 2024 Cost of Care Survey, March 2025. Important Disclosures Investing involves risk, including the possible loss of principal. Past performance is no guarantee of future results. This information should not be construed as investment advice and is subject to change. It is provided for informational purposes only and is not intended to be either a specific offer by Bank of America, Merrill or any affiliate to sell or provide, or a specific invitation for a consumer to apply for, any particular retail financial product or service that may be available. The Chief Investment Office (CIO) provides thought leadership on wealth management, investment strategy and global markets; portfolio management solutions; due diligence; and solutions oversight and data analytics. CIO viewpoints are developed for Bank of America Private Bank, a division of Bank of America, N.A., (“Bank of America”) and Merrill Lynch, Pierce, Fenner & Smith Incorporated (“MLPF&S” or “Merrill”), a registered broker-dealer, registered investment adviser and a wholly owned subsidiary of Bank of America Corporation (“BofA Corp.”). Investments have varying degrees of risk. Some of the risks involved with equity securities include the possibility that the value of the stocks may fluctuate in response to events specific to the companies or markets, as well as economic, political or social events in the U.S. or abroad. Bonds are subject to interest rate, inflation and credit risks. Treasury bills are less volatile than longer-term fixed income securities and are guaranteed as to timely payment of principal and interest by the U.S. government. Investments in foreign securities (including ADRs) involve special risks, including foreign currency risk and the possibility of substantial volatility due to adverse political, economic or other developments. These risks are magnified for investments made in emerging markets. Investments in a certain industry or sector may pose additional risk due to lack of diversification and sector concentration. Investments in real estate securities can be subject to fluctuations in the value of the underlying properties, the effect of economic conditions on real estate values, changes in interest rates, and risk related to renting properties, such as rental defaults. There are special risks associated with an investment in commodities, including market price fluctuations, regulatory changes, interest rate changes, credit risk, economic changes and the impact of adverse political or financial factors. All guarantees and benefits of an insurance policy are backed by the claims-paying ability of the issuing insurance company. They are not backed by Merrill or its affiliates, nor does Merrill or its affiliates make any representations or guarantees regarding the claims-paying ability of the issuing insurance company. Long-term care insurance coverage contains benefits, exclusions, limitations, eligibility requirements and specific terms and conditions under which the insurance coverage may be continued in force or discontinued. Not all insurance policies and types of coverage may be available in your state. This material does not take into account a client’s particular investment objectives, financial situations, or needs and is not intended as a recommendation, offer, or solicitation for the purchase or sale of any security or investment strategy. Merrill offers a broad range of brokerage, investment advisory (including planning) and other services. There are important differences between brokerage and investment advisory services, including the type of advice and assistance provided, the fees charged, and the rights and obligations of the parties. It is important to understand the differences, particularly when determining which service or services to select. For more information about these services and their differences, speak with your Merrill financial advisor. Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Switch to Accessibility Friendly View Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Give your wealth purpose Navigate the complexities and opportunities in planning the impact of your wealth. Explore solutions More for you More for you Read this next: “How will you replace your salary when you retire?” Sign up Get monthly insights from our Perspectives newsletter Related Articles 4 big retirement risks — and how to prepare for them Read more Do you have a retirement spending plan? Read more How much to spend on leisure in retirement? Read more","title":"3 steps to help your money last in retirement","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/make-your-money-last-in-retirement.html","id":418},{"content_hash":"2124b40347e290a1ecabcd95fa5dbb566633bf237767be90e05d19625bb61c92","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:34.067064+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/stories-from-the-sky-astronomy-in-indigenous-knowledge-33140","language":"","text":"Stories from the sky: astronomy in Indigenous knowledge Published: November 30, 2014 1:52pm EST https://theconversation.com/stories-from-the-sky-astronomy-in-indigenous-knowledge-33140 https://theconversation.com/stories-from-the-sky-astronomy-in-indigenous-knowledge-33140 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Indigenous Australian practices, developed and honed over thousands of years, weave science with storytelling. In this Indigenous science series, we’ll look at different aspects of First Australians’ traditional life and uncover the knowledge behind them – starting today with astronomy. This article contains the names of Aboriginal people who have passed away. Indigenous Australians have been developing complex knowledge systems for tens of thousands of years. These knowledge systems - which seek to understand, explain, and predict nature - are passed to successive generations through oral tradition. As Ngarinyin elder David Bungal Mowaljarlai explains: “Everything under creation […] is represented in the ground and in the sky.” For this reason, astronomy plays a significant role in these traditions. Western science and Indigenous knowledge systems both try to make sense of the world around us but tend to be conceptualised rather differently. The origin of a natural feature may be explained the same in Indigenous knowledge systems and Western science, but are couched in very different languages. A story recounted by Aunty Mavis Malbunka, a custodian of the Western Arrernte people of the Central Desert, tells how long ago in the Dreaming, a group of women took the form of stars and danced a corroboree (ceremony) in the Milky Way. One of the women put her baby in a wooden basket (coolamon) and placed him on the edge of the Milky Way. As the women danced, the baby slipped off and came tumbling to Earth. When the baby and coolamon fell, they hit the ground, driving the rocks upward. The coolamon covered the baby, hiding him forever, and the baby’s parents – the Morning and Evening Stars – continue to search for their lost child today. If you look at the evening winter sky, you will see the falling coolamon in the sky, below the Milky Way, as the arch of stars in the Western constellation Corona Australis – the Southern Crown. The place where the baby fell is a ring-shaped mountain range 5km wide and 150m high. The Arrernte people call it Tnorala. It is the remnant of a giant crater that formed 142 million years ago, when a comet or asteroid struck the Earth, driving the rocks upward. Tnorala (Gosses Bluff crater). Dementia/Flickr, CC BY-SA Predicting seasonal change When the Pleiades star cluster rises just before the morning sun, it signifies the start of winter to the Pitjantjatjara people of the Central Desert and tells them that dingoes are breeding and will soon be giving birth to pups. The evening appearance of the celestial shark, Baidam traced out by the stars of the Big Dipper (in Ursa Major) tells Torres Strait Islanders that they need to plant their gardens with sugarcane, sweet potato and banana. When the nose of Baidam touches the horizon just after sunset, the shark breeding season has begun and people should stay out of the water as it is very dangerous! Torres Strait Islanders use constellations, such as the shark ‘Baidam’ pictured here, for practical purposes. Brian Robinson Torres Strait Islanders’ close attention to the night sky is further demonstrated in their use of stellar scintillation (twinkling), which enables them to determine the amount of moisture and turbulence in the atmosphere. This allows them to predict weather patterns and seasonal change. Islanders distinguish planets from stars because planets do not twinkle. In Wergaia traditions of western Victoria, the people once faced a drought and food was scarce. Facing starvation, a woman named Marpeankurric set out in search of tucker for the group. After searching high and low, she found an ant nest and dug up thousands of nutritious ant larvae, called bittur. This sustained the people through the winter drought. When she passed away, she ascended to the heavens and became the star Arcturus. When Marpeankurric rises in the evening, she tells the people when to harvest the ant larvae. Arcturus (Marpeankurric – on the lower left) and the Milky Way over Lake Hart. Alex Cherney In each case, Indigenous astronomical knowledge was used to predict changing seasons and the availability of food sources. Behind each of these brief accounts is a complex oral tradition that denotes a moral charter and informs sacred law. An important thing to consider is that small changes in star positions due to stellar proper motion (rate of angular change in position over time) and precession (change in the orientation of Earth’s rotational axis) means that a few thousand years ago, these sky/season relationships would have been out of sync. This means knowledge systems had to evolve over time to accommodate a changing sky. This shows us that what we know about Indigenous astronomical knowledge today is only a tiny fraction of the total knowledge developed in Australia over the past 50,000-plus years. Moving forward As we increase our understanding of Indigenous knowledge systems, we see that Indigenous people did develop a form of science, which is used by Indigenous and non-Indigenous people today. Students in Albury/Wodonga learning about Indigenous astronomy through the Charcoal Nights initiative. Murray Arts Traditional fire practices are used across the country, bush medicines are being used to treat disease, and astronomical knowledge is revealing an intellectual complexity in Indigenous traditions that has gone largely unrecognised. It is time we show our appreciation for Indigenous knowledge and celebrate the many ways we can all learn from this vast accumulation of traditional wisdom. Duane is speaking at The Edges of Astronomy symposium at the Australian Academy of Science in Canberra on December 4, 2014. See also: Indigenous medicine – a fusion of ritual and remedy Astronomy Seasonal change Indigenous knowledge Indigenous science Indigenous science series Duane Hamacher, UNSW Author Duane Hamacher Lecturer and ARC Discovery Early Career Research Fellow, UNSW Disclosure statement Duane Hamacher receives funding from Australian Research Council project DE140101600. Partners UNSW provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.e74cyrqfd","title":"Stories from the sky: astronomy in Indigenous knowledge","url":"https://theconversation.com/stories-from-the-sky-astronomy-in-indigenous-knowledge-33140","id":419},{"content_hash":"6d24b9ac703dab340fc310087946bcf3085e9bd50c53f0d737d8b8949acadca3","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:34.005379+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/etienne-lemaitre-cambrioleur-meurtrier_2072520_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives ETIENNE LEMAITRE cambrioleur meurtrier Par J.-M. TH. Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"ETIENNE LEMAITRE cambrioleur meurtrier","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/etienne-lemaitre-cambrioleur-meurtrier_2072520_1819218.html","id":420},{"content_hash":"dbd0a2ae53469c892b3ee1e9924d5bcff1037f850deacf8858b40cac8951ae9e","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:32.797083+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/promotion-du-burlesque_2071915_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives PROMOTION DU BURLESQUE Par PIERRE DROUIN. Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"PROMOTION DU BURLESQUE","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/promotion-du-burlesque_2071915_1819218.html","id":421},{"content_hash":"6d464fbf8107b349ae5e3908bc2b2f2fe6784631a38ce35b8abdb245c50ad0db","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:32.707033+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-you-need-to-know-about-mucus-and-phlegm-33192","language":"","text":"Health Check: what you need to know about mucus and phlegm Published: November 30, 2014 10:29pm EST Not all gross: mucus is vital for healthy airways. William Brawley/Flickr, CC BY https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-you-need-to-know-about-mucus-and-phlegm-33192 https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-you-need-to-know-about-mucus-and-phlegm-33192 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article We tend to notice mucus only when it’s abnormal and the sticky fluid is expelled from orifices. But actually it’s pretty amazing stuff. Every moment of our lives mucus is protecting our internal organs, including the sexual organs and bowels. Here, though, we’ll focus on the airways. What is mucus? Mucus is 95% water, 3% proteins (including mucin and antibodies), 1% salt and other substances. Mucin droplets absorb water and swell several hundred times in volume within three seconds of release from mucus glands. Mucus strands form cross links, producing a sticky, elastic gel. The solid gel layer acts as a physical barrier to most pathogens and the constant flushing movement prevents the establishment of bacterial biofilms. However, the pore size of the gel mesh means small viruses can easily penetrate it. Anatomy of the airways Both nasal cavities combined have a surface area of 150 square centimetres, aided by bony folds on their side walls. The turbulent airstream means 80% of particles are filtered here, so the adhesive properties of mucus are vital. The blood flow to the nose varies with changing outside temperature, acting like a reverse-cycle air conditioner for the lungs. Mucus is constantly produced (although in lesser quantities during sleep) and moved along. The mucus carries with it dead cells and other dust and debris, ending up in the stomach for recycling. Many cells lining the airways have a long, tail-like hair, called cilia. Cilia beat at ten to 12 times per second, propelling mucus at one millimetre per minute. Lung airways also have cilia, working hard to move mucus uphill against gravity. Mucus from the lungs is sometimes termed “phlegm”, and then “sputum” once it has been spat out. The nose produces over 100 millilitres of mucus a day and the lungs produce approximately 50 millilitres daily. Mucus and airways disease Mucus assists in fighting infection when white blood cells and antibodies are excreted into the mucus film. The amount of mucus and watery liquid is increased to flush away infection, irritants or allergens. Viruses that damage respiratory lining cells also damage cilia, so a runnier mucus layer is more easily propelled. When the cilia just can’t keep up, the body deploys other strategies such as coughing, blowing the nose, sneezing and every parent’s favourite, the snotty nose. Chronic lung disease such as chronic bronchitis and cystic fibrosis cause mucus glands to multiply three to four times above normal levels, resulting in more viscous mucus that the cilia can’t easily clear. Your airways use mucus to catch particles and cellular debris and move it uphill for excretion. Hey Paul Studios/Flickr, CC BY Dehydration and some medications such as nasal decongestants reduce the effectiveness of cilia by lowering the ciliary beat frequency. Even a frequent and repetitive cough can fatigue the cilia, leading to slower transit and increased stickiness of the mucus. That’s why many people have a prolonged “post-nasal drip” cough after colds and hay fever, as the mucus that has dripped down from the back of the nasal cavity isn’t cleared. Saline (salt solutions) increase ciliary beat frequency and have demonstrated benefit in respiratory disease, from sinusitis to cystic fibrosis. A ‘chesty cough’? There is a common belief that a moist (chesty) cough indicates a chest infection. But in young, healthy people, post-nasal drip of mucus is more common than bronchitis or a chest infection. It is extremely difficult to judge whether the sputum in the throat arose in the lungs or dripped down from the back of the nasal cavity. And mucus vibrating near the vocal cords sounds chesty no matter where it came from. But the timing of cough may be helpful for diagnosis: a post-nasal drip cough is worse when lying down and for a while after rising from bed in the morning. Green phlegm Another misconception is that green mucus indicates bacterial infection and thus requires antibiotic treatment. A number of research studies have shown poor correlation between mucus colour and significant infection. The yellow and green colours actually come from white blood cells (leucocytes) that fight infection, but are also more prominent the longer the mucus has “stuck around for”. So morning sputum may be more coloured than later in the day. The diagnosis of a bacterial infection is made when a combination of symptoms and findings exists, with mucus colour not being the most important of these. Unfortunately this misconception extends to some GPs. Patients with green sputum are prescribed antibiotics three times more often than patients coughing clear sputum. However, for patients with a bad cough, this prescription did not improve their recovery. Snotty noses are a flood of mucus to wash away the viral ‘cold’. But it’s no excuse not to use a tissue. Joshua Wachs/Flickr, CC BY-NC-ND Mucus can be colourful stuff, extending from clear to yellowy-green, but also orange, brown and grey. Orange and brown comes from the presence of blood in mucus, of variable concentrations and ages. This blood commonly comes from the nose, due to inflammation, infection or side effects of nasal medication, without an obvious nose bleed. Blood-stained sputum from the lungs may indicate a more serious illness. Milk and mucus Many people believe milk and dairy products stimulate the production of extra mucus, so should be avoided in those with hay fever and asthma. This perception arises from the short-term change in consistency of mucus and saliva in the mouth and throat. But research evidence shows no difference in measured mucus output. Another “blinded” study compared identical-looking dairy and soy products and found no difference in amount or perception of mucus. To spit or swallow? I’m occasionally asked whether swallowing mucus produced with a respiratory infection is harmful. It’s not; luckily the stomach works to neutralise bacteria and recycle the other cellular debris. Some people do report a queasy feeling in the stomach during such infections. This is more likely due to air swallowed from repeated throat clearing and the infection itself, rather than increased mucus getting to the stomach. Freelance journalist and translator Claire Dupré assisted with the drafting of this article. Influenza Biology Respiratory illness Colds Physiology Respiratory disease Health Check Winter health David King, The University of Queensland Author David King David King is a Friend of The Conversation. Senior Lecturer, The University of Queensland Disclosure statement David King does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Queensland provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.47akm9e5p","title":"Health Check: what you need to know about mucus and phlegm","url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-what-you-need-to-know-about-mucus-and-phlegm-33192","id":422},{"content_hash":"61ae004b2605b21ba1c111a522f675ea2a793dd0d4fdbd6bcb85604576f472ef","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:32.624304+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-documents-are-required-for-kyc","language":"","text":"What Documents Are Required For KYC?ByPriscilla, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 30, 2023•5mins ReadDocuments Required for KYC3. Proof of Income4. Business Proof5. Other DocumentsFAQBottom LineShare this postKYC, which stands for Know Your Customer, is a term used to describe the process of identifying and verifying the identity of customers or clients. This process is important for businesses in various industries, such as banking, finance, and real estate, as it helps to prevent fraud, money laundering, and other illegal activities. KYC compliance is mandated by various government agencies and regulators around the world. The documents required for KYC vary depending on the industry and the country in which the business is operating. However, some general documents are commonly required for KYC purposes. In this article, we will discuss the documents required for KYC and their importance. Documents Required for KYC The most important documents required for KYC include: 1. Proof of Identity The most important document for KYC is proof of identity. This is a document that proves the identity of the customer. The most common documents used for proof of identity are government-issued identification documents like a passport, driver’s license, or national ID card. a. Passport A passport is a travel document issued by the government that proves the identity and nationality of the holder. It is an internationally recognized document and is often used as proof of identity for KYC purposes. To verify the authenticity of the passport, the business must check the security features of the passport, such as holograms, watermarks, and biometric information. b. Driver’s License A driver’s license is a government-issued document that allows the holder to operate a motor vehicle. It contains the name, address, and date of birth of the holder, and is often used as proof of identity for KYC purposes. To verify the authenticity of the driver’s license, the business must check the security features of the license, such as holograms, watermarks, and microprinting. c. National ID Card A national ID card is a government-issued document that contains the name, address, and date of birth of the holder. It is often used as proof of identity for KYC purposes in countries where it is mandatory to have a national ID card. To verify the authenticity of the national ID card, the business must check the security features of the card, such as holograms, watermarks, and microprinting. d. Voter ID Card A voter ID card is a government-issued document that contains the customer's name, photograph, address, and date of birth. It is commonly used as an identity proof document in India. e. PAN Card A PAN card is a government-issued document that is used for tax purposes in India. It contains the customer's name, photograph, and date of birth. 2. Proof of Address In addition to proof of identity, businesses also need to collect and verify proof of address. This is a document that proves the address of the customer. The most common documents used for proof of address are utility bills, bank statements, and government-issued documents like a driver’s license or national ID card. a. Utility bills Utility bills like electricity bills, water bills, or gas bills are often used as proof of address for KYC purposes. The bill must be recent (usually within the last three months) and must contain the name and address of the customer. To verify the authenticity of the bill, the business must check that it is an original bill and not a photocopy or printout. b. Bank statements Bank statements are also commonly used as proof of address for KYC purposes. The statement must be recent (usually within the last three months) and must contain the name and address of the customer. To verify the authenticity of the statement, the business must check that it is an original statement and not a photocopy or printout. c. Driver’s license or national ID card In some countries, a driver’s license or national ID card can be used as both proofs of identity and proof of address. The address on the document must be the same as the address of the customer. To verify the authenticity of the document, the business must check the security features of the document, such as holograms, watermarks, and microprinting. d. Bank Statement A bank statement is another commonly accepted address proof document. It contains the customer's name and address, as well as details of their bank account. e. Rental Agreement A rental agreement is a document that is used to verify the customer's residential address if they are living in a rented property. It contains the customer's name and address, as well as details of the rental agreement. f. Passport A passport can also be used as an address proof document if the customer's address is mentioned in the passport. g. Voter ID Card A voter ID card can also be used as an address proof document if the customer's address is mentioned in the card. Recommended - How To Get A Proof of Address 3. Proof of Income Some businesses may also require proof of income as part of the KYC process. This is a document that proves the income of the customer. The most common documents used for proof of income are: a. Salary Slip A salary slip is a document that is provided by the employer to the employee. It contains details of the employee's salary, including the amount, deductions, and taxes. b. Bank Statement A bank statement can also be used as an income proof document. It contains details of the customer's income, including deposits, withdrawals, and other transactions. c. Income Tax Return An income tax return is a document that is filed by the customer with the government to report their income and taxes. It contains details of the customer's income, 4. Business Proof Business proof is required for KYC when the customer is a business entity or a self-employed individual. The purpose of business proof is to establish the existence of the business and to confirm the identity of the owner. Examples of business proof documents include: a. Business registration certificate b. Partnership deed c. Memorandum of Association d. Articles of Association Business registration certificate is the most commonly accepted business proof document as it establishes the existence of the business and contains the name and address of the owner. Partnership deeds, Memorandum of Association, and Articles of Association are also acceptable as they contain details about the business and its ownership. 5. Other Documents Apart from the above-mentioned documents, some institutions may require additional documents for KYC purposes. These documents may include: a. References from other financial institutions b. Photographs c. Signature specimens References from other financial institutions are required to establish the customer's financial history and to confirm their identity. Photographs and signature specimens are required to ensure that the customer's photograph and signature match the ones on the identity proof document.INTREASTING READ: What is KYC? Understanding KYC & How it Works (Updated) FAQ 1. What documents are required for KYC in bankWhen opening an account, banks must comply with regulatory standards, making it important to know what documents are required for KYC in bank processes. Typically, banks request:1. Proof of Identity: Passport, national ID card, driver’s license, or voter’s card.2. Proof of Address: Utility bill, tenancy agreement, or recent bank statement.3. Passport-sized Photographs: For proper identification records.Understanding what documents are required for KYC in bank ensures smooth onboarding while helping institutions prevent fraud and money laundering. Whether for savings or business accounts, the answer to what documents are required for KYC in bank is centered on valid identity and address verification. 2. What documents are required for KYC verificationMany customers ask what documents are required for KYC verification, especially when completing digital onboarding or in-branch checks. Generally, the following are needed:1. A government-issued ID such as a passport, national ID, or driver’s license.2. Proof of residential address, such as a utility bill or bank statement (not older than 3 months).3. Additional documentation, like employment letters or tax records, may be required depending on the institution or reason for KYC verification.By clarifying what documents are required for KYC verification, financial institutions ensure transparency, compliance, and fraud prevention. Always check with your bank to confirm exactly what documents are required for KYC verification, since requirements may differ. 3. What documents are required for KYC current accountOpening a current account usually demands more details than a savings account, making it necessary to know what documents are required for KYC current account checks. These often include:1. For Individuals: National ID, proof of address, and passport photographs.2. For Businesses: Certificate of incorporation, tax identification number (TIN), board resolution, and valid IDs of signatories.Banks enforce these rules to strengthen compliance and protect against illicit activities. By understanding what documents are required for KYC current account, applicants avoid delays during account opening. Whether you’re an entrepreneur or individual, ensure you provide all documents required for KYC current account requirements upfront. 4. What are KYC requirementsMany people ask, what are KYC requirements, since they form the foundation of customer due diligence in banking. KYC requirements generally include:1. Identity Verification – A government-issued ID such as passport, driver’s license, or national ID.2. Proof of Address – Utility bills, tenancy agreements, or recent bank statements.3. Risk Assessment – Source of funds and occupation details.4. Ongoing Monitoring – Regular checks for suspicious activity.Understanding what are KYC requirements ensures customers meet compliance obligations while institutions mitigate risks. In practice, KYC requirements is about proving identity, confirming addresses, and monitoring transactions for security.Bottom Line When it comes to identifying and verifying your clients, certain documents will make for a smooth process. Documents used in formal transactions such as Banks statements or Government issued/approved documents are best used in this process. Youverify is your go-to RegTech Company for all your financial transactions. Request a Demo. Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"What Documents Are Required For KYC?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-documents-are-required-for-kyc","id":423},{"content_hash":"2dbb87fb84527602a9dfc7a204cdaabdfc26ce8eb04c6383f2a65bef19ee3539","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:32.332977+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/csp/","language":"","text":"Content Security Policy¶ Content Security Policy (CSP) is a web security standard that helps prevent content injection attacks by restricting the sources from which content can be loaded. It plays an important role in a comprehensive security strategy. For configuration instructions in a Django project, see the Using CSP documentation. For an HTTP guide about CSP, see the MDN Guide on CSP. Overview¶ The Content-Security-Policy specification defines two complementary headers: Content-Security-Policy: Enforces the CSP policy, blocking content that violates the defined directives. Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: Reports CSP violations without blocking content, allowing for non-intrusive testing. Each policy is composed of one or more directives and their values, which together instruct the browser on how to handle specific types of content. When the ContentSecurityPolicyMiddleware is enabled, Django automatically builds and attaches the appropriate headers to each response based on the configured settings, unless they have already been set by another layer. Settings¶ The ContentSecurityPolicyMiddleware is configured using the following settings: SECURE_CSP: defines the enforced Content Security Policy. SECURE_CSP_REPORT_ONLY: defines a report-only Content Security Policy. These settings can be used independently or together Use SECURE_CSP alone to enforce a policy that has already been tested and verified. Use SECURE_CSP_REPORT_ONLY on its own to evaluate a new policy without disrupting site behavior. This mode does not block violations, it only logs them. It’s useful for testing and monitoring, but provides no protection against active threats. Use both to maintain an enforced baseline while experimenting with changes. Even for well-established policies, continuing to collect reports can help detect regressions, unexpected changes in behavior, or potential tampering in production environments. Policy violation reports¶ When a CSP violation occurs, browsers typically log details to the developer console, providing immediate feedback during development. To also receive these reports programmatically, the policy must include a reporting directive such as report-uri that specifies where violation data should be sent. Django supports configuring these directives via the SECURE_CSP_REPORT_ONLY settings, but reports will only be issued by the browser if the policy explicitly includes a valid reporting directive. Django does not provide built-in functionality to receive, store, or process violation reports. To collect and analyze them, you must implement your own reporting endpoint or integrate with a third-party monitoring service. CSP constants¶ Django provides predefined constants representing common CSP source expression keywords such as 'self', 'none', and 'unsafe-inline'. These constants are intended for use in the directive values defined in the settings. They are available through the CSP enum, and using them is recommended over raw strings. This helps avoid common mistakes such as typos, improper quoting, or inconsistent formatting, and ensures compliance with the CSP specification. class CSP[source]¶ Enum providing standardized constants for common CSP source expressions. NONE¶ Represents 'none'. Blocks loading resources for the given directive. REPORT_SAMPLE¶ Represents 'report-sample'. Instructs the browser to include a sample of the violating code in reports. Note that this may expose sensitive data. SELF¶ Represents 'self'. Allows loading resources from the same origin (same scheme, host, and port). STRICT_DYNAMIC¶ Represents 'strict-dynamic'. Allows execution of scripts loaded by a trusted script (e.g., one with a valid nonce or hash), without needing 'unsafe-inline'. UNSAFE_EVAL¶ Represents 'unsafe-eval'. Allows use of eval() and similar JavaScript functions. Strongly discouraged. UNSAFE_HASHES¶ Represents 'unsafe-hashes'. Allows inline event handlers and some javascript: URIs when their content hashes match a policy rule. Requires CSP Level 3+. UNSAFE_INLINE¶ Represents 'unsafe-inline'. Allows execution of inline scripts, styles, and javascript: URLs. Generally discouraged, especially for scripts. WASM_UNSAFE_EVAL¶ Represents 'wasm-unsafe-eval'. Permits compilation and execution of WebAssembly code without enabling 'unsafe-eval' for scripts. NONCE¶ Django-specific placeholder value (\"<CSP_NONCE_SENTINEL>\") used in script-src or style-src directives to activate nonce-based CSP. This string is replaced at runtime by the ContentSecurityPolicyMiddleware with a secure, random nonce that is generated for each request. See detailed explanation in Nonce usage. Decorators¶ Django provides decorators to control the Content Security Policy headers on a per-view basis. These allow overriding or disabling the enforced or report-only policy for specific views, providing fine-grained control when the global settings are not sufficient. Applying these overrides fully replaces the base CSP: they do not merge with existing rules. They can be used alongside the constants defined in CSP. Warning Weakening or disabling a CSP policy on any page can compromise the security of the entire site. Because of the “same origin” policy, an attacker could exploit a vulnerability on one page to access other parts of the site. csp_override(config)(view)[source]¶ Overrides the Content-Security-Policy header for the decorated view using directives in the same format as the SECURE_CSP setting. The config argument must be a mapping with the desired CSP directives. If config is an empty mapping ({}), no CSP enforcement header will be added to the response returned by that view, effectively disabling CSP for that view. Examples: from django.http import HttpResponse from django.utils.csp import CSP from django.views.decorators.csp import csp_override @csp_override( { \"default-src\": [CSP.SELF], \"img-src\": [CSP.SELF, \"data:\"], } ) def my_view(request): return HttpResponse(\"Custom Content-Security-Policy header applied\") @csp_override({}) def my_other_view(request): return HttpResponse(\"No Content-Security-Policy header added\") csp_report_only_override(config)(view)[source]¶ Overrides the Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only header for the decorated view using directives in the same format as the SECURE_CSP_REPORT_ONLY setting. Like csp_override(), the config argument must be a mapping with the desired CSP directives. If config is an empty mapping ({}), no CSP report-only header will be added to the response returned by that view, effectively disabling report-only CSP for that view. Examples: from django.http import HttpResponse from django.utils.csp import CSP from django.views.decorators.csp import csp_report_only_override @csp_report_only_override( { \"default-src\": [CSP.SELF], \"img-src\": [CSP.SELF, \"data:\"], \"report-uri\": \"https://mysite.com/csp-report/\", } ) def my_view(request): return HttpResponse(\"Custom Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only header applied\") @csp_report_only_override({}) def my_other_view(request): return HttpResponse(\"No Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only header added\") The examples above assume function-based views. For class-based views, see the guide for decorating class-based views. Nonce usage¶ A CSP nonce (“number used once”) is a unique, random value generated per HTTP response. Django supports nonces as a secure way to allow specific inline <script> or <style> elements to execute without relying on 'unsafe-inline'. Nonces are enabled by including the special placeholder NONCE in the relevant directive(s) of your CSP settings, such as script-src or style-src. When present, the ContentSecurityPolicyMiddleware will generate a nonce and insert the corresponding nonce-<value> source expression into the CSP header. To use this nonce in templates, the csp() context processor needs to be enabled. It adds a csp_nonce variable to the template context. Changed in version 6.1: The csp_nonce_attr template tag was added, including support for rendering Media objects. For inline <script> and <style> elements, include the nonce directly using the context variable: <script nonce=\"{{ csp_nonce }}\"> // This inline JavaScript will be allowed. </script> For external <script src=\"...\"> and <link rel=\"stylesheet\"> elements, use the csp_nonce_attr template tag: <script src=\"/path/to/script.js\" {% csp_nonce_attr %}></script> <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/path/to/style.css\" {% csp_nonce_attr %}> To render a Media object’s assets with the nonce applied, pass the object to the csp_nonce_attr template tag: {% csp_nonce_attr form.media %} The browser will only execute inline elements that include a nonce=<value> attribute matching the one specified in the Content-Security-Policy (or Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only) header. This mechanism provides fine-grained control over which inline code is allowed to run. If a template includes the CSP nonce but the policy does not include NONCE, the HTML will include a nonce attribute, but the header will lack the required source expression. In this case, the browser will block the inline script or style (or report it for report-only configurations). Nonce generation and caching¶ Django’s nonce generation is lazy: the middleware only generates a nonce if {{ csp_nonce }} is accessed during template rendering. This avoids unnecessary work for pages that do not use nonces. However, because nonces must be unique per request, extra care is needed when using full-page caching (e.g., Django’s cache middleware, CDN caching). Serving cached responses with previously generated nonces may result in reuse across users and requests. Although such responses may still appear to work (since the nonce in the CSP header and HTML content match), reuse defeats the purpose of the nonce and weakens security. To ensure nonce-based policies remain effective: Avoid caching full responses that include {{ csp_nonce }} or csp_nonce_attr. If caching is necessary, use a strategy that injects a fresh nonce on each request, or consider refactoring your application to avoid inline scripts and styles altogether.","title":"Content Security Policy¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/csp/","id":424},{"content_hash":"f5e7d6df4611c5991a637da666e97b745b13e2db3311d91b6ff7b2628f3a9c36","domain":"reallygoodemails.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:32.098344+00:00","final_url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/healthy-skin-month","language":"","text":"Healthy Skin Month | Email template - RGE StudioNow you have zero excuses to NOT design really good emails. Introducing RGE StudioRead more Find inspirationAll categoriesBrowse by typePopular right nowBehavioral Enhancement Inaugural Industry Miscellaneous Promotional Punctual Seasonal Templates BehavioralAbandoned CartComment NotificationCurated ContentCustomer AppreciationCustomer ServiceDiscoveryEngagementFeedback and Survey and PollFollow-UpLoyalty / RewardsPost Purchase / Recent PurchaseProduct RecommendationsReferralRetention / Re-engagementReview and TestimonialSummaryThank YouUnsubscribeCurated by humans since 2014.Browse all categoriesPopular right nowCreate emailsProductWhat is RGE StudioThe email builder that gets you.FeaturesEverything you need to make email happen.Product tourSnoop around at your own pace.Works withEmail & page templatesStart from something really good.IntegrationsYour favorite ESP or CRM can come too.Salesforce ecosystemCreate in Studio, send from Salesforce.MCPBuild emails from your AI tools.SolutionsHigher educationBetter email for busy campus teams.NonprofitsMore mission, less busywork.IntegrationPerfect for Salesforce usersThe perfect addition to your Salesforce setup? An email editor that actually works.Learn moreSOC 2 Type II · ISO 27001 · DPF · AWS PartnerPricingTalk to usResourcesLearnBlogTips, trends, and teardowns.AcademyGet better at email. For free.Help centerStuck with RGE Studio? Start here.ConnectVirtual eventsCome learn stuff with email people.UnspamFor folks who hate boring email conferences.Feedback FridayReal emails, honest critique.NewsletterReally good stuff, in your inbox.ToolsAI prompt librarySmart starting points for your emails.Email signature generatorLook pro in every sign-off.Report2026 RGE Email SurveyFresh data on how email teams actually work: email got faster, but not for everyone.Read the report300,000 email pros learn and hang out here.Next webinarsJoin the newsletterPricingTalk to SalesBack to the template catalogREALLY GOOD EMAILSAboutSchoolFeedback FridayEventsContactCode of ConductEmail signature generatorPrompt libraryRGE STUDIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"Healthy Skin Month | Email template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/healthy-skin-month","id":425},{"content_hash":"5486a9df2483bbee83c789e576386061aa50219e534dd9dbb1a851332bdcf38f","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:31.733619+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/astrology/horoscope/aquarius-horoscope-today-october-09-2026-stuck-money-returns-as-patience-helps-you-avoid-impulsive-choices/articleshow/134793539.cms","language":"","text":"Aquarius Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: Stuck money returns as patience helps you avoid impulsive choices - The Times of IndiaEditionININUSGCCEnglishEnglishहिन्दीमराठीಕನ್ನಡதமிழ்বাংলাമലയാളംతెలుగుગુજરાતીWeatherSign InTOIAstrologyHoroscopeSun Sign PredictionsCompatibility CalculatorYearly Horoscope 2026Chinese HoroscopeNumerologyNumerology PredictionsPlanets TransitsGemstones Vastu & feng shuikundali dasha & RemediesReligionMonthly HoroscopeOthersToday's ePaperLiveNewsAstrology NewsHoroscope NewsAquarius Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: Stuck money returns as patience helps you avoid impulsive choicesTrendingHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayAquarius Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: Stuck money returns as patience helps you avoid impulsive choicesAstroDevam, Dr Achary Kalki Krishnan & Anita Baranwal / TOI Astrology / Oct 09, 2026, 05:30 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint General:Some old block begins to loosen today. Money that was stuck for a long time can finally move towards you, and that alone may change your mood. Yet the day is mixed. The emotional restlessness and dissatisfaction of some results is reflected in the same chart as the financial release. Yes, something does get better, but your heart may grumble. Human, isn't it?Sensitive ground of the Moon and impulsive reactions of Mars. That is why it is not a good idea to make emotional decisions now. You will have to stop and think before you answer, purchase, resign, or make statements of the family you can't take back. Help needy people if you can. This is a simple thing you can do that will calm your own mind and alleviate the heaviness you feel.Love and Relationship:Children today do better and you get support from them, this can help to take the edge off. If you have grown children; they may help with technology, errands, travel plans or one overdue conversation. If they are younger, they may make you feel happier with their effort or affection than you might expect.In romance, be cautious of over emotionalizing. You might assume that you need more evidence or see more in one delayed call or short message. Venus doesn't cause the problem of love, only your own mind can create ripples in the still waters. After cooling off, speak, not before!Education and Career:Despite putting in the necessary work, you may be disappointed with outcomes. This is because Saturn does not like to see you being impatient and to have to make slow progress. A good plan shouldn't be abandoned because today it did not go as you hoped. Check, modify, and repeat.When you're in business or a side hustle, speculation can work in your favour, but play it safe! In employment, precautions are to be taken during commuting to meetings or shuttle trips from one branch of the office to another. Avoid getting too fast on the road, when your mind is not present. Children or junior colleagues may actually be more helpful than expected today.Money and Finance:Blocked money returning is the headline, and that's a relief. It may come as a pending payment, refund, client clearance, or someone finally paying back dues. Good time too for sensible speculation, but only if you stay detached and don't bet from frustration.Avoid emotional purchases. That means costly online orders following an unpleasant discussion or poor performance at work. Save recovered funds in a secure location before saving other funds. There's a time and place for bigger plans in the future.Health and Well-being:Care must be taken when driving along the road. This warning is important now because Mars can bring quick, but not necessarily balanced responses. Never use the phone when crossing, driving or even walking in traffic. It's only a split second of inattention.The day might seem more burdensome than it actually is. Support your nervous system with meals, water and a pause from over-thinking. More can be settled with prayer, charity or quiet breathing, than argument.Tip for the Day: Pause before reacting, especially while driving, spending, or sending messages.Get the latest astrology news and horoscope updates. Download the TOI App.Read Also: Aquarius Daily Horoscope | Aquarius Weekly Horoscope | Aquarius Monthly HoroscopeAbout the AuthorAstroDevamAstroDevam is a premium organisation providing ancient and authentic knowledge of Astrology, Vastu, Numerology, and Innovative Corporate Solutions with a contemporary perspective. AstroDevam, having patrons in more than 100 countries, has been promoted by Achary Anita Baranwal and Achary Kalki Krishnan, who not only have Master's Degrees in Astrology, but are engaged in teaching Scientific Astrology, Vastu, and Numerology for more than three decades.Read MoreEnd of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaHot PicksCBSE Date SheetGeorgia Medical Degree RowAparna JhaStock market crashH-1B ProgramRBI Repo Rate HikeNana PatekarWeather tomorrowUS Marines in JapanAmazon PrimeTop TrendingDonna KelcePatrick Mahomes MotherSam BealAsim MunirUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestSimone BilesCJP Chappal ProtestAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTrending StoriesIn AstrologyEntire WebsiteCapricorn Horoscope Today: Smooth progress in work, love and money; use focus to finish tasksPractise Peace Amid Problems: Dhammapada 202 urges calm now; small rests build steadinessSagittarius Horoscope Today: Career gains and side income loom; support your child gentlyDaily Scorpio Horoscope: Support boosts gains; put savings before pleasureLibra Horoscope Today: Low confidence and money strain; avoid junk food and impulse buysLeo Horoscope Today: Use your words gently to heal family strains; favour steady saving nowVirgo Horoscope Today: Moon brings clarity and calm focus; love 