DIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"Healthy Meal Prep Webinar Reminder | Email template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/healthy-meal-prep-webinar-reminder","id":442},{"content_hash":"73a36db07a0a041caa220dd82fd9c157b9432c6a3ed8881ba63cad8b1eefdabd","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:27.226092+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-28/kazem-hamad-sentenced-to-life-in-iraq-jail/106968276","language":"","text":"Former Melbourne organised crime kingpin sentenced to life in prison by Iraqi authoritiesBy Middle East correspondent Matthew Doran with staffTopic:Law, Crime and JusticePosted Tue 28 Jul 2026 at 4:40pmTue 28 Jul 2026 at 4:40pmTue 28 Jul 2026 at 4:40pm, updated Tue 28 Jul 2026 at 6:34pmTue 28 Jul 2026 at 6:34pmTue 28 Jul 2026 at 6:34pmAn arrest photo of Kazem Hamad released by Australian Federal Police. (Supplied: Australian Federal Police)In short:Kazem Hamad, a former Melbourne organised crime kingpin who was deported in 2023, has been sentenced to life in jail by an Iraqi court.Iraq's National Center for International Judicial Cooperation said Hamad was responsible for importing large quantities of drugs into Iraq and Australia.Hamad has been a key person of interest relating to several firebombings of Victorian tobacco shops and businesses.abc.net.au/news/kazem-hamad-sentenced-to-life-in-iraq-jail/106968276Link copiedShareShare articleA former Melbourne organised crime kingpin who was deported to Iraq in 2023, has been sentenced to life in jail by an Iraqi court after being convicted of drug trafficking.Kazem Hamad, also known as Kadhim Malik Hamad Rabah al-Hajami, was alleged to be one of the key players in Melbourne's tobacco wars.In a statement, Iraq's National Center for International Judicial Cooperation (NCIJC) announced the life sentence had been imposed by the Karkh Criminal Court in Baghdad, and said he was responsible for importing large quantities of drugs into Iraq and Australia.\"Kaz\" Hamad, also known as Kadhim Malik Hamad Rabah al-Hajami, is alleged to be one of the key players in Melbourne's tobacco wars. (Supplied)\"The convict is considered one of the most wanted individuals by Iraqi and international judiciaries,\" the NCIJC said.\"He is responsible for importing large quantities of narcotics into Iraq and Australia, smuggling heroin, and is linked to transnational organised crime gangs involved in drug trafficking, money laundering, murder, kidnapping, extortion, and organised violence.\"The accused is one of the most dangerous wanted men in the world,\" a statement from Iraq's Supreme Judicial Council also said in January.\"He is responsible for importing large quantities of drugs into Iraq and Australia, as well as smuggling heroin.\"He is also involved with the most prominent organised crime gangs in Australia — Sydney, responsible for shootings, murders, kidnappings, violent assaults, extortion, and drug imports.\"Hamad has been a person of interest relating to firebombings at Victorian tobacco shops and businesses.His arrest in Iraq was announced in January, after the Australian Federal Police provided information to local authorities in late 2025 as part of its months-long Operation Carmen.Kazem \"Kaz\" Hamad was deported from Australia in 2023. (Supplied)A month later, the AFP said Hamad's arrest had sparked infighting among the crime syndicate he was accused of leading.AFP Commissioner Krissy Barrett described Hamad at the time as her \"number one target\", as her agency released a photo of him in Iraqi custody.\"I want every offender connected to Hamad or his syndicate to look long and hard at this photo,\" Commissioner Barrett said in a statement.\"And I want to be very clear — just because Hamad is in custody, the AFP's job is not done.\"She said his deportation had caused a power vacuum and infighting within his crime gang.The AFP said it had been informed by Iraqi authorities that the life sentence had been handed down against Hamad.\"The AFP notes the work of our important partner. The AFP will provide further comment at an appropriate time,\" a spokesperson told the ABC.Victorian authorities remained tight-lipped about Hamad's sentencing on Tuesday.In a statement, a spokeswoman said: \"Victoria Police acknowledges the reported sentencing decision of the National Center for International Judicial Cooperation in Iraq today\".\"All further enquiries should be directed to the NCIJC.\"Deported amid tobacco warsHamad was deported from Australia in 2023 and has been a key person of interest relating to firebombings at Victorian tobacco shops and businesses.Kazem Hamad imagesImages of alleged tobacco wars ringleader Kazem Hamad have been released after his arrest in Iraq last month.In a Victorian court case, police said they believed Hamad orchestrated a series of firebombing attacks on rivals or Melbourne shop owners who defied his alleged standover men.They said exorbitant extortion fees became known in Melbourne as the \"Kaz tax\".Senior police earlier confirmed that he was being looked at as part of the investigation into the firebombing of the Adass Israel Synagogue in December 2024.The AFP suspects the syndicate linked to Hamad is operating in five Australian states and one territory.After Hamad's arrest, Home Affairs Minister Tony Burke celebrated the moment.\"He thought he was offshore, he was out of reach … well he wasn't,\" Mr Burke told 7.30.\"We are talking about someone at the apex of a criminal network. For a whole process of organised crime in Australia road after road after road has continued to lead back to one individual.\"He came to Australia on a visa as a teenager from Iraq with his family in the late 1990s.Over the next 15 years he transformed into a career criminal, surrounding himself with major underworld figures.Former Federal Police officer and illicit tobacco expert Rohan Pike said Hamad's incarceration had so far done little to stop waves of firebombings and extortions in Victoria.\"He has had trusted lieutenants all along,\" Mr Pike told the ABC.\"Even if you arrest a few at the top, some will step up and take their place.\"Referring to the many lower-level alleged offenders believed to have carried out arsons and extortions in Victoria on behalf of those connected to Hamad, Mr Pike said: \"There could be hundreds ready to work for his 313 syndicate.\"Mr Pike said Iraqi authorities' decision to sentence him to life behind bars rather than to execute him, which is a possible sentence for drug trafficking in the Middle Eastern country, would have been a positive diplomatic choice for its relations with Australia.\"If he was executed that would have caused immense concern for Australian authorities.\"There are so many unknowns about the Iraqi system, but I assured they did have evidence of drug trafficking against him.\"Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerRelated storiesImages of alleged tobacco wars kingpin released as police vow to crush syndicateTopic:Law, Crime and Justice'Number one target' tobacco kingpin Kazem Hamad arrested in IraqTopic:Drug OffencesCaptured tobacco kingpin thought 'he was out of reach', says BurkeTopic:CrimeRelated topicsIraqLaw, Crime and JusticeMelbourneTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Former Melbourne organised crime kingpin sentenced to life in prison by Iraqi authorities","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-07-28/kazem-hamad-sentenced-to-life-in-iraq-jail/106968276","id":443},{"content_hash":"3222a154967de63cf84245ab3bddd3b549c7f957be101ec64e977307b923e3a6","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:26.760331+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/l-archipretre-liperovsky-devra-quitter-l-eglise-de-sainte-genevieve-des-bois_2073377_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives L'ARCHIPRÊTRE LIPEROVSKY DEVRA QUITTER l'église de Sainte-Geneviève-des-Bois Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"L'ARCHIPRÊTRE LIPEROVSKY DEVRA QUITTER l'église de Sainte-Geneviève-des-Bois","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/l-archipretre-liperovsky-devra-quitter-l-eglise-de-sainte-genevieve-des-bois_2073377_1819218.html","id":444},{"content_hash":"24815a947b28e19f8f07a83115477209cbf4e162ba0abdc1a8099a8c61790636","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:26.692849+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-how-to-avoid-getting-ill-from-chicken-34795","language":"","text":"Health Check: how to avoid getting ill from chicken Published: December 14, 2014 10:24pm EST Avoiding undercooked chicken meat or liver will help prevent infection. Alexa Clark/Flickr, CC BY-NC https://theconversation.com/health-check-how-to-avoid-getting-ill-from-chicken-34795 https://theconversation.com/health-check-how-to-avoid-getting-ill-from-chicken-34795 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Chicken meat is an extremely popular and affordable source of animal protein, and each Australian now consumes approximately 43 kilograms of it every year. But chicken is also uniquely prone to making people ill, due to contamination with bacteria. Most people would know chicken meat can cause Salmonella infections, but there is a lesser known yet more common bacteria that also loves chicken meat and can make you ill. Campylobacter actually causes very similar clinical illness to Salmonella, but is less likely to cause outbreaks (defined as two or more people infected after eating a common food or meal) so gets less publicity than the outbreak-prone Salmonella. Making you ill Campylobacter recently caused a stir in the United Kingdom, after a survey found eight out of ten portions of poultry meat sold in supermarkets there were contaminated. The situation in Australia is likely to be similar although limited recent data have been published. Campylobacter is, in fact, one of the leading causes of gastroenteritis from food around the world. Infection with this bacteria results in diarrhoea, abdominal cramping, and fever lasting up to a week. And while it’s rarely fatal, it can also result in chronic illnesses, such as reactive arthritis, irritable bowel syndrome, and Guillain–Barré syndrome. Campylobacter is commonly found in the intestinal tracts of birds, but may not make them sick. Matt Davis/Flickr, CC BY You can become ill from Campylobacter after eating contaminated foods, having close contact with infected animals or people, or by drinking contaminated water or unpasteurised milk. The link between contaminated chicken meat and Campylobacter infections in humans is not new, but it is possible to reduce the risk. Consider the case of New Zealand. In the mid-2000s, that country had among the highest rates of Campylobacter infection in the world. Studies examining the causes of infection highlighted contaminated chicken meat as the main source. In 2006, advocacy by New Zealand public health professionals led to improved controls, including interventions by the chicken meat industry and government, resulting in dramatic declines in infection. Since that time, New Zealand has conducted surveillance of molecular strains of Campylobacter to understand the sources of infections and monitor interventions in the food supply. Australia has had consistently lower rates of Campylobacter infections than New Zealand, but higher incidence than countries such as the United States. State-based health departments in Australia have actually reported a steady increase in cases in recent decades, although there has been a decline in the last couple of years as shown by the figure below. But these numbers don’t provide the whole picture. Many people who become infected don’t go to a doctor or get tested, meaning their illness is never reported. There are probably as many as 179,000 cases of foodborne Campylobacter infection in Australia annually. A 2008 Australian study on the sources of such infection in 2001-2, found 29% were likely to be due to chicken meat, 2% due to offal, 3% due to contact with puppies and 2% due to chickens less than six months old. But the majority of the infection sources were unable to be identified. Protecting against infection Campylobacter is commonly found in the intestinal tracts of birds, but may not make them sick. Meat as well as the processing environment can become contaminated during the slaughter and processing of chickens, particularly when removing the feathers and intestines. Recent declines in Campylobacter infection in Australia are encouraging and have occurred despite increasing consumption of chicken meat. Nevertheless, consumers need to be on their game when it comes to handling and cooking meat from chickens and other types of poultry. There are steps you can take to minimise your risk of infection. First, when storing chicken meat in the fridge, ensure it doesn’t touch food that will be eaten without cooking. It’s also important not to cut chicken meat up on a board that’s used for ready-to-eat foods, such as salads. Thoroughly wash all chopping boards and utensils used for raw chicken meat, as well as your hands. suanie/Flickr, CC BY-NC-SA Even the juices from chicken meat and packaging can be contaminated with Camplyobacter. Avoid washing poultry prior to cooking to not contaminate your kitchen sink and benches. Make sure juices can’t drip on other foods in the fridge. And thoroughly wash all chopping boards and utensils used for raw chicken meat, as well as your hands; hot soapy water is vital. Finally, cook poultry meat to an internal temperature of 75° Celsius (using a thermometer) and don’t eat undercooked meat or liver. One reason we don’t see many foodborne Campylobacter outbreaks is because the bacteria doesn’t grow well on food, having a narrow growth range of between 37°to 42°C. The bacteria is, in fact, quite fragile, and can be killed by drying, exposure to oxygen-rich environments, and highs and lows in temperature, such as that achieved by cooking or freezing. But lowering rates of contamination of chickens with Campylobacter requires government, industry, retailers, and consumers to work together, which is what the UK is doing to resolve its poultry crisis. In 2012, the Australian government and industry developed primary production and processing standards. It may be some time before their effects can be assessed. In the meantime, people cooking with raw chicken meat should be fastidious about food safety in the kitchen. Food-borne illness Health Check Bacterial contamination Campylobacter Salmonella Poultry Martyn Kirk, Cameron Moffatt, Australian National University Authors Martyn Kirk Convener, Master of Philosophy in Applied Epidemiology, Australian National University Cameron Moffatt PhD candidate, Australian National University Disclosure statement Martyn Kirk receives funding from the Australian Research Council, the Commonwealth Department of Health, Food Standards Australia New Zealand, and NSW Food Authority. Cameron Moffatt receives funding from the National Health and Medical Research Council. Mr Moffatt works part time for the ACT Government Health Directorate. The views expressed in this article do not reflect those of his employer. Partners Australian National University provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.4vq9jxdxt","title":"Health Check: how to avoid getting ill from chicken","url":"https://theconversation.com/health-check-how-to-avoid-getting-ill-from-chicken-34795","id":445},{"content_hash":"a17c0db9c90dc5883a2ab1fd4085e3365bcbb77bc56c3ec5fb8393a0f076e931","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:26.668140+00:00","final_url":"https://i0.wp.com/prnigeria.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/efcc-chairman-olukoyede-1024x1020-1-qkr7d6xkdra6tie5xbqp12jgi8eu4jmo81rfufglco2.jpg?fit=783%2C724&ssl=1","language":"","text":"ÿØÿà�\u0010JFIF�\u0001\u0001��\u0001�\u0001��ÿá�`Exif��MM�*���\b�\u0004\u0001��\u0004���\u0001��\u0003\u000f\u0001\u0001�\u0004���\u0001��\u0002Ô\u00012�\u0002���\u0014���>i�\u0004���\u0001���R����2024:04:09 14:25:33�������ÿâ\u0002(ICC_PROFILE�\u0001\u0001��\u0002\u0018����\u0002\u0010��mntrRGB XYZ ������������acsp���������������������������\u0001��öÖ�\u0001����Ó-��������������������������������������������������� desc���ð���trXYZ��\u0001d���\u0014gXYZ��\u0001x���\u0014bXYZ��\u0001���\u0014rTRC��\u0001 ���(gTRC��\u0001 ���(bTRC��\u0001 ���(wtpt��\u0001È���\u0014cprt��\u0001Ü���JGaG ×T~åÒ;ÇP_y¶óºtöÎPAïm-¾wüÇÆÏ\u001b{Ö ¶\u0013kY²�+&³\u0014 ã+0ºzÖBUõ']Á¹p Õe\