n? POTUS Resets Ties After Public Epstein Feud, Calls Him 'Modern Edison'Zohran Mamdani Grilled Over Oct. 7 Post; Reporter Asks: ‘Will You Apologize To Netanyahu?’‘Canada-EU Deal Done’: Mark Carney Slams Trump's Tariff Move In New PresserChrista HELPED Executioners? Pike Lawyers' SHOCKER Adds BIG Twist To Failed Execution MysteryRiyadh Airspace SHUT! Airlines Cancel Hundreds Of Flights After Houthis BLOW UP Saudia Airbus JetPresley Gerber’s Drug Battle Blamed For Tragic Final Chapter As Years-Long Addiction Struggle EmergeTrump-Vance Clash? POTUS Honors Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella As VP Targets Tech Giant Over H-1B VisasMen Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​ : Jessie Buckley and Rory Kinnear Starrer Men Official Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​Latest News“I do think the Clippers are cursed”: Ex-Warriors champion makes bold revelation involving Steve Ballmer following a series of unfortunate events for the team‘Drunk men all around me’: Assam dancer’s last video from train before she went missing; found dead laterIndian Railways or Blinkit? Passenger’s diaper request for 6-month-old gets a quick response, internet reactsPrem Nazir’s daughter Laila Rasheed passes away at 76; veteran actor's Chirayinkeezhu home was named ‘Laila Cottage’ after herPsychology says children who ask the same question repeatedly may not be ignoring the answer; they may be searching for an explanationHow did Jason Kelce go from criticising NFL referees to defending them? His stance shifted after two controversial incidentsNumerology Lucky Number & Colour, 9th October: Number 2 Day Meets Libra Moon-Partnerships, Patience, and Emotional Balance Could Decide the Day‘Kaala’ director Pa Ranjith mourns Nana Patekar’s death, says ‘Indian cinema has lost an icon’ after veteran actor’s death'ICE out of our neighbourhoods’: Shooting of Dominican man sparks protests in New YorkUS suspends 8 tech firms from PERM: What happens next for Indian workers seeking green cards?Anthropic is updating its Claude usage policy for the first time after almost a year, may start 'banning' users who ...Helen remembers Nana Patekar as respectful actor; recalls their bond on 'Khamoshi: The Musical'; 'We danced on the beach in Goa''This is emergency': 10,000 detained across India ahead of Delhi's Jantar Mantar stir, says CJPNana Patekar passes away: When he recalled his mother's reaction to his National Award for 'Parinda'; 'My mother didn’t quite understand its significance'California is set to become the first US state to ban popular kitchen countertops made with engineered stone, Australia did the same in 2024; 'fear' is growing ...Air India, Akasa Air and IndiGo suspend Riyadh flights after airport blast reportsJerry Jones put football aside during the Cowboys’ Week 4 game to help a player be there for a life-changing moment'There is pressure': Sahibzada Farhan on replacing Salman Ali Agha as Pakistan's T20I captainCopyright © 2026 Bennett, Coleman & Co. Ltd. All rights reserved. For reprint rights: Times Syndication ServiceFollow Us On","title":"October 09, 2026, Birthday Forecast: Discover what the next 12 months hold for you","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/astrology/horoscope/october-09-2026-birthday-forecast-discover-what-the-next-12-months-hold-for-you/articleshow/134793796.cms","id":454},{"content_hash":"1161432b98e1b4547d268504162811f17c1b9a1743a16e97a1df0def48a2e523","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:24.353564+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/en-italie-le-projet-de-loi-sur-le-droit-de-greve-souleve-dans-les-syndicats-et-au-parlement-une-vive-opposition_2072284_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Rome, 7 novembre. Il y a plus d'un an déjà que le gouvernement italien avait étudié un projet de loi sur le droit de grève. Il l'a mis dernièrement au point et a décidé de le soumettre aux Chambres à la rentrée. L'idée du gouvernement, ou si l'on préfère le but qu'il cherche à atteindre, est d'établir une nette distinction entre les conflits du travail proprement dits et ceux qui revêtent un caractère politique, et de définir les droits respectifs des employeurs et des employés dans les différends qui les opposent. Le texte élaboré par le ministre du travail M. Rubinacci, démocrate chrétien venu du syndicalisme au Parlement et de là au pouvoir, vaut la peine qu'on s'y arrête. On l'épluchera même en Italie. Le projet dès qu'il a été connu dans ses grandes lignes a été combattu par l'opposition et aussi par les syndicats, qui estiment qu'il porte essentiellement atteinte au droit de grève reconnu et sanctionné par la Constitution italienne. Que dit donc la Constitution? Ceci à l'article 40 : \" Le droit de grève s'exerce dans le cadre des lois qui le règlent. \" Le ministre du travail prétend n'avoir pas fait autre chose. Il y avait une lacune, il la comble. Le projet tend d'abord à mettre fin au grève phénomène de l'évasion des contrats collectifs. Il précise que les employés comme les employeurs sont libres de constituer des associations pour la défense de leurs propres intérêts, étant entendu que les syndicats ainsi formés réuniront au moins 10 % des intéressés. Les contrats collectifs stipulés par les syndicats seront valables pour tous les travailleurs de chaque catégorie, même pour ceux qui n'auront pas adhéré à un syndicat. Il considère ensuite comme seule grève légitime celle déclarée pour obtenir de meilleurs salaires aux traitements ou de meilleures conditions de travail, c'est-à-dire celles qui ne provoquent pas une rupture, mais une simple suspension les contrats. Toutes les autres formes de grèves fomentées par solidarité ou découlant de positions politiques ou résultant de licenciements sont implicitement interdites dans le sens que ces manifestations violent les contrats de travail. Elles seront illicites sans constituer toutefois un délit, et de ce fait, relèveront de sanctions professionnelles, sauf en cas de litige devant la magistrature. Il vous reste 40.2% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"En Italie le projet de loi sur le droit de grève soulevé dans les syndicats et au Parlement une vive opposition","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/en-italie-le-projet-de-loi-sur-le-droit-de-greve-souleve-dans-les-syndicats-et-au-parlement-une-vive-opposition_2072284_1819218.html","id":455},{"content_hash":"248e343ca9a81f73abdb4da287116999515791fff4c8e0f0dc86f284f1e3869c","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:24.306344+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/news-corps-siege-coverage-built-on-a-take-no-prisoners-culture-35656","language":"","text":"News Corp’s siege coverage built on a ‘take‑no‑prisoners’ culture Published: December 18, 2014 7:04pm EST There are ways for the media to cover stories such as the Sydney siege without committing gross ethical violations. AAP/Joel Carrett https://theconversation.com/news-corps-siege-coverage-built-on-a-take-no-prisoners-culture-35656 https://theconversation.com/news-corps-siege-coverage-built-on-a-take-no-prisoners-culture-35656 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article AUST gets wake-call with Sydney terror. Only Daily Telegraph caught the bloody outcome at 2.00 am. Congrats.— Rupert Murdoch (@rupertmurdoch) December 15, 2014 In one brutally insensitive tweet, Rupert Murdoch told the world everything it ever needed to know about the central tenet of the News Corp culture: nothing matters except the story. It is a culture in which the ends justify the means. It is a culture that celebrates cruel vulgarity, infamously exemplified by the headline “Gotcha” in the London Sun when, during the Falklands War, the British forces sank the Argentine warship the General Belgrano, with the loss of 368 lives. In Stick It Up Your Punter!, their account of life on The Sun, Peter Chippindale and Chris Horrie wrote that although even the editor, the egregious Kelvin MacKenzie, had second thoughts about the heading, Murdoch said: I rather like it. This is a culture that ultimately leads to the kind of criminality exposed in the phone-hacking scandal that engulfed the British branch of Murdoch’s empire in 2011. It is a culture that says if that’s what it takes to get the story or sell a newspaper, let’s do it. In the case of the Lindt Café siege, it is a culture that permitted the publishing of the faces of hostages as they were forced at gunpoint to hold up the gunman’s black flag in the café window. There was a strong news case for showing them holding up the flag but no case for showing their faces. These are images that are likely to haunt those hostages all their lives. The risk of doing harm should have been obvious. The disregarding of that risk is unjustifiable and unforgivable. It is a culture that permits the publication of a door-stop photo of the father and husband of Katrina Dawson, who died at the gunman’s hands. They are leaving the hospital where Dawson died. The photo is clearly taken against the husband’s wishes: he is covering his face with his hand. The father’s face is a mask of shock. The intrusion on their grief is another unforgivable act. There are ways to cover these stories without committing these gross ethical violations, and much of the other media showed how to do it. Channel Nine’s graphic live footage of the final police assault, and other television footage of hostages dashing from the scene, were vivid and immensely strong pieces of news reporting. ABC TV’s careful pixelating of faces of hostages in footage taken during the siege was another example of good ethical decision-making. However, the newspapers – and not just News Corp’s but Fairfax’s too – seemed to think that material posted by the hostages on Facebook was simply public property to be exploited for media purposes. This is a clear violation of a foundational privacy principle that says material supplied for one purpose shall not be used for another purpose without the provider’s consent. Many people – young people in particular – post material on Facebook for the purpose of sharing it with their friends. They do not anticipate that it will be used by the media in whatever context or for whatever purpose the media thinks fit. The focus of this article has been on News Corp because the connection between its performance and Murdoch’s tweet is the principal point of argument. However, that is not to say News Corp coverage was all bad, nor that others were blameless. The coverage of the Lindt Café siege is as a strong a candidate as we have seen in recent years for the Australian Press Council to conduct an investigation into the performance of the newspapers generally, and for the Australian Communications and Media Authority to use its own-motion powers to do the same in respect of radio and television. The mixed quality of the media performance was illustrated by the responses to it by the NSW Police Commissioner, Andrew Scipione, and the chair of the Australian Press Council, Professor Julian Disney. Scipione publicly thanked the media for acting responsibly in the way they covered the siege: For you to act the way you did, to be responsible, all I can say is “thank you”. Disney issued a statement, saying: Much of the coverage has been excellent and has not hesitated to tell painful truths when necessary. But there have been some deeply regrettable errors and exaggerations, spreading dangerous misinformation without any reasonable basis. This type of material can be a serious risk to public safety, as well as causing an unjustified level of fear and distrust across the community. It was a general statement of assessment, and did not make specific allegations against any particular media outlet. However, it provoked a response from News Corp broadsheet The Australian, which has been running a campaign to undermine Disney in his last year as chair of the Press Council. In a front-page story, it accused Disney of “triggering concerns” – by whom, one wonders – about “whether his organisation has abandoned the rules of procedural fairness”. The basis for this accusation was that Disney had spoken without hearing the media’s side of the story. The weakness in this argument is that Disney was not making a finding against a specific newspaper, but making a general statement about the performance of the newspapers as a whole. However, the motive for the story became clear in its last paragraph. There, The Australian quoted its own editor-in-chief, Chris Mitchell, as saying Disney: … has just dealt the Press Council out of any future complaints about the role of the media during this week’s events. This was clearly meant as a shot across the bow of the Press Council. In the event that the Press Council does decide to hear complaints about the coverage of the siege, it is reasonable to suppose that News Corp will challenge its fitness to do so. This may not thwart any such inquiry, but it might make it more difficult to accomplish, especially if News Corp decided not to co-operate on the grounds of apprehended bias. This brings us finally to another aspect of the News Corp culture: every critic is an enemy, and we take no prisoners. Rupert Murdoch Journalism Press Council Australian media Media ethics News Corp Sydney siege Denis Muller, The University of Melbourne Author Denis Muller Senior Research Fellow in the Centre for Advancing Journalism, The University of Melbourne Disclosure statement Denis Muller does not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and has disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment. Partners University of Melbourne provides funding as a founding partner of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.4v6p6vcyx","title":"News Corp’s siege coverage built on a ‘take‑no‑prisoners’ culture","url":"https://theconversation.com/news-corps-siege-coverage-built-on-a-take-no-prisoners-culture-35656","id":456},{"content_hash":"512244ce93f3bf58f410a3a5c7ad9e8825a1b6aca1257b623b34ec416d4efaba","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:24.186439+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/olivia-jenkins/106842872","language":"","text":"Olivia Jenkins - ABC NewsSkip to main contentSkip to news navigation, settings and searchExplore the ABC More from ABCClose menuABC ABC News ABC iview ABC listen Kids & Students Indigenous Information & ServicesEditorial PoliciesRead our editorial guiding principlesFollow the ABCnewsiviewlistenLoadingABC NewsJust InFor YouPoliticsWorldBusinessAnalysisSportLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMoreSearchNews HomeJust InFor YouAnalysisRuralWatch LiveHealthIndigenousPoliticsScienceDeep TimeElectionsWorldEnvironmentInvestigationsBusinessLocal newsQuizzesSportAFLNRLFootballTennisCricketNetballLifestyleWellbeingRelationships & FamilyFood & RecipesPersonal FinanceHome & GardenEntertainmentTV & MoviesBooksMusicPop CultureArtsFacebookYouTubeInstagramTwitterEmergencyBackstoryNewsletters中文新闻BERITA BAHASA INDONESIATOK PISIN Appearance (BETA) Follow system settings Select an optionAlways lightAlways darkFollow system settingsABC iViewABC ListenOlivia JenkinsOlivia Jenkins is the ABC's crime reporter in Melbourne.She is the winner of a Young Walkley for public service journalism and has been the Melbourne Press Club's young journalist of the year.She was previously a crime reporter at the Herald Sun.You can reach Olivia by email at olivia.jenkins@abc.net.au, on Instagram at @by_oliviajenkins or on Signal at @oliviajenkins.22Contact Olivia JenkinsLatest by Olivia Jenkins'Essentially unstoppable': How tobacco cartels funnel cash offshoreAustralia's illicit tobacco trade has become so lucrative that criminal overlords have built a secret pipeline suspected of smuggling tens of millions of dollars overseas.Topic:Organised CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 5:00amFri 9 Oct 2026 at 5:00amFri 9 Oct 2026 at 5:00amSchoolboy suffers burns to 90pc of his body after car firebombing goes wrongThe teenager is fighting for life after being engulfed in flames during an alleged arson attack in Melbourne's north-west on Monday.Topic:ArsonThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:23pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:23pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 8:23pmMan accused of stabbing woman in neck on Melbourne tram faces courtThe court has been told the accused man has schizophrenia.Topic:CourtsWed 7 Oct 2026 at 1:39pmWed 7 Oct 2026 at 1:39pmWed 7 Oct 2026 at 1:39pmMan charged after woman allegedly stabbed on Melbourne tramA man is charged after a woman was allegedly stabbed in the neck while travelling on a Melbourne tram on Tuesday.Topic:PoliceTue 6 Oct 2026 at 8:04pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 8:04pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 8:04pmExtensive phone data to be analysed in boxer's drug trafficking caseProsecutors obtained more than 70,000 pages of phone data collected as part of an investigation into Melbourne gym owner Ray \"Razor\" Dimachki.Topic:CourtsFri 2 Oct 2026 at 3:05pmFri 2 Oct 2026 at 3:05pmFri 2 Oct 2026 at 3:05pmVictoria's crime crisis is turning, but a looming election means everythingFresh data shows crime levels may be beginning to plateau, but as the state election looms, the political crisis fuelled by the community's perception of safety shows little sign of easing.OAnalysis by Olivia JenkinsSat 26 Sep 2026 at 7:02amSat 26 Sep 2026 at 7:02amSat 26 Sep 2026 at 7:02amYouth crime down as Victoria's crime rate continues to fallHome invasions, carjackings and robberies all fell in the 12 months to the end of June, but police say their work is \"far from over\".Topic:CrimeThu 24 Sep 2026 at 3:47pmThu 24 Sep 2026 at 3:47pmThu 24 Sep 2026 at 3:47pmOne-time champion boxer suspected to be key player in tobacco warsKhalid Baker has fought to clear his name since being released in 2018 after being jailed for murder. Court documents now allege that Baker has run illicit tobacco stores with a member of one of the nation's most prominent organised crime families.Topic:CrimeWed 23 Sep 2026 at 10:04amWed 23 Sep 2026 at 10:04amWed 23 Sep 2026 at 10:04amKnives stashed in public places across Melbourne, police believeIntelligence briefs obtained by the ABC show police are concerned by a rise in \"communal weapons storage\" at major public transport precincts across Melbourne.Topic:CrimeSat 19 Sep 2026 at 7:14amSat 19 Sep 2026 at 7:14amSat 19 Sep 2026 at 7:14amMelbourne woman extradited after gun allegedly linked to Sydney killingNSW Police have extradited a Melbourne woman after alleging they linked a gun used in two other shootings to the death of Marco Tapia in Sydney's south-west in early September.Topic:CrimeFri 18 Sep 2026 at 10:36pmFri 18 Sep 2026 at 10:36pmFri 18 Sep 2026 at 10:36pmImitation gun found in Melbourne primary school playgroundThe ABC understands two students found the imitation firearm at Princes Hill Primary School at lunchtime yesterday.Topic:PoliceFri 11 Sep 2026 at 11:09amFri 11 Sep 2026 at 11:09amFri 11 Sep 2026 at 11:09amOfficer in Dezi Freeman search given job back after on-duty drink drivingAfter rear-ending a vehicle in September last year, the officer blew a 0.07 breath test and told Victoria's police disciplinary board that he drank two \"very strong\" bourbon and cokes about three hours earlier.Topic:PoliceThu 10 Sep 2026 at 11:05amThu 10 Sep 2026 at 11:05amThu 10 Sep 2026 at 11:05amMurder charges laid over shooting that killed young Melbourne plumberPolice investigating the fatal shooting of 22-year-old plumber Daniel Hussein in Melbourne's north have charged two men with his murder.Topic:ShootingsThu 10 Sep 2026 at 3:55pmThu 10 Sep 2026 at 3:55pmThu 10 Sep 2026 at 3:55pmVictims of serious youth crimes to be told when offenders leave prisonVictims of the most serious youth crimes could be notified when offenders leave prison under significant changes to Victoria's youth justice system due at the end of the month.Topic:Youth CrimeWed 9 Sep 2026 at 5:25amWed 9 Sep 2026 at 5:25amWed 9 Sep 2026 at 5:25amPlumber with 'whole life ahead of him' shot dead in Melbourne's northPolice appeal for witnesses to come forward after 22-year-old Daniel Hussein was shot outside a property in Thomastown on Sunday night. Topic:CrimeMon 7 Sep 2026 at 4:21pmMon 7 Sep 2026 at 4:21pmMon 7 Sep 2026 at 4:21pmMelbourne man jailed for four years for keeping woman as slaveChee Kit Chong was found guilty of intentionally keeping a slave and assault over his treatment of an Indonesian woman aged in her 60s.Topic:CourtsFri 4 Sep 2026 at 2:19pmFri 4 Sep 2026 at 2:19pmFri 4 Sep 2026 at 2:19pmKey player in Melbourne tobacco wars to spend at least three years behind barsA key lieutenant of jailed tobacco kingpin Kazem Hamad will spend at least three years behind bars for orchestrating arsons and car thefts at the height of Victoria's tobacco wars.Topic:CourtsWed 2 Sep 2026 at 3:30pmWed 2 Sep 2026 at 3:30pmWed 2 Sep 2026 at 3:30pmSex crimes detectives told of drug use in Sydney Swans hotel roomDetectives investigating a sexual assault scandal engulfing the Sydney Swans have been told some players paid female adult entertainers to consume drugs off the women's bodies.Topic:AFLFri 21 Aug 2026 at 6:28amFri 21 Aug 2026 at 6:28amFri 21 Aug 2026 at 6:28amVictorian detectives to head to NSW as Swans hotel investigation expandsSydney Swans chairman Andrew Pridham and chief executive Matthew Pavlich explain the decision to suspend five players for the rest of the AFL season amid an investigation into an alleged incident at a Melbourne hotel.Topic:AFLThu 20 Aug 2026 at 1:47pmThu 20 Aug 2026 at 1:47pmThu 20 Aug 2026 at 1:47pmA driveway shooting that could spark trouble in Melbourne's 'big war'The attempted assassination of gangland family patriarch Fadi Haddara may trigger more violence between Melbourne's crime networks, law enforcement and underworld sources fear. Topic:CrimeTue 18 Aug 2026 at 4:01pmTue 18 Aug 2026 at 4:01pmTue 18 Aug 2026 at 4:01pmPolice hope shooting of alleged gangland boss not the start of feudDetectives are appealing for public assistance after alleged gangland boss Fadi Haddara, the head of the Haddara crime clan, was shot in Melbourne's inner west overnight.Topic:CrimeMon 17 Aug 2026 at 12:57pmMon 17 Aug 2026 at 12:57pmMon 17 Aug 2026 at 12:57pmMelbourne 'hero' shop owner dies from alleged assault injuriesVan Viet Truong, 60, was hailed as a hero after coming to the aid of a fellow retail worker after five youths tried to rob a store in Sunshine.Topic:CrimeThu 6 Aug 2026 at 5:19pmThu 6 Aug 2026 at 5:19pmThu 6 Aug 2026 at 5:19pmPolice to increase patrols after alleged assault of grocery store ownerPolice ramp up patrols in Melbourne's west after a group of teenagers allegedly bash a local grocer so severely he is now in hospital.Topic:CrimeWed 5 Aug 2026 at 9:53pmWed 5 Aug 2026 at 9:53pmWed 5 Aug 2026 at 9:53pmKath Pettingill, mother of accused Walsh Street police killers, diesKath Pettingill was the matriarch of the accused Melbourne crime family that was fictionalised in the 2010 movie Animal Kingdom.Topic:CrimeThu 6 Aug 2026 at 11:15amThu 6 Aug 2026 at 11:15amThu 6 Aug 2026 at 11:15amRalph Carr named as Melbourne businessman found guilty of rapeA suppression order has been lifted on identifying celebrity agent Ralph Carr as the Melbourne businessman found guilty of rape last month.Topic:CourtsMon 3 Aug 2026 at 5:40pmMon 3 Aug 2026 at 5:40pmMon 3 Aug 2026 at 5:40pmLoad more stories aboveGot a news tip?If you have inside knowledge of a topic in the news, contact the ABC.Confidential Tip‑offIf you are a whistleblower, believe you have sensitive information that should be made public, or wish to protect your identity, find our more secure options.Confidential Tip‑offNot confidential? Contact ABC News and investigations.Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashWoman, 71, treated on 'cold floor' of Adelaide hospitalData centre operator Firmus pulls blockbuster ASX float after lacklustre demandMass evacuations, cars washed away in Chilean flooding disasterSydney councillor accuses Indigenous elder Mick Mundine of sexual assault'Beau L-C' rental car card among items found near bodies, jury hearsliveButters unveiled by Bulldogs after 'megadeal', Blakey makes his decisionFederal government backtracks on disaster funding splitUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicThese are the frontrunners for this year's Nobel Peace Prize Powderfinger confirm reunion, 16 years after breaking upBack to topABC NewsContact ABC NewsYour home of Australian stories, conversations and events that shape our nation.This service may include material from Agence France-Presse (AFP), APTN, Reuters, AAP, CNN and the BBC World Service which is copyright and cannot be reproduced.Follow ABC NewsSectionsABC News HomeJust InFor YouWatch Live NewsLive ABC NewsRadioPoliticsElectionsAustraliaWorldSportLive SportRuralBusinessAnalysisScienceHealthLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMore TopicsNews in LanguageChinese 中文Tok PisinBerita Bahasa IndonesiaRelatedABC News appLocal NewsABC EmergencyABC IndigenousABC Religion & EthicsABC AsiaABC PacificMusic & AudioABC Listen Hometriple jLive RadioMovies & TVABC iview HomeList of ABC ShowsLive TVTV GuideKids & StudentsBTNEducationHeywireTakeoverABC KidsGet the ABC News AM newsletter Get the latest ABC News deliv