lies | WatchTrump Readies MASSIVE Iran Attack? Pentagon Prepares For Strikes On Nuclear SitesNidal Hasan Execution: Pete Hegseth Orders Ford Hood Shooter's 'Firing Squad' Live Streaming‘Why The Silence On Oct 7?’: Abdul El-Sayed Walks Away From Reporter; Then Rips TrumpA Very Merry Breakup Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​ : Elizabeth Guest and Timm Sharp Starrer A Very Merry Breakup Official Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​‘Not Playing This Game’: Marco Rubio Unloads On Reporter Over Kimberly Guilfoyle’s $100,000 BribeRoyal GP Murder Shock: Husband’s ‘Burglar’ Account Emerges After Hannah Cain Found DeadMusk's Team Trump Return? POTUS Resets Ties After Public Epstein Feud, Calls Him 'Modern Edison'Zohran Mamdani Grilled Over Oct. 7 Post; Reporter Asks: ‘Will You Apologize To Netanyahu?’‘Canada-EU Deal Done’: Mark Carney Slams Trump's Tariff Move In New PresserChrista HELPED Executioners? Pike Lawyers' SHOCKER Adds BIG Twist To Failed Execution MysteryRiyadh Airspace SHUT! Airlines Cancel Hundreds Of Flights After Houthis BLOW UP Saudia Airbus JetPresley Gerber’s Drug Battle Blamed For Tragic Final Chapter As Years-Long Addiction Struggle EmergeTrump-Vance Clash? POTUS Honors Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella As VP Targets Tech Giant Over H-1B VisasMen Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​ : Jessie Buckley and Rory Kinnear Starrer Men Official Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​Latest News‘Fixing broken immigration by taking hammer to it’: Lawmaker defends Microsoft after Trump’s Green Card moveVaibhav Sooryavanshi’s presence puts Sanju Samson under pressure ahead of 2nd T20IIn 1985, this Gond artist’s painting sold for ₹1,500; over four decades later, one of his works fetched ₹18 crore at auction: The story of Jangarh Singh ShyamDua Lipa and Callum Turner’s Yankees outing gets an unexpected twist as a famous baseball fan grabs MLB’s attentionBCCI Centre of Excellence treated 32 top cricketers last yearTaylor Swift to perform at Academy Museum Gala on October 17; 'Toy Story 5' song could earn her first Oscar nomination“I expose myself to judgment constantly”: Connor McDavid’s wife Lauren Kyle opens up on the difficult side of successDavid Harbour and John Cena to star in comedy 'The Family Disappears'; film to begin production in Atlanta on November 10“I do think the Clippers are cursed”: Ex-Warriors champion makes bold revelation involving Steve Ballmer following a series of unfortunate events for the team‘Drunk men all around me’: Assam dancer’s last video from train before she went missing; found dead later near Andhra tracksIndian Railways or Blinkit? Passenger’s diaper request for 6-month-old gets a quick response, internet reactsPrem Nazir’s daughter Laila Rasheed passes away at 76; veteran actor's Chirayinkeezhu home was named ‘Laila Cottage’ after herPsychology says children who ask the same question repeatedly may not be ignoring the answer; they may be searching for an explanationHow did Jason Kelce go from criticising NFL referees to defending them? His stance shifted after two controversial incidentsNumerology Lucky Number & Colour, 9th October: Number 2 Day Meets Libra Moon-Partnerships, Patience, and Emotional Balance Could Decide the Day‘Kaala’ director Pa Ranjith mourns Nana Patekar’s death, says ‘Indian cinema has lost an icon’ after veteran actor’s death'ICE out of our neighbourhoods’: Shooting of Dominican man sparks protests in New YorkUS suspends 8 tech firms from PERM: What happens next for Indian workers seeking green cards?Copyright © 2026 Bennett, Coleman & Co. Ltd. All rights reserved. For reprint rights: Times Syndication ServiceFollow Us On","title":"Shani-Pitru Amavasya Daan by Rashi: What Each Sign Should Donate on 10 October","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/astrology/horoscope/shani-pitru-amavasya-daan-by-rashi-what-each-sign-should-donate-on-10-october/articleshow/134789883.cms","id":474},{"content_hash":"34dcb9c00a7bd824c8a5e2f95ab734bea5c95e48f1d35442c18a6e9e7dfee61e","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:20.800269+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-eden-rencontre-mm-schuman-et-acheson_2073346_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives M. EDEN RENCONTRE MM. SCHUMAN ET ACHESON Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"M. EDEN RENCONTRE MM. SCHUMAN ET ACHESON","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-eden-rencontre-mm-schuman-et-acheson_2073346_1819218.html","id":475},{"content_hash":"9eb8959aff19686297865833acded38ba8963552149c1f89fe375c93de5a0fd4","domain":"reallygoodemails.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:20.795219+00:00","final_url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/health-wellness-survey-request","language":"","text":"Health & Wellness Survey Request | Email template - RGE StudioNow you have zero excuses to NOT design really good emails. Introducing RGE StudioRead more Find inspirationAll categoriesBrowse by typePopular right nowBehavioral Enhancement Inaugural Industry Miscellaneous Promotional Punctual Seasonal Templates BehavioralAbandoned CartComment NotificationCurated ContentCustomer AppreciationCustomer ServiceDiscoveryEngagementFeedback and Survey and PollFollow-UpLoyalty / RewardsPost Purchase / Recent PurchaseProduct RecommendationsReferralRetention / Re-engagementReview and TestimonialSummaryThank YouUnsubscribeCurated by humans since 2014.Browse all categoriesPopular right nowCreate emailsProductWhat is RGE StudioThe email builder that gets you.FeaturesEverything you need to make email happen.Product tourSnoop around at your own pace.Works withEmail & page templatesStart from something really good.IntegrationsYour favorite ESP or CRM can come too.Salesforce ecosystemCreate in Studio, send from Salesforce.MCPBuild emails from your AI tools.SolutionsHigher educationBetter email for busy campus teams.NonprofitsMore mission, less busywork.IntegrationPerfect for Salesforce usersThe perfect addition to your Salesforce setup? An email editor that actually works.Learn moreSOC 2 Type II · ISO 27001 · DPF · AWS PartnerPricingTalk to usResourcesLearnBlogTips, trends, and teardowns.AcademyGet better at email. For free.Help centerStuck with RGE Studio? Start here.ConnectVirtual eventsCome learn stuff with email people.UnspamFor folks who hate boring email conferences.Feedback FridayReal emails, honest critique.NewsletterReally good stuff, in your inbox.ToolsAI prompt librarySmart starting points for your emails.Email signature generatorLook pro in every sign-off.Report2026 RGE Email SurveyFresh data on how email teams actually work: email got faster, but not for everyone.Read the report300,000 email pros learn and hang out here.Next webinarsJoin the newsletterPricingTalk to SalesBack to the template catalogREALLY GOOD EMAILSAboutSchoolFeedback FridayEventsContactCode of ConductEmail signature generatorPrompt libraryRGE STUDIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"Health & Wellness Survey Request | Email template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/health-wellness-survey-request","id":476},{"content_hash":"2e4693052e9195db59a45436f1309c22810ee07b0c33a39f98361798675e7d90","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:20.706458+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-business-economy-35667","language":"","text":"2014, the year that was: Business + Economy Published: December 25, 2014 2:40pm EST Amidst the hugging of cuddly animals, G20 leaders talked growth. Andrew Taylor/G20 Australia/AAP https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-business-economy-35667 https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-business-economy-35667 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article In April, Treasurer Joe Hockey set the tone for his economics policies in a speech in New York on what he referred to as ending the “entitlement culture”. Hockey, who had given a defining speech in 2012 about ending the age of entitlement, outlined his mission: Waste and inefficiency in government spending must be rooted out. Government must live within its means. Government benefits must be sustainable, fair and targeted to those in genuine need. Welfare must be a safety net, not a cargo net. We cannot allow vast numbers in society to remain in an entitlement culture. Thus the script was set. But it has not exactly gone to to plan. Recommendations of a wide-ranging Commission of Audit set the scene for what many presumed would be a bloody May budget. Hockey announced a A$29.8 billion deficit; but it was clear few in the government realised how unpopular proposed cuts to family payments, health reform, programs targeting the unemployed (particularly young people) and measures affecting pensioners would prove to be. With the budget quickly defined as unfair, the following six months would be marked by intense political gridlock and disastrous electoral fallout. Australians clear valued their entitlements. December’s Mid-Year Economic and Fiscal Outlook (MYEFO) revealed a A$40.4 billion deficit, which Hockey attributed to plunging iron ore prices leading to Australia’s worst terms of trade in 50 years, and a recalcitrant Senate blocking many of the government’s key spending cuts. The government had managed to push through a number of measures, such as a fuel tariff, the axing of Labor’s carbon and mining taxes and the implementation of a 2% deficit levy on high income earners. After a backflip from the Palmer United Party, the Coalition scrapped a planned increase in superannuation contributions and axed the low income super contribution, agreeing to dealy this a year. However, other key initiatives such as $7 GP co-payment have been dropped (reintroduced as a cut to the Medicare rebate); redrafted, such as deregulation of the education sector, defeated before being put straight back onto the agenda by Education Minister Christopher Pyne; or in flux, such as the proposal to force jobseekers under 30 to wait six months to access welfare payments. Nevertheless, the Coalition is still clinging to hopes of a surplus, however distant - even as Australians learned a new economic term: income recession. The government’s message has this year consistently been that of budget crisis, led by unsustainable government spending and debt. Joe Hockey maintained a consistent message of crisis. AP/Lukas Coch But experts such as Professor Max Corden argue that what Australia faces is a revenue crisis, which needs to be addressed through taxation. Despite calls for the GST to be raised and a re-think of generous tax measures for property owners such as negative gearing and tax subsidies for superannuants, the government is so far standing firm. Integral to any decisions will be two important government white paper policy processes due to report next year, focusing on Australia’s system of federalism - which among other things will tackle which tiers of government have fiscal responsibility for services - and taxation, now due next year. Other key policies to look out for will be Professor Ian Harper’s final recommendations from the Competition Policy Review. In business, there was much pain for Qantas this year as it sought to stem the bleeding caused by a savage domestic capacity war with Virgin and continuing losses in its international division. In February, the company announced it would cut 5000 jobs. Chief executive Alan Joyce was not one of those, despite loud calls. It wasn’t the best year for Qantas chief Alan Joyce. Dean Lewins/AAP In September the airline posted its largest ever loss - A$2.8 billion - that included a A$2.6 billion writedown on its fleet. Hamza Bendemra wondered at the time whether it had invested in the wrong planes. Meanwhile, Toyota joined Ford and Holden in announcing it would cease manufacturing cars in Australia by 2017, leaving Australia without a local automotive industry. The move, while expected, caused soul searching over the future of Australia’s manufacturing industry more widely. 2014 was the year of the free trade agreement, with Australia finalising FTAs with South Korea and Japan - and most notably China, after a 10 year gestation. Also on the international scene, in what many insiders dubbed a summit of two agendas, Australia’s first G20 summit in Brisbane in November will be remembered for wrangling over climate change, and world leaders hugging koalas. Wishing for a lower dollar? Dan Peled/AAP But beneath the superficial, there was news on the plan to crack down on tax cheats, and real movement to close the gender gap on female workforce participation. G20 countries endorsed treasurer Joe Hockey’s 2.1% collective growth target with infrastructure and trade lead items. Achieving the US$2 trillion growth target by 2018 is far from certain, but by implementing a monitoring plan to keep G20 countries honest, Australia made accountability a key agenda item. As the Australian economy is weaned off its reliance on the very much waning commodities boom, there is very much a wait-and-see sense about the future. Will there be further interest rate cuts? How will the newly inked free trade agreements, (notably that with Japan, which kicks off early next year) affect Australia’s economy? Will Reserve Bank of Australia (RBA) governor Glenn Stevens be granted his wish for the Australian dollar to be at US75c by next December? We’ll bring it all to you in 2015. Top five business and economy stories: Infographic: the promises vs budget measures Who will bear the $60m cost of the search for MH370? Infographic: federal budget at a glance A thin blue line: how Facebook deals with controversial content Unemployment … coming to a suburb near you G20 Automotive industry MYEFO Commission of Audit Free trade agreement 2014, the year that was National Commission of Audit Helen Westerman, The Conversation Author Helen Westerman Business + Economy Editor Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.ee7fmg6jf","title":"2014, the year that was: Business + Economy","url":"https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-business-economy-35667","id":477},{"content_hash":"049910ea80fccf32e662582336b9693cb97bb1c51dd5aefdf294843fb955395d","domain":"www.dw.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:20.671629+00:00","final_url":"https://www.dw.com/en/ukraine-russia-set-for-new-round-of-us-mediated-talks/a-79261147","language":"","text":"Ukraine, Russia set for new round of US-mediated talksGrzegorz Szymanowski in Kyiv09/14/2026September 14, 2026New trilateral talks between Ukraine, Russia and the US could begin in October, according to Kyiv and Moscow. Few expect a breakthrough, but Ukraine is seeking a partial de-escalation to help it get through the winter.https://p.dw.com/p/5MZRbUkrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy has offered to meet with Vladimir Putin face-to-faceImage: Office of the President of Ukraine/B66/Avalon/Photoshot/picture allianceAdvertisementUkraine and Russia have signaled their readiness to resume trilateral talks under US mediation. \"We're preparing. For October,\" said Kyrylo Budanov, head of the Ukrainian president's office. Similar signals came from Moscow: \"We do not rule out such a possibility. We are talking about the foreseeable future,\" Kremlin spokesperson Dmitry Peskov said. US envoys Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner aimed to revive trilateral talks to end Russia's war in Ukraine, visiting Moscow and Kyiv in the first week of September. The last round of trilateral talks took place in January and February 2026 in Abu Dhabi and Geneva, without producing a breakthrough, as the issue of territory control in eastern Ukraine remained unresolved. Territory remains a difficult subject Vladimir Putin still demands more territory than Russia currently controls, Kushner confirmed on September 12, speaking online at the YES security conference in Kyiv. \"President Putin [has] laid out his line of what he wants to achieve. If Ukraine wanted to withdraw to that line, then obviously we have the rest of the deal set up and put together. Ukraine right now does not want to do that,\" the US envoy said. The Ukrainian government has indicated it is willing to freeze the fighting along the current frontline in exchange for security guarantees. Such a step could be supported by 60% of Ukrainians, according to a poll by the Kyiv International Institute of Sociology conducted in summer 2026. A similar majority of respondents rejected giving up territory that Russia hasn't been able to conquer. 33% said Ukraine should give up territory that Putin demands. Russia's air superiority is a problem for Ukraine Few expect a breakthrough anytime soon. \"For there to be a negotiated end to the war, there has to be parity. Right now, there's a stalemate on the front lines and not much is changing there, but in the air war, unfortunately, Russia currently has a significant advantage,\" Volodymyr Fesenko, a Kyiv-based political scientist, told DW. Ukraine is running low on interceptors for Russian ballistic missiles. Russia has also stepped up attacks with new jet-powered drones, which fly faster than older gasoline-powered models and are harder to intercept. Russia turns to jet-powered drones to wear Kyiv downTo view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video Russian airstrikes are increasingly targeting Ukraine's economy, including warehouses, energy facilities, gas stations and railway infrastructure. The Kremlin has accused Ukraine of opening a \"Pandora's box\" by targeting Russia's economy and fuel and energy facilities with deep strikes since the summer. Ukraine says the attacks are intended to force Russia to the negotiating table. Ukraine is worrying about the winter Ukraine's supporters say Kyiv's strategy is working. \"The escalation ... is not a problem. It's a sign of success,\" said Jonathan Powell, the United Kingdom's National Security Adviser, speaking at the Yalta European Strategy conference in Kyiv on September 11. \"Of course [Putin] doesn't go straight to negotiations, he tries to escalate in the ways that he can.\" The West must \"keep its foot on the gas\" to make Putin negotiate in earnest, Powell said. Such optimism contrasts with concerns in Ukraine about the upcoming winter. In 2025, Russian attacks on energy infrastructure caused extended blackouts and left people without heating in freezing temperatures. \"We anticipate a very difficult winter in terms of both critical infrastructure, which is being destroyed every day by Russian attacks, but also in terms of overall economic conditions in the country,\" said Ukraine's Economy Minister, Oleksandr Kravchenko. Zelenskyy calls for partial de-escalation Russia rejected the idea of a ceasefire during the upcoming talks. Ukraine is now calling for a de-escalation of attacks on energy infrastructure and the unblocking of grain exports via the Black Sea instead. \"What can bring us to any kind of negotiations? So, energy and grain. This is step number one. What we can achieve,\" Ukraine's President Volodymyr Zelenskyy told DW in an interview aired on September 12. Zelenskyy backs Putin meeting at Miami G20: 'Results matter'To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that supports HTML5 video Political scientist Fesenko says an agreement on grain exports could prove the easier task: \"In the Black Sea, both Ukraine and Russia are facing major problems. Grain exports have been blocked; ports have been shut down and therefore both sides have a stake in the matter.\" An energy truce, however, remains elusive, as Ukraine's vulnerability in the winter gives Putin leverage he would not give up easily, Fesenko said. At the same time, the US is increasing pressure on Ukraine to halt its attacks on Russian energy facilities. \"Mr. Zelenskyy has to do one thing: He has to ‌stop ⁠knocking out diesel fuel in Russia,\" Donald Trump said on Sunday. \"There are plenty of other targets. Don't hit diesel fuel. That's hurting the world.\" A day later, Trump claimed on social media that Ukraine and Russia had agreed to an energy truce. “Ukraine has agreed not to hit Russian Energy targets. Russia has agreed to do, likewise!” Trump wrote on Truth Social. However, neither Ukraine nor Russia immediately confirmed the reported agreement. Kremlin rejected Zelenskyy-Putin meeting The Kremlin has already rejected another of Zelenskyy's proposals: a meeting between the Ukrainian and Russian leaders in December on the sidelines of the G20 summit in Miami. \"Putin doesn't want to negotiate with Zelenskyy. It's been obvious since last year that he wants to reach an agreement with Trump,\" said Fesenko. \"If Trump insists, Putin may agree to a meeting between the three leaders. But he has no intention of negotiating with Zelenskyy.\" If negotiations start in October as expected, negotiating teams rather than the leaders themselves will conduct them. But Ukraine insists that only the presidents can decide the territorial issue. \"The territorial question remains a pressing one... Complex issues such as this are only resolved at the level of leaders,\" Zelenskyy said during the US envoys' visit to Kyiv. Edited by: Carla Bleiker This article was updated to include a claim by Donald Trump that Ukraine and Russia had agreed to stop striking energy targets. Neither government immediately publicly confirmed that. Grzegorz Szymanowski Reporter specializing on geopolitics and security in Central and Eastern EuropeSend us your feedbackYour feedbackAdvertisement","title":"Ukraine, Russia set for new round of US-mediated talks","url":"https://www.dw.com/en/ukraine-russia-set-for-new-round-of-us-mediated-talks/a-79261147","id":478},{"content_hash":"f21e26629bfd6c153d49cc3e220f71fabea1d21b173ee58172bbabc1aa71b94f","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:20.225154+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/13/just-army-debunks-abduction/","language":"","text":"JUST IN: Army Debunks Abduction of Islamic Scholars at Yariâs Residence in Zamfara By Prnigeria - September 13, 2026 Nigerian Army JUST IN: Army Debunks Abduction of Islamic Scholars at Yariâs Residence in Zamfara The Nigerian Army has refuted reports alleging that a group of Islamic scholars was abducted at the residence of former Zamfara State Governor and Senator representing Zamfara West, Abdulâaziz Abubakar Yari, in Talata Mafara Local Government Area of the state. The Army described the reports circulating on various media platforms as misleading, saying no Islamic cleric was abducted at the senatorâs residence. The clarification was contained in a situation report issued on Sunday by the Acting Deputy Director, Army Public Relations, 8 Division, Nigerian Army and Sector 2, Operation FANSAN YAMMA, Lt. Col. Olaniyi Osoba. According to the statement, intelligence reports indicated that some persons were abducted while travelling from the Sububu forest axis in Bakura Local Government Area towards Talata Mafara. Read Also: 2027: Former Gombe Electoral Commissioner Endorses Pantami for Governor NWDC, Kano Task Force Move to Tackle Drug Abuse, Insecurity in North-West Nigeria Has Lost Elder Statesman, Tinubu Mourns Bamanga Tukur It stressed that the incident did not occur at Yariâs residence, contrary to claims circulating online. âThe narrative that the abduction took place at the Senator’s residence in Talata Mafara is entirely false,â the Army stated. The military said troops, working in close coordination with sister security agencies and local intelligence networks, had intensified efforts to locate and rescue the victims. It urged members of the public to disregard sensationalised reports and unverified information capable of causing unnecessary panic or compromising ongoing tactical operations. The Army also appealed to residents to remain calm and vigilant while providing security agencies with timely and actionable intelligence to support efforts to rescue the abducted persons and address the security threat. The clarification comes amid heightened security concerns in parts of Zamfara State, where criminal groups have continued to carry out kidnapping and other violent activities. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSAbductionAbdulaziz YariarmyBanditsFansan YammaIslamic Clericszamfara Previous article2027: Former Gombe Electoral Commissioner Endorses Pantami for Governor Prnigeria","title":"JUST IN: Army Debunks Abduction of Islamic Scholars at Yariâs Residence in Zamfara","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/13/just-army-debunks-abduction/","id":479},{"content_hash":"88a25452c274f283f77f79af569cb55bf471248f4094f62ac40406cb14f21681","domain":"tutorialspoint.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:20.209149+00:00","final_url":"https://www.tutorialspoint.com/article/Add-elements-to-a-Queue-using-Javascript","language":"","text":"Add elements to a Queue using Javascript Javascript Web Development Front End Technology In JavaScript, there is no built-in queue data structure like other programming languages. However, you can implement a queue using an array object and perform operations like push() to add elements at the end and shift() to remove the first element. The following diagram illustrates the queue data structure and its FIFO (First In, First Out) principle: 10 20 30 40 FRONT REAR Remove from Front Add to Rear Queue (FIFO - First In, First Out) A Queue is a linear data structure that follows the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle. Elements are added at the rear and removed from the front, making it ideal for managing tasks in order. Here is a basic example of adding elements to a queue using the push() method: const queue = []; queue.push(10); // Add element to rear console.log(queue); [ 10 ] Adding Elements to a Queue using JavaScript There are various approaches to add elements to a queue implemented using an array: Using push() Method Using Two Pointer Approach Using push() Method The push() method adds elements to the end of an array, which represents the rear of the queue. This is the most straightforward approach for implementing queue insertion. Syntax arr.push(value) Where value is the element to be added to the queue. Example The following example demonstrates a Queue class with an addElement() method that uses push() to add elements: class Queue { constructor() { this.queue = []; } addElement(value) { this.queue.push(value); console.log(`Added ${value} to queue`); } display() { console.log(\"Current queue:\", this.queue); } } // Creating queue instance const q = new Queue(); console.log(\"Initial queue:\"); q.display(); // Adding elements q.addElement(10); q.addElement(20); q.addElement(30); q.addElement(40); console.log(\"\\nFinal queue:\"); q.display(); Initial queue: Current queue: [] Added 10 to queue Added 20 to queue Added 30 to queue Added 40 to queue Final queue: Current queue: [ 10, 20, 30, 40 ] Using Two Pointer Approach The two-pointer approach uses separate variables to track the front and rear positions of the queue. This method provides better control over queue operations and is more memory efficient for certain scenarios. Algorithm Steps to implement queue using two pointers: Step 1: Initialize an empty array and two pointers Step 2: Set front = 0 (points to first element) Step 3: Set rear = 0 (points to next insertion position) Step 4: To add element: queue[rear] = value, then increment rear Example Here's an implementation using the two-pointer approach: class Queue { constructor() { this.queue = []; this.front = 0; this.rear = 0; } addElement(value) { this.queue[this.rear] = value; this.rear++; console.log(`Added ${value} at position ${this.rear - 1}`); } display() { if (this.rear === this.front) { console.log(\"Queue is empty\"); } else { const elements = this.queue.slice(this.front, this.rear); console.log(\"Queue elements:\", elements); console.log(`Front at index: ${this.front}, Rear at index: ${this.rear}`); } } } // Testing the queue const q = new Queue(); console.log(\"Initial state:\"); q.display(); console.log(\"\\nAdding elements:\"); q.addElement(10); q.addElement(20); q.addElement(30); q.addElement(40); console.log(\"\\nFinal state:\"); q.display(); Initial state: Queue is empty Adding elements: Added 10 at position 0 Added 20 at position 1 Added 30 at position 2 Added 40 at position 3 Final state: Queue elements: [ 10, 20, 30, 40 ] Front at index: 0, Rear at index: 4 Comparison Method Complexity Best For Memory Usage push() Method O(1) Simple queue operations Standard Two Pointer Approach O(1) Advanced queue management More efficient Conclusion Both methods effectively add elements to a queue in JavaScript. Use push() for simple implementations, while the two-pointer approach offers better control and efficiency for complex queue operations. Vivek Verma Updated on: 2026-03-15T23:18:59+05:30 661 Views Learn More in Our Tutorials Circular Queue Data Structure Data Structures Algorithms How to implement Queue in Java Javaexamples jQuery queue() Method Jquery Print Page Previous Next Advertisements","title":"Add elements to a Queue using Javascript","url":"https://www.tutorialspoint.com/article/Add-elements-to-a-Queue-using-Javascript","id":480},{"content_hash":"cfeb71ee05856eb88c119a00d480dfc5f53fb1fcbd4c121aabeda74e659ed75e","domain":"www.chinadaily.com.cn","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:20.036692+00:00","final_url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202609/14/WS6aa7c179e4b06d4aa055df32.html","language":"","text":"Highlights abound at China's trade in services fair - Chinadaily.com.cn Search HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER MOBILE Global EditionASIA 中文双语Français HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER China Daily PDF China Daily E-paper MOBILE Business Macro Companies Industries Technology Motoring China Data Finance Top 10 Home / Business / CIFTIS 2026 Home Business CIFTIS 2026 Highlights abound at China's trade in services fair By Song Yi | chinadaily.com.cn | Updated: 2026-09-14 17:42 Share Share - WeChat CLOSE Visitors explore the 2026 China International Fair for Trade in Services (CIFTIS) at Shougang Park in Beijing, on Sept 13, 2026. The 2026 CIFTIS concluded in Beijing on Sunday. [Photo/Xinhua] The 2026 China International Fair for Trade in Services (CIFTIS) concluded in Beijing on Sept 13, reaching more than 1,200 deals, according to Xinhua News Agency last Sunday. The five-day event featured about 200 forums, meetings and business matchmaking and promotional activities, with more than 1,830 companies participating on site, and over 5,300 companies tuned in online. \"Ninety countries, regions and international organizations set up exhibitions or held events at this year's CIFTIS, and a number of innovative technologies, products and services made their debut at the fair,\" Zhu Guangyao, an official from the Ministry of Commerce, told Xinhua News Agency. The event also featured the release of several reports, presentations on cutting-edge research and discussions on the latest trends in the services trade, helping promote international exchanges and cooperation, Zhu said. The fair produced fruitful outcomes across multiple sectors, with 103 companies and institutions unveiling 233 new products, technologies and other achievements, according to Lu Huiling, deputy head and spokesperson of the Beijing municipal commerce bureau. \"Norwegian exhibitors, representing this year's guest country of honor, signed six cooperation agreements with Chinese companies and institutions,\" Lu said. The 2026 CIFTIS was held at Shougang Park in Beijing from Sept 9 to 13. Photo Pop Land City Carnival Tour arrives in Shanghai Gansu's traditional medicine industry embraces tech and global markets China International Big Data Industry Expo showcases data achievements in Guiyang World Humanoid Robot Games open in Beijing Tourists shop for Taiwan goods in Fujian The 'Vibrant China' media tour in Hunan: Changsha blends history with creativity Most Viewed in 24 Hours State Council News A look at China's economy in H1 of 2026 Policies concerning expats, foreign enterprises in August 2026 Top 10 Top 10 countries attracting Chinese tourists Business focus Manufacturing edge a win-win multiplier abroad 'New new three' power China's trade China Data China's CPI up 0.8% in August SOEs SOEs return more profits to State China's central SOEs invest 1.1t yuan in Xinjiang during 14th Five-Year Plan period Special 2026 CIFTIS China International Supply Chain Expo Top BACK TO THE TOP English 中文 HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER China Daily PDF China Daily E-paper MOBILE Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form. Note: Browsers with 1024*768 or higher resolution are suggested for this site. License for publishing multimedia online 0108263 Registration Number: 130349 About China Daily Advertise on Site Contact Us Job Offer Expat Employment FOLLOW US Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form. CLOSE","title":"Highlights abound at China's trade in services fair","url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202609/14/WS6aa7c179e4b06d4aa055df32.html","id":481},{"content_hash":"36174568c4b697a3c52213d14b0f4863f31d7de41b674b643d973b5472895d87","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:19.992922+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/sports-entertainment-professionals.html","language":"","text":"Sports & Entertainment Professionals Being in the sports and entertainment industries means there’s extra complexity to your financial life. Our advisors understand the strategies to help you manage your finances and the unique opportunities that come with it. Let’s start a conversation. Dedicated Advisors Our Sports & Entertainment Advisors understand why and how your financial life is different, and can help guide you in pursuit of your unique goals. Focus on You You’ve worked hard to get where you are. At Merrill, we can help simplify your financial life by working with you on a customized strategy that makes sense for you. Powerful Solutions Bank of America and Merrill offer an extensive range of solutions to help you pursue your financial goals, whether they are immediate or years away. Ready to build your team? Let us help you navigate your unique planning needs. We can work together to build a wealth management strategy tailored for your needs. Start here Resources to help you plan your future Explore content and articles crafted to help you navigate your financial journey. Beyond the page: How a successful writer pursues financial security Best-selling author Elin Hilderbrand has been lucky, but she’s also been disciplined as she pursues her goals, artistic and financial MegaTrends/FinancialPlanning The value of financial planning while pursuing artistic goals A financial advisor who understands a writing or artistic career can help you find the freedom to create and work toward long-term goals Beyond the field: Planning for a future after professional soccer For the Aaronson brothers, success means not taking their good fortune for granted and planning for whatever the future holds MegaTrends/FinancialPlanning Realizing the financial potential and avoiding the pitfalls of athletic success Mastering the good fortune that can come from being a successful athlete today calls for help from a specialized team MegaTrends/FinancialPlanning The financial reality of today’s young athletes New research reveals a generation of young athletes facing unprecedented financial complexities Financial Education Matters: A Conversation with Darren Woodson In this video, we talk to Darren Woodson, a former Dallas Cowboy and three-time Super Bowl champion, about what he believes every athlete should know when thinking about their financial wellbeing. Protecting yourself and your future 6 key insurance considerations for athletes and entertainers MegaTrends/FinancialPlanning Stepping out onto the financial stage A wealth management checklist for entertainment professionals MegaTrends/FinancialPlanning Explore more articles Stepping it up! Learn why professional guidance is critical for athletes and entertainers Read more “Sports and entertainment professionals often face unique financial challenges — from sudden wealth, to uneven or multiple income streams, to unknown career spans. We understand those challenges.” Greg McGauley — Head of Merrill Private Wealth Management, International and Institutional Start here Section Related Articles Athletes and entertainers work hard to build successful careers. Read how Merrill advisors can help manage a complex financial life and the unique opportunities that come with it. Going beyond the gold You have your NIL money, what’s next? Changing the game: women are ready to invest in their financial future It's tax time. Get filing tips to keep you on track. Wealth planning considerations for LGBTQ+ married couples in the entertainment industry Striking the right chord when selling your music catalog Name, Image, Likeness (NIL): What are your key money moves once you’ve signed the deal? What’s in a name? Easy, and essential, estate planning fundamentals Safeguard your privacy as you build your celebrity Seven tax planning tips for professional athletes and entertainers","title":"Sports & Entertainment Professionals","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/sports-entertainment-professionals.html","id":482},{"content_hash":"605431cd3d571bc2e287efbf7df1a1ab75f81e6ec6e79965c2eaaeddeb2a79cd","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:19.662676+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/l-egypte-fait-des-avances-a-l-est_2072013_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Selon le journal wafdiste Al Nida une haute personnalité égyptienne \" est partie pour la Russie chargée d'une \" mission importante et secrète \". Al Nida écrit notamment : \" Cette personnalité, qui bénéficie de l'amitié des dirigeants soviétiques, prendra d'importants contacts avec les responsables du ministère soviétique ces ci-faires étrangères. On attend d'importants résultats de ces contacts. \" De son côté Al Ahram a déclaré que d'importants \" échanges de vues \" ont eu lieu au Caire entre M. Semyon P. Kozyrev, ministre soviétique, et Ibrahim Farrag pacha, ministre des affaires étrangères par intérim. Ce journal ajoute que l'expansion du commerce extérieur du gouvernement égyptien avec la Russie et les pays de l'Europe orientale découlera probablement de ces entretiens. La commission du commerce extérieur du gouvernement égyptien se réunira le 11 novembre pour examiner la question des relations commerciales entre l'Egypte et les pays du bloc soviétique. La commission étudiera un mémorandum de la section économique du ministère des affaires étrangères recommandant que l'Égypte développe son commerce avec des pays autres que la Grande-Bretagne, et notamment avec la Russie et les pays de l'Est européen. Le mémorandum suggère que pour faciliter la solution du problème des paiements l'Égypte échange notamment son coton contre le blé, le charbon, les armes, les munitions et les autres produits manufacturés que peuvent lui fournir les pays de l'Europe 'orientale. Pendant ce temps l'ancien ambassadeur d'Égypte à Moscou, Bindari pacha, surnommé le \" pacha rouge \", déclarait au Conseil mondial de la paix, réuni à Vienne: \" Le peuple égyptien a dépassé le stade des signatures. Il est passé à la lutte concrète. Il verse son sang pour défendre sa liberté et la paix mondiale. Les conséquences de la lutte et de la victoire de l'Égypte, a-t-il conclu, seront plus lointaines. Elles créeront le climat favorable pour que l'Allemagne puisse réaliser son unité sous un gouvernement démocratique et pacifique. \" Il vous reste 30.1% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"L'ÉGYPTE FAIT DES AVANCES A L'EST","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/l-egypte-fait-des-avances-a-l-est_2072013_1819218.html","id":483},{"content_hash":"7822d80d4e22f106cc2c31893877c0812e6f77dda32095c038383ab8aa7de34a","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:19.502930+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-education-35418","language":"","text":"2014, the year that was: Education Published: December 25, 2014 2:40pm EST Higher education got the most attention it’s had in decades, thanks to the proposed shake up by this man. AAP https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-education-35418 https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-education-35418 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article While 2013 was all about schools and their funding (remember Gonski, anyone?), 2014 was the year of higher education reform. Or, at least, proposed higher education “reform”. With cuts to higher education funding and the prospect of fee deregulation being some of the most maligned aspects of the May federal budget, it was surprising that as much attention was being paid to Australia’s institutes of higher learning as is usually paid to our schools, hospitals and transport. Most vice-chancellors, albeit a few glaring exceptions, were in support of fee deregulation. They argued that the current funding system of unlimited student places (which was reviewed in April and found to be a keeper) and decreasing government support was unsustainable and universities needed to be set free. However, many of our experts feared Australia’s politicians were unaware of the drastic effect this could have on our world-class system of higher education, and especially access to it for all groups of Australian society. After months of debate, negotiation and much hand-wringing, the Senate finally voted down the bill in parliament’s last sitting week, only to have the bill’s champion, Education Minister Christopher Pyne, reintroduce it the very next day. So, is another tumultuous year on the horizon in higher education? We’ll have to wait and see. The debate separated university staff from their leaders and prestigious universities from middle-tier ones. There was never a question that the prestigious Group of Eight would have more pricing power in a market system. One of our most-read pieces of 2014 outlined the worth of attending an elite university, which research found results in a slight salary increase across a lifetime. Prestigious universities tended to be in support of fee deregulation - because they would have the pricing power in a marketised system. AAP Paying for education was an important focus this year. We closely examined private schooling and whether the cost pays off. We found public school kids do better at university than private school kids with the same tertiary entrance score, and post-university employment prospects and wages were much the same. We didn’t only look at public and private schools, though. There’s been an increased interest in doing education differently, so we looked at alternative forms of education and options outside of public, private and Catholic schools. Steiner, Montessori, and Democratic schools are on the rise, and we looked at how these work in our alternative schooling series. While thinking about different ways of doing things, our authors challenged what we thought we knew works in education. Misty Adoniou asked if we should scrap homework altogether, and Rebecca English asked if parents should stop punishing and rewarding their kids, and instead teach them to be good just for the sake of it. The biggest news in schools this year was the government-commissioned review into the national curriculum. The review was just as controversial in its recommendations to have more of a focus on Australia’s “Judeo-Christian” heritage as it was for its appointments. However, we’re yet to see changes actually reach our classrooms, and could be waiting for a while. You guys sure do like spelling, or at least reading about it. Shutterstock What you seemed to enjoy the most though was looking at language: spelling, grammar, Aussie slang, annoying misuses in English, Americanisms, and whether your kids were using language in the way they should and at the right ages. You also enjoyed our series on bullying in schools, including what is actually bullying and what is just the argy bargy of everyday life. Our exam guide, which led up to end of year final exams, let you know how to study, what to eat, and how to support loved ones during this stressful time. But in case you missed them, here were our top five education stories for the year: Why some kids can’t spell and why spelling tests won’t help State school kids do better at uni Private schooling has little long term pay off ‘Gentle parenting’ explainer: no rewards, no punishments, no misbehaving kids How to tell if your child has a speech or language impairment And finally, we realise the education section has bombarded you with close-ups of this man all year. So here we pay homage to that with a few of our favourites. We’ve called it “The Many Faces of Pyne”: The Many Faces of Pyne. AAP images Education Parenting The year that was Higher education policy Fee deregulation Gonski Grammar Christopher Pyne Higher education funding Spelling 2014, the year that was Higher Education Bill 2014 Speech impairments Gentle parenting Alexandra Hansen, The Conversation Author Alexandra Hansen Deputy Editor and Chief of Staff, The Conversation AUNZ Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.p67xtyt9d","title":"2014, the year that was: Education","url":"https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-education-35418","id":484},{"content_hash":"6e728aeb3e430be51d3bda84858f3db7879e7bce5041e7c4a5bc8aa204d9dc07","domain":"supabase.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:19.264499+00:00","final_url":"https://supabase.com/features/realtime-presence","language":"","text":"Realtime - PresenceSynchronize shared state between users through websockets.Stage:General AvailabilityAvailable on self-hosted:YesSupabase's Realtime Presence feature allows you to track and synchronize shared state between connected users in real-time. This capability enables you to build collaborative applications where users can see each other's status, actions, or any custom state information instantly. Key features Real-time state synchronization: Track and update shared state across multiple clients. Event-based tracking: Listen to 'sync', 'join', and 'leave' events for state changes. Custom state definition: Share any type of state information between users. Unique client identification: Use auto-generated or custom keys to identify clients. Channel-based organization: Group users and their states into specific channels or rooms. Easy state management: Track, untrack, and update state with simple method calls. Benefits: Real-time user tracking: Monitor which users are currently active or online. Enhanced interactivity: Create more engaging, collaborative user experiences. Reduced complexity: Implement shared state features without building custom synchronization logic. Flexible use cases: Adapt the feature for various applications, from simple online indicators to complex collaborative tools. Realtime Presence is particularly valuable for: Collaborative document editing platforms Multiplayer games showing player positions or status Team collaboration tools with user availability indicators Live streaming platforms displaying viewer counts and engagement Project management applications with real-time task assignments Social media platforms showing active users in a group or chat Supabase's Realtime Presence feature provides a powerful tool for creating interactive, real-time applications that require shared state management, allowing developers to focus on creating unique and valuable user experiences.Read DocumentationShareRealtime - Postgres changesRealtime - Presence AuthorizationBuild in a weekend, scale to millionsStart your projectRequest a demo","title":"Realtime - Presence","url":"https://supabase.com/features/realtime-presence","id":485},{"content_hash":"86bc7ce98b6f22c732fe2de3b20550d548c029504662294e274dec53c2e2f8e3","domain":"son.gov.ng","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:19.117574+00:00","final_url":"https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/21/son-retirees-commend-dg-son-for-payment-of-benefits/","language":"","text":"DG SON and SON Retirees in a Group PhotopreghCross Section of the Retirees at the EventSON Retirees at the MeetingOther Retirees Present SON RETIREES COMMEND DG SON FOR PAYMENT OF BENEFITS Retirees of the Standards Organisation of Nigeria (SON) recently visited the Director General and Chief Executive, Dr. Ifeanyi C. Okeke, at the Operational Headquarters in Lagos to express their appreciation for his intervention in facilitating the payment of their retirement benefits. Speaking on behalf of the group, Dr. (Mrs.) Margaret Eshiet, former Director and Codex Contact Point, commended the DG for his leadership and described the gesture as timely and impactful. She noted that the disbursement of benefits had restored dignity to the retirees and demonstrated a high level of empathy and administrative responsibility. In his remarks, Dr. Okeke thanked the retirees for their service and reiterated his commitment to fairness and equity in the treatment of all staff—past and present. He emphasized that the payment of entitlements is not merely a statutory obligation, but a moral duty to honour the sacrifices made by those who contributed to the organisation’s growth. He urged current staff to draw inspiration from the retirees’ dedication and integrity, reminding them that the legacy they leave behind will define how they are remembered. Also speaking at the event, Engr. Richard Adewumi, former Director of Laboratory Services, presented a token of appreciation to the Director General and led a prayer on behalf of the group.","title":"SON Retirees Commend DG SON for Payment of Benefits","url":"https://son.gov.ng/2025/04/21/son-retirees-commend-dg-son-for-payment-of-benefits/","id":486},{"content_hash":"a2da17932f455225bde5926f6b6484e1c4f8f495a61e92ea53be366ab16b2dd0","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:18.446011+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/le-retrait-de-toiles-jugees-subversives-au-salon-d-automne-provoque-un-incident_2073078_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives LE RETRAIT DE TOILES jugées subversives au Salon d'automne PROVOQUE UN INCIDENT Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"LE RETRAIT DE TOILES jugées subversives au Salon d'automne PROVOQUE UN INCIDENT","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/le-retrait-de-toiles-jugees-subversives-au-salon-d-automne-provoque-un-incident_2073078_1819218.html","id":487},{"content_hash":"45b60e7490a873c4d987b337d80c526585db8096bbceb4feb8b13b7f0686d655","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:18.359205+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/giselle-wakatama/7316700","language":"","text":"Giselle Wakatama - ABC NewsSkip to main contentSkip to news navigation, settings and searchExplore the ABC More from ABCClose menuABC ABC News ABC iview ABC listen Kids & Students Indigenous Information & ServicesEditorial PoliciesRead our editorial guiding principlesFollow the ABCnewsiviewlistenLoadingABC NewsJust InFor YouPoliticsWorldBusinessAnalysisSportLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMoreSearchNews HomeJust InFor YouAnalysisRuralWatch LiveHealthIndigenousPoliticsScienceDeep TimeElectionsWorldEnvironmentInvestigationsBusinessLocal newsQuizzesSportAFLNRLFootballTennisCricketNetballLifestyleWellbeingRelationships & FamilyFood & RecipesPersonal FinanceHome & GardenEntertainmentTV & MoviesBooksMusicPop CultureArtsFacebookYouTubeInstagramTwitterEmergencyBackstoryNewsletters中文新闻BERITA BAHASA INDONESIATOK PISIN Appearance (BETA) Follow system settings Select an optionAlways lightAlways darkFollow system settingsABC iViewABC ListenGiselle WakatamaNewcastle, NSWGiselle Wakatama is a senior broadcast journalist and has been reporting for the ABC since 2000. Follow Giselle on Twitter: @gisellewakContact Giselle WakatamaLatest by Giselle WakatamaTrio who murdered man over $22k fake gun sale jailedNSW Supreme Court Justice Peter Hammill jails three men over a gun deal payback murder in Newcastle in 2022.Topic:CourtsFri 9 Oct 2026 at 2:21pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 2:21pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 2:21pmWoman loses medical negligence claim over partially amputated legA NSW woman loses a medical negligence claim over the amputation of part of her left leg.Topic:CourtsThu 8 Oct 2026 at 2:58pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 2:58pmMan who allegedly crashed into Newcastle Knights fans refused bailThe Sydney teenager who was allegedly behind the wheel of a car that crashed into and injured 10 Newcastle Knights fans has been refused bail. Topic:CourtsSun 4 Oct 2026 at 12:51pmSun 4 Oct 2026 at 12:51pmSun 4 Oct 2026 at 12:51pmTeen charged after crashing into crowd of Newcastle Knights supportersThe teenaged driver of the car that allegedly ploughed into a crowd of Newcastle Knights supporters has been charged.Topic:CrimeSun 4 Oct 2026 at 9:52amSun 4 Oct 2026 at 9:52amSun 4 Oct 2026 at 9:52amMan charged after toy poodle suffers broken jaw in alleged attackCentral Coast woman Tania Gorry says she was left traumatised after a stranger allegedly snatched and attacked her pet poodle Bella.Topic:PoliceFri 2 Oct 2026 at 5:40pmFri 2 Oct 2026 at 5:40pmFri 2 Oct 2026 at 5:40pmMan facing trial over 63-gun stash argues they are antiquesA NSW court is told Andrew David Rankmore contends that his stash of guns should be classified as antiques and not illegal weapons.Topic:CourtsThu 24 Sep 2026 at 2:34pmThu 24 Sep 2026 at 2:34pmThu 24 Sep 2026 at 2:34pmAnger over lives lost as Jehovah's Witnesses shift on blood transfusionThe daughter of a Jehovah's Witness who died after refusing a blood transfusion on religious grounds in 2019 has expressed anger after the church altered its longstanding opposition last week.Topic:HealthThu 24 Sep 2026 at 5:15amThu 24 Sep 2026 at 5:15amThu 24 Sep 2026 at 5:15amTwo NSW footballers behind bars after being found guilty of rapeAfter deliberating for one day, a jury has found two country New South Wales footballers guilty of raping a woman at Scone in 2023.Topic:Sexual OffencesWed 16 Sep 2026 at 3:21pmWed 16 Sep 2026 at 3:21pmWed 16 Sep 2026 at 3:21pmFootballers accused of rape thought they 'could be on' for group sexA jury hears two Scone footballers believed they \"could be on\" for group sex on the night they allegedly raped a woman.Topic:Sexual OffencesMon 14 Sep 2026 at 5:20pmMon 14 Sep 2026 at 5:20pmMon 14 Sep 2026 at 5:20pmMedia personality Isaac Compton found not guilty of assaulting womenThe media personality and musician has been found not guilty of assaulting and sexually touching two women at a Newcastle house party.Topic:CourtsFri 11 Sep 2026 at 2:35pmFri 11 Sep 2026 at 2:35pmFri 11 Sep 2026 at 2:35pmTrial hears woman repeated 'no' to footballers before alleged rapeA District Court jury has been told a woman was allegedly raped by two amateur footballers in the NSW Hunter Valley in 2023.Topic:CourtsWed 9 Sep 2026 at 4:48pmWed 9 Sep 2026 at 4:48pmWed 9 Sep 2026 at 4:48pm'Backyard' e-bike battery repairs starting deadly fires, inquest told Do-it-yourself and YouTube-inspired lithium-ion battery modifications and repairs have been blamed for fires, some of which were deadly.Topic:FiresThu 27 Aug 2026 at 3:31pmThu 27 Aug 2026 at 3:31pmThu 27 Aug 2026 at 3:31pmBus firm fined $650k for hiring driver in fatal wedding crashThe bus company and its directors were fined for failing in their duty of care, including hiring driver Brett Button, who was impaired by drugs at the time of the crash that killed 10 passengers.Topic:CourtsWed 26 Aug 2026 at 5:50pmWed 26 Aug 2026 at 5:50pmWed 26 Aug 2026 at 5:50pm14yo Chinese exchange student to stand trial for murderThe girl is accused of killing another Chinese student in August last year, making her one of the youngest people to stand trial for murder in NSW.Topic:CourtsMon 24 Aug 2026 at 5:37pmMon 24 Aug 2026 at 5:37pmMon 24 Aug 2026 at 5:37pmCourt hears public at risk from bus driver for months before deadly crashA court is told a bus company exposed the public to continual risks by employing a driver who later crashed, killing 10 people and injuring 25 others.Topic:Road Accidents and IncidentsMon 24 Aug 2026 at 2:23pmMon 24 Aug 2026 at 2:23pmMon 24 Aug 2026 at 2:23pmDrug importer who used forged documents to flee rehab jailedDrug importer-turned fugitive Hussein Chamas is jailed for trafficking and absconding from a NSW rehabilitation facility before trying to flee Australia.Topic:CourtsWed 12 Aug 2026 at 2:36pmWed 12 Aug 2026 at 2:36pmWed 12 Aug 2026 at 2:36pmCoal locomotive derails weeks after runaway train incidentAnother coal train run by haulage operator Pacific National is being investigated in New South Wales just weeks after a runaway train incident involving the same operator.Topic:Rail Accidents and IncidentsMon 10 Aug 2026 at 7:06pmMon 10 Aug 2026 at 7:06pmMon 10 Aug 2026 at 7:06pmMan granted bail over alleged violent threats against Paralympian MPA man has been granted strict conditional bail, including staying off social media, over alleged threats made against New South Wales Gosford MP Liesl Tesch. Topic:CrimeThu 6 Aug 2026 at 5:28pmThu 6 Aug 2026 at 5:28pmThu 6 Aug 2026 at 5:28pmReport finds runaway coal train reached 90km/h on 20km journeyAn interim report has confirmed a runaway coal train reached speeds well in excess of the safe limit and rolled backwards for 5 kilometres before coming to a halt.Topic:Rail Transport IndustryWed 5 Aug 2026 at 3:48pmWed 5 Aug 2026 at 3:48pmWed 5 Aug 2026 at 3:48pmSon who helped dad retrieve cocaine from ship jailedThe son of a convicted drug smuggler has been sentenced to four years' jail for helping to retrieve cocaine from a ship in the Port of Newcastle.Topic:CourtsFri 31 Jul 2026 at 4:59pmFri 31 Jul 2026 at 4:59pmFri 31 Jul 2026 at 4:59pmEscort agency bosses lied about model's disappearance, coroner findsA NSW deputy state coroner has found evidence escort agency bosses knew what happened to Revelle Balmain, who vanished in 1994.Topic:Missing PersonFri 31 Jul 2026 at 2:26pmFri 31 Jul 2026 at 2:26pmFri 31 Jul 2026 at 2:26pmConvicted paedophile to argue multiple-personality evidence unlawfulA father jailed for abusing two children is appealing against his conviction, arguing evidence given by the multiple personalities of the same witness was unlawful.Topic:Court Ordered Mental Health TreatmentMon 13 Jul 2026 at 6:50amMon 13 Jul 2026 at 6:50amMon 13 Jul 2026 at 6:50amWhy a telco outage has stopped some trains but not othersTrains relying on the 4G network of freight rail track operator ARTC went down amid the Telstra failure.Topic:Telecommunications Services IndustryThu 9 Jul 2026 at 5:53pmThu 9 Jul 2026 at 5:53pmThu 9 Jul 2026 at 5:53pmJury told accused used a 'Razor' scooter to kill axe-wielding manA Supreme Court jury hears a Razor-branded scooter was the alleged murder weapon used to kill a man who was armed with an axe during a violent brawl north of Newcastle.Topic:HomicideTue 7 Jul 2026 at 12:29pmTue 7 Jul 2026 at 12:29pmTue 7 Jul 2026 at 12:29pmTwo men charged as NSW Police seize 64 kilograms of drugsPolice have charged two men and seized almost 64 kilograms of drugs with an estimated street value of more than $2.5 million, north of Newcastle.Topic:Drug OffencesFri 26 Jun 2026 at 1:21pmFri 26 Jun 2026 at 1:21pmFri 26 Jun 2026 at 1:21pmLoad more stories aboveGot a news tip?If you have inside knowledge of a topic in the news, contact the ABC.Confidential Tip‑offIf you are a whistleblower, believe you have sensitive information that should be made public, or wish to protect your identity, find our more secure options.Confidential Tip‑offNot confidential? Contact ABC News and investigations.Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashWoman, 71, treated on 'cold floor' of Adelaide hospitalData centre operator Firmus pulls blockbuster ASX float after lacklustre demandMass evacuations, cars washed away in Chilean flooding disasterSydney councillor accuses Indigenous elder Mick Mundine of sexual assault'Beau L-C' rental car card among items found near bodies, jury hearsliveButters unveiled by Bulldogs after 'megadeal', Blakey makes his decisionFederal government backtracks on disaster funding splitUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicThese are the frontrunners for this year's Nobel Peace Prize Powderfinger confirm reunion, 16 years after breaking upBack to topABC NewsContact ABC NewsYour home of Australian stories, conversations and events that shape our nation.This service may include material from Agence France-Presse (AFP), APTN, Reuters, AAP, CNN and the BBC World Service which is copyright and cannot be reproduced.Follow ABC NewsSectionsABC News HomeJust InFor YouWatch Live NewsLive ABC NewsRadioPoliticsElectionsAustraliaWorldSportLive SportRuralBusinessAnalysisScienceHealthLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMore TopicsNews in LanguageChinese 中文Tok PisinBerita Bahasa IndonesiaRelatedABC News appLocal NewsABC EmergencyABC IndigenousABC Religion & EthicsABC AsiaABC PacificMusic & AudioABC Listen Hometriple jLive RadioMovies & TVABC iview HomeList of ABC ShowsLive TVTV GuideKids & StudentsBTNEducationHeywireTakeoverABC KidsGet the ABC News AM newsletter Get the latest ABC News delivered to your email inboxEmail addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. All ABC newslettersStay informed and connected with the ABC. Covering a wide range of topics and brands including News, iview, listen, and more.Browse all ABC newslettersABC homeInformation & ServicesEditorial PoliciesAccessibilityHelpContact UsCommercialCareersAbout the ABCPrivacy PolicyTerms of Use© 2026 ABCFollow the ABCWe acknowledge Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples as the First Australians and Traditional Custodians of the lands where we live, learn and work.","title":"Giselle Wakatama","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/giselle-wakatama/7316700","id":488},{"content_hash":"192c38b2cab4e121711701273d4897b7206d5b4857b70b4ac1fd907260337fec","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:18.303359+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-arts-culture-35486","language":"","text":"2014, the year that was: Arts + Culture Published: December 28, 2014 3:12pm EST How’s your year been? Thanks for sharing it with us. AAP/MONA, Exxopolis by Architects of Air https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-arts-culture-35486 https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-arts-culture-35486 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Hey luvvies! We’ve made it! 2014 is in the bank – and here’s what we did, what you read, how we all came through it. Think of the following as a music festival: loads of highlights, one after the other. Read them until you’re reduced to a quivering pulp. In that festival spirit, we covered – deep breath, a few lunges – the Sydney Festival, the Adelaide Biennial, Melbourne Fringe, Sydney Biennale, Adelaide Festival, Brisbane Festival, Melbourne Writers Festival, Sydney Writers Festival, Melbourne Fashion Festival, Fashion Week Australia, Sydney and Melbourne’s film festivals. Our Indigenous coverage included the stellar “Dreamtime” series, by Christine Nicholls, traversed fashion, music, design, visual arts, TV and film. Jasmine Togo Brisby’s work was on display at Deadly Nui Art at the Melbourne Fringe Festival. Kimba Thomson/Blak Dot Gallery In other film news, we tried to peek behind the public persona of Cate Blanchett at the Golden Globes; thought long and hard about the Oscars, at which the Aussie accent was often heard. The Great Gatsby blitzed the AACTA awards in January, and Deb Verhoeven argued that Australian cinema isn’t dead. We also paid tribute to the Whitlam government’s embrace of arts and screen culture in Australia. In March, our in-depth coverage of Sydney Biennale’s split from its major sponsor Transfield brought new perspectives to the politics of arts funding, artist boycotts and philanthropy. In June, Opera Australia had a boycott threat of it own, relating to the Georgian soprano Tamar Iveri. The Klinghoffer protests at the Met in New York in October showed opera’s contemporary relevance. Like all years 2014 was a doozy for dying, as reflected in our many obituaries. Take your pick from Maya Angelou, Rik Mayall, Peaches Geldof, Bobby Womack, Peter Sculthorpe, Gavin Jones, Robin Williams, Lauren Bacall, Richard Attenborough, Joan Rivers, Philip Seymour Hoffman and Pete Seeger. Hoffman helped steer American cinema back to an emotional integrity it had lost. Daniel Dal Zennaro/EPA It was a year of terrific voices on feminism in literature, music, mainstream media, social media, television, comedy and gaming. The role of our public broadcasters, ABC and SBS, were under intense scrutiny before the 2014 federal budget in May, and our series on the Future of Public Broadcasting considered key issues. In arts policy, we dived headlong into the budget, OzCo’s new strategic plan and the importance of ABS arts and sports data to better understand our culture. In books, we looked at prizes such as the Stella, the Miles Franklin, the Man Booker, the Prime Minister’s and even the Most Underrated Book of the Year. Our The Case For series nominated Australian books – fiction or non-fiction, contemporary or historical – that authors thought deserved closer attention. Got an idea for another one? Hint, hint hint, hint, hint, hint, hint … DROP US A LINE! The Most Underrated Book Award rewarded off-beat, experimental and innovative books. The Conversation The topic of anonymous book reviews got a little hotter than we’d expected, with a back and forth of articles that drew academic blood. Things continued to build (pun win!) with our architecture coverage. Our Sublime Design series took a closer look at some design classics such as the Moog synthesiser and Lego. We ran comment on the 2014 National Architecture Awards and on the Frank Gehry effect in Sydney. And rest assured news/calligraphy hounds, when a new font breaks, we’re on it. Comme des Garçons (Rei Kawakubo) / Spring/Summer 1997 / Collection: Kyoto Costume Institute. Photo: Takashi Hatakeyama Clothes took over our galleries this year, from the Golden Age of Hollywood in Brisbane to Jean Paul Gaultier and his fashion stripe in Melbourne. And our Story of series explained the origins of iconic fashion items from tweed to the top hat. Visual art is amazing, in case you hadn’t noticed. We ran loads on this, as you’d expect, including Sasha Grishin on how hierarchies happen in contemporary art, Ted Snell on Western Australian’s exclusion from the national art conversation, and Joanna Mendelssohn on mass media’s influence on visual culture. It’s been an interesting year in Australian digital copyright law, with many of us wondering what’s at stake in the debate, particularly if it would delay our Monday night viewing of Game of Thrones. We used the 2014 World Cup, the Winter Olympics and the Tour de France as an excuse to talk about Brazilian street art, architecture and the samba; brilliant Russian writers, Sochi on screen and whether the French still care about the Tour. Our series on public morality generated much discussion, as did our ongoing series on the history of violence and another on death and dying. Before you check out for the year, take a look at our end of year series, see a summer art blockbuster, hark back to the Christmas’s pagan origins and enjoy some jolly good Christmas pud. Top ten Arts + Culture stories by readership in 2014 Weighing up the evidence for the ‘Historical Jesus’ by Raphael Lataster, University of Sydney Peaches Geldof and the new, hyper-experience of mortality by Rosemary Overell, University of Otago Actually, women, you do need feminism by Michelle Smith, Deakin University Three questions not to ask about art – and four to ask instead by Kit Messham-Muir, University of Newcastle Death and families – when ‘normal’ grief can last a lifetime by Zoë Krupka, La Trobe University The rape scene in Brad Pitt’s Fury no-one is talking about by Melanie O'Brien, University of Technology, Sydney We don’t need no (moral) education? Five things you should learn about ethics by Patrick Stokes, Deakin University SBS’s First Contact is the real ‘festering sore’ of the nation by Chelsea Bond, The University of Queensland Lorde vs Miley: where young feminism meets old class bias by Rosemary Overell, University of Otago Girl crush anyone? The evolution of ‘lesbian chic’ by Kate Farhall, University of Melbourne Are you an academic or researcher? Would you like to write for the Arts + Culture section of The Conversation? Contact us with your idea. Australian culture 2014, the year that was Alix Bromley, The Conversation, Catriona Menzies-Pike, The Conversation, Paul Dalgarno, The Conversation Authors Alix Bromley Editor Catriona Menzies-Pike Editor Paul Dalgarno Editor Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.p7nxujadr","title":"2014, the year that was: Arts + Culture","url":"https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-arts-culture-35486","id":489},{"content_hash":"b9a9bc4b98e83e8ea44cf5357ec12b35d036084a5fe64edc1ce7f4ccd1078bed","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:17.861332+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/sitemaps/","language":"","text":"The sitemap framework¶ Django comes with a high-level sitemap-generating framework to create sitemap XML files. Overview¶ A sitemap is an XML file on your website that tells search-engine indexers how frequently your pages change and how “important” certain pages are in relation to other pages on your site. This information helps search engines index your site. The Django sitemap framework automates the creation of this XML file by letting you express this information in Python code. It works much like Django’s syndication framework. To create a sitemap, write a Sitemap class and point to it in your URLconf. Installation¶ To install the sitemap app, follow these steps: Add 'django.contrib.sitemaps' to your INSTALLED_APPS setting. Make sure your TEMPLATES setting contains a DjangoTemplates backend whose APP_DIRS options is set to True. It’s in there by default, so you’ll only need to change this if you’ve changed that setting. Make sure you’ve installed the sites framework. (Note: The sitemap application doesn’t install any database tables. The only reason it needs to go into INSTALLED_APPS is so that the Loader() template loader can find the default templates.) Initialization¶ views.sitemap(request, sitemaps, section=None, template_name='sitemap.xml', content_type='application/xml')¶ To activate sitemap generation on your Django site, add this line to your URLconf: from django.contrib.sitemaps.views import sitemap path( \"sitemap.xml\", sitemap, {\"sitemaps\": sitemaps}, name=\"django.contrib.sitemaps.views.sitemap\", ) This tells Django to build a sitemap when a client accesses /sitemap.xml. The name of the sitemap file is not important, but the location is. Search engines will only index links in your sitemap for the current URL level and below. For instance, if sitemap.xml lives in your root directory, it may reference any URL in your site. However, if your sitemap lives at /content/sitemap.xml, it may only reference URLs that begin with /content/. The sitemap view takes an extra, required argument: {'sitemaps': sitemaps}. sitemaps should be a dictionary that maps a short section label (e.g., blog or news) to its Sitemap class (e.g., BlogSitemap or NewsSitemap). It may also map to an instance of a Sitemap class (e.g., BlogSitemap(some_var)). Sitemap classes¶ A Sitemap class is a Python class that represents a “section” of entries in your sitemap. For example, one Sitemap class could represent all the entries of your blog, while another could represent all of the events in your events calendar. In the simplest case, all these sections get lumped together into one sitemap.xml, but it’s also possible to use the framework to generate a sitemap index that references individual sitemap files, one per section. (See Creating a sitemap index below.) Sitemap classes must subclass django.contrib.sitemaps.Sitemap. They can live anywhere in your codebase. An example¶ Let’s assume you have a blog system, with an Entry model, and you want your sitemap to include all the links to your individual blog entries. Here’s how your sitemap class might look: from django.contrib.sitemaps import Sitemap from blog.models import Entry class BlogSitemap(Sitemap): changefreq = \"never\" priority = 0.5 def items(self): return Entry.objects.filter(is_draft=False) def lastmod(self, obj): return obj.pub_date Note: changefreq and priority are class attributes corresponding to <changefreq> and <priority> elements, respectively. They can be made callable as functions, as lastmod was in the example. items() is a method that returns a sequence or QuerySet of objects. The objects returned will get passed to any callable methods corresponding to a sitemap property (location, lastmod, changefreq, and priority). lastmod should return a datetime. There is no location method in this example, but you can provide it in order to specify the URL for your object. By default, location calls get_absolute_url() on each object and returns the result. Sitemap class reference¶ class Sitemap[source]¶ A Sitemap class can define the following methods/attributes: items()[source]¶ Required. A method that returns a sequence or QuerySet of objects. The framework doesn’t care what type of objects they are; all that matters is that these objects get passed to the location, lastmod, changefreq and priority methods. location[source]¶ Optional. Either a method or attribute. If it’s a method, it should return the absolute path for a given object as returned by items(). If it’s an attribute, its value should be a string representing an absolute path to use for every object returned by items(). In both cases, “absolute path” means a URL that doesn’t include the protocol or domain. Examples: Good: '/foo/bar/' Bad: 'example.com/foo/bar/' Bad: 'https://example.com/foo/bar/' If location isn’t provided, the framework will call the get_absolute_url() method on each object as returned by items. To specify a protocol other than 'http', use protocol. lastmod¶ Optional. Either a method or attribute. If it’s a method, it should take one argument – an object as returned by items() – and return that object’s last-modified date/time as a datetime. If it’s an attribute, its value should be a datetime representing the last-modified date/time for every object returned by items(). If all items in a sitemap have a lastmod, the sitemap generated by views.sitemap() will have a Last-Modified header equal to the latest lastmod. You can activate the ConditionalGetMiddleware to make Django respond appropriately to requests with an If-Modified-Since header which will prevent sending the sitemap if it hasn’t changed. paginator[source]¶ Optional. This property returns a Paginator for items(). If you generate sitemaps in a batch you may want to override this as a cached property in order to avoid multiple items() calls. changefreq¶ Optional. Either a method or attribute. If it’s a method, it should take one argument – an object as returned by items() – and return that object’s change frequency as a string. If it’s an attribute, its value should be a string representing the change frequency of every object returned by items(). Possible values for changefreq, whether you use a method or attribute, are: 'always' 'hourly' 'daily' 'weekly' 'monthly' 'yearly' 'never' priority¶ Optional. Either a method or attribute. If it’s a method, it should take one argument – an object as returned by items() – and return that object’s priority as either a string or float. If it’s an attribute, its value should be either a string or float representing the priority of every object returned by items(). Example values for priority: 0.4, 1.0. The default priority of a page is 0.5. See the sitemaps.org documentation for more. protocol¶ Optional. This attribute defines the protocol ('http' or 'https') of the URLs in the sitemap. If it isn’t set, the protocol with which the sitemap was requested is used. If the sitemap is built outside the context of a request, the default is 'https'. limit¶ Optional. This attribute defines the maximum number of URLs included on each page of the sitemap. Its value should not exceed the default value of 50000, which is the upper limit allowed in the Sitemaps protocol. i18n¶ Optional. A boolean attribute that defines if the URLs of this sitemap should be generated using all of your LANGUAGES. The default is False. languages¶ Optional. A sequence of language codes to use for generating alternate links when i18n is enabled. Defaults to LANGUAGES. alternates¶ Optional. A boolean attribute. When used in conjunction with i18n generated URLs will each have a list of alternate links pointing to other language versions using the hreflang attribute. The default is False. x_default¶ Optional. A boolean attribute. When True the alternate links generated by alternates will contain a hreflang=\"x-default\" fallback entry with a value of LANGUAGE_CODE. The default is False. get_latest_lastmod()[source]¶ Optional. A method that returns the latest value returned by lastmod. This function is used to add the lastmod attribute to Sitemap index context variables. By default get_latest_lastmod() returns: If lastmod is an attribute: lastmod. If lastmod is a method: The latest lastmod returned by calling the method with all items returned by items(). get_languages_for_item(item)[source]¶ Optional. A method that returns the sequence of language codes for which the item is displayed. By default get_languages_for_item() returns languages. Shortcuts¶ The sitemap framework provides a convenience class for a common case: class GenericSitemap(info_dict, priority=None, changefreq=None, protocol=None)[source]¶ The django.contrib.sitemaps.GenericSitemap class allows you to create a sitemap by passing it a dictionary which has to contain at least a queryset entry. This queryset will be used to generate the items of the sitemap. It may also have a date_field entry that specifies a date field for objects retrieved from the queryset. This will be used for the lastmod attribute and get_latest_lastmod() methods in the generated sitemap. The priority, changefreq, and protocol keyword arguments allow specifying these attributes for all URLs. Example¶ Here’s an example of a URLconf using GenericSitemap: from django.contrib.sitemaps import GenericSitemap from django.contrib.sitemaps.views import sitemap from django.urls import path from blog.models import Entry info_dict = { \"queryset\": Entry.objects.all(), \"date_field\": \"pub_date\", } urlpatterns = [ # some generic view using info_dict # ... # the sitemap path( \"sitemap.xml\", sitemap, {\"sitemaps\": {\"blog\": GenericSitemap(info_dict, priority=0.6)}}, name=\"django.contrib.sitemaps.views.sitemap\", ), ] Sitemap for static views¶ Often you want the search engine crawlers to index views which are neither object detail pages nor flatpages. The solution is to explicitly list URL names for these views in items and call reverse() in the location method of the sitemap. For example: # sitemaps.py from django.contrib import sitemaps from django.urls import reverse class StaticViewSitemap(sitemaps.Sitemap): priority = 0.5 changefreq = \"daily\" def items(self): return [\"main\", \"about\", \"license\"] def location(self, item): return reverse(item) # urls.py from django.contrib.sitemaps.views import sitemap from django.urls import path from .sitemaps import StaticViewSitemap from . import views sitemaps = { \"static\": StaticViewSitemap, } urlpatterns = [ path(\"\", views.main, name=\"main\"), path(\"about/\", views.about, name=\"about\"), path(\"license/\", views.license, name=\"license\"), # ... path( \"sitemap.xml\", sitemap, {\"sitemaps\": sitemaps}, name=\"django.contrib.sitemaps.views.sitemap\", ), ] Creating a sitemap index¶ views.index(request, sitemaps, template_name='sitemap_index.xml', content_type='application/xml', sitemap_url_name='django.contrib.sitemaps.views.sitemap')¶ The sitemap framework also has the ability to create a sitemap index that references individual sitemap files, one per each section defined in your sitemaps dictionary. The only differences in usage are: You use two views in your URLconf: django.contrib.sitemaps.views.index() and django.contrib.sitemaps.views.sitemap(). The django.contrib.sitemaps.views.sitemap() view should take a section keyword argument. Here’s what the relevant URLconf lines would look like for the example above: from django.contrib.sitemaps import views urlpatterns = [ path( \"sitemap.xml\", views.index, {\"sitemaps\": sitemaps}, name=\"django.contrib.sitemaps.views.index\", ), path( \"sitemap-<section>.xml\", views.sitemap, {\"sitemaps\": sitemaps}, name=\"django.contrib.sitemaps.views.sitemap\", ), ] This will automatically generate a sitemap.xml file that references both sitemap-flatpages.xml and sitemap-blog.xml. The Sitemap classes and the sitemaps dict don’t change at all. If all sitemaps have a lastmod returned by get_latest_lastmod() the sitemap index will have a Last-Modified header equal to the latest lastmod. You should create an index file if one of your sitemaps has more than 50,000 URLs. In this case, Django will automatically paginate the sitemap, and the index will reflect that. If you’re not using the vanilla sitemap view – for example, if it’s wrapped with a caching decorator – you must name your sitemap view and pass sitemap_url_name to the index view: from django.contrib.sitemaps import views as sitemaps_views from django.views.decorators.cache import cache_page urlpatterns = [ path( \"sitemap.xml\", cache_page(86400)(sitemaps_views.index), {\"sitemaps\": sitemaps, \"sitemap_url_name\": \"sitemaps\"}, ), path( \"sitemap-<section>.xml\", cache_page(86400)(sitemaps_views.sitemap), {\"sitemaps\": sitemaps}, name=\"sitemaps\", ), ] Template customization¶ If you wish to use a different template for each sitemap or sitemap index available on your site, you may specify it by passing a template_name parameter to the sitemap and index views via the URLconf: from django.contrib.sitemaps import views urlpatterns = [ path( \"custom-sitemap.xml\", views.index, {\"sitemaps\": sitemaps, \"template_name\": \"custom_sitemap.html\"}, name=\"django.contrib.sitemaps.views.index\", ), path( \"custom-sitemap-<section>.xml\", views.sitemap, {\"sitemaps\": sitemaps, \"template_name\": \"custom_sitemap.html\"}, name=\"django.contrib.sitemaps.views.sitemap\", ), ] These views return TemplateResponse instances which allow you to easily customize the response data before rendering. For more details, see the TemplateResponse documentation. Context variables¶ When customizing the templates for the index() and sitemap() views, you can rely on the following context variables. Index¶ The variable sitemaps is a list of objects containing the location and lastmod attribute for each of the sitemaps. Each URL exposes the following attributes: location: The location (url & page) of the sitemap. lastmod: Populated by the get_latest_lastmod() method for each sitemap. Sitemap¶ The variable urlset is a list of URLs that should appear in the sitemap. Each URL exposes attributes as defined in the Sitemap class: alternates changefreq item lastmod location priority The alternates attribute is available when i18n and alternates are enabled. It is a list of other language versions, including the optional x_default fallback, for each URL. Each alternate is a dictionary with location and lang_code keys. The item attribute has been added for each URL to allow more flexible customization of the templates, such as Google news sitemaps. Assuming Sitemap’s items() would return a list of items with publication_data and a tags field something like this would generate a Google News compatible sitemap: <?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?> <urlset xmlns=\"https://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9\" xmlns:news=\"https://www.google.com/schemas/sitemap-news/0.9\"> {% spaceless %} {% for url in urlset %} <url> <loc>{{ url.location }}</loc> {% if url.lastmod %}<lastmod>{{ url.lastmod|date:\"Y-m-d\" }}</lastmod>{% endif %} {% if url.changefreq %}<changefreq>{{ url.changefreq }}</changefreq>{% endif %} {% if url.priority %}<priority>{{ url.priority }}</priority>{% endif %} <news:news> {% if url.item.publication_date %}<news:publication_date>{{ url.item.publication_date|date:\"Y-m-d\" }}</news:publication_date>{% endif %} {% if url.item.tags %}<news:keywords>{{ url.item.tags }}</news:keywords>{% endif %} </news:news> </url> {% endfor %} {% endspaceless %} </urlset>","title":"The sitemap framework¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/sitemaps/","id":490},{"content_hash":"cdb3838cfc0f61978e012941cc1fa4eb22fa043c5d420c65d4ea10ce05bda725","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:17.812748+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/how-to-change-bvn-phone-number","language":"","text":"How To Change BVN Phone Number in NigeriaByEmmanuel AgwuMay 22, 2023•5mins ReadKey TakeawaysChanging your BVN phone number requires an in-person bank visit with a valid ID, your BVN, and your new SIM. You cannot update your BVN phone number online, through a mobile app, or via USSD.The CBN now allows BVN phone number changes only once in a lifetime, making it essential to choose a permanent phone number that is registered in your name and will remain active for the long term.Your 11-digit BVN number cannot be changed, but certain BVN details can be updated, including your phone number, name, or date of birth, subject to CBN rules and supporting documentation.Protect your BVN from fraud by avoiding unofficial BVN update websites and agents, keeping your registered SIM active, and reporting lost SIM cards immediately to reduce the risk of SIM swap and identity fraud. Important: The CBN BVN Phone Number Update Now Once-in-a-Lifetime RuleCan You Change Your BVN Phone Number Online?Can You Change Your BVN Number Itself?BVN Update Process: What You Can and Cannot UpdateIs there a BVN self-service portal or app?What You Need to Change or Update Your BVN Phone NumberHow to Change Your BVN Phone Number: Step by StepHow to Link a New Phone Number to Your BVNHow Long Does It Take to Change or Update a BVN Phone Number?Can I Change My BVN Phone Number at Any Bank?How to Change Your BVN Phone Number at GTBank, First Bank, UBA and Other BanksHow to Change the Name or Date of Birth on Your BVNHow to Check Which Phone Number Is Linked to Your BVNProtect Yourself From BVN FraudBVN Verification for BusinessesShare this post To change your BVN phone number, you must visit a branch of your bank in person with a valid ID, your BVN, and proof of the new number. You cannot change your BVN phone number online, by app, or over the phone. Since 1 May 2026, the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) allows you to change the phone number on your BVN only once in your lifetime, so the number you switch to should be one you intend to keep permanently. check your BVN on Access Bank Important: The CBN BVN Phone Number Update Now Once-in-a-Lifetime Rule This is the single most important thing to know before you start.In a circular issued to banks on 13 March 2026 and effective from 1 May 2026, the CBN restricted BVN phone number updates to once in a lifetime. The rule applies across every bank, fintech, microfinance bank and mobile money operator in Nigeria, and affects all BVN holders. NIBSS recorded BVN enrolment at 68.59 million as of March 2026. What this means for you in practice: If you have never changed your BVN phone number, you have one change available. Use it carefully.Once you use it, the number is locked. Any further change requires exceptional regulatory approval, involving extensive documentation and verification.Choose a number you control personally and intend to keep for years. Do not use a work line, a borrowed phone, or a number tied to a network you plan to leave.Before you change anything, confirm the new SIM is registered in your own name and is active. Why the CBN did this. The linked phone number is used for one-time passwords (OTPs), transaction alerts and account recovery, which makes it the main target in SIM-swap fraud. A fraudster swaps your SIM, requests a BVN phone number change, then resets your banking credentials and drains the account. NIBSS recorded 62,901 fraud cases linked to social engineering schemes in 2023, many involving compromised phone numbers. Capping changes closes that route. Other BVN changes that took effect alongside it. The CBN also directed banks to operate a temporary watchlist, where a BVN linked to suspicious activity can be flagged for up to 24 hours while the bank contacts you to verify a transaction. BVN enrolment is also restricted to individuals aged 18 and above. Can You Change Your BVN Phone Number Online? No. There is no official online portal, mobile app, or USSD code that lets you change your BVN phone number online. Any website or app claiming to do it is not legitimate, and you should not enter your BVN or bank details there.The change must be done in person at a bank branch. This is deliberate, for four reasons: 1. Identity verification: The bank must confirm the person requesting the change is the actual BVN holder, which requires physical ID checks and often biometric confirmation.2. Fraud prevention:An online change route would be exactly the weakness SIM-swap fraudsters exploit. In-person verification removes it.3. Regulatory compliance.Nigerian banks must meet CBN requirements on customer identification and fraud prevention, and in-person verification is how they evidence it.4. Data protection: Your BVN links to every bank account you hold. Requiring physical presence limits unauthorised access to that record.Many people ask how to change a BVN phone number without going to the bank. There is currently no legitimate way to do this. If your bank offers to start the request through its app or customer service line, you will still be required to complete it at a branch. Can You Change Your BVN Number Itself? No, and this is a different question worth separating clearly. People often ask can BVN be changed, or how to change my BVN number, when what they actually need is a phone number update.Your BVN number is permanent. The 11-digit BVN issued when you enrolled cannot be changed, replaced, renewed or reissued. It is your identifier across every bank in Nigeria for life.You cannot have two BVNs. Enrolling more than once is not permitted, and duplicate BVNs are flagged and merged or deactivated. If you believe you have two, report it to your bank rather than using both.What you can change is the information attached to your BVN, such as your phone number, name, or date of birth, subject to the rules and limits below.So, to answer directly: can I change my BVN number? No. Can I change my BVN phone number? Yes, once in a lifetime. BVN Update Process: What You Can and Cannot Update The BVN update process and BVN modification cover several different requests, and they follow different rules. This table sets out what is possible. What you want to updatePossible?HowLimitPhone number on your BVNYesIn person at a bank branchOnce in a lifetime since 1 May 2026Name or spelling correctionYesIn person, with documentary evidenceReviewed case by caseDate of birth correctionYes, restrictedIn person, with birth certificate or age declarationStrictly limited, repeat changes not permittedAddress or contact detailsYesIn person at your bankNo published limitThe 11-digit BVN number itselfNoNot possiblePermanent for lifeHolding a second BVNNoNot permittedDuplicates are merged or deactivated Is there a BVN self-service portal or app? No. There is no NIBSS or CBN BVN portal, app, or website that lets you modify your BVN details yourself. NIBSS operates the NIBSS BVN validation portal infrastructure for banks, not a public modification service. Any app or website offering BVN self-service modification is not legitimate, and entering your BVN there puts your bank accounts at risk.Every BVN modification runs through a licensed bank, in person, with identity verification. If you are told otherwise by a message, social media post, or someone offering to do it for a fee, treat it as fraud.What \"modify your BVN\" means when your bank asks. If a bank prompts you to modify or update your BVN, it usually means the details on your BVN record no longer match the details on your bank account, most often the phone number. Banks require these to match before granting mobile banking access. Resolving it means visiting a branch to align the two records. What You Need to Change or Update Your BVN Phone Number If you are wondering what do I need to change my BVN phone number, bring all of the following to the branch. Missing one item is the most common reason people are turned away and have to return. A valid government-issued ID. An international passport, driver's licence, National ID card (NIN slip or card), or voter's card.Your BVN. Bring your BVN acknowledgement slip if you still have it. If not, you can retrieve your BVN by dialling 5650# from the phone currently linked to it.The new SIM card, active and in your name. Some banks ask for proof of ownership, such as the SIM pack or a printout from your network showing the number is registered to you.Your account details for the bank where you are making the request.The old phone number if you still have access to it, since some banks send a confirmation OTP to the existing number as part of verification. You will complete a BVN update or change request form at the branch, and you may be asked to redo biometric capture, meaning fingerprints and a photograph, to authenticate the change against your BVN record. How to Change Your BVN Phone Number: Step by Step Follow these steps in order to change the phone number on your BVN.Confirm you have not already used your one change. If you have changed your BVN phone number before, ask your bank whether a further change is possible before making the trip.Register and activate the new SIM in your own name. Complete NIN-SIM linkage with your network first, since a number not registered to you will fail verification.Visit a branch of your bank in person. Go to the bank where you hold your primary account. Some banks require the branch where your BVN was originally enrolled, so call ahead to confirm.Request the BVN update or change request form at the customer service or BVN desk.Complete the form accurately, entering your BVN, your current registered number, and the new number exactly.Submit your ID and supporting documents, including proof of ownership of the new number if requested.Complete biometric verification if the bank requires it, usually fingerprints and a photograph.Collect your acknowledgement or reference for the request before leaving, so you can follow up.Confirm the update has gone through after a few working days by checking that OTPs and transaction alerts arrive on the new number. How to Link a New Phone Number to Your BVN Linking a new number to your BVN and changing your BVN phone number are the same process. There is no separate procedure: whether you search how to link my BVN to my new number or how to change it, the bank updates the single phone number field on your BVN record, replacing the old number with the new one. This matters because your BVN holds one phone number, not several. You cannot link two numbers to one BVN, and you cannot add a second number while keeping the first. Linking a new number means replacing the existing one, which is why it counts against the CBN's once-in-a-lifetime limit. To link your new number to your BVN:Register and activate the new SIM in your own name, completing NIN-SIM linkage with your network.Visit your bank branch in person with a valid ID and your BVN.Request the BVN update form and enter the new number you want linked.Complete identity and biometric verification.Confirm after a few working days that OTPs and alerts now arrive on the new number. One point often confused: linking your BVN to a bank account is a different task entirely, and that one can be done remotely. Most banks let you link an existing BVN to an account by USSD, such as GTBank's 73720*BVN#, or through internet banking. That process attaches your BVN to an account. It does not change the phone number stored on the BVN itself, which still requires a branch visit. How Long Does It Take to Change or Update a BVN Phone Number? How long it takes to change a BVN phone number depends on your bank. Most complete the update within a few working days of a correctly submitted request, and some process it within 24 to 48 hours. Timing depends on internal verification and how quickly the change reflects in the NIBSS BVN database. If OTPs and alerts are still going to your old number after about five working days, return to the branch with your acknowledgement slip and ask them to confirm the update was submitted and reflected. Can I Change My BVN Phone Number at Any Bank? Not necessarily. Many people ask can I change my BVN phone number in any bank, and the safest answer is to go to the bank where you hold your primary account, ideally the bank where your BVN was originally enrolled. Because your BVN is shared across all Nigerian banks, an update made by one bank updates the central NIBSS record. However, individual banks set their own internal rules about which branches can process BVN modifications, and some will only handle it where the BVN was enrolled or where you hold an active account. Call your bank's customer service line before travelling to a branch. How to Change Your BVN Phone Number at GTBank, First Bank, UBA and Other Banks Whether you are searching how to change my BVN phone number online GTB, First Bank, or UBA, the process is broadly identical at every Nigerian bank, because the rules are set by the CBN and NIBSS rather than by individual banks. In every case the change is made in person at a branch, using a BVN update form, valid ID, and biometric verification where required. No major Nigerian bank offers a fully online BVN phone number change. What differs between banks is only the practical detail: which branches process BVN modifications, whether they require the branch of original enrolment, and what proof of new-number ownership they ask for. GTBank, for example, directs customers to visit a branch to update a registered phone number. The reliable step is to call your bank's official customer service line first and confirm the branch and documents before you travel. Use the number printed on your debit card or on the bank's official website, never a number from a social media post or WhatsApp message. What If You No Longer Have Access to Your Old Number? If you lost the SIM linked to your BVN, this is now a more serious problem than it used to be, because of the once-in-a-lifetime cap. Your first step is to try to recover the same number from your network operator. Most Nigerian networks will reissue a SIM with your existing number if you visit their office with the ID used to register it. Recovering the original number avoids using up your single BVN change entirely. Only if the number is genuinely unrecoverable should you use your one lifetime change. In that case, take your ID and a police report if the phone was stolen, and explain the situation at your bank branch. Because you will be verified without an OTP to your old number, expect stricter identity checks, including biometrics. How to Change the Name or Date of Birth on Your BVN Yes, you can. How to change your BVN name and how to change your BVN date of birth are a separate process from a phone number change, known as BVN modification, and both are handled in person at a bank branch. For a BVN name change or correction, bring supporting evidence of the correct name, such as a birth certificate, sworn affidavit, marriage certificate for a change of surname, or newspaper publication where required. Banks will not amend a name without documentary proof. For a BVN date of birth correction, banks apply strict limits, because date of birth is a core identity field used in verification. Bring your birth certificate or age declaration and a valid ID. Expect the request to be reviewed rather than processed on the spot, and be aware that repeated date of birth changes are not permitted. In both cases you complete a BVN modification form at the branch and may need to redo biometric capture. Processing usually takes longer than a phone number change, since the request is reviewed against your submitted evidence. How to Check Which Phone Number Is Linked to Your BVN Before changing anything, confirm which number is currently on your BVN record. This answers how to know the number linked to my BVN. Dial *565*0# from the phone number you believe is linked. If the BVN is returned, that number is the one on file. If nothing comes back, that is not the registered number. You can also ask at your bank branch, or check within your bank's mobile app profile section where your registered contact details are displayed. Never use third-party websites or apps that ask for your BVN to check your details, as these are a common phishing route. BVN guides:How to Check BVN on Access BankHow to Check BVN on First BankHow to Check BVN on MTN, Glo, 9mobile and AirtelHow to Check My BVN on GTBank Mobile App. Protect Yourself From BVN Fraud Your BVN is the backbone of your banking identity, and fraudsters know it.Never share your BVN with anyone who contacts you unsolicited. Legitimate banks do not call and ask for it.No one should charge you to change your BVN phone number. The process at your bank does not require paying an agent.Ignore \"BVN update\" links sent by SMS, WhatsApp, or social media. There is no legitimate online BVN modification portal.Report a lost SIM immediately to both your network and your bank, since a SIM swap is the first step in most account takeovers.Keep your registered number active. A number deactivated for long inactivity can be recycled to another user, which creates a serious security exposure on your bank accounts. BVN Verification for Businesses For banks, fintechs and other regulated businesses, the CBN's tightening of BVN rules raises the bar on customer verification. A phone number that no longer belongs to the customer, or a BVN record that does not match the person presenting it, is exactly the gap fraudsters exploit during onboarding. Youverify provides BVN verification and identity verification built for Nigeria and the wider African market, letting compliance teams confirm in real time that a BVN is valid, that it belongs to the customer presenting it, and that the associated details match official records. This supports KYC and AML obligations while keeping onboarding fast. Top 6 Identity Verification Methods for Businesses Book a demo to see how Youverify handles BVN verification at scale. FAQsFrequently Asked Questions1. Can I change my BVN phone number online?No. There is no official online portal, app, or USSD code for changing the phone number linked to your BVN. The change must be made in person at a bank branch with a valid ID, because the bank has to verify your identity and often your biometrics. Any website or app offering an online BVN phone number change is not legitimate. 2. How many times can I change my BVN phone number?Once. Since 1 May 2026, a CBN directive limits BVN phone number updates to once in a lifetime, across all banks, fintechs and mobile money operators in Nigeria. After you use that single change, further updates require exceptional regulatory approval with extensive documentation, so choose a number you intend to keep permanently. 3. Can I change my BVN number itself?No. Your 11-digit BVN is permanent and cannot be changed, replaced or reissued. It identifies you across every bank in Nigeria for life. You also cannot hold two BVNs. What you can update is the information attached to it, such as your phone number, name or date of birth. 4. How do I link my new phone number to my BVN?Linking a new number to your BVN is the same process as changing it. Your BVN stores one phone number only, so linking a new one replaces the existing one and counts against your single lifetime change. Visit your bank branch in person with a valid ID, your BVN, and the new SIM registered in your name, then complete the BVN update form and identity verification. Linking your BVN to a bank account is a separate task that can often be done by USSD or internet banking. 5. Is there a BVN self-service portal or app?No. There is no official NIBSS or CBN self-service portal, app, or website that lets you modify your BVN details. NIBSS runs BVN validation infrastructure for banks, not a public modification service. Every BVN update must go through a licensed bank in person. Any app or site offering BVN self-service modification is not legitimate and should not be given your BVN. 6. What does the BVN update process involve?You complete a BVN update or modification form at a bank branch, submit a valid government-issued ID and supporting documents, and complete biometric verification if the bank requires it. Phone number updates are capped at once in a lifetime since 1 May 2026. Name and date of birth corrections need documentary evidence such as a birth certificate, sworn affidavit or marriage certificate, and are reviewed rather than processed immediately. Your 11-digit BVN number itself can never be changed. 7. What do I need to change my BVN phone number?Bring a valid government-issued ID such as an international passport, driver's licence, National ID or voter's card, your BVN or acknowledgement slip, and the new SIM card registered in your own name. Some banks also ask for proof of ownership of the new number and may require biometric verification at the branch. 8. How long does it take to change a BVN phone number?Most banks complete the update within a few working days, and some within 24 to 48 hours, depending on internal verification and how quickly the change reflects in the NIBSS database. If alerts are still going to your old number after about five working days, return to the branch with your acknowledgement slip. 9. Can I change my BVN phone number at any bank?Go to the bank where you hold your primary account, ideally the one where your BVN was enrolled. Although the update reflects across all banks through the central NIBSS record, individual banks set their own rules about which branches handle BVN modifications. Call your bank's official customer service line to confirm before travelling. 10. How do I know which phone number is linked to my BVN?Dial *5650# from the number you think is linked. If your BVN is returned, that is the registered number. You can also confirm at your bank branch or in your bank's mobile app profile section. Never use third-party websites that ask for your BVN to check details, as these are commonly used for phishing. 11. What if I lost the SIM linked to my BVN?Try to recover the same number from your network operator first, by visiting their office with the ID used to register the SIM. Recovering the original number avoids using your single lifetime BVN change. If the number is genuinely unrecoverable, visit your bank with your ID, and a police report if the phone was stolen, and expect stricter identity checks including biometrics.Related ArticlesView All PostsIdentity DocumentsEmmanuel Agwu•May 26, 2023How to Perform Driver's License Verification OnlineRead MoreIdentity DocumentsEmmanuel Agwu•June 6, 2023How To Renew Your Driver's License In South AfricaRead MoreIdentity DocumentsPrince, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•March 15, 2023What are the Acceptable Employment Verification Documents? Read More","title":"How To Change BVN Phone Number in Nigeria","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/how-to-change-bvn-phone-number","id":491},{"content_hash":"876ea646bb943bbdb0abbbb06e2fb3801d00ac272fb443897dfb0b69b67f4fc8","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:17.169774+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-edgar-faure-propose-l-institution-en-faveur-des-magistrats-d-une-indemnite-de-services-judiciaires_2073244_1819218.html","language":"","text":"M. EDGAR FAURE PROPOSE L'INSTITUTION EN FAVEUR DES MAGISTRATS D'UNE \" INDEMNITÉ DE SERVICES JUDICIAIRES \" Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"M. EDGAR FAURE PROPOSE L'INSTITUTION EN FAVEUR DES MAGISTRATS D'UNE \" INDEMNITÉ DE SERVICES JUDICIAIRES \"","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-edgar-faure-propose-l-institution-en-faveur-des-magistrats-d-une-indemnite-de-services-judiciaires_2073244_1819218.html","id":492},{"content_hash":"ab97c6b9e2cf0e5b0c5dd6d96588f1f7de05bd84958525074515d46d4f6acb1a","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:17.112451+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-politics-society-35415","language":"","text":"2014, the year that was: Politics + Society Published: December 28, 2014 3:12pm EST Tony Abbott promised a government of no surprises and no excuses. 2014 delivered anything but. AAP/Lukas Coch https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-politics-society-35415 https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-politics-society-35415 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article In 1996, John Howard offered this aspiration for Australians: I would like to see them comfortable and relaxed about their history; I would like to see them comfortable and relaxed about the present and I’d also like to see them comfortable and relaxed about the future. Howard’s Coalition successor, Tony Abbott, assumed the prime ministership in 2013 with a characteristically pithier undertaking: “a government of no surprises and no excuses”. This year brought us none of the above. Whether by chance or design, Australia returned in 2014 to Howard-era preoccupations: asylum seekers, terrorism, juggling relations with superpowers and allies old and new, budget cuts, national identity, trust in government and the role of the media. Our troops are back at war, too. We are blessed, however, by comparison with other nations. Thais lost the government they elected to a coup for the 19th time. Hong Kong is cleaning up after protests that remind us that China, for all its progress on other fronts, suffers the same democracy deficit that was laid bare in Tiananmen Square 25 years ago. The United Kingdom escaped an existential crisis when Scots voted not to break away after all. Across Asia, fledgling democracies struggled to bury old repressive ways. In a hopeful sign, Indonesia elected a president drawn from outside the old political elite. India put on the biggest democratic show on earth, despite concerns about the victor, Narendra Modhi. And American voters added to Barack Obama’s difficulties by giving his Republican opponents control of Congress. Australians certainly aren’t making life comfortable for their leaders. Opinion polls and the “polls that count” paint a picture of voters disillusioned even with democracy itself. The federal Coalition enjoys the lower house majority that its predecessor lacked. Its problems lie in the Senate. After a re-vote in Western Australia, micro-party senators put on something of a political circus, with Jacqui Lambie leading the way and Clive Palmer doing his bit from the House. State elections have put the Abbott government on notice. While Tasmania evicted a 16-year-old ALP government, Labor defied the odds in South Australia, then ousted a one-term government in Victoria for the first time since 1955. Challenges to the old order The major political parties and media institutions face many similar challenges. The Coalition and Labor’s failure to embrace opportunities for innovation in public participation and representation mirror the print giants’ struggle to renew fragmenting and ageing audiences. Old loyalties and habits have been swept away. Issues of political dysfunction and public information, participation and trust are becoming pressing. This is why The Conversation has devoted series to reforming political parties, the Federation, federal-state relations, the state of Australia and public broadcasting. Vested interests and Big Data-driven party branding threaten to squeeze out the vitality, vision and conviction that give voters reason to believe. The response to Gough Whitlam’s death may have been more a mark of what Australians feel is missing from their politics than nostalgia for his government. The mixing of money and power by party machines that resist public scrutiny and input makes for an unhealthy brew. ICAC lifted the lid on corruption that crosses party lines in New South Wales. In Victoria, the toothless IBAC is an issue in the debate on integrity in government. And in Queensland, the Newman government seemed intent on rolling back the years to pre-Fitzgerald Inquiry days. The need for scrutiny to ensure accountable government is obvious. Yet Australian media operate under more constraints than ever. Security laws that target whistleblowers and journalists, online surveillance and data mining all distort the balance between the powers of the state and the rights of citizens. Moves away from open government exacerbate the imbalance. Operation Sovereign Borders took the political desire to control information to absurd lengths with its secrecy about operations to “stop the boats”. A deal to resettle refugees in Cambodia and violent death, abuse and mental trauma in offshore detention centres illustrate how the politics of asylum seekers still takes us to morally and legally dubious places. Gough Whitlam passed away in October 2014 at the age of 98. AAP/Paul Miller Old fears, new wars Only terrorism inspires more disproportionately fearful responses. And we saw terror hit central Sydney when self-proclaimed cleric Man Haron Monis held 17 people hostage in a cafe for 16 hours in late December. Two people and Monis died. Multicultural Australia has been tested since the emergence of Islamic State in Syria and Iraq attracted Australian and other westerners to the conflict. The tenor of the debate may have contributed to a groundswell of resistance to changes to the Racial Discrimination Act. Two moments of poor judgement – George Brandis’s assertion of a “right to be a bigot” and a short-lived parliamentary “burqa ban” – didn’t help. Age-old debates on freedom of expression gained new life. Conflicts in Europe and the Middle East – including Ukraine, Syria, Iraq and Gaza – undeniably make the world a more dangerous place. This was brought home to Australians by the downing of flight MH17. A century after the war to end all wars – the subject of another series – humanity still succumbs to the animal instincts and fears that conflict arouses. Even as more abuses and excesses of the “War on Terror” are exposed, Australia reprises its knee-jerk responses to 9/11. Once it was Reds under the beds; today it’s foreign fighters in our midst. Salafist extremism and Islamophobia are both polarising forces. In this context, Abbott’s call to “Team Australia” divided as much as it united. The rise of Islamic State in parts of Iraq and Syria drew foreign military forces back into the region. EPA Australia redeemed itself as chair of the UN Security Council. The government’s star performer, Foreign Minister Julie Bishop, even shrugged off the embarrassment of aid cuts being the single biggest budget saving. Abbott attracted mixed reviews as host of the G20 leaders’ summit. He did better in ongoing global trade deals (the subject of another series). Abbott wrapped up agreements with South Korea, Japan and China in quick succession. Institutional challenges Religious institutions still have much work to do to recover from the Royal Commission’s exposure of the sexual abuse of children – and the cover-ups. The Catholic Church, in particular, might benefit from the soul-searching approach of Pope Francis. The scandals in sport roll on too. NRL club Cronulla accepted suspensions for doping. In the AFL, the legal battle between ASADA and Essendon drags on. The Socceroos gave us hope but lost their way in a World Cup that, as our extensive coverage explained, deserved its “world game” billing. Late in the year, the death of cricketer Phillip Hughes put the “triumphs and tragedies” of sport in perspective. Phillip Hughes’ death on the cricket pitch put Australia’s love of sport into perspective. AAP/Dan Peled What next? Ideas about politics and society are naturally and properly contested in a democracy like ours. How we govern ourselves in the 21st century is up for debate. We must take stock of our resources and priorities, and be open to alternatives. These big-picture debates may fail as media clickbait but are important in every other way. The Conversation is dedicated to covering such issues properly. Treasurer Joe Hockey’s first budget sought to redefine the role of government. That is why the directions taken by the budget and the path-breaking Commission of Audit – both the subjects of extensive coverage – have been more than usually contentious. Work and welfare policies, for instance, can make or break the budget and individuals too - especially when the odds are against the jobless. When simple recipes for life fail us, philosophy offers deep stores of wisdom. Articles on philosophy, thought, decision-making and love invariably engaged readers – even when the moral dilemmas were those of the Ice Bucket Challenge. Most enduring political and social solutions require us first to test prevailing beliefs and assumptions, to thrash out our differences before we can make progress. This is a perennial test of national maturity. No such challenge is bigger than reconciliation between Indigenous and non-Indigenous Australians starting with proper recognition of our first peoples: this has eluded us since 1788. Two of our most successful series in 2014, on class and youth in Australia, asked provocative questions about how we see ourselves and our future, and how to make it better. And that sums up our journalistic mission. As we head into 2015, we look forward to engaging a growing global community of readers in The Conversation. Top ten most read stories in 2014 for Politics + Society No, you’re not entitled to your opinion by Patrick Stokes, Deakin University The end justifies the means: why Queensland is losing the bikie war by Terry Goldsworthy, Bond University Islamic State wants Australians to attack Muslims: terror expert by Nick O'Brien, Charles Sturt University How to help take control of your brain and make better decisions by Daniel Bennett, University of Melbourne Crime stats provide reality check in Queensland’s bikie crackdown by Terry Goldsworthy, Bond University ‘Fair trade’ cocaine and ‘conflict-free’ opium: the future of online drug marketing by James Martin, Macquarie University Walter Mitty and The Secret Life of MTV by Lauren Rosewarne, University of Melbourne Bogans and hipsters: we’re talking the living language of class by Christopher Scanlon, LaTrobe University Explainer: can the Senate block the budget? by Adam Webster, University of Adelaide Income and wealth inequality: how is Australia faring? by Peter Whiteford, Australian National University Tony Abbott National security Democracy Federal politics 2014, the year that was John Watson, The Conversation Author John Watson Cities Editor and Deputy Energy + Environment Editor Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.swp6xhc39","title":"2014, the year that was: Politics + Society","url":"https://theconversation.com/2014-the-year-that-was-politics-society-35415","id":493},{"content_hash":"160cca4ee96136e17ac4c5ff0f7a76f0c84d3d52e8e819d4e740bbd2c913892d","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:17.085272+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/astrology/horoscope/daily-career-horoscope-october-09-2026-success-challenges-and-breakthroughs-know-about-your-career/articleshow/134793759.cms","language":"","text":"Daily Career Horoscope, October 09, 2026: Success, challenges, and breakthroughs; Know about your career - The Times of IndiaEditionININUSGCCEnglishEnglishहिन्दीमराठीಕನ್ನಡதமிழ்বাংলাമലയാളംతెలుగుગુજરાતીWeatherSign InTOIAstrologyHoroscopeSun Sign PredictionsCompatibility CalculatorYearly Horoscope 2026Chinese HoroscopeNumerologyNumerology PredictionsPlanets TransitsGemstones Vastu & feng shuikundali dasha & RemediesReligionMonthly HoroscopeOthersToday's ePaperLiveNewsAstrology NewsHoroscope NewsDaily Career Horoscope, October 09, 2026: Success, challenges, and breakthroughs; Know about your careerTrendingHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayDaily Career Horoscope, October 09, 2026: Success, challenges, and breakthroughs; Know about your careerAstroDevam / TOI Astrology / Oct 09, 2026, 05:30 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint AriesCompetition can be a test of your composure thus the best bet is to focus on the performance not the competition. Do not get lost in useless comparisons and arguments and self-justifications. Let your work speak for itself for quality and consistency. If someone is extremely competitive, keep a professional distance and concentrate on your own work. Rivalry may be good when it spurs you on to better, not to become sidetracked. Stay calm, think strategically and focus on delivering concrete results to safeguard your reputation and grow your professional stature.TaurusAt work, you can fix a screw-up you’ve made in the past and get an opportunity to reestablish trust and go on without the weight of unwanted professional baggage. Don’t repeat the mistake. Focus on what you learned from the event. If clarification or remedial action is still necessary, deal with it directly and professionally. Acknowledging a problem and changing your approach might really build confidence in you by others. The secret to professional redemption is frequently not in avoiding any mistakes, but rather showing a sense of responsibility, adaptability, and superior judgment in the wake of them.GeminiThe division of duties into well defined and manageable groups can increase the effectiveness of the expert. Don’t flit from one unrelated job to another – batch similar work together and prioritize before starting. A more systematic approach will help you clear your mind and accomplish your work more accurately. This is especially beneficial when you have multiple tasks operating at the same time. Don’t confuse constant activity with productivity. With better task separation you may manage your time more effectively, stay focused longer, and develop a better rhythm for your job without having to do more work.CancerYou may see some notable advances in your career as the fruits of your previous work start to be acknowledged. Work that may have gone undetected before can now get acknowledgment from seniors, clients or other decision makers. Performance-related incentives can also help build confidence and demonstrate that your participation is making a measurable impact. Let this recognition be the momentum, not an excuse to become complacent. Keep doing what you are doing to get the favorable attention. Being consistent after you’re recognized can earn you professional reputation and can even help to provide the foundation for greater duties, better pay or future development.LeoWorkwise things can feel a bit all over the place so it’s important to order things clearly. Trying to do everything at once can be confusing and have little real progress. Start with the assignments that count the most professionally and work your way down. Don’t assume you know what your obligations are – clarify if you’re not sure. A minimal functioning framework can get control back in no time. Once you prioritize your productivity can go through the roof. You will get professional effectiveness from careful attention direction, not striving to be occupied all day long.VirgoSudden changes at work may create new professional opportunities, but you’ll need to be flexible. It can be disruptive at first but ultimately a helpful chance for shift in responsibility, project structure, team dynamics or expectations. Don’t discount a change just because it wasn’t part of your initial plan. See what the new scenario has to offer and how you may use your current skills to best benefit. Adaptability lets you turn uncertainty into progress. Keeping an open mind professionally can expose you to situations that can bolster your job prospects over the longer term.LibraProfessional camaraderie and better cooperation can help workplace relationships. A combined strategy can facilitate the sharing of duties and help to solve problems that would be difficult to manage on an individual basis. Share information freely. Recognize good contributions. Not let small differences get in the way of larger goals. At the same time, be sure to set proper boundaries so that collaboration does not become reliance. Good professional connections can increase communication, efficiency and morale. Consistent teamwork can develop trust and maybe offer a more supportive environment for future projects and opportunities.ScorpioSkills you learn at work may happen gradually, so it’s more important to be consistent than to master anything right away. It may take some repetition before you feel totally secure with a new task, technique, system, or area of knowledge. If things aren’t improving as quickly as you would like, don’t get disheartened. Apply what you learn in real life and see where you need to make further refinements. Gradual progress can finally result in substantial career benefits. Carefully developed skills tend to be more reliable than hurried skills and can help you build a better foundation for future jobs and special opportunities.SagittariusYou may find more promise for professional advancement through broader relationships and deeper contacts. New collaborators. New clients. New industry contacts or strategic alliances. These are opportunities that don’t come from your current professional network. Seek out collaborations where both sides benefit, not just networking for networking’s sake. If the conversations turn to teamwork, clear expectations will be crucial. Having a bigger professional network can help your firm grow its reach, open new markets and attract valuable viewpoints. Strategic relationship building can be a vital trigger for your next stage of growth.Capricorn Client-centric insights can improve professional outcomes by helping you understand what people really want, not what you think they want. Listen carefully for criticism, questions, objections and preferences that are repeated. These patterns may point to specific ways you might improve your service, communication, product or strategy. Those professionals who can see things from the client’s perspective typically see opportunities that an internal examination does not. Don’t gather feedback, translate relevant observations into specific changes. Better customer orientation can lead to increased satisfaction, connection building and more sustainable professional or corporate success.AquariusBeing fair and clear in your office dealings can enable you to maintain a sense of professional steadiness. Be clear about roles, expectations, and communication, especially when decisions impact coworkers or shared projects. Avoid creating undue ambiguity regarding promises or ownership of work. A balanced approach can build trust and lessen the chances of misunderstandings. Stability is not necessarily spectacular progress, but it is a useful basis for future growth.” An even-handed professional attitude can help to establish your reputation and make co-workers or decision makers more comfortable in depending on your judgment and contribution.PiscesA mentor’s professional assistance can be particularly helpful in providing perspective on responsibilities or career decisions. Listen closely to experienced advice, but analyze how it pertains to your personal situation, rather than taking every proposal as gospel. A mentor can help you spot qualities, weaknesses or possibilities that are hard to identify from where you are now. Ask precise inquiries, helpful direction into actual action. With the correct professional support, you can reduce the learning curve and make smarter decisions about your next steps.This article is written by, Sidhharrth S Kumaar, Registered Pharmacist, Astro Numerologist, Life & Relationship Coach, Vaastu Expert, IKS Expert, I Ching Expert, Energy Healer, Music Therapist, Author of “Think like a Sage Lead like a CEO” series and Chief Astro Strategy Officer (CASO), NumroVani. Get the latest astrology news and horoscope updates. Download the TOI App.About the AuthorAstroDevamAstroDevam is a premium organisation providing ancient and authentic knowledge of Astrology, Vastu, Numerology, and Innovative Corporate Solutions with a contemporary perspective. AstroDevam, having patrons in more than 100 countries, has been promoted by Achary Anita Baranwal and Achary Kalki Krishnan, who not only have Master's Degrees in Astrology, but are engaged in teaching Scientific Astrology, Vastu, and Numerology for more than three decades.Read MoreEnd of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaHot PicksCBSE Date SheetGeorgia Medical Degree RowAparna JhaStock market crashH-1B ProgramRBI Repo Rate HikeNana PatekarWeather tomorrowUS Marines in JapanAmazon PrimeTop TrendingDonna KelcePatrick Mahomes MotherSam BealAsim MunirUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestSimone BilesCJP Chappal ProtestAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTrending StoriesIn AstrologyEntire WebsiteCapricorn Horoscope Today: Smooth progress in work, love and money; use focus to finish tasksPractise Peace Amid Problems: Dhammapada 202 urges calm now; small rests build steadinessSagittarius Horoscope Today: Career gains and side income loom; support your child gentlyDaily Scorpio Horoscope: Support boosts gains; put savings before pleasureLibra Horoscope Today: Low confidence and money strain; avoid junk food and impulse buysLeo Horoscope Today: Use your words gently to heal family strains; favour steady saving nowVirgo Horoscope Today: Moon brings clarity and calm focus; love and money look steadyCancer Horoscope Today: Extra effort lifts work and love; avoid big spendsDaily Gemini Horoscope: Family support steadies you; hidden money and property gains beckonLucky Day For Taurus: Horoscope says calm