template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/health-wellness-newsletter-email-template","id":520},{"content_hash":"16239e2c49b9ecdfec4c367be008a58e6cf801cb692c020cc5f0f089a988e5a8","domain":"www.chinadaily.com.cn","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:12.832065+00:00","final_url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202609/24/WS6ab44f3de4b06d4aa055fda2.html","language":"","text":"Guan Gong spirit resonates with youth worldwide - Chinadaily.com.cn WS6ab44f3de4b06d4aa055fda2 China Edition | Global Edition | ASIA | 中文 China Latest Society Innovation Education Cross-Strait HK/Macao Health Environment Military Video World Asia-Pacific Americas Europe Middle East Africa Business Policies Companies Industries Technology China Up-close China Data Motoring Video Culture Art Music and Theater Film and TV Books Heritage Events and Festivals People Cultural Exchange Video Photo Gallery Fashion Health Travel News City Tours Guides and Tips My Footprints Around the World Features Photo Video Food Sports Soccer Basketball Volleyball Tennis Golf Track and field Swimming Motor China Stars Opinion Cartoons Editorials Global Lens We Comment Columnists Global Views Chinese Perspectives Opinion Line Regional Newspaper China Daily PDF China Daily E-Paper China Daily Global PDF China Daily Global E-Paper Mobile CULTURE News Heritage Art Film Theater Fashion Cultural Exchange Books Photo Video HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER MOBILE 中文 CULTURE News Heritage Art Film Theater Fashion Cultural Exchange Books Photo Video Home / Culture / Cultural Exchange Guan Gong spirit resonates with youth worldwide By ZHU XINGXIN in Yuncheng, Shanxi and ZHOU HUIYING | China Daily | Updated: 2026-09-24 06:14 Share - WeChat Li Zijun (right), a scholar of the Chinese Theatre Literature Society, displays a photo of his grandfather, Li Hongchun, portraying the legendary Chinese figure Guan Gong during the International Youth Dialogue on Loyalty and Honor Without Borders in Yuncheng, Shanxi province, on Wednesday. [Photo by Zhu Xingxin/China Daily] While delving into the essence of traditional Chinese medicine on the Chinese mainland, Lo Chin-Ying, a 24-year-old student from Taiwan, continually reflects on the enduring spirit of loyalty and righteousness embodied by Guan Gong. For many Taiwan compatriots, the belief in Guan Gong carries a profound emotional connection. \"In Taiwan, Guan Gong culture has long been integrated into everyday life,\" Lo, a master's student at Beijing University of Chinese Medicine, said at the International Youth Dialogue on Loyalty and Honor Without Borders. \"Many people visit Guandi temples to pray for blessings when opening businesses, taking exams or moving homes.\" The event, co-hosted by the cyberspace administration of Shanxi province, China Daily Website and the Yuncheng People's Government, kicked off in Yuncheng, Shanxi province, on Wednesday. On the same day, the 37th Guan Gong Cultural Tourism Festival kicked off in Yuncheng, attracting thousands of domestic and international devotees. Guan Gong, also known as Guandi, or Emperor Guan, is the honorific title of Guan Yu, a general of the late Eastern Han Dynasty (25-220) and the Three Kingdoms period (220-280) who is revered as a symbol of loyalty and righteousness. Zhang Xuanjie, a 34-year-old native of Yuncheng who was the champion of the 2011 Miss Tourism World China Finals and International Communication Ambassador for Yuncheng, is deeply involved in the innovative promotion of Guan Gong culture, striving to adapt the ancient culture of loyalty and righteousness to appeal to young people's aesthetics and integrate it into modern life. \"My team is dedicated to developing Guan Gong-themed cultural and creative products, embedding the traditional virtues of loyalty, filial piety, propriety and righteousness into various cultural and creative mediums and daily social interactions,\" she said. Having long been engaged in cultural dissemination, Zhang has witnessed countless touching moments of cultural integration between the two sides of the Taiwan Strait. \"In the spring of 2013, a 500-year-old statue of Guan Gong of the Ming Dynasty (1368-1644) from the Haizhou Guandi Temple made its first journey across the sea to Taiwan,\" she said. \"The cultural pilgrimage across the Strait carried the shared hopes of people from both sides. \"Guan Gong is the spiritual core deeply rooted in Yuncheng's bloodline, and it is also our cultural confidence as we reach out to the world,\" she added. The influence of Guan Gong belief has extended to the whole world, becoming a spiritual bond that unites Chinese people globally. Currently, there are over 30,000 Guandi temples and Guan Gong halls in at least 168 countries and regions worldwide. In Chinese communities in countries like the United States, Singapore, and Thailand, Guandi temples and shrines are commonly seen. Dylan Austin Walker, a US youth representative based in China, noted that Guan Gong is a highly inclusive cultural symbol in the Chinese communities in the US. \"Despite generational differences, diverse origins, and varying lifestyles, overseas Chinese feel a sense of shared roots and genuine reverence when standing before a statue of Guan Gong,\" he said. This year marks the 90th anniversary of the Long March victory. Walker recently retraced parts of the Long March route. \"The righteousness embodied by Guan Gong's legendary journey alone parallels the righteousness demonstrated by the Red Army during the Long March, both representing commitment to the mission of seeking happiness for all mankind,\" he said. Mei Zhengzheng, a researcher at the Chengdu Wuhou Shrine Museum and presenter of the \"Decoding Guan Gong\" series of the Lecture Room, a renowned cultural program on CCTV, said that the culture of loyalty and righteousness of Guan Gong, honed over time, still possesses unique contemporary value and offers practical inspiration for today's youth. \"Loyalty is the foundation of one's character, manifesting as steadfast belief and selfless dedication to one's country, nation and duties, as well as sincerity and keeping promises in interpersonal relationships,\" he said. \"For contemporary youth, loyalty is more about having a sense of mission for national rejuvenation and unwavering responsibility to the country, career and ideals.\" Leveraging digital means, Guan Gong culture is continually bursting with new vitality. Related Stories Three-day festival celebrates Guangong's lasting heritage Confucius cultural festival to bring tradition to life in Qufu Guangxi Normal University Press shines at CAEXPO with books and AI tools Forum highlights cultural heritage, mutual learning Rails and language build bridges Photo Gallery Artworks explore legacy of the Yongding River Solo exhibition celebrates life across mediums Wuriqiefu explores humanity beyond the grasslands Tales and Trails Qingdao 'city drink': Brewing coastal charm China Vibes Factory tourism combines fun and learning 24 Solar Terms End of Heat Editor's Pick Fahai Temple murals: 'Naked-eye 3D' from 500 years ago Serving up emotions: A chat with director Wen Muye Video Culture in the saddle Intangible cultural heritage helps multi-ethnic community prosper Chinaculture.org The ancient ritual of respecting teachers Five great Chinese love legends: Romance across millennia Copyright 1994 - . HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER MOBILE Registration Number: 130349 Mobile English 中文 Desktop Copyright 1994-. All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form.","title":"Guan Gong spirit resonates with youth worldwide","url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/a/202609/24/WS6ab44f3de4b06d4aa055fda2.html","id":521},{"content_hash":"1bf971a55bb295a2e4b3b86026283494ff284a6f4214464621da64577b9bad9e","domain":"developers.google.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:12.769661+00:00","final_url":"https://developers.google.com/photos/picker/reference/rest/v1/mediaItems/list?hl=vi","language":"","text":"Method: mediaItems.list Sử dụng bộ sưu tập để sắp xếp ngăn nắp các trang Lưu và phân loại nội dung dựa trên lựa chọn ưu tiên của bạn. Yêu cầu HTTP Tham số truy vấn Nội dung yêu cầu Nội dung phản hồi Biểu diễn dưới dạng JSON Phạm vi uỷ quyền Trả về danh sách các mục nội dung nghe nhìn do người dùng chọn trong phiên đã chỉ định.Lỗi FAILED_PRECONDITION sẽ được trả về nếu người dùng chưa chọn xong các mục nội dung nghe nhìn cho phiên đã chỉ định. Trong trường hợp này, ứng dụng của bạn sẽ thăm dò ý kiến sessions.get cho đến khi phiên sẵn sàng trước khi thử lại phương thức này. Yêu cầu HTTP GET https://photospicker.googleapis.com/v1/mediaItemsURL sử dụng cú pháp Chuyển mã gRPC. Tham số truy vấn Thông số sessionId string Bắt buộc. Giá trị nhận dạng do Google tạo cho phiên này. pageSize integer Không bắt buộc. Số lượng mục nội dung nghe nhìn tối đa cần trả về.Dịch vụ có thể trả về ít hơn giá trị này (bao gồm cả kết quả không có). Nếu không được chỉ định hoặc được đặt thành 0, hệ thống sẽ trả về tối đa 50 mục nội dung nghe nhìn. Giá trị tối đa là 100; các giá trị trên 100 sẽ được chuyển đổi thành 100. Giá trị âm sẽ dẫn đến lỗi INVALID_ARGUMENT. pageToken string Không bắt buộc. Mã thông báo trang, nhận được từ lệnh gọi mediaItems.list trước đó. Cung cấp giá trị này để truy xuất trang tiếp theo. Nội dung yêu cầu Nội dung yêu cầu phải trống. Nội dung phản hồi Phản hồi cho phương thức mediaItems.list. Nếu thành công, phần nội dung phản hồi sẽ chứa dữ liệu có cấu trúc sau: Biểu diễn dưới dạng JSON { \"mediaItems\": [ { object (PickedMediaItem) } ], \"nextPageToken\": string } Trường mediaItems[] object (PickedMediaItem) Trang chứa các mục nội dung nghe nhìn đã chọn. nextPageToken string Nếu được đặt, trường này có thể được dùng làm pageToken trong lệnh gọi mediaItems.list tiếp theo để truy xuất trang tiếp theo của các mục nội dung nghe nhìn đã chọn.Nếu bạn bỏ qua trường này, thì sẽ không có trang tiếp theo. Phạm vi uỷ quyền Yêu cầu phạm vi OAuth sau: https://www.googleapis.com/auth/photospicker.mediaitems.readonly","title":"Method: mediaItems.list\n\n    \n    \n      \n    \n\n    \n      \n      Sử dụng bộ sưu tập để sắp xếp ngăn nắp các trang\n    \n    \n      \n      Lưu và phân loại nội dung dựa trên lựa chọn ưu tiên của bạn.","url":"https://developers.google.com/photos/picker/reference/rest/v1/mediaItems/list?hl=vi","id":522},{"content_hash":"4acf16d1ac1a482208b496ffa5546684b93f4fdde55b166b009fe08cfe135d9e","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:12.363881+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/relevement-du-plafond-et-reduction-du-taux-de-cotisation-a-la-securite-sociale_2071884_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives RELÈVEMENT DU PLAFOND ET RÉDUCTION DU TAUX DE COTISATION A LA SÉCURITÉ SOCIALE ? Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"RELÈVEMENT DU PLAFOND ET RÉDUCTION DU TAUX DE COTISATION A LA SÉCURITÉ SOCIALE ?","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/relevement-du-plafond-et-reduction-du-taux-de-cotisation-a-la-securite-sociale_2071884_1819218.html","id":523},{"content_hash":"6122cbd4f61e03275dd2f3557e22c765f1ab66d578453c4bdca4b16640351356","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:12.312270+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/international-stage-a-respite-for-gillard-12984","language":"","text":"International stage a respite for Gillard Published: March 27, 2013 3:05pm EDT Michelle Grattan, University of Canberra Author Michelle Grattan Professorial Fellow, University of Canberra Partners University of Canberra provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.cyauq73gj https://theconversation.com/international-stage-a-respite-for-gillard-12984 https://theconversation.com/international-stage-a-respite-for-gillard-12984 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Gillard’s talks will try to give a push to negotiations on the Australia-China free trade agreement. AAP/Torsten Blackwood Julia Gillard will be among the first heads of government to meet the new leadership when she visits China in just over a week. After the government’s traumas, stepping back onto the international stage will be an attractive proposition for the Prime Minister before she has to face the rigours of battling with the states over education funding at the Council of Australian Governments meeting on April 19 and then the test of the May budget, which will be critical in Labor’s attempt to regroup. Gillard met the new Chinese president, Xi Jinping at a dinner at the Lodge, hosted by then PM Kevin Rudd in 2010, on the Sunday night before the coup that made her leader. She has also met the new premier, Li Keqiang on a couple of occasions, and they co-chaired a meeting of business CEOs in May 2011 when she was in Beijing. Leaving late next week, Gillard will first attend the annual Boao Forum for Asia, before going on to Beijing. The forum’s theme is “Asia Seeking Development for All”, and comes with addresses by leaders and extensive meetings between business executives. Her Beijing talks will try to give a push to negotiations on the proposed Australia-China free trade agreement – after 19 rounds with no great prospect of early progress - as well as canvass economic and regional issues. Climate change will also be discussed. Yesterday the Australia-China ministerial dialogue on climate change was held in Sydney; Minister Greg Combet said afterwards: “China is closely monitoring the implementation of Australia’s emissions trading scheme… In the future, we would like to work towards the development of an Asia-Pacific carbon market including major emerging economies like China and South Korea”. Combet added that later this year China would begin pilot emission trading schemes in a number of provinces and major cities and aimed to move to a national scheme after 2015. As well as her political talks, in Beijing Gillard will have a business round table, similar to the 2011 one. The delegation accompanying the PM is a who’s who of business names, including Gail Kelly, from Westpac, the ANZ’s Mike Smith, David Peever from Rio Tinto, and Tony Shepherd, president of the Business Council of Australia. For Gillard, the China visit feeds into the government’s narrative of Australia in the Asian Century, and the opportunities that closer integration provide for long-term, secure jobs for Australians. One initiative that could come out of the visit is some more formal architecture to guide how the relationship develops. While China is part of the great Asian opportunity, the relationship has some more difficult and delicate aspects, especially defence issues. The Chinese were upset by the 2009 Australian Defence white paper, which they they took as reflecting suspicion of China. More recently, Australia’s involvement in the America “pivot” towards Asia, with the rotation of US troops through the north, was not received well. Before he was recruited to join the Gillard government as Foreign Minister, Bob Carr expressed concern about how Australia’s involvement would go down with the Chinese. The government has imminent another Defence white paper – defence minister Stephen Smith said at the weekend it would be out by May-June. James Brown, from the Lowy Institute, says the Chinese will only be interested in two aspects of this paper. The first is “what they can read [in it] about the US intentions”, while the other is “what force structure and budget we come up with, and how that plays into the US network”. Hugh White, Professor of Strategic Studies at the Australian National University, sees some pulling back by the government from its language around the time of President Obama’s 2011 visit, when the rotation was announced. “Gillard has well and truly understood that the Chinese were very displeased with the language she used during the Obama visit and she is is trying to reposition herself,” White says. “All the evidence one sees is that 2013 white paper will be much more cautious in its references to China than the 2009 one”. Gillard last week blew Kevin Rudd’s leadership hopes out of the water. But he remains to haunt. The former prime minister arrived in China yesterday. He’s having talks with Chinese officials to support businesses in his electorate that want to get into the Chinese market, and he will address the National Defence University today. He is expected to break into Mandarin but perhaps avoid any humour about leadership transition. Previous post Published: March 26, 2013 Emerson anxious to consult, but too busy to dine Next post Published: April 1, 2013 The cost of connection","title":"International stage a respite for Gillard","url":"https://theconversation.com/international-stage-a-respite-for-gillard-12984","id":524},{"content_hash":"8b730606702a0a00652c706a583540f49f11b00b48a4d707c7758bcaebe5346b","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:12.300311+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/13/frsc-army-strengthen/","language":"","text":"FRSC, Army Strengthen Partnership on Training, Security By Prnigeria - September 13, 2026 Corps Provost of the FRSC, Headquarters Abuja, Corps Commander Livinus S. Audu and the Provost Marshal (NACMP), Major General Mathias Erebulum at the Headquarters of Nigerian Army Corps of Military Police FRSC, Army Strengthen Partnership on Training, Security The Federal Road Safety Corps (FRSC) has sought stronger collaboration with the Nigerian Army Corps of Military Police (NACMP) in training, security, discipline and conflict management as part of efforts to enhance national safety and security. The Corps Provost of the FRSC, Headquarters Abuja, Corps Commander Livinus S. Audu, made the call on Thursday, September 10, 2026, during a courtesy visit to the Headquarters of the Nigerian Army Corps of Military Police. Audu said the visit was aimed at strengthening the existing relationship between the two organisations and establishing a more strategic partnership to advance their complementary roles in promoting national safety and security. He said the FRSC Provost Office had benefited significantly from military training and professional engagements over the years, particularly in discipline, administration and investigations. According to him, the Corps was interested in expanding the relationship through additional training programmes, seminars and workshops with the Army Military Police to improve the professional competence and institutional capacity of its personnel. Read Also: JUST IN: Army Debunks Abduction of Islamic Scholars at Yari’s Residence in Zamfara 2027: Former Gombe Electoral Commissioner Endorses Pantami for Governor NWDC, Kano Task Force Move to Tackle Drug Abuse, Insecurity in North-West Responding, the Provost Marshal (Army), Major General Mathias Erebulu, welcomed the visit and stressed the importance of inter-agency cooperation, intelligence sharing and sustained professional interaction in addressing Nigeria’s complex security and safety challenges. Erebulu said no single service or organisation could effectively tackle the country’s multifaceted security challenges alone, making collaboration and the sharing of complementary capabilities imperative. He described discipline as the bedrock of every organisation and assured the FRSC of the readiness of the Nigerian Army Corps of Military Police to support relevant capacity-building initiatives, subject to necessary approvals. The Army Provost Marshal also noted that both organisations had complementary responsibilities and could benefit from each other’s expertise in areas including traffic management, security, discipline and emerging operational challenges. The engagement ended with both organisations reaffirming their commitment to deepening inter-agency cooperation, strengthening professional capacity and leveraging their respective capabilities in support of national safety and security. The visit was attended by the Chief of Staff, Headquarters NACMP, principal staff officers of the headquarters and officers of the FRSC. The highlight of the engagement was the presentation of a memento by the visiting FRSC delegation, followed by a group photograph. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGS(FRSC)NACMPPartnership on Training & Security Previous articleNigeria, Benin Customs Move to Modernise Seme-Krake Border for Seamless Trade Prnigeria","title":"FRSC, Army Strengthen Partnership on Training, Security","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/13/frsc-army-strengthen/","id":525},{"content_hash":"5c44fab179cb670aaf677afee2bdeeb90a8ef4ddb5eb263503eeaf38a408f82d","domain":"www.chinadaily.com.cn","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:12.206522+00:00","final_url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/china/special_coverage/6aa0f5c4e4b06d4aa055d221","language":"","text":"Explore Shanxi - Chinadaily.com.cn Search HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER MOBILE Global EditionASIA 中文双语Français HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER China Daily PDF China Daily E-paper MOBILE China News Society Innovation HK/Macao Cross-Strait Cover Story Photo Environment Health Military Video Home / China / Explore Shanxi Home China Explore Shanxi Intl vloggers explore Pingyao Ancient City in Shanxi 2026-09-20 16:36 Youyu's racing industry gallops ahead 2026-08-31 09:12 Archaeologists discover cemetery of high-ranking Xia Dynasty noble in Shanxi's Xiyang county 2026-08-28 09:36 Red Flag Canal: A shared legacy of resilience and cooperation 2026-08-24 09:06 Yanmen Pass, Xinzhou, Shanxi 2026-08-12 07:00 Exploring China's ancient architectural soul 2026-07-28 09:43 Ancient military sage leads ranks of tourists 2025-09-19 10:07 Shanxi's cultural heritage remains timeless 2024-11-25 08:50 Yuncheng Salt Lake offers up natural marvels 2024-10-02 12:11 Guan Gong Cultural Festival celebrates Chinese heroism 2024-09-24 19:00 Shanxi's wonders inspire art of hit game 2024-09-06 07:36 1 1/1 Latest Former senior Xinjiang official prosecuted for bribery Fishing lights create autumn spectacle on Yunnan's Erhai Lake Train carriages renovated to better serve farmers in Yunnan Missing workers found dead following explosion in Guangxi Hong Kong draws 1.6m visitor arrivals during National Day holiday Top court promotes mediation in business disputes Exclusive How China's 'backyard program' works for Africa Hands off! Do robots do it all by themselves? Middle school students teach humanoid robots to dance! Newsmaker English changed his life and then that of others A lifelong guardian of children's breath Special Coverage Loyalty and Honor without Borders 105th founding anniv. of the CPC From Qiushi Boosting High-Quality Belt and Road Cooperation Through Rigorous and Sustained Efforts China’s Hydropower: Empowering a Clean and Beautiful World Top BACK TO THE TOP English 中文 HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER China Daily PDF China Daily E-paper MOBILE Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form. Note: Browsers with 1024*768 or higher resolution are suggested for this site. License for publishing multimedia online 0108263 Registration Number: 130349 About China Daily Advertise on Site Contact Us Job Offer Expat Employment FOLLOW US Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form.","title":"Explore Shanxi - Chinadaily.com.cn","url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/china/special_coverage/6aa0f5c4e4b06d4aa055d221","id":526},{"content_hash":"a5f8fbd83807d3e7a321f8b97f12be2e2586e8ed7400946334a351b89e06754f","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:12.072571+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/protect-against-online-shopping-scams.html","language":"","text":"Help protect against online shopping scams Stay alert when engaging with websites, computer pop up ads and apps that sell merchandise, especially through social media. Transcript Learn how you can avoid even the most sophisticated online shopping scams and advertisements by: Determining if an offer is really too good to be true.Identifying fraudulent online sellersPaying for your online purchases more safely Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Switch to Accessibility Friendly View Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog","title":"Help protect against online shopping scams","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/protect-against-online-shopping-scams.html","id":527},{"content_hash":"498ff28580cb7267d4f56d109b6dee3c48efa81d7088224d1d89b73f6b018ea7","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:11.869854+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/article-it-turns-out-canadas-population-isnt-shrinking-after-all/","language":"","text":"Open this photo in gallery:Pedestrians crossing University Ave, while walking on Queen St. West in downtown Toronto.Fred Lum/The Globe and Mail CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountAfter several years of spectacular population growth in Canada, the story of 2026 has been the country experiencing the first outright population decline since Confederation. While Canada showed a small quarter-over-quarter population decline during the fall months of 2025, revised numbers now show the population has generally been growing – contrary to previous counts that pointed to consistent and sizeable decreases. As of July 1, Canada added roughly 190,000 people since the same date last year, a 0.5-per-cent increase that brought the country’s population to 41.8 million, according to Statistics Canada. At the same time, the agency revised Canada’s population count going back to 2021, with the biggest changes occurring since mid-2025. Under the agency’s previous count, Canada’s population shed 236,000 from that point to April 1 this year. With the update, Canada actually added 190,000 people, instead. “Rapid changes to immigration policies in recent years have not only made the future trajectory of the population much more volatile ... even recent history may be subject to change,” Daren King and Matthieu Arseneau, economists at National Bank of Canada, wrote in a note. The agency regularly revises its past population counts, but this revision was particularly large. The economists determined most of the adjustment in population came down to the estimated number of non-permanent residents. Statscan revised that number higher after changing how it counts non-permanent residents leaving the country, and those staying while permit extensions are processed.The government has set a goal of shrinking the non-permanent resident population to 5 per cent of the total population by the end of 2027. The revised count shows that share is declining more slowly than originally thought. That will make it difficult for the government to reach its target, the economists wrote. With demographic data in such flux, it’s all the more tricky for economists and policy-makers to gauge vital economic indicators around employment, housing and productivity. Even something as fundamental as forecasting economic growth becomes more difficult. Under the old population count, real per-capita gross domestic product in the second quarter leapt 1.6 per cent from the year before. With the revelation that Canada’s population growth was stronger than expected, per-capita growth shrank a full percentage point.Decoder is a weekly feature that unpacks an important economic chart.","title":"It turns out Canada’s population isn’t shrinking after all","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/article-it-turns-out-canadas-population-isnt-shrinking-after-all/","id":528},{"content_hash":"75e0d906ff86940940356b170d239104558e7e8243eae42e17efde5154ad71bd","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:11.177028+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/la-cour-d-appel-de-nancy-va-dire-si-le-docteur-ernst-bourgmestre-de-strasbourg-pendant-l-occupation-est-francais-ou-allemand_2072525_1819218.html","language":"","text":"LA COUR D'APPEL DE NANCY VA DIRE SI LE DOCTEUR ERNST BOURGMESTRE DE STRASBOURG PENDANT L'OCCUPATION EST FRANÇAIS OU ALLEMAND Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"LA COUR D'APPEL DE NANCY VA DIRE SI LE DOCTEUR ERNST BOURGMESTRE DE STRASBOURG PENDANT L'OCCUPATION EST FRANÇAIS OU ALLEMAND","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/la-cour-d-appel-de-nancy-va-dire-si-le-docteur-ernst-bourgmestre-de-strasbourg-pendant-l-occupation-est-francais-ou-allemand_2072525_1819218.html","id":529},{"content_hash":"f1cd1c7be6a7fbfe9d9fc5aa247aeff6faf0c43a1a4d2572899047a6a317b76c","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:11.115360+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/politics-of-the-penis-13389","language":"","text":"Politics of the penis Published: April 15, 2013 10:32pm EDT Rob Brooks, UNSW Author Rob Brooks Professor of Evolutionary Ecology; Director, Evolution & Ecology Research Centre, UNSW Partners UNSW provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.dm6jvmc6t https://theconversation.com/politics-of-the-penis-13389 https://theconversation.com/politics-of-the-penis-13389 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Brian Mautz has had a big week. Since his paper on the importance of penis size in attractiveness judgements was published in PNAS on Tuesday morning, news outlets and blogs have proved near-insatiable. The story mutated, and the mutants proliferated so wildly that many accounts bear only faint resemblance to the paper he published a few short days ago. When he hasn’t been fielding interview requests, he’s been wading through the mostly unsolicited mail, much of it hostile and a great deal of it Not Safe For Work (even the work of a guy who studies penis evolution and mate choice). I wrote about Brian’s paper for The Conversation. In the intervening week, Conversation readers have engaged in a mostly-sensible discussion about the paper and my analysis of it. There was, as expected, plenty of discussion about the paper’s limitations: ratings from a sample of women who represent only a small and rather non-random slice of humanity, the fact that the authors manipulated only three traits (height, torso shape and penis size), but there are so many more dimensions to attractiveness, the fact that the penises were flaccid, and that flaccid size is only a poor predictor of erect length and, especially, girth (see footnote). These limitations and the many others raised by thoughtful readers reflect the dilemma of doing research in this sphere. You cannot study every nuance of attractiveness at the same time and still have the statistical power necessary to gain interesting insights. But this frustrating reality often gets readers backs up when they read a news article that trumpets the importance of, say, penis size. There is a difference between saying “Penis size matters” and “Penis size is the only thing that matters”. I read the PNAS paper pretty closely, and it stuck faithfully to the former tack. But somewhere along the way, in all the breathless enthusiasm, a lot readers took the second message. Goldilocks While this neatly executed and necessarily constrained study of human penis size revealed something about human behaviour, the truest insights into human nature came from the responses to the paper. In my analysis piece I mentioned that public discussion about penis size seems reflexively to opt for what I called “the Goldilocks cop-out”: Most media stories on the topic of penis size conclude that as long as the penis in question isn’t way too big or way too small, it’s likely to be “just right”. And within the large zone of “just-rightness”, few commentators are willing to claim that size really matters. Consider how Nature, arguably the worlds most august scientific journal and news outlet, portrayed the story: Nature ran with the rather bizarre, and altogether wrong headline: “Bigger not always better for penis size”. Screengrab from nature.com. Whatever you think of the limitations of the PNAS study, it did not claim that “bigger is not always better”. In fact it showed that larger penis sizes are more attractive, although the incremental rise in attractiveness was not as fast near the top of the size distribution as it was near the bottom. So strong is the urge to mollify male insecurity or, perhaps, to dampen rampant phallocentrism that Nature saw fit to somehow imagine that this paper said something it most certainly did not. Goldilocks doesn’t get any more confused than that. Fig-leaves A lot of the reaction that Mautz and his collaborators elicited illustrates the conflict so many people feel about the subject. On one hand, stories about penis evolution exert a compelling, voyeuristic pull. And yet they also elicit a repulsion and an urge to protest. Perhaps, methinks, just a little too much. As Brian Mautz put it to me in an email: The amount of media [the paper] garnered speaks volumes about how interested people are even though some people claim it was a frivolous or pointless study. It also highlights how taboo talking about penises is to everyone. I doubt it would spawned over 1400 news article in 48 hours if it wasn’t so taboo. Image from the Facebook page of Discover Magazine, after the original image was taken down by Facebook because it was, apparently, offensive. Facebook So taboo, in fact, that Facebook took offense at the main image of the avatars used in the study. Both Discover magazine and Scientific American found themselves on the wrong end of the Facebook fun police who deemed the image offensive and took it down. I can see how the penis pics, even though they are on humanoid greyscale avatars rather than real people, could be confronting. And I’m grateful to Facebook for valiantly attempting to maintain some kind of standards in their shrinking corner of the internet. But Facebook have form when it comes to leaden-footed censorship decisions, particularly pictures of breastfeeding (see the delightfully subversive Breastfeeding Photos are NOT Obscene page). In rushing to censor the images accompanying a science story that is explicitly about penises, Facebook look a little lost. Brian Mautz tells me that: CNN also had it on their front page this weekend (in the ‘Just for Fun’ section sort of ironically). They too started off with the photo of figure 1 as the associated picture, but it had been replace by the end of Saturday with an innocuous picture of two meters stick. I suspect it is because readers complained. Intriguingly, The Conversation’s Facebook page hasn’t been censored yet. It has long been my ambition to be banned by some withering institution like Facebook or The Vatican. Perhaps one of you who has made it this deep into the article might want to complain? The image remains … intact … on the Conversation’s Facebook page. Facebook We biologists tend to underestimate how squeamish other people get about body parts and functions. I’m intrigued now to read about penis taboos and the psychology behind them. One criticism that emerged again and again in this discussion about the penis paper was that scientists should turn their attention to the study of breast size or vaginal dimensions. Those who would claimed that men are victims, here, of a double standard, might have done well to search the scientific literature. Or to look a popular culture. Breast size (and shape, and “bounciness”) has received far more than its share of attention. And often the reporting is far less sensitive to the delicate nature of the topic and the potential to offend. Casual conversations with colleagues give me the distinct impression that it is far easier to gain ethics committee approval to study women’s breasts than men’s penises. And that’s just the flaccid kind. And frontal nudity in the cinema seems to be far common when the nude in question is a woman. And yet squeamishness about nudity, and about penises in particular, is far from uniform. It follows fashions and cultural norms seem quite malleable. Sculptors and painters in some periods show a deep fascination with genital anatomy, whereas others show an equal fondness for the leaf of the fig tree. My friend and colleague Karl Vernes, who conducts field work in Bhutan, sent me several images of painted houses and businesses there, including the one below. Paintings adorning a restaurant in the town of Nobding, Bhutan. Associate Professor Karl Vernes Apparently the phallus paintings that adorn many Bhutanese houses and shops date back to the late 15th century to a buddhist teacher, Drukpa Kunley, variously known as ‘the divine madman’ or ‘The Saint of 5,000 Women’. He travelled the country simultaneously teaching Buddhism and conferring his blessing - in the form of sex - on women who sought it. He is considered a saint of fertility, and the images are nowadays considered fertility symbols. I wonder how Facebook would deal with that? Much ado about penis size I am impressed that a single scientific paper such as the one by Brian Mautz and his colleagues reached so many people. I am decidedly pleased that it made an impact and perhaps got people talking and thinking. And I hope it persuaded some people that science can be a lot of fun. That would be a lasting positive contribution that might outweigh the specifics of what this study said (and is incorrectly alleged to have said) about the importance of penis size. Footnote: The published literature shows that flaccid size is a pretty good predictor of ‘stretched length’, and stretched length is a good predictor of erect size. Perhaps this is part of the Goldilocks effect? (A famously profane evolutionary biologist is alleged to have said, about the idea that flaccid size doesn’t predict erect dimensions: “that sounds like something small-dicked guys would say”). Previous post Published: April 4, 2013 Just your initials, please! Subverting sexist biases in peer‑review Next post Published: April 22, 2013 The placenta and the pace of life","title":"Politics of the penis","url":"https://theconversation.com/politics-of-the-penis-13389","id":530},{"content_hash":"83ecd58561567e474aed176abc632d04dedafa203e88f9395a4dcad8065392b7","domain":"www.chinadaily.com.cn","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:10.985054+00:00","final_url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/china/special_coverage/6aa0f5c4e4b06d4aa055d21b","language":"","text":"Perspective - Chinadaily.com.cn Search HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER MOBILE Global EditionASIA 中文双语Français HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER China Daily PDF China Daily E-paper MOBILE China News Society Innovation HK/Macao Cross-Strait Cover Story Photo Environment Health Military Video Home / China / Perspective Home China Perspective From Guan Gong to the Long March: An American youth's view on 'righteousness' 2026-09-23 16:41 How a Shanxi village keeps Guan Gong's spirit alive 2026-09-22 17:12 Master of stone carves out new branches for a historic lineage 2026-09-22 14:57 Seeing ecological civilization come alive in Shanxi 2026-09-09 07:53 Shanxi short film festival spotlights emerging young talent 2026-08-31 09:35 Ancient drainage system ensures Zhangbi Castle weathers the storm 2026-08-18 10:23 China's tourism market sees steady growth and shifting trends 2026-08-14 15:25 Digital tours help display Shanxi's ancient wonders 2026-08-10 09:14 Where the past still feels present 2026-08-08 10:57 Tour group reveals the treasures of Shanxi 2026-08-07 09:39 Livestreamer brings life in Shanxi to Latin America 2026-07-31 09:28 Seeing Shanxi through global eyes 2026-07-28 09:28 1 1/1 Latest Former senior Xinjiang official prosecuted for bribery Fishing lights create autumn spectacle on Yunnan's Erhai Lake Train carriages renovated to better serve farmers in Yunnan Missing workers found dead following explosion in Guangxi Hong Kong draws 1.6m visitor arrivals during National Day holiday Top court promotes mediation in business disputes Exclusive How China's 'backyard program' works for Africa Hands off! Do robots do it all by themselves? Middle school students teach humanoid robots to dance! Newsmaker English changed his life and then that of others A lifelong guardian of children's breath Special Coverage Loyalty and Honor without Borders 105th founding anniv. of the CPC From Qiushi Boosting High-Quality Belt and Road Cooperation Through Rigorous and Sustained Efforts China’s Hydropower: Empowering a Clean and Beautiful World Top BACK TO THE TOP English 中文 HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER China Daily PDF China Daily E-paper MOBILE Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form. Note: Browsers with 1024*768 or higher resolution are suggested for this site. License for publishing multimedia online 0108263 Registration Number: 130349 About China Daily Advertise on Site Contact Us Job Offer Expat Employment FOLLOW US Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form.","title":"Perspective - Chinadaily.com.cn","url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/china/special_coverage/6aa0f5c4e4b06d4aa055d21b","id":531},{"content_hash":"0ebc6460f29dcd9c4a05a8736131aeb7a0a98b45e328e141e842ffbd19e8cad1","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:10.464125+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/anti-money-laundering-aml-regulations-in-the-uk","language":"","text":"Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Regulations in the UKByEmmanuel AgwuJune 23, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Are Anti-Money Laundering Regulations?Current Major Components of Anti-Money Laundering Measures In The United KingdomWays To Get Compliant With Anti-money Laundering Requirements In The UKWhat Businesses do AML regulations apply to In The UK?AML Sanctions In The United KingdomAchieving AML Regulations with YouverifyShare this postMoney laundering poses a significant threat to the integrity of the global financial system, undermining trust and facilitating criminal activities. In response, governments around the world have implemented stringent Anti-Money Laundering (AML) regulations to combat this illicit practice. This article focuses on AML regulations specifically in the United Kingdom, exploring their purpose, key components, and the obligations they impose on businesses operating within the country. What Are Anti-Money Laundering Regulations? Anti Money Laundering (AML) entails laws, policies, as well as regulations that prevent financial crimes and illegal activity. Global and local regulators worldwide stringently help prevent financial crimes; they also build policies that help to mitigate financial crimes, especially money laundering. To better learn more about anti-money laundering, read this article. Current Major Components of Anti-Money Laundering Measures In The United Kingdom 1. Legislated Laws In The UK The primary or main legislation that governs anti-money laundering and compliance are the; Money Laundering, Terrorist Financing and Transfer of Funds (Information on the Payer) Regulations 2017; included in these regulations is the EU's Fourth Money Laundering Directive (4MLD) and the Funds Transfer Regulation (FTR). These laws direct the obligations of businesses to prevent money laundering and terrorist financing. 2. Authorities That Enforce Anti-money Laundering Regulations The main regulatory bodies that are responsible for enforcing AML and compliance regulations in the UK include the following: Financial Conduct Authority(FCA)Gambling CommissionInformation Commissioner's Office Each of the regulatory bodies listed above has definite oversight over different sectors, such as banks, casinos and data protection. 3. Customer Due Diligence (CDD) UK firms are required to conduct thorough customer due diligence measures in order to verify the identity of their customers and understand their business activities. This entails performing identity checks, assessing the risk associated with customers and monitoring transactions for suspicious activities. 4. Legal Obligation To Report Suspicious Activity Firms in the United Kingdom are legally obligated to report any suspicious activities that may have an allusion to money laundering or terrorist financing to the National Crime Agency. This allows for a collaborative approach between businesses and law enforcement to easily detect and mitigate financial crimes. 5. Compliance Programs: Commercial entities in Uk, are obligated to establish robust AML and compliance programs that are tailored to their unique industry and risk profile. These programs include internal controls, adequate staff training as well as risk assessment and frequent independent audits to ensure adherence to AML regulations. 6. Penalties and Enforcement Non-compliance with anti-money laundering regulations can result in severe penalties, which include fines and criminal prosecution. Regulatory authorities that enforce compliance have the power to investigate businesses that fail to comply and take appropriate enforcement action. Ways To Get Compliant With Anti-money Laundering Requirements In The UK 1. Reporting Although this can be regarded as a basic measure, it can be effective, especially when done promptly. Business entities are obligated or mandated by the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 to report any kind of suspicious activity that they detect. A Suspicious Activity Report (SAR must be handed over to the National Crime Agency (NCA) by a nominated officer deemed appropriate as soon as the suspicion arises. It can also be done online on the go by accessing this online portal. 2. Refining Organisation Structure To Support Compliance Companies can ensure that organisational structures should be well utilised to combat financial crimes like money laundering. Structures may differ and be innovative. Companies can create a separate Anti-money laundering department or an audit department that is well brief on anti-money laundering regulations and can also detect irregularities in financial transactions. No matter the structure adopted, duties and roles should be clear and well-strategised to actually mitigate risks. Companies are required to appoint a nominated officer. Also, FCA-regulated businesses must select a Money Laundering Officer (MLRO). Both officers are expected to perform distinct but important duties. The MLRO is an employee appointed to oversee a company's compliance with AML regulations and to alert the company when they detect a suspicious activity that could be related to money laundering, while the nominated officer is nominated to receive and consider suspicious activity reports (SARs), they are also obligated to make external suspicious activity to the FCA. Asides from relying on official departments and officials with distinct duties related to AML regulations, companies are also required to monitor their employed staff and provide adequate AML training; employees who are exposed to a higher risk of money laundering should be subject to more stringent vetting processes. 3. Customer Due Diligence Checks Customer due diligence, with the popular CDD, refers to a process necessarily adopted by financial institutions and businesses in order to gather data or information about their clients or customers so that they can identify and mitigate risks related to illicit activities like; identity fraud, money laundering, etcetera. Customer Due Diligence in the UK includes record keeping, enhanced due diligence, continuous monitoring, identity verification, risk assessment, etcetera. 4. Anti-Money Laundering Screening Effective and up-to-date systems should be set up in order to complete regular AML screening; the systems should be appropriate to the nature, size and risk level of the companies. Legal and natural individuals should be checked against multiple watchlists like the UK government's financial sanctions list, trade sanctions list, European Commission's list of high-risk third countries and the Treasury’s list of high-risk countries as well as countries to which enhanced due diligence is required. 5. Ardent Record Keeping UK businesses are required to keep records of all CDD measures implemented, including copies of identification documents, transaction records, risk assessments, and any other pertinent documentation. These records should be retained for a specific amount of time (typically five years) and made available to regulatory authorities upon request. What Businesses do AML regulations apply to In The UK? Here is a summary of the businesses that are mandated to oblige AML regulations: 1. Money Service Businesses MSBs, such as money transmitters, providers of currency exchange, and payment processors, are governed by AML laws. They have to submit an application to the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) and abide by the MLR 2017 specifications. 2. Banks and Financial Institutions Both conventional banks and Internet payment service providers are subject to extensive AML requirements, as are banks, credit unions, building societies, and other financial institutions operating in the UK. 3. Casinos and other Gambling Facilities The UK Gambling Commission controls and monitors the adherence to AML requirements by casinos, betting shops, online gambling platforms, and other gambling facilities. For client due diligence, the reporting of suspicious transactions, and continuous monitoring, there are certain standards. 4. Providers of Trust and Company Services AML requirements apply to those who offer trust and company services, such as formation agents, trust administrators, and corporate service providers. They need to register with the FCA or HM Revenue and Customs (HMRC) and follow the MLR 2017 guidelines. 5. Estate Agents AML requirements apply to real estate agents and developers. When dealing in high-value transactions, such as the sale or purchase of real estate, they have a responsibility to exercise customer due diligence. 6. Accountants and Tax Advisers AML requirements apply to those who work in the accounting and tax advisory fields. When offering services pertaining to tax planning, financial advising, or auditing activities, they must adhere to AML obligations. 7. Lawyers and Legal Professionals AML requirements also apply to barristers, solicitors, and other legal professionals. Due diligence must be done, suspect transactions must be reported, and client confidentiality must be upheld. 8. Dealers in high-value products Such as those that deal in precious metals, fine art, or other high-value items, are subject to AML legislation. They have a responsibility to investigate consumers thoroughly and report any questionable transactions. AML Sanctions In The United Kingdom AML sanctions in the UK play an important role in combating and mitigating money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illegal financial activities. These sanctions are imposed by the government and regulatory authorities to restrict the transfer of funds, freeze assets, and deter individuals or entities engaged in illicit activities. The following AML sanctions are applicable in the UK. 1. United Nations Sanctions The UK is committed to implementing and enforcing United Nations (UN) sanctions. These sanctions are imposed by the UN Security Council and are binding on all member states. They aim to address threats to international peace and security, including terrorism, weapons proliferation, and human rights violations. 2. European Union Sanctions Prior to the UK's withdrawal from the European Union, it implemented and enforced EU sanctions. These sanctions targeted individuals, entities, and countries involved in activities such as terrorism, arms proliferation, and human rights abuses. After Brexit, the UK has adopted its own autonomous sanctions framework. 3. UK Autonomous Sanctions The UK now has the power to impose its own autonomous sanctions independent of the EU framework. This allows the UK to design targeted measures against individuals, entities, or countries involved in activities that pose a threat to national security or international stability. 4. Office of Financial Sanctions Implementation (OFSI) The OFSI, which operates under the UK Treasury, is responsible for implementing and enforcing financial sanctions in the UK. They maintain and update the Consolidated List of individuals, entities, and countries subject to financial sanctions. It is the responsibility of businesses and individuals to check this list and comply with any sanctions in place. 5. Sanctions List Checking UK businesses are required to conduct sanctions list checks as part of their customer due diligence processes. This involves screening customers, clients, and other transaction counterparts against the relevant sanctions lists to ensure they are not involved in sanctioned activities. 6. Penalties and Enforcement Non-compliance with AML sanctions can result in significant penalties, including fines and criminal prosecution. The OFSI has the authority to investigate and take enforcement action against businesses and individuals that breach sanctions regulations. Achieving AML Regulations with Youverify It is important to implement anti-money laundering measures across all kinds of businesses. However, financial entities or institutions will always be held to the highest scrutiny. This is why financial institutions need to have departments dedicated to track and fraud and money laundering; all employees should also be educated on the best AML practices. For small, medium and large-scale businesses, it is important that reliable KYC and AML automation tools are leveraged to avoid sanctions and avoidable losses. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for KYC and AML screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today.Related ArticlesAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Priscilla, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 28, 2023What is a Sanctions List?Read MoreAnti-Money Laundering (AML)Emmanuel Agwu•February 14, 2024RegTech's Influence On Regulatory Policy and ReformRead More","title":"Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Regulations in the UK","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/anti-money-laundering-aml-regulations-in-the-uk","id":532},{"content_hash":"d8808be0f70032fa5c1d80a724b6ad15daecf70fbc828f819779b077128e3c7e","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:10.147033+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-padilla-nervo-president-de-la-6e-session_2073053_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives M. PADILLA NERVO PRÉSIDENT DE LA 6e SESSION Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"M. PADILLA NERVO PRÉSIDENT DE LA 6e SESSION","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-padilla-nervo-president-de-la-6e-session_2073053_1819218.html","id":533},{"content_hash":"27f706f8437cc4cc7a7ea488722a5901fef9eb784427cbd9bfb382b091760f0d","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:09.914535+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/world-homoeopathy-awareness-week-has-passed-13567","language":"","text":"World Homoeopathy Awareness Week Has Passed Published: April 17, 2013 1:38am EDT Ian Musgrave, University of Adelaide Author Ian Musgrave Senior lecturer in Pharmacology, University of Adelaide Partners Adelaide University provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.s6ga6wg7w https://theconversation.com/world-homoeopathy-awareness-week-has-passed-13567 https://theconversation.com/world-homoeopathy-awareness-week-has-passed-13567 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Illustrating Homoeopathic dilutions using coffee. Ian Musgrave World Homoeopathy Awareness Week has just passed (10 April to 16 April), but I would still like to take this opportunity to make people aware that Homoeopathy is expensive placebo that has been failing clinical trials since 1835. I’ve written about homoeopathy before*, showing that homoeopathic treatments, which either have no trace of active ingredient or levels so low as to be biologically meaningless, have no good evidence of therapeutic benefit. This video on homoeopathy covers a lot of similar ground to my articles. You may be amused at the sight of me taking an overdose of homoeopathic sleeping pills (around 3:52 into the video). That sequence had a great missed opportunity moment. When Frank Pangallo asks me if I feel sleepy, I should have replied “if this was real medicine, we would be in hospital now”. There are a number of other discussions relevant to World Homoeopathy week. This article at Science Based Pharmacy about the implications of homoeopathic products for Pharmacists is thought provoking reading. The Guardian’s report that even homoeopaths disavow homoeopathic vaccination should give you pause as well. There are several relevant videos aside from the one linked above. Here’s Richard Dawkins investigating homoeopathy. James Randi’s TED talk on homoeopathy, and this video from CBC marketplace. For more general infomation on homoeopathy start at the 10:23 campaign for a good one-stop basic information source. For further detail, over at Science Based Medicine, there is a good, easily readable series on homeopathy. Then there is the weighty House of Commons Science and Technology Committee (STC) report, Evidence Check 2: Homeopathy, which concludes that there is no evidence that homeopathy works any better than placebos (commentary here). A more general article is the Ars Technica report on homoeopathy. If you are wondering why people would use homoeopathy when it doesn’t work, this article might help you understand. For some light relief, a homoeopath explains how physics works, then there’s an amusing poster from the Centre for Inquiry Canada and a song from Australia’s Tim Minchin (warning: rude words). *My Conversation article ‘Doctors’ orders: debunking homeopathy once and for all’, was included in The Best Australian Science Writing 2012 anthology. Previous post Published: April 11, 2013 Brainy Sports Supplements or Voodoo Pseudoscience Next post Published: May 8, 2013 Annular Solar Eclipse, May 10, 2013","title":"World Homoeopathy Awareness Week Has Passed","url":"https://theconversation.com/world-homoeopathy-awareness-week-has-passed-13567","id":534},{"content_hash":"5757ad9428dca25f71463766dffd5dd602208a14f8cdaaea993379fa26a7bb76","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:09.853917+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/validators/","language":"","text":"Validators¶ These validators are available from the django.contrib.postgres.validators module. KeysValidator¶ class KeysValidator(keys, strict=False, messages=None)[source]¶ Validates that the given keys are contained in the value. If strict is True, then it also checks that there are no other keys present. The messages passed should be a dict containing the keys missing_keys and/or extra_keys. Note Note that this checks only for the existence of a given key, not that the value of a key is non-empty. Range validators¶ RangeMaxValueValidator¶ class RangeMaxValueValidator(limit_value, message=None)[source]¶ Validates that the upper bound of the range is not greater than limit_value. RangeMinValueValidator¶ class RangeMinValueValidator(limit_value, message=None)[source]¶ Validates that the lower bound of the range is not less than the limit_value.","title":"Validators¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/validators/","id":535},{"content_hash":"e25cc09dd1293a18db7dbaa1736492ee30b3fe16522da5a9348f23e996e6fbf5","domain":"www.chinadaily.com.cn","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:09.755254+00:00","final_url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/china/special_coverage/6aa0f5c4e4b06d4aa055d20f","language":"","text":"Latest - Chinadaily.com.cn Search HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER MOBILE Global EditionASIA 中文双语Français HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER China Daily PDF China Daily E-paper MOBILE China News Society Innovation HK/Macao Cross-Strait Cover Story Photo Environment Health Military Video Home / China / Latest Home China Latest Guan Gong's legacy continues to inspire today's youth 2026-09-28 19:25 Voices reflect on Guan Gong's legacy 2026-09-24 17:13 International Youth Dialogue on Loyalty and Honor Without Borders in review 2026-09-24 14:59 Global campaign sheds new light on timeless Guan Gong culture 2026-09-23 16:47 From Guan Gong to the Long March: An American youth's view on 'righteousness' 2026-09-23 16:41 Bringing Guan Gong's spirit to life for a wider audience 2026-09-23 16:00 Millennium wooden miracle: Sakyamuni Pagoda of Fogong Temple in Shanxi 2026-09-23 14:54 37th Guan Gong Cultural Tourism Festival kicks off in Yuncheng city 2026-09-23 14:31 Cloisonné: Ancient craft, new life in Yuncheng 2026-09-23 13:41 Shanxi cave temple conservation forum reviews protection efforts among climate change 2026-09-22 18:53 Who is Guan Gong? 2026-09-22 17:44 How a Shanxi village keeps Guan Gong's spirit alive 2026-09-22 17:12 Foreign vloggers experience Jin Opera in Shanxi 2026-09-22 13:12 How Guan Gong comes alive on the Chinese opera stage 2026-09-22 08:00 Intl delegation explores loyalty and righteousness 2026-09-21 17:52 Intl vloggers explore Pingyao Ancient City in Shanxi 2026-09-20 16:36 Intl photography festival spotlights 20,000 works in Shanxi 2026-09-19 14:42 Global online campaign on Guan Gong culture to launch in Shanxi 2026-09-18 17:58 Shanxi upgrading industrial landscape 2026-09-11 09:46 Shanxi show explores millennia of painted pottery art 2026-08-11 08:30 1 2 Next >>| 1/2 Next Latest Former senior Xinjiang official prosecuted for bribery Fishing lights create autumn spectacle on Yunnan's Erhai Lake Train carriages renovated to better serve farmers in Yunnan Missing workers found dead following explosion in Guangxi Hong Kong draws 1.6m visitor arrivals during National Day holiday Top court promotes mediation in business disputes Exclusive How China's 'backyard program' works for Africa Hands off! Do robots do it all by themselves? Middle school students teach humanoid robots to dance! Newsmaker English changed his life and then that of others A lifelong guardian of children's breath Special Coverage Loyalty and Honor without Borders 105th founding anniv. of the CPC From Qiushi Boosting High-Quality Belt and Road Cooperation Through Rigorous and Sustained Efforts China’s Hydropower: Empowering a Clean and Beautiful World Top BACK TO THE TOP English 中文 HOME CHINA WORLD BUSINESS CULTURE TRAVEL VIDEO SPORTS OPINION REGIONAL NEWSPAPER China Daily PDF China Daily E-paper MOBILE Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form. Note: Browsers with 1024*768 or higher resolution are suggested for this site. License for publishing multimedia online 0108263 Registration Number: 130349 About China Daily Advertise on Site Contact Us Job Offer Expat Employment FOLLOW US Copyright 1994 - . All rights reserved. The content (including but not limited to text, photo, multimedia information, etc) published in this site belongs to China Daily Information Co (CDIC). Without written authorization from CDIC, such content shall not be republished or used in any form.","title":"Latest - Chinadaily.com.cn","url":"https://www.chinadaily.com.cn/china/special_coverage/6aa0f5c4e4b06d4aa055d20f","id":536},{"content_hash":"f35efa5995f90ae935bc72bd23ca6100551b9d9c130d3fc16aea7fb319425345","domain":"www.theguardian.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:09.628273+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/09/criminal-charges-letitia-james-new-york-attorney-general","language":"","text":"Letitia James criminally charged in Trumpâs latest effort to punish rivalsThis article is more than 11 months oldProsecutor who sought charges was installed amid Trump frustrations with pace of investigations against rivalsSam Levine and Edward Helmore in New YorkFri 10 Oct 2025 13.51 EDTFirst published on Thu 9 Oct 2025 16.07 EDTSharePrefer the Guardian on GoogleA federal grand jury has indicted Letitia James, the New York attorney general, for bank fraud and making false statements. Two charges were brought against James, who had brought a civil fraud case against the Trump Organization in 2022 that Trump claimed was a malicious prosecution.Lindsey Halligan, the US attorney for the eastern district of Virginia, personally presented the case to the grand jury on Thursday, the person said. US attorneys do not typically present to a grand jury.âThis is nothing more than a continuation of the presidentâs desperate weaponization of our justice system. He is forcing federal law enforcement agencies to do his bidding, all because I did my job as the New York state attorney general,â James said in a recorded video statement on Thursday.âThese charges are baseless, and the presidentâs own public statements make clear that his only goal is political retribution at any cost. The presidentâs actions are a grave violation of our constitutional order and have drawn sharp criticism from members of both parties.âHalligan was installed in the role last month after Donald Trump became frustrated with the pace of investigations against his rivals. âNo one is above the law,â Halligan said in a statement. âThe charges as alleged in this case represent intentional, criminal acts and tremendous breaches of the publicâs trust. The facts and the law in this case are clear, and we will continue following them to ensure that justice is served.âAccording to a copy of the federal indictment, James is charged with one count of bank fraud and one count of making false statements to a financial institution. Jamesâs attorney, Abbe Lowell, said his client âflatly and forcefully denies these chargesâ.James is set to appear in a Virginia court on 24 October. Her attorneyswill probably argue that the charges arise from a vindictive and selective prosecution and there is no âmaterial factâ to the charges and should be dismissed.Democratic party officials were quick to defend James.âNew Yorkers know @NewYorkStateAG James for her integrity, her independence, and her relentless fight for justice,â New Yorkâs governor, Kathy Hochul, posted on X. âWhat weâre seeing today is nothing less than the weaponization of the Justice Department to punish those who hold the powerful accountable.âThe Democratic mayoral candidate for New York City, Zohran Mamdani, said in a statement: âNo one should be surprised that Donald Trump is employing fascist tactics â prosecuting his opponents, weaponizing the federal government, and attacking the very fabric of our democracy. And Trump should not be surprised when millions of Americans stand up to his authoritarianism and his greed.âNew York Cityâs comptroller, Brad Lander, condemned the indictment, pointing to the mortgage fraud case James brought against the Trump organization in 2022 that resulted in a $464m judgment that was later overturned as a excessive.âTish James knew the risk that she was taking when she brought that prosecution, and I, like many other people, said something to her at the time,â Lander said. âAnd when she says âwithout fear or favorâ, you know she means it.âNew York Democrats rally behind Letitia James day after indictment for mortgage fraudRead moreThe US president has made little secret of his desire to use the justice department to punish his rivals. âWhat about Comey, Adam âShiftyâ Schiff, Leticia [sic]??? Theyâre all guilty as hell, but nothing is going to be done,â he said in a September post on Truth Social that was addressed to Pam Bondi, the US attorney general. âWe canât delay any longer, itâs killing our reputation and credibility.âThe move is Trumpâs latest effort to weaponize the Department of Justice to punish political rivals. It comes two weeks after Halligan secured charges against James Comey, the former FBI director. Career prosecutors prepared a memo in that case outlining why charges were not warranted. Comey pleaded not guilty on Wednesday.A grand jury empaneled earlier this year had been investigating allegations that James may have committed fraud when she helped her niece buy a home in Virginia.James received a $137,000 mortgage on the home in 2020. The indictment alleges that James got a more favorable mortgage rate of 3% because she signed a âSecond Home Riderâ, which required her to use the property as a secondary residence.But prosecutors say she rented the property out to others, which meant that she should have paid a mortgage rate of 3.815%. According to the indictment, she saved a total of $18,933.It was not known that prosecutors were scrutinizing Jamesâ 2020 home purchase. Prosecutors were believed to be investigating a 2023 home she purchase in Norfolk she assisted her niece with. On a document that was part of that transaction, there was a box checked indicating James intended to use it as her primary residence, which would make better mortgage rates available. But in other documents and emails with her mortgage broker, James clearly indicated she did not intend to use the home as her primary residence.A career prosecutor in the eastern district of Virginia, Elizabeth Yusi, had determined there was not probable cause to file mortgage fraud charges against James and had been preparing to present her thinking to Halligan.James attracted Trumpâs ire after she led a civil fraud case against the president and his business that resulted in a $500m fine. The fine was recently overturned by an appellate court.There has been considerable turmoil in the US attorneys office for the eastern district of Virginia in recent weeks. Erik Siebert, Halliganâs predecessor, was removed from his post for failing to bring charges against Comey and James. Maya Song, one of his top deputies, was also fired. A third prosecutor, Michael BenâAry, who was not involved in the Comey case, was also fired after being targeted by Trump allies online.William Pulte, the head of the Federal Housing Finance Agency and a staunch Trump ally, has made criminal referrals on mortgage issues against several Trump rivals, including James, the California senator Schiff, and the Federal Reserve governor Lisa Cook.Explore more on these topicsLetitia JamesVirginiaLaw (US)Trump administrationDonald TrumpNew YorkUS politicsnewsShareReuse this content","title":"Letitia James criminally charged in Trumpâs latest effort to punish rivals","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/oct/09/criminal-charges-letitia-james-new-york-attorney-general","id":537},{"content_hash":"46e75fcca2f4e590b3c5d5b1edda54912ed3535ae3c0a41d0d5978d24c20f2fa","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:09.368758+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-09-27/earthquake-recorded-in-east-gippsland/107200738","language":"","text":"Magnitude-3.9 earthquake recorded near Ensay in East GippslandBy Bec SymonsABC GippslandTopic:EarthquakesPosted Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 2:11pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 2:11pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 2:11pm, updated Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 2:25pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 2:25pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 2:25pmThe magnitude-3.9 earthquake was felt in Swifts Creek, in Victoria's High Country. (ABC News: Madeleine Stuchbery)In short:A magnitude-3.9 earthquake was recorded at Ensay, in eastern Victoria, on Saturday night.Residents say the earthquake shook their homes.What's next?An expert says that aftershocks may be felt in the coming weeks.abc.net.au/news/earthquake-recorded-in-east-gippsland/107200738Link copiedShareShare articleA magnitude-3.9 earthquake in Victoria's High Country is the most powerful recorded in the area in more than a decade.The earthquake's epicentre was recorded near Ensay, about 350 kilometres east of Melbourne, about 9:30pm on Saturday.Geosciences Australia has received 346 reports from people who felt the earthquake.Residents in the neighbouring town of Swifts Creek said the earthquake shook their houses.\"I actually thought, holy hell, that was a bloody a gumtree or something that landed right behind the house,\" Swifts Creek resident Stephen Richardson said.\"And then I thought, no, there was nothing near my house that could do that.Ensay in far eastern Victoria was the epicentre of the earthquake. (Supplied: State Emergency Service)\"It was a big thump beside the house and then I ran to the back door and heard it rumble up the valley.\"He said the rumble sounded like a \"massive bit of machinery was driving up the highway towards Omeo\".But he said his house was not damaged.Swifts Creek resident Ray Gallagher said the earthquake \"felt different to anything else I'd ever felt before\". \"Our house is on a concrete floor, but it rattled the joint and you could hear the glass going,\" Mr Gallagher said.\"One thing we did think, because it was stormy, [was that] maybe lightning had hit a tree nearby.\"'A decent shake'Geosciences Australia senior seismologist Jonathan Griffin said the organisation had recorded 27 earthquakes within 100 kilometres of Ensay in the past decade.But he said Saturday's earthquake was the most powerful.\"We wouldn't say it's particularly big, but it's not tiny either,\" Dr Griffin said.\"If you happen to be close to it, it would have given you a decent shake.\"Jonathan Griffin says 27 earthquakes have been recorded in Gippsland in the past decade. (Supplied: Geoscience Australia)He said the earthquake occurred as a result of Australia's position at the centre of a tectonic plate which was moving about 7 centimetres a year.\"Because of that movement, it's getting squeezed and compressed from the forces at the plate boundaries,\" Mr Griffin said.The stresses from that movement occasionally cause earthquakes in south-eastern Australia.He said aftershocks were possible in the coming days and weeks.\"We can't predict earthquakes, so we never know and you can never rule out the possibility of getting a larger one as well,\" Dr Griffin said.ABC Gippsland — local news in your inboxGet our local newsletter, delivered free each TuesdayEmail addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleABC EmergencySearch any location in Australia to find nearby active incidentsFind an incidentPlan for an emergencyPrepare for a TsunamiPlan for an EarthquakeTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerPopular nowHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicTopic:HomicideMcLaren driver clocked allegedly doing 259km/h on WA highway Topic:Traffic OffencesRelated topicsBairnsdaleEarthquakesEnsaySwifts CreekTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Magnitude-3.9 earthquake recorded near Ensay in East Gippsland","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-09-27/earthquake-recorded-in-east-gippsland/107200738","id":538},{"content_hash":"e9b43717963c5341b9c9e10f301af494b1fdac06fbacf6a24e759e7b46b910b4","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:09.338161+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/astrology/horoscope/pisces-horoscope-today-october-09-2026-family-joy-fills-the-day-as-love-and-togetherness-take-centre-stage/articleshow/134793675.cms","language":"","text":"Pisces Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: Family joy fills the day as love and togetherness take centre stage - The Times of IndiaEditionININUSGCCEnglishEnglishहिन्दीमराठीಕನ್ನಡதமிழ்বাংলাമലയാളംతెలుగుગુજરાતીWeatherSign InTOIAstrologyHoroscopeSun Sign PredictionsCompatibility CalculatorYearly Horoscope 2026Chinese HoroscopeNumerologyNumerology PredictionsPlanets TransitsGemstones Vastu & feng shuikundali dasha & RemediesReligionMonthly HoroscopeOthersToday's ePaperLiveNewsAstrology NewsHoroscope NewsPisces Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: Family joy fills the day as love and togetherness take centre stageTrendingHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayPisces Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: Family joy fills the day as love and togetherness take centre stageAstroDevam, Dr Achary Kalki Krishnan & Anita Baranwal / TOI Astrology / Oct 09, 2026, 06:00 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint General:The joy of the family is in the day like the soft breeze of open windows. You can have fun with family and friends, and for many Pisces, enjoy a picnic, an outing, a walk in the park, a visit to a temple, or a long meal that you spend laughing with your family. The Moon calls for teamwork and unity, and Jupiter provides for comfort and ease of emotions. Don't go into your shell, it's a day to share space.The overall atmosphere is pleasant, and individuals are more inclined to accept your tenderness. So if you are busily scheduled in the morning, by afternoon the heart has its own space. Keep plans flexible. Unplanned conversations, small gifts and how people gather around food may all be the best moments of the day.Love and Relationship:It's a beautiful day for love. The lovers are able to state their feelings with greater confidence and the words get out less jumbled normally. This is a day to be honest and warm if you have been holding something back.Natives can accept marriage proposals via family and friends. Sitting right in the family rhythm of the day.If you're married, giving a small gift to your spouse or loved one strengthens the bond. It need not be expensive. A favorite sweet, flowers on the way home or something helpful they said last week will do the trick beautifully.Education and Career:While work may not be the biggest sound in the evening, it can still be done well if it is done with a good degree of organisation. The mind is inclined towards family and emotions, so in the office, keep things orderly and time limited. Complete important e-mails in time, if possible, and do not take the job home.Students can improve their performance in areas requiring imagination, writing and/or recall by association. If you have plans for studying, set up a brief timetable and adhere to it before venturing out with family. A duty is sweeter when it's done.Money and Finance:This isn't an awful money day at all. You can spend money on outing expenses, snacks, gifts, gas or anything else to make the family happy, but it should be a pleasure and not regret. Venus urges you to be generous but Jupiter reminds you, be sensible.If a marriage proposal comes through known people, then possibly the nuptials' monetary discussion can start in a relaxed manner. Avoid making spur-of-the-moment promises. Take the time to savor the experience, and then talk to trusted family members later about the details.Health and Well-being:Do not take care of your health if it concerns anything which is connected with the interior discomfort or with the urination system. Even if you are out for long hours, drink plenty of water; the body will tell you when it needs water, listen to it, even if the mood is cheerful.That's where trouble begins.If traveling with family, do not eat food that's very spicy on the side of the road. Take it easy when you come home, rather than going on with another social round. You are feeling good today and your body still needs tender loving care.Tip for the Day: Carry water and choose a thoughtful gift over an expensive one.Get the latest astrology news and horoscope updates. Download the TOI App.Read Also: Pisces Daily Horoscope | Pisces Weekly Horoscope | Pisces Monthly HoroscopeAbout the AuthorAstroDevamAstroDevam is a premium organisation providing ancient and authentic knowledge of Astrology, Vastu, Numerology, and Innovative Corporate Solutions with a contemporary perspective. AstroDevam, having patrons in more than 100 countries, has been promoted by Achary Anita Baranwal and Achary Kalki Krishnan, who not only have Master's Degrees in Astrology, but are engaged in teaching Scientific Astrology, Vastu, and Numerology for more than three decades.Read MoreEnd of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaHot PicksCBSE Date SheetGeorgia Medical Degree RowAparna JhaStock market crashH-1B ProgramRBI Repo Rate HikeNana PatekarWeather tomorrowUS Marines in JapanAmazon PrimeTop TrendingDonna KelcePatrick Mahomes MotherSam BealAsim MunirUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestSimone BilesCJP Chappal ProtestAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTr