​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​ : Elizabeth Guest and Timm Sharp Starrer A Very Merry Breakup Official Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​‘Not Playing This Game’: Marco Rubio Unloads On Reporter Over Kimberly Guilfoyle’s $100,000 BribeRoyal GP Murder Shock: Husband’s ‘Burglar’ Account Emerges After Hannah Cain Found DeadMusk's Team Trump Return? POTUS Resets Ties After Public Epstein Feud, Calls Him 'Modern Edison'Zohran Mamdani Grilled Over Oct. 7 Post; Reporter Asks: ‘Will You Apologize To Netanyahu?’‘Canada-EU Deal Done’: Mark Carney Slams Trump's Tariff Move In New PresserChrista HELPED Executioners? Pike Lawyers' SHOCKER Adds BIG Twist To Failed Execution MysteryRiyadh Airspace SHUT! Airlines Cancel Hundreds Of Flights After Houthis BLOW UP Saudia Airbus JetPresley Gerber’s Drug Battle Blamed For Tragic Final Chapter As Years-Long Addiction Struggle EmergeTrump-Vance Clash? POTUS Honors Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella As VP Targets Tech Giant Over H-1B VisasMen Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​ : Jessie Buckley and Rory Kinnear Starrer Men Official Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​JD Vance Exposes H-1B Scam!: 6,000 Americans Duped; Microsoft, Wipro & TCS On Visa HitlistLatest News“Take the family down there and make a donation”: How Travis Kelce’s mother Donna Kelce and Tammy Reid helped bring Christmas cheer to families in needIndia joins US-led group on structural excess capacityVaibhav Sooryavanshi's explosive net session raises big question: Will he play in Ranchi?Trumpled talentMDR on UPI may now take effect in January 2027'Baththa' box office collection day 8 vs 'Dorothy': Vijay Sethupathi's film earns Rs 15.42 cr India net; Karthik Subbaraj's thriller reaches Rs 11.97 crFaceless system in place for central GST payersWhat students wantBhagyank vs Namank: Which Number Matters More When Your Name and Birth Date Disagree?Pisces Shani Horoscope Today, 9 October 2026: Saturn Says State Your Terms Early; Uttara Phalguni Moon Brings Partners ForwardTom Brady’s strategic investment pays off as Las Vegas Raiders reach a $13 billion valuation in the middle of the seasonVishal Parekh on 'Mitrata': 'I didn't want to do the same Vishal that people already see on Instagram' - EXCLUSIVESupreme Court: For post-split suicide, can't always accuse ex of abetmentIIT Bombay fire: How gas leak triggered hostel flare-up, 5 injured, including 2 minorsPerfect storm: Sensex slips amid rising oil, bond yields'Tell your story': Pat Cummins' blunt response to Usman Khawaja's book on SandpapergateChris Hemsworth and Elsa Pataky helped return Tasmanian devils to mainland Australia after 3,000 years; the endangered marsupials could help restore ecosystems'Workers with AI skills in demand, but GenAI is hitting white-collar hiring'Copyright © 2026 Bennett, Coleman & Co. Ltd. All rights reserved. For reprint rights: Times Syndication ServiceFollow Us On","title":"Lucky Number and Colour Today, 9 October 2026: Moon Enters Libra as Mercury Challenges Saturn, Balance, Patience and Timing Become Important","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/astrology/horoscope/lucky-number-and-colour-today-9-october-2026-moon-enters-libra-as-mercury-challenges-saturn-balance-patience-and-timing-become-important/articleshow/134785337.cms","id":574},{"content_hash":"64f2a44be8268f8cb898845da7e889f9e81dc0df114cd9d4ba1a58c23a920ee3","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:02.790812+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-felix-tchicaya-est-reelu-au-moyen-congo_2073010_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives M. FÉLIX TCHICAYA EST RÉÉLU AU MOYEN-CONGO Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"M. FÉLIX TCHICAYA EST RÉÉLU AU MOYEN-CONGO","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-felix-tchicaya-est-reelu-au-moyen-congo_2073010_1819218.html","id":575},{"content_hash":"1ed580e7d925a926a06b27b85fc988016172fc8ac976c4c0a043a0b9a93cde4d","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:02.708008+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/sports-journos-declare-the-election-17942","language":"","text":"Sports journos declare the election Published: September 5, 2013 11:50pm EDT Sinclair Davidson, RMIT University Author Sinclair Davidson Professor of Institutional Economics, RMIT University Partners RMIT University provides funding as a strategic partner of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.hmhea9r36 https://theconversation.com/sports-journos-declare-the-election-17942 https://theconversation.com/sports-journos-declare-the-election-17942 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Last night on the AFL 360 show Gerard Whateley and Mark Robinson summed up the election: “It’s been a crap election campaign”. I think that’s right - it has been very difficult to maintain interest in the campaign. The media have given it a red-hot go - after all they have to cover the election but still attract eye-balls. The team at Sky News have done a magnificent job in covering the election. That a small under-resourced organisation could maintain coverage of such a high standard for so long is a credit to them. For the rest of the media election coverage has fallen off somewhat. The election does seem to have fallen off the front pages and moved ever further into the papers. I don’t blame them - one side of politics has not been competitive. In time we might look back and wonder what “that” was all about? By “that” I mean the return of Kevin Rudd. Yesterday the Financial Review was reporting that he might have saved a net two seats compared to expectation that the ALP would only win 49 seats under Julia Gillard. This last week much of the coverage has been the composition of the Senate and whether Tony Abbott will be able to implement his policies. The battle for government was clearly over. In the wash up the media will be blamed, of course, for not giving the (former) government good coverage, or being biased, or whatever. But the media have no obligation to run political campaigns on behalf of political parties. The media can only work with what they get given - the government seemed to be under-prepared for the election. To be fair Mr Rudd returned to the job with only weeks to spare, but nonetheless the government must have been planning for the election for months. Previous post Published: September 5, 2013 Fixing the fulcrum on the freedom of speech seesaw: the government’s work is cut out Joseph Fernandez Next post Published: September 6, 2013 Costings row torpedoes the media Marcus O'Donnell","title":"Sports journos declare the election","url":"https://theconversation.com/sports-journos-declare-the-election-17942","id":576},{"content_hash":"88b362dfab70f212a30a546c6bf6e90faf169c9941601dc6f5bb374d45c15ca8","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:02.495425+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/drive/reviews/article-kias-ev4-will-be-canadas-least-expensive-ev-but-doesnt-feel-like-it/","language":"","text":"Open this photo in gallery:The EV4 looks like a cross between a sedan and a hatchback.Mark Richardson/The Globe and Mail CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountOf all the many numbers and statistics attached to the Kia EV4, there’s only one that really matters: 38,995. That’s the price, in Canadian dollars, of the Korean maker’s new all-electric sedan, and it makes it the least expensive new production EV in Canada. Finally, a car more people can actually afford. That’s the Manufacturer’s Suggested Retail Price (MSRP) for the most basic edition, which will have just over $2,000 added to it for freight and pre-delivery inspection and various fees, and then taxes on top of that. It means you should be able to drive one out of the showroom door next spring for about $45,000 all in – though your insurance premium will add to it, of course.This makes the EV4 about $1,000 cheaper than the least-costly new Chevrolet Bolt and about $6,000 cheaper than the least-costly new Nissan Leaf. The prices aren’t directly comparable, of course, because each car offers different features, but the basic EV4 “Light” is well equipped for its price. It has a smaller, 58.3-kilowatt-hour battery that offers a maximum claimed range of just 391 kilometres, but the next trim level, the $42,995 “Wind”, has an 81.4-kilowatt-hour battery and a heat pump, with a range of 552 kilometres. Open this photo in gallery:The cabin feels upscale for the price and there are few physical buttons and switches.Mark Richardson/The Globe and MailYes, it’s still expensive compared to a gas-powered vehicle. The Kia K4 sedan, which is an almost identical size, starts at $23,995, which is a full $15,000 cheaper than its all-electric sibling. But that’s not the point. The EV4’s price stands out because it’s finally dropping to affordable levels. It’s well below the average price of a new car in Canada, which AutoTrader currently lists at just over $63,000.Some provinces still have small EV rebate programs, though they’re soon to end, and the federal government says it will reinstate its own rebate program for EVs, scrapped a year ago, but has not given any details nor an expected date. It’s best not to include such money in any purchase decision. What you can consider, however, is that the greater initial capital cost of an electric vehicle is often repaid over its lifetime in savings of fuel consumption and maintenance. The actual amounts and timeframe to break even will depend on your usage and lifestyle. LooksThe EV4 is not ugly, but it does look like a mish-mash between sedan and hatchback. There’s a traditional trunk that’s integrated into a sloping rear window that extends from the roof with a distinctive split roof spoiler. Kia calls the design “futuristic,” and says it creates a slippery drag co-efficient of 0.23. Open this photo in gallery:There’s a traditional trunk that’s integrated into a sloping rear window that extends from the roof with a distinctive split roof spoiler.Mark Richardson/The Globe and MailInteriorThe cabin feels surprisingly upscale for the price, with plenty of recycled materials in the plastic and seats and carpets that don’t feel cheap and create a quiet and comfortable ride. It’s spacious, like most EVs with flat floors, and there are few physical buttons and switches. Most everything is organized through the large touchscreen or by voice commands. You’ll either love that or hate it.Open this photo in gallery:The rear seats on the EV4, which almost fold flat in a 60-40 split.Mark Richardson/The Globe and MailPerformancePower is unremarkable for an electric vehicle – there’s no neck-snapping acceleration from the single motor, but most buyers wouldn’t expect that and it’s not slow. An all-wheel-drive version is expected later in the year that will undoubtedly be quicker and more costly. More impressive is the smooth ride on exceptional suspension that uses multi-link geometry at the rear and McPherson struts at the front. The driving range depends on the trim level. The cheapest edition, with the smaller battery and no heat pump, has a claimed range of 391 kilometres, while the larger battery varies from 488 kilometres in the most expensive editions (which are heavier and have 19-inch wheels) to a respectable 552 kilometres in the more-affordable Wind edition. TechnologyThere’s a full range of driver’s assistance features sold with the least expensive editions, including smart cruise control, lane-keeping assistance and rear-blind-spot monitoring and assistance. You have to go to the most expensive trim to get the really clever stuff, such as remote smart parking and enhanced collision avoidance. It all works well in the background, where it should be.The EV4 uses 400-volt technology, unlike the more costly 800-volt architecture of the outgoing EV6. Its bigger battery can recharge from 10-to-80 per cent in about half an hour at a (still rare) 350-kilowatt charger, or about 80 minutes at a 50-kilowatt charger. The charging port is the North American Charging Standard (NACS), developed by Tesla and allowing charges at most Tesla stations.CargoCargo space is adequate, with 408 litres in the trunk (just five litres less than the gas-powered K4) and no frunk under the hood. The rear seats fold almost flat in a 60-40 split, which makes the trunk considerably more practical for bulky luggage.Open this photo in gallery:Cargo space is adequate with 408 litres in the trunk.Mark Richardson/The Globe and MailThe verdictFive years ago, the Kia EV4 would have been considered miraculous for an electric vehicle, with its extensive standard features and a range of more than 500 kilometres. These days, it’s more average as other automakers have caught up, but now it’s the price that makes it exceptional. Most buyers will probably opt for the long-range Wind edition for an extra $4,000, and for that price – below $50,000 after all taxes and fees are included – they’ll get good value indeed. Tech specs2026 Kia EV4Base price / as tested: $38,995 / $51,995, plus freight and pre-delivery inspection, plus fees and taxMotor / battery: Single motor on 400-volt architecture / 58.3 kilowatt-hour or 81.4 kilowatt-hourHorsepower / torque (lb-ft): 201 / 209 Drive: Front-wheel drivePower consumption (NRCan ratings)/Charging capacity: n/a (16.9 kWh observed) / 250 kW Range (claimed and observed): 391 – 552 kilometres Alternatives: Chevrolet Bolt, Nissan Leaf, Tesla Model 3The writer was a guest of the automaker. Content was not subject to approval.Shopping for a new car? Check out the new Globe Drive Build and Price Tool to see the latest discounts, rebates and rates on new cars, trucks and SUVs. Click here to get your price.","title":"Kia’s EV4 will be Canada’s least expensive EV, but doesn’t feel like it","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/drive/reviews/article-kias-ev4-will-be-canadas-least-expensive-ev-but-doesnt-feel-like-it/","id":577},{"content_hash":"0b01cd9349285b90729b684c9483fdfd0f51e66f6bd6770ebe23c4ecfbfc9f16","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:02.317774+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2013-09-13/hundreds-risk-jail-by-importing-illegal-euthanasia-drug/4956922","language":"","text":"Hundreds risk jail to import illegal drug promoted by euthanasia campaignersBy Sashka KoloffLatelineTopic:EuthanasiaPosted Fri 13 Sep 2013 at 11:02pmFri 13 Sep 2013 at 11:02pmFri 13 Sep 2013 at 11:02pm, updated Sat 14 Sep 2013 at 2:46pmSat 14 Sep 2013 at 2:46pmSat 14 Sep 2013 at 2:46pmPowdered barbiturate nembutal is being imported illegally into Australia. (ABC)abc.net.au/news/hundreds-risk-jail-by-importing-illegal-euthanasia-drug/4956922Link copiedShareShare articleA lethal drug promoted by euthanasia campaigners as a peaceful way to die is easily being illegally imported into Australia.Known as the \"peaceful pill\", barbiturate Nembutal is promoted by the euthanasia movement as the best and most peaceful way to end your life.Most have bought the illicit drug online from China, a country that in the last two years has come to dominate the market.The drug kills within an hour of being consumed.\"There are very few lethal drugs and the best lethal drug is Nembutal,\" euthanasia advocate Dr Philip Nitschke told a recent workshop.\"I've been with a lot of people who have died using this drug, it just seemed to get into to a deeper and deeper sleep and then finally stop breathing.\"And for those watching they see this as the most peaceful of deaths, it certainly looks to be.\"Be careful, because it's illegal.\"The penalty for importing or possessing Nembutal is a prison sentence or a fine of up to $825,000.Dr Nitschke says hundreds of Australians have illegally brought the drug into the country over the past couple of years.The Australian Federal Police have told the ABC's Lateline program that they have seized almost 15 kilograms of the drug since 2007.But so far, no one has been convicted.Nembutal easily ordered by terminally ill onlineMexico used to dominate the market but now most Nembutal is bought over the internet from China.Increasingly, there are reports of internet scams and Lateline has spoken to several people who have lost hundreds of dollars trying to buy Nembutal online.Dr Philip Nitschke tests imported nembutal for quality assurance. (Supplied)Arriving in flat packed envelopes, Chinese Nembutal comes in the form of a white powder, and Dr Nitschke says people are worried about its quality.\"People obtain this white powder in envelopes from China and they're concerned in a way that I never saw, the same concern with the South American product,\" he said.\"They worry that maybe there's someone selling them something that is contaminated or not pure or maybe cheating them completely and selling them castor sugar or something.\"To combat concerns, Dr Nitschke has set up a mobile testing laboratory, and he says there is a long queue of people across Australia waiting to use it.\"I think we can say the vast majority, all except one, have shown to be exactly what they've purported to be, so in other words high quality Nembutal,\" he said.Dr Nitschke has tested the Nembutal which terminal cancer patient David illegally imported earlier this year.David says the drug was easy to obtain online and he was not fearful of any legal consequences.I sent an email requesting a sample, I received a reply asking me to forward $US360 and I duly arranged that, and two weeks later, my package arrived through the mail.\"At 81 years of age, I don't think that crossed my mind. If they want to jail me, well I think that would be a very stupid action,\" he said.\"I sent an email requesting a sample, I received a reply asking me to forward $US360 and I duly arranged that, and two weeks later, my package arrived through the mail.\"David says knowing he now has a choice is a comfort.\"Euthanasia goes against what most of us believe in, but you've got to say 'OK, there are other people there, who have a problem, so why not give them the right to decide',\" he said.\"Sorry, I want out of here.\"Nembutal link to a Sydney woman's suicideDavid bought his Nembutal from a supplier listed in Dr Nitschke's book The Peaceful Pill Handbook. The book is also banned in Australia, but for $80 you can buy online access to the publication.Dr Nitchske's group also produces instructional videos for those considering euthanasia.In one tragic case, a young Sydney woman who was not terminally ill but suffering from depression accessed this information.She also attempted to buy Nembutal online, but lost her money.In May, the 30-year-old committed suicide.Her family told Lateline: \"The internet is a tool and has many good uses, but when vulnerable people can so easily get hold of information and videos that can do a great deal of harm, it can be devastating.\"However, Dr Nitschke defended this advocacy, saying the dissemination of euthanasia material information should not be banned simply because it is used for purposes other than those he intended.\"It's an age-old question about censorship,\" he said.\"I can't guarantee where the information is going to, but I don't think that's a good reason for stopping giving out information.\"But the chairman of the Federal Government's advisory council on suicide prevention, Professor Ian Webster, disagrees.There's a very fundamental principle in medicine, primum non nocere, a Latin statement for 'above all do no harm', so no action that a physician or a surgeon or a doctor should take, should increase the likelihood of harm.\"I don't think that's an acceptable risk,\" he said.\"There's a very fundamental principle in medicine, primum non nocere, a Latin statement for 'above all do no harm', so no action that a physician or a surgeon or a doctor should take, should increase the likelihood of harm.\"It's a very difficult balance to strike.\"Overwhelmingly I put my weight on preventing suicide and against promoting suicide methods to elements of the population which are at high risk.\"Dr Nitschke is currently facing two investigations by the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency over claims he is not a fit and proper person to hold medical registration, but he says losing his licence will not stop his work.\"In a practical sense it would make very little difference, because of the growing demand if you like, of a growing or increasing number of elderly people who are demanding control over the end of their own life,\" he said.If you or someone you know requires help you can contact Lifeline on 13 11 14, visit Beyond Blue or young people can visit Headspace.Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleIf you need someone to talk to, call:Lifeline on 13 11 14Kids Helpline on 1800 551 800MensLine Australia on 1300 789 978Suicide Call Back Service on 1300 659 467Beyond Blue on 1300 22 46 36Headspace on 1800 650 890QLife on 1800 184 527Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerRelated storiesAustralians importing illegal euthanasia drugNitschke detained by immigration in LondonTopic:EuthanasiaRelated topicsAustraliaCommunity and SocietyCrimeDeath and DyingDrug OffencesDrugsEuthanasiaHealthLaw, Crime and JusticeNSWSuicideSydneyTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Hundreds risk jail to import illegal drug promoted by euthanasia campaigners","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2013-09-13/hundreds-risk-jail-by-importing-illegal-euthanasia-drug/4956922","id":578},{"content_hash":"30ffa7a037a6172fc12ee118d05f08ed78f595e4d30947b7a80a4c500d20fd70","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:01.680655+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/drive/reviews/article-kias-new-ev5-compact-suv-will-face-tough-rivals-but-could-be-priced/","language":"","text":"Open this photo in gallery:The EV5 in front of the Korean War Monument to the Canadian Armed Forces.Mark Richardson/The Globe and Mail CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountIt’s tough to make an electric vehicle that’s not just a cookie-cutter copy of everything else. The technology is relatively similar for all EVs: large, heavy battery under the floor for a low centre of gravity; large, high-resolution screens with a multitude of options; and a smooth, quiet ride. The most distinctive feature is often the headlights.The challenge is even greater when the vehicle jostles for attention in the popular compact and sub-compact SUV market, as Kia’s new EV5 is about to do. It has to be really good at everything if it’s to be successful, as well as have a couple of tricks up its sleeve. Fortunately, the EV5 doesn’t put a wheel wrong, as the all-electric equivalent of the company’s top-selling gas-powered Sportage SUV. It has a reasonable range, reasonable power and will come with a choice of motors and drivetrains. The first model we’ll see in Canada, early next year, will be the “long-range” two-wheel-drive version, which is what I drove recently in South Korea. It will have a 160-kilowatt motor powering the front wheels, and an 81.4-kilowatt-hour battery that should provide around 500 kilometres of driving on a single charge. That ought to be good for charging from 10 per cent to 80 per cent in 30 minutes at a 150-kilowatt fast charger.Open this photo in gallery:The first model we’ll see in Canada, early next year, will be the “long-range” two-wheel-drive version.Mark Richardson/The Globe and MailThere’ll also be a more powerful, all-wheel-drive version that will come on sale next spring, and then a shorter-range, two-wheel-drive version sometime later in the year. They’ll be sold in GT-Line trims as well, for a sportier look. There may even be an even more powerful GT edition in the summer, but Kia officials here were quiet about that.Almost everyone else in the world will get these same vehicles a little sooner than us because the EV5 will not be sold in the United States. Those same Kia officials would not comment on this, but the major reason is clearly because of the uncertainty of U.S. tariffs, since the EV5 is assembled in South Korea. Canada has a free-trade agreement withSouth Korea and our imports are not affected, but the vehicles must still be homologated to Canadian safety standards, which takes extra time.Kia would also not confirm Canadian pricing, though prices are known for the rest of the world, where a basic EV5 costs about 25 per cent more than a basic Sportage. If you take that formula to Canada, the EV5 could cost as little as $40,000 before fees and taxes, though that’s optimistic thinking. It’s more likely to be $45,000, rising to almost $60,000 for the bigger battery, additional features and extra motor on the rear wheels. (Update: Kia Canada has now revealed its pricing for the EV5, with online reservations beginning Dec. 4. The most basic front-wheel-drive “Light” edition is $43,495, plus all fees and taxes, rising through nine editions to the all-wheel-drive GT-Line Limited, with a price of $61,495.)Open this photo in gallery:The controls are a satisfying mix of both physical switches and digital buttons.Mark Richardson/The Globe and MailLooksThe EV5 is unashamedly boxy, which is not a bad thing these days – it allows for a more practical use of its less-restricted inside space. Thin LED lights at front and back are effective and also help you pick out the car in a crowded parking lot. A tapered rear side window adds some much-needed dynamic appeal.InteriorThere’s loads of space inside for a vehicle that borders between compact and sub-compact. Seats are comfortable for four people – five in a pinch. The panoramic roof on my tester made the cabin feel larger than it actually is and the controls are a satisfying mix of both physical switches and digital buttons. They’re quite instinctive to use, which is just as well considering the sheer number of feature choices for the driver and passengers. Open this photo in gallery:The seats are comfortable for four people – five in a pinch.Mark Richardson/The Globe and MailPerformance It’s not quick, but it’s no slouch either. Official zero-to-100 kilometres-an-hour time is 8.4 seconds for the single-motor car I drove, shortening by just over a second with the twin-motor all-drive version. The rumoured GT will probably shave another second off that, too.The performance of the EV5 is probably best measured in how it manages its battery and energy consumption, and it’s very smart. My average power consumption during a 200-kilometre, warm-weather drive with plenty of traffic came in at 16.9 kilowatt-hours per 100 kilometres, which is really quite frugal for a car that still weighs more than two tonnes.TechnologyThe EV5 includes all the driver’s safety assistance you can think of as well as adjustable regeneration from paddle shifters and one-pedal driving, like other Kias. It has a crisp sound system, too. However, my tester also included several features we will not be getting in Canada: smart acceleration, where the throttle was limited in its performance in lower-speed zones (this can be turned off, but left active for when your kids drive the car), a truly helpful AI voice assistant, lounge seats in the front and augmented reality navigation, similar to the system already offered by Mercedes-Benz.One welcome feature we will get in Canada, which was first offered by Tesla, is Pet Mode. This allows you to set the climate inside the car to a comfortable temperature for your pet, and then displays a message on the central screen that announces it’s active and you’ll return soon. If not for such a message, bystanders would have no way to know the electric car was keeping your pet comfortable. It’s simple and it’s smart. Open this photo in gallery:The EV5 comes with something Tesla offers - Pet Mode, which allows you to set the climate inside the car and then displays a message on the central screen.Mark Richardson/The Globe and MailCargoThere’s plenty of clever space, with storage cubbies for all passengers and seats that fold almost completely flat. Leave the seats in place and there’s 965 litres behind the second row, and fold them down to create 2,080 litres. The frunk underneath the hood has an extra 44 litres for power cables and the like. Open this photo in gallery:The frunk underneath the hood has an extra 44 litres for power cables and the like.Mark Richardson/The Globe and MailThe verdictThe EV5 is a very capable, if unremarkable, all-electric compact SUV. If it comes on sale in Canada next year for less than $50,000, it will be very good value for money and may attract more drivers to the gas-free ownership experience.Tech specs2026 Kia EV5Base price: $45,000 (estimated), plus freight, pre-delivery inspection, fees and taxMotor / battery: 2WD: 160 kW / 60.3 kWh or 81.4 kWh; AWD: 125 kW + 70 kW / 81.4 kWhHorsepower / torque (lb-ft): 2WD: 214 / 229 AWD: 308 / 354 Drive: Two-wheel drive or all-wheel drivePower consumption (NRCan ratings) / charging capacity: 16.9 km/kWh (observed) / 150 kilowattsRange (claimed and observed): 530 km estimated (WLTP), 513 km observedCurb weight: 2,092 kilogramsAlternatives: Chevrolet Blazer EV, Honda Prologue, Nissan Ariya, Volkswagen ID.4, Ford Mustang Mach-E Open this photo in gallery:Leave the seats in place and there’s 965 litres behind the second row. Fold them down to create 2,080 litres.Mark Richardson/The Globe and MailThis article was updated on Dec. 4, 2025, to add Kia’s official pricing for the vehicle.The writer was a guest of the automaker. Content was not subject to approval.Shopping for a new car? Check out the new Globe Drive Build and Price Tool to see the latest discounts, rebates and rates on new cars, trucks and SUVs. Click here to get your price.","title":"Kia’s new EV5 compact SUV will face tough rivals, but could be priced well to compete","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/drive/reviews/article-kias-new-ev5-compact-suv-will-face-tough-rivals-but-could-be-priced/","id":579},{"content_hash":"7f813707b620a39743abb9bf21e6019289454e992261317ecb7c97002fdfe15a","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:01.587097+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/les-trois-ministres-occidentaux-des-affaires-etrangeres-rencontreraient-m-adenauer_2071854_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives Les trois ministres occidentaux des affaires étrangères rencontreraient M. Adenauer Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"Les trois ministres occidentaux des affaires étrangères rencontreraient M. Adenauer","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/les-trois-ministres-occidentaux-des-affaires-etrangeres-rencontreraient-m-adenauer_2071854_1819218.html","id":580},{"content_hash":"ff76c7db171a90d0d84e9d4e6161fb50b8ca5d508d8fd2bb617fa37fea2598a0","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:01.500262+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/news-corps-carbon-reporting-puts-the-heat-on-politics-itself-18235","language":"","text":"News Corp’s carbon reporting puts the heat on politics itself Published: September 15, 2013 9:36pm EDT David Holmes, Monash University Author David Holmes Senior Lecturer, Communications and Media Studies, Monash University Partners Monash University provides funding as a founding partner of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.n4njtwyfq https://theconversation.com/news-corps-carbon-reporting-puts-the-heat-on-politics-itself-18235 https://theconversation.com/news-corps-carbon-reporting-puts-the-heat-on-politics-itself-18235 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article ustaxi Possibly the most poignant irony of the federal election was to see the outskirts of Sydney burn while the votes were still being counted. While no records had been broken in the election itself, we saw yet another record for extreme weather events: the hottest start to spring in Sydney since records began. And with these fires, this irony quickly turned to farce, as the News Corp press launched a new kind of offensive on carbon reduction policies by singling them out as the sole cause of Labor’s loss. On the front page of the Weekend Australian, the services of former ACTU secretary Bill Kelty were enlisted to endorse the thesis that the carbon tax was responsible for Labor’s punishment on polling day. Does this argument make sense? Yes, if you look at the influence of climate change denialism in the Murdoch press. No, however, if you examine the carbon politics leading up to the election and the surveys measuring the importance of carbon reduction to Australian voters. Carbon politics Kevin Rudd’s first step in taking back the Labor leadership was to neutralise something called a “carbon tax” as an issue. Voters had long forgotten that the so-called tax was introduced as an emissions trading scheme in November 2011. It was Abbott and the press that later labelled it as a tax, setting up the mantra to “get rid of the tax”. But because of the effectiveness of the mantra, Rudd’s focus groups told him to bring forward the abolition of the fixed price on carbon trading himself, which he did. If anything, it is more likely that Labor’s constant backflips on carbon policy translated into the protest vote that went to the Greens, not the Coalition. Abbott could see this by the final week and, after having said barely anything about carbon policies except for the slogan, suddenly declared that the election was “all about carbon”. And this final message of Abbott’s campaign has now been echoed by the News Corp press, both in Australia and overseas. There is now a manufactured but concerted campaign to make carbon policy and global warming an issue that is guaranteed to lose an election for any party that dares take it on. Last Monday in Murdoch’s Wall Street Journal, the editorial was entitled Aussie Restoration: The perils of a carbon tax and other lessons from Down Under. It made a pitch not seen before in News Corp papers: an editorial-led push to banish climate change reform from electoral politics altogether. US politicians were advised: One lesson is to beware the faddish politics of climate change’ which supposedly proved to be a turn-off for voters who ‘will figure out that the politicians are merely looking for one more way to tap into their incomes. Whilst we have seen the Murdoch press aggressively oppose climate change reform and consensus science systematically for years now - in parallel with extremely wealthy private think tanks and lobby groups - we have not witnessed this kind of attempt to scare politicians off dealing with climate change. The US website Media Matters even reports the case of a US senator who has avoided climate change legislation for fear of facing the wrath of Fox News’ ongoing campaign against climate change action. Importance of carbon policy to voters But actually, climate change is important to mainstream voters, and Abbott himself knows this. He personally may not believe it, but he does believe that a majority of Australians are concerned about it. Activists often quote Abbott’s famous Beaufort speech where he declared that the argument for human-forced climate change is\"‘absolute crap\" but fail to quote the next two sentences: However, the politics of this are tough for us. Eighty per cent of people believe climate change is a real and present danger. Hence, Abbott will retain some kind of minimalist policy toward the issue, as long as the surveys are showing voter concern about it. As of August 27, the ABC’s successful Vote Compass survey of 900,000 participants found that 61% of Australians believe the government should do more to tackle global warming. And in case you think that this just reflects an ABC audience that is assumed to mirror a Left political class, Vote Compass also handily breaks down the doing-more-about-climate-change question into Green Voters (82% support), Labor (71%) and Coalition (24%). A CSIRO survey on the importance of climate change to individuals conducted in late 2011 showed that 65% of Australians believed climate change was either “somewhat”, “very” or “extremely” important. The 22% that believed it was not that important strongly correlated with a view that climate change was happening but that it was “natural”. But you wouldn’t know this from reading News Corp papers. News has been consistent in peddling climate change denial for almost five years now. The difference now is that there is an attempt to influence the political process directly by labelling it an issue for political losers. To the extent that this is true, Australia and the US (the two countries that did not sign up to Kyoto from the beginning) really are heading down the road of climate policy suicide, which will one day be held up to ridicule by the rest of the world, where it hasn’t been already. Of course, as many commentators point out, carbon tax, ETS, CPRS, (take your pick) are not as important as the carbon footprint created by Australian coal exports. And they are right. But at an ideological level of struggle, carbon reduction policies connect symbolically with crucial questions of public awareness, ongoing initiatives in the future, civic responsibility, and policy around renewable energy. That carbon reduction could possibly become a taboo topic, right at the moment when the earth’s energy imbalance is more visibly translating into some of the most extreme weather we have ever seen in Australia is as inconceivable as it is farcical. Next post Published: September 19, 2013 Fossil‑fuelling the climate wars","title":"News Corp’s carbon reporting puts the heat on politics itself","url":"https://theconversation.com/news-corps-carbon-reporting-puts-the-heat-on-politics-itself-18235","id":581},{"content_hash":"76f6d873b3bb04fccc27f405dfae84831545090dc253a7695d801ea51f22aae1","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:00.767014+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/matawalle-urges-african/","language":"","text":"Matawalle Urges African Defence Chiefs to Unite Against Terrorism, Emerging Security Threats By Prnigeria - September 20, 2026 Minister of State for Defence, Bello Muhammed Matawalle, and some Defence Chiefs in Burundi Matawalle Urges African Defence Chiefs to Unite Against Terrorism, Emerging Security Threats The Minister of State for Defence, Bello Muhammed Matawalle, has called for enhanced continental cooperation, intelligence sharing and interoperability among African armed forces to tackle terrorism, transnational organised crime and emerging security threats. Matawalle made the call while delivering Nigeria’s address at the 18th Ordinary Session of the Specialised Technical Committee on Defence, Safety and Security (STCDSS) and the 21st Ordinary Meeting of African Chiefs of Defence Staff and Heads of Safety and Security in Bujumbura, Burundi. Addressing defence ministers, the African Union Commissioner for Political Affairs, Peace and Security, military chiefs and members of the diplomatic corps, the minister said the STCDSS had become increasingly important amid the complex and interconnected security challenges confronting the continent. He identified terrorism, violent extremism, transnational organised crime, human and arms trafficking, piracy, cyber threats, unconstitutional changes of government and communal conflicts as threats that required collective action. “These threats are not confined by national borders. Consequently, no single country can effectively address them in isolation,” Matawalle stated. He urged stronger collaboration among the African Union, Regional Economic Communities and member states, stressing the need for improved intelligence sharing, joint and multinational operations, and sustained political commitment to the African Peace and Security Architecture (APSA) and the African Standby Force (ASF). According to him, the successes recorded through joint operations demonstrate that coordinated action and intelligence sharing can significantly degrade terrorist and criminal networks. Read Also: Zakari Mohammed: Kwara Must Rise to Full Potential as ADC Candidate Demands Public Debate WSCIJ Hails PRNigeria for Sustaining Young Communication Fellowship Beyond Grant Funding The People Who Made My PRNigeria Fellowship Memorable By Adegbite Anuoluwapo Rafat Matawalle also highlighted the evolving security risks associated with technological advancements, including the deployment of drones, artificial intelligence and cyber capabilities for recruitment, radicalisation, financial crimes and other illicit activities. “Africa must invest in technological innovation, research and development, intelligence capabilities and human capital, while ensuring that technology is employed responsibly and in accordance with international law,” he said. The minister, however, emphasised that military interventions alone could not resolve the continent’s security challenges without addressing their underlying socioeconomic and governance-related causes. He identified poverty, unemployment, weak institutions, inadequate access to education and social services, climate pressures and limited economic opportunities as factors that must be tackled alongside military and security operations. “Our approach to security must therefore be comprehensive. Military and security responses must be complemented by effective governance, youth empowerment, education, economic development, strengthened justice systems and social inclusion,” he added. Reaffirming Nigeria’s commitment to the principle of African solutions to African problems, Matawalle said the country would continue contributing to regional and continental peace support operations, counter-terrorism initiatives and capacity-building programmes. He commended the leadership of the African Union and the STCDSS for their contributions to peace, security and stability across the continent, while wishing delegates fruitful deliberations. The meeting brought together defence ministers, chiefs of defence staff and heads of safety and security from AU member states to deliberate on practical and sustainable responses to Africa’s evolving security challenges. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSAfrican Defence ChiefsBello Muhammed MatawalleMinister DefenceTerrorism Previous articleZakari Mohammed: Kwara Must Rise to Full Potential as ADC Candidate Demands Public Debate Prnigeria","title":"Matawalle Urges African Defence Chiefs to Unite Against Terrorism, Emerging Security Threats","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/matawalle-urges-african/","id":582},{"content_hash":"e2c974823950d0afbb141e3fcb9a8deeea701fbbd415da719d75a364e7ef54be","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:00.535396+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/the-request-for-proposal.html","language":"","text":"The Request for Proposal (RFP) Finding the right investment service provider for your organization For nonprofit organizations that have long-term investment pools, there are few decisions more important than securing an investment service provider. The RFP: Both document and process An RFP is a document that facilitates the formalized bid-seeking process that a nonprofit organization conducts when searching for an investment service provider. The RFP document requests information on the candidate firms’ services, levels of expertise, performance, fees and other important factors. For new nonprofits embarking on an initial search for an investment service provider, and established organizations whose practice it is to periodically evaluate their current provider by seeking comparative proposals, the RFP process requires a high degree of planning and coordination to help ensure that the organization obtains the services it needs to pursue its financial goals and mission. RFP goals and objectives The primary goal of the RFP process is to solicit complete and comparable proposals from qualified investment service providers and to evaluate those proposals in a fair and objective manner. Why do an RFP? Why dedicate time and effort to the RFP process? A successful RFP procedure should: Document a fair and thoughtful set of steps that provide an objective rationale for the selection of the investment service providerExemplify the high standards that board members and donors expect for the prudent stewardship of the organization’s assetsServe as a guide to the organization in finding the right provider to support its goals andProvide a structured opportunity for reflection on the services that the organization needs and the type of relationship that will serve it best Before you begin Careful planning can help to ensure a higher probability of success, both in the RFP process itself and in finding a provider that fits your needs. Before issuing the RFP: √ Set your goals: There are many ways in which your portfolio can be managed. They range from a traditional structure, in which your board or investment committee — perhaps guided by an external consultant — selects and dismisses individual managers, to a comprehensively outsourced structure where a single firm is hired by your board or committee to serve as an extension of your staff, with full discretion over portfolio implementation decisions. Before embarking upon the RFP process, you should determine where on this spectrum your organization wants to position itself. This can be accomplished by self-assessment, external research and, if necessary, via an external consultant. The efficiency and effectiveness of your RFP process will be greatly enhanced if you’ve decided in advance on the structure you wish to employ and can use the relevant metrics of that structure to assess the merits of potential candidates. Conversely, using the RFP process as a means of making this decision can easily lead to delays, confusion and a result that’s less than optimal for your organization. The following questions are relevant to this goal-setting process: How important is a broad range of investment expertise?What’s the importance of portfolio liquidity to our organization? Are we interested in including less-liquid investments in the portfolio, such as hedge funds, private equity, venture capital and real estate?How do we define risk? Is risk equivalent to volatility of returns, or are there other risks — such as the risk of capital loss, operational risk or reputational risk — that are equally worthy of consideration? When considering the provider’s capabilities, how highly do we value dedicated resources such as a robust risk modeling team that can perform stress tests and scenario analyses on the portfolio?If we seek a fiduciary partner who’ll exercise discretion over the portfolio, what level of financial strength will we require from that firm? Will a boutique manager or registered investment adviser (RIA) with comparatively little capital be adequate, or will we require a more substantial institution with a larger capital and resource base and stronger operational protocols?In addition to demanding a level of fees that’s reasonable and prudent for the strategies in which we invest, how important is the absolute level of costs that we’ll incur? When considering the potential benefits of a more highly diversified portfolio that includes alternative investments, are additional costs such as performance fees an obstacle? If low total costs are a priority, what role should indexing play in our portfolio?What other services beyond investment — such as education, training, service and support, and specialized reporting — will we expect from the investment service provider we select? Set your goals From: Stand-alone consultingTo: Nondiscretionary advice and implementationTo: Single firm — full discretion over portfolio √ Assess your resources: Who’ll serve on your RFP committee? What time, experience and skill do they bring to the process? How many firms will you invite to submit proposals? The amount of information requested of each candidate firm should be limited to what your team and internal staff can reasonably handle. As a rough indicator, the number of firms invited to submit proposals multiplied by the number of questions asked will give you a general idea of the scale and scope of the task ahead. √ Determine your needs: To the extent possible, you should undertake sufficient internal evaluation and conduct enough advance research on potential providers to ensure that you invite only firms that offer the range of services you desire. Depending on the type and size of your organization, those services could include: Investment and management of endowments and other institutional fundsAdministration of charitable trusts and gift annuitiesCustody servicesReportingLong-term investment planningConversion of assetsAbility to value, manage and, if necessary, dispose of nontraditional gifts (such as real estate or mineral rights) that you may receiveTrust servicesAdministrative and record-keeping assistance with grant-making and other distributionsAdvisory support for board governance and self-evaluation Formulating the request The RFP should provide information about your organization that will help potential providers understand your mission, structure and long-term goals. If available, you might also include a copy of your investment policy statement (IPS), your spending policy (if not included in the IPS), the asset allocation of your current portfolio and a recent performance report. The questionnaire you provide to candidate firms should ask for their history, the structure of their firm and details regarding their qualifications, services, investment policies, performance results and fee structure. The RFP process Define requirementsForm a committeePrepare the RFP and questionnaireCreate an invitee listSend the RFPAllow a time period for potential providers to ask clarifying questions.Review responses/check for completenessEvaluation 1: GradingEvaluation 2: Committee discussionSelect finalistsConduct reference checksEvaluation 3: Face-to-face meetingsFinal investment service provider selection Six steps to getting the process right 1. Put someone in charge Appoint one person to oversee the development of the RFP. This individual should be knowledgeable about your organization, its mission, and the relationship between the organization and the investment assets. The appointed individual will need to be able to devote sufficient time to the project (an estimated 20 to 30 hours for the entire project). He or she should work collaboratively with a small multidisciplinary committee, including representatives from the finance, human resources, communications, donor development and other areas, as applicable, to support these efforts. 2. Be specific One of the most important goals for the RFP document should be to express in specific terms what your organization is — and is not — looking for. If you use an existing RFP template, make the necessary changes so that the form reflects your organization’s goals and objectives; otherwise, you may receive responses that lack the information necessary to make an informed decision. It may be helpful to provide candidate firms with a sample asset allocation to ensure that you’ll be able to objectively compare returns and cost estimates. Firms should also be instructed to specify whether the returns presented include fees, are hypothetical, are based on a representative client or are composites that include multiple accounts. 3. Decide on weighting factors early What matters more to your organization? What matters less? Are investment services more important than administrative services, or are they equally important? Does your organization place a high value on a firm’s ability to provide strategic consulting services in addition to investment management? Make your priorities clear to the candidate firms. Describe which service aspects are most important to your organization so that they may provide in-depth responses in those areas. 4. Consider a face-to-face meeting Apart from objective factors, considerations such as “fit” — the comfort level that you have on a personal basis with your provider — can play an important role in your final decision. To determine comfort level, organizations typically require an in-person interview (often referred to as a “finals presentation”) to be delivered by the top three prospective providers. 5. Spell out schedules and expectations Make sure to clearly state the timeline for proposal submission, evaluation, presentations and decision-making. 6. Allow sufficient time Give the respondents at least three to four full weeks after receiving the RFP document to respond to your questions. It may also be useful to build in a period during which you’ll take questions from respondents before they submit their proposals. Finally, it’s essential that you leave sufficient time for your RFP committee to deliberate so it isn’t rushed during the important evaluation stage. Evaluate responses The first task in evaluation is to ascertain whether you’ve received complete proposals from all respondents. If you find that questions have been left unanswered or responses are in some other way incomplete, your committee can decide whether to contact respondents and allow them an opportunity to forward the missing information or whether to hold a hard line regarding completion deadlines. It’s often helpful to use an evaluation structure that enables the committee to grade respondents’ answers to each question on a scale from excellent to unacceptable. By judging each respondent using the same criteria, you level the playing field. You can then combine this formal grading structure with information from the face-to-face meetings or other factors that address issues of “fit” with your organization, its mission and goals. Other factors that can be important in the evaluation process include: Emphasizing fairness and taking each proposal at face valueEnabling multiple individuals to contribute to the scoring process and allowing their scores to be contrasted or aggregated (for example, averaged)Allowing criteria to be grouped into sections, with weightings assigned both at the section and individual response levelEncouraging accurate comparisons of candidates against individual RFP criteria After the individual reviewers have scored the proposals, you can initiate a meeting in which members of the RFP committee discuss their common understanding of the responses and try to iron out any wide discrepancies in scoring. At that time, you can identify any additional clarification needed from the respondents and, if desired, put a process in place to contact the respondents to receive that further information. Making the decision The next step in the evaluation process is narrowing down the field to several finalists — often three or so firms — with whom you’ll meet face to face. These meetings can be the most important part of the process, since it’s here that you should expect to encounter at least some of the individuals who would be actively working with your organization and to judge their “fit” with your committee, staff and operational structure. The answers and impressions you receive in this face-to-face setting, supplemented by a final reference check, should enable you to bring your RFP process to a successful conclusion with the selection of the investment service provider that’s right for you. Make every question count To obtain a sample RFP questionnaire, please email psrfpteam@bofa.com. Institutional Investments & Philanthropic Solutions (also referred to as “Philanthropic Solutions” or “II&PS”) is part of Bank of America Private Bank, a division of Bank of America, N.A., Member FDIC and a wholly owned subsidiary of Bank of America Corporation (“BofA Corp.”). Trust, fiduciary, and investment management services are provided by wholly owned banking affiliates of BofA Corp., including Bank of America, N.A. and its agents. MLPF&S makes available certain investment products sponsored, managed, distributed or provided by companies that are affiliates of BofA Corp. MLPF&S is a registered broker-dealer, registered investment adviser, Member SIPC and a wholly owned subsidiary of BofA Corp. Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Switch to Accessibility Friendly View Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog How we can help Explore what it’s like to work with a Merrill Financial Advisor Start here More for you More for you For private foundations and public charities, the investment policy statement is crucial Connect Find out how our advisors can help Sign up Get monthly insights from our Perspectives newsletter Related Articles How a governance budget can empower you Read more Should your nonprofit find an outside investment manager? Read more","title":"The Request for Proposal (RFP)","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/the-request-for-proposal.html","id":583},{"content_hash":"4da8f7388fc94999f4546eb39219b1959f3e7aac7dc1307cc5eb896ac2945670","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:00.405547+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/les-rapports-entre-la-compagnie-a-canal-de-suez-et-le-gouvernement-egyptien-sont-excellents_2073241_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives \" Les rapports entre la Compagnie à canal de Suez et le gouvernement égyptien sont excellents \" Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"\" Les rapports entre la Compagnie à canal de Suez et le gouvernement égyptien sont excellents \"","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/les-rapports-entre-la-compagnie-a-canal-de-suez-et-le-gouvernement-egyptien-sont-excellents_2073241_1819218.html","id":584},{"content_hash":"4ff75b5c6746a74ca1e73995cff63f41cc5e23434759c1409a6dbc480179ed5f","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:28:00.294872+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/european-research-council-tours-australia-18894","language":"","text":"European Research Council tours Australia Published: October 3, 2013 11:00am EDT Carlos M. Duarte, The University of Western Australia Author Carlos M. Duarte Director, Oceans Institute, The University of Western Australia Partners University of Western Australia provides funding as a founding partner of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.5rfqycf6r https://theconversation.com/european-research-council-tours-australia-18894 https://theconversation.com/european-research-council-tours-australia-18894 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article The Headquarters of the European Research Council Executive Agency in Brussels. (c) ERC The Secretary General of the European Research Council (ERC), Prof. Donald B. Dingwell, is visiting Australia as part of a global campaign to raise awareness on the opportunities to participate in this exciting science funding program . Prof. Donald B. Dingwell, Secretary General of the European Research Council. (c) ERC In Australia during a “marathon tour” between October 2 and 11, Prof. Dingwell will meet the Australian Academy of Science (ARC), Australian Antarctic Division and a number of universities (Australia National University, University of Adelaide, Monach University, University of Melbourne, Sunshine Coastal University, University of Queensland, University of Sydney, University of Tasmania and the University of Western Australia). In this tour Prof. Dingwell will be accompanied by Dr. Gemma Solomon, who was granted a Starting Grant in 2010 to study how molecules carrying current heat up and cool down, potentially paving the way to new frontiers in power-generating materials, which she is developing in the University of Copenhagen. Dr. Dr Gemma Solomon is an Australian scientist leading an ERC grant at the University of Copenhagen. This tour offers a great opportunity for Australian researchers to learn how to become involved in EU-funded research and profit from its attractive funding. Secondly, the visit will allow Australia research funding organizations , such as the ARC, to be updated on the major effort done to streamline the application and review processes to the highest efficiency ever achieve in the EU, or worldwide. Indeed, I have drawn attention in the past to the ERC program as one that has managed to minimize all unnecessary red tape, and thereby avoiding loss of productivity by all involved in the process. Moreover, the ERC provides generous budgets, from 2 million euros to 3.5 million euros, to individual researchers at the start of or advanced in their careers, respectively. Furthermore, reviewers and evaluation panels are instructed not to trim budgets, unlike the usual 30 to 40 % trimming in the Australian ARC. Since its inception in 2007, the ERC executes the IDEAS program of the Research Framework Program of the EU, aimed at supporting excellence in research. Although part of the EU’s Framework Program, the European Research Council schemes are open to participation of scientists from around the world. In fact, the motto on the ERC’s web page reads “Supporting Top Researchers from Anywhere in the World”, with the sole requirement that they should have at least a part-time appointment with a research institute belonging to the EU and spend substantial time in Europe during the length of the grant. Being ‘investigator-driven’ and ‘bottom-up’, in nature, the ERC approach allows researchers to identify new opportunities and directions in any field of research, from social sciences and humanities to high-energy physics and life sciences, rather than being led by priorities set by politicians. This approach ensures that funds are channeled(?) into new and promising areas of research with a greater degree of flexibility. ERC grants are awarded through open competition to projects headed by earl-career and established researchers, irrespective of their origins, who are working or moving to work in Europe - the sole criterion for selection is scientific excellence. The aim here is to recognise the best ideas, and retain and confer status and visibility to the best brains in Europe, while also attracting talent from abroad. However, the ERC aims to do more than simply fund research. In the longer term, it looks to substantially strengthen and shape the European research system, and as a matter of fact already does so (?). This is done through high quality peer review, the establishment of international benchmarks of success, and the provision of up-to-date information on who is succeeding in its highly competitive funding calls and why. The total budget allocated to the ERC for the period 2007 to 2013 was € 7.5 billion, with a substantial increase anticipated under the new Framework Program, ‘Horizon 2020’, between 2014 and 2020. Since 2007, more than 3,860 projects have been selected for funding from more than 43,000 applications. The success of the ERC is for instance indicated by the fact that it counts seven Nobel laureates and three Fields Medalists among its grant holders, and that over 10,000 articles have been published acknowledging ERC-funding in peer-reviewed high impact journals between 2008 and 2011, including papers published every week in the top interdisciplinary journals, such as Nature and Science. The impact of ERC goes beyond the specific outcomes of the projects to serve as a mechanism to render excellence a ‘contagious disease’. Each ERC grantee employs on average six team members, thus contributing to training a new generation of excellent researchers. By 2013, it is estimated that the ERC will have funded over 4 000 grantees and will have supported some 23,500 other team members, offering cutting-edge research training for nearly 7,000 doctoral students and 9,000 postdoctoral researchers from around the world. Because of these achievements during its seven years of existence, the ERC has established itself as a benchmark against which national and international programs and institutions compare the effectiveness of their own programs and the quality of their researchers. Australian scientists are not alien to the ERC, as the ERC already supports projects lead by 21 Australian scientists. However, the excellence of Australian scientists suggests that this number could be much higher, hence the visit of Prof. Dingwell. The benefit of a broader engagement of Australian scientists will extend beyond the benefits of the individual grants and the opportunities to bring excellent ideas to live, as participation in the ERC will provide close connections with European research networks, which is also beneficial for the internationalization strategies of Australian institutions. Disclosure: Carlos M. Duarte is a member of the Scientific Council of the European Research Council Previous post Published: September 24, 2013 Deep sea mining: coming soon to an ocean near you Next post Published: October 22, 2013 Is the ocean broken?","title":"European Research Council tours Australia","url":"https://theconversation.com/european-research-council-tours-australia-18894","id":585},{"content_hash":"c743b658fc82f9f6ca9c195969af9115a2a8827289c11656a6fc1357cf4341ee","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:59.761975+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/brianna-morris-grant/13965022","language":"","text":"Brianna Morris-Grant - ABC NewsSkip to main contentSkip to news navigation, settings and searchExplore the ABC More from ABCClose menuABC ABC News ABC iview ABC listen Kids & Students Indigenous Information & ServicesEditorial PoliciesRead our editorial guiding principlesFollow the ABCnewsiviewlistenLoadingABC NewsJust InFor YouPoliticsWorldBusinessAnalysisSportLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMoreSearchNews HomeJust InFor YouAnalysisRuralWatch LiveHealthIndigenousPoliticsScienceDeep TimeElectionsWorldEnvironmentInvestigationsBusinessLocal newsQuizzesSportAFLNRLFootballTennisCricketNetballLifestyleWellbeingRelationships & FamilyFood & RecipesPersonal FinanceHome & GardenEntertainmentTV & MoviesBooksMusicPop CultureArtsFacebookYouTubeInstagramTwitterEmergencyBackstoryNewsletters中文新闻BERITA BAHASA INDONESIATOK PISIN Appearance (BETA) Follow system settings Select an optionAlways lightAlways darkFollow system settingsABC iViewABC ListenBrianna Morris-GrantBrianna Morris-Grant got their start as a journalist for the Gold Coast Bulletin in 2018, specialising in local politics, state government and health. They also worked as the GCB's Social Media Editor before joining the ABC National News Desk as a digital producer in 2022.Latest by Brianna Morris-Grant'Excruciating' pain and 'dead' tissue: The drug of choice for US executionsOn paper, Tennessee's death penalty protocol looks neat. But experts say the drug used in lethal injection risks severe pain and a feeling of drowning.Topic:Law, Crime and JusticeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 5:00amFri 9 Oct 2026 at 5:00amFri 9 Oct 2026 at 5:00amA second execution attempt for Christa Pike would be 'highly problematic'As Christa Pike wakes in hospital following two doses of lethal injection drug, experts say Tennessee could still try to execute her.Topic:HomicideWed 7 Oct 2026 at 5:40pmWed 7 Oct 2026 at 5:40pmWed 7 Oct 2026 at 5:40pmDeath row inmate Christa Pike wakes after botched executionChrista Pike wakes up and speaks almost a week after a botched execution attempt at a US prison.Topic:PrisonsWed 7 Oct 2026 at 9:28amWed 7 Oct 2026 at 9:28amWed 7 Oct 2026 at 9:28amNo-one has ever survived a lethal injection — Christa Pike may not eitherAs death row inmate Christa Pike remains in hospital following a failed lethal injection, we take a closer look at the track record for botched executions in the US. Topic:Law, Crime and JusticeTue 6 Oct 2026 at 4:31pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 4:31pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 4:31pmA 'bent' syringe, a closed curtain: Inside Christa Pike's botched US executionConvicted killer Christa Pike remains in hospital receiving emergency treatment after surviving two doses of the lethal injection drug pentobarbital.Topic:PrisonsSun 4 Oct 2026 at 12:09pmSun 4 Oct 2026 at 12:09pmSun 4 Oct 2026 at 12:09pmTeenage killer survives execution attempt after 30 years on death rowChrista Pike was set to become the first woman executed in the US state of Tennessee in more than 200 years. Instead, she is fighting for life.Topic:HomicideFri 2 Oct 2026 at 5:19amFri 2 Oct 2026 at 5:19amFri 2 Oct 2026 at 5:19amUS woman still alive after lethal injection dose, legal team saysMedia witnesses say Christa Pike was still alive when they were ordered to leave the prison by officials. Topic:HomicideThu 1 Oct 2026 at 11:30pmThu 1 Oct 2026 at 11:30pmThu 1 Oct 2026 at 11:30pm'When fear takes over': Responding to the DRC's largest Ebola outbreakAmid the largest Ebola virus outbreak recorded in the DRC's history, healthcare workers try to keep the system running. Topic:EbolaSun 27 Sep 2026 at 5:13amSun 27 Sep 2026 at 5:13amSun 27 Sep 2026 at 5:13amLatest population data for Australia reveals fastest and slowest growing statesNew data shows Australia's population has grown by 1.4 per cent over the past year. Topic:Community and SocietyFri 18 Sep 2026 at 7:45amFri 18 Sep 2026 at 7:45amFri 18 Sep 2026 at 7:45amCharlie Kirk's family files wrongful death notice after his shooting deathThe family of slain podcaster Charlie Kirk blame officials at the university campus where he was killed for his death. Topic:ShootingsThu 17 Sep 2026 at 11:27amThu 17 Sep 2026 at 11:27amThu 17 Sep 2026 at 11:27amHow a flooded basement earned one Australian a spot in Muppets historyIt's been 50 years since The Muppet Show hit TV screens around the world. For one Australian puppeteer, it was the adventure of a lifetime.Topic:PuppetryWed 16 Sep 2026 at 4:30amWed 16 Sep 2026 at 4:30amWed 16 Sep 2026 at 4:30amWould a 'kill switch' stop artificial intelligence going rogue?As the heads of the biggest AI companies in the world call for a \"kill switch\" to be mandated, an expert says it's only a \"distraction\". Topic:AITue 15 Sep 2026 at 12:34pmTue 15 Sep 2026 at 12:34pmTue 15 Sep 2026 at 12:34pm'Not sick enough': Woman with eating disorder details 55 hours in emergency departmentAs Australia marks Body Image and Eating Disorder Awareness Week, one woman shares her 55-hour long ordeal seeking treatment for anorexia in a hospital emergency department. Topic:Eating DisordersFri 11 Sep 2026 at 5:00amFri 11 Sep 2026 at 5:00amFri 11 Sep 2026 at 5:00amPhones and computers are about to be more expensive — thanks to AIThe price of phones, laptops and game consoles are rising thanks to a global shortage of their most important part. Topic:TechnologyTue 1 Sep 2026 at 1:56pmTue 1 Sep 2026 at 1:56pmTue 1 Sep 2026 at 1:56pm'Darn good' or 'functioning': Closing arguments in mother's murder trialA jury is left to decide the fate of US mother Lindsay Clancy, who has spent five weeks on trial for the murder of her three young children.Topic:Law, Crime and JusticeSat 29 Aug 2026 at 5:09amSat 29 Aug 2026 at 5:09amSat 29 Aug 2026 at 5:09amThe fiery threats that made Dolly Parton a 'natural-born movie star'With a few fiery words and a mountain of blonde curls, Dolly Parton went from country music singer to a \"natural-born movie star\" in 9 to 5.Topic:MoviesThu 27 Aug 2026 at 4:45amThu 27 Aug 2026 at 4:45amThu 27 Aug 2026 at 4:45amOnce-discreet museum thefts turn violent with explosives and axesEurope's museum thieves once relied on deception, stealth and reconnaissance, but sledgehammers, axes, and explosives are now the weapons of choice, according to Europol. Topic:CrimeTue 25 Aug 2026 at 11:34amTue 25 Aug 2026 at 11:34amTue 25 Aug 2026 at 11:34am13 medications, 30 prescriptions led up to US mother's killing of three childrenA mother who says she was suffering postpartum psychosis when she killed her three children and attempted to take her own life is now on trial for their murder.Topic:HomicideSat 15 Aug 2026 at 1:26pmSat 15 Aug 2026 at 1:26pmSat 15 Aug 2026 at 1:26pmReport confirms cause of deadly California wildfire that killed 19Eighteen months on, a new report confirms the cause of one of the deadliest wildfires in California's history as an alleged arsonist faces a retrial for a second blaze. Topic:WildfiresFri 14 Aug 2026 at 5:21amFri 14 Aug 2026 at 5:21amFri 14 Aug 2026 at 5:21amTrading platforms offer cash vouchers for 'high-risk' investments, watchdog saysA review of nine online trading companies finds several are encouraging Australians to jump on \"complex\" deals, putting them at risk of major losses.Topic:Personal FinanceThu 13 Aug 2026 at 10:44amThu 13 Aug 2026 at 10:44amThu 13 Aug 2026 at 10:44amDiarrhoea parasite spreads across US as more than 10,000 cases recordedAt least two people have died amid a growing outbreak of the Cyclospora parasite, linked to contaminated lettuce. Topic:Disease OutbreakWed 5 Aug 2026 at 1:02pmWed 5 Aug 2026 at 1:02pmWed 5 Aug 2026 at 1:02pmInfluencers pushing 'ideal of masculinity' drive male eating disordersAbout 365,000 men across Australia are living with an eating disorder, but they face a system still dismissing them. Topic:Eating DisordersTue 28 Jul 2026 at 9:07amTue 28 Jul 2026 at 9:07amTue 28 Jul 2026 at 9:07amHear that siren? That was the first test of a national emergency alertAustralia gets its first taste of a new nationwide emergency warning system as millions of devices across the country sound off simultaneously. Topic:ExplainerMon 27 Jul 2026 at 2:00pmMon 27 Jul 2026 at 2:00pmMon 27 Jul 2026 at 2:00pmSerial harassers given '19 strikes' on Meta, eSafety boss saysElon Musk's X Corp is fighting the eSafety Commissioner on three different take-down orders of violent content, including the killing of Charlie Kirk.Topic:Royal CommissionsFri 3 Jul 2026 at 7:12amFri 3 Jul 2026 at 7:12amFri 3 Jul 2026 at 7:12amExtremists using 'codes' to evade detection online, royal commission hearsFar-right individuals adopt memes as a way to push \"more palatable\" antisemitism, which is less likely to be picked up by social media moderators. Topic:Royal CommissionsThu 2 Jul 2026 at 9:35amThu 2 Jul 2026 at 9:35amThu 2 Jul 2026 at 9:35amLoad more stories aboveGot a news tip?If you have inside knowledge of a topic in the news, contact the ABC.Confidential Tip‑offIf you are a whistleblower, believe you have sensitive information that should be made public, or wish to protect your identity, find our more secure options.Confidential Tip‑offNot confidential? Contact ABC News and investigations.Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashWoman, 71, treated on 'cold floor' of Adelaide hospitalData centre operator Firmus pulls blockbuster ASX float after lacklustre demandMass evacuations, cars washed away in Chilean flooding disasterSydney councillor accuses Indigenous elder Mick Mundine of sexual assault'Beau L-C' rental car card among items found near bodies, jury hearsliveButters unveiled by Bulldogs after 'megadeal', Blakey makes his decisionFederal government backtracks on disaster funding splitUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicThese are the frontrunners for this year's Nobel Peace Prize Powderfinger confirm reunion, 16 years after breaking upBack to topABC NewsContact ABC NewsYour home of Australian stories, conversations and events that shape our nation.This service may include material from Agence France-Presse (AFP), APTN, Reuters, AAP, CNN and the BBC World Service which is copyright and cannot be reproduced.Follow ABC NewsSectionsABC News HomeJust InFor YouWatch Live NewsLive ABC NewsRadioPoliticsElectionsAustraliaWorldSportLive SportRuralBusinessAnalysisScienceHealthLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMore TopicsNews in LanguageChinese 中文Tok PisinBerita Bahasa IndonesiaRelatedABC News appLocal NewsABC EmergencyABC IndigenousABC Religion & EthicsABC AsiaABC PacificMusic & AudioABC Listen Hometriple jLive RadioMovies & TVABC iview HomeList of ABC ShowsLive TVTV GuideKids & StudentsBTNEducationHeywireTakeoverABC KidsGet the ABC News AM newsletter Get the latest ABC News delivered to your email inboxEmail addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. All ABC newslettersStay informed and connected with the ABC. Covering a wide range of topics and brands including News, iview, listen, and more.Browse all ABC newslettersABC homeInformation & ServicesEditorial PoliciesAccessibilityHelpContact UsCommercialCareersAbout the ABCPrivacy PolicyTerms of Use© 2026 ABCFollow the ABCWe acknowledge Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples as the First Australians and Traditional Custodians of the lands where we live, learn and work.","title":"Brianna Morris-Grant","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/brianna-morris-grant/13965022","id":586},{"content_hash":"6ef2c56f0feb80499b9e272633ad318ca932d8fc40ad01cf469034e5b03636e2","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:59.505169+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/importance-of-kyc-remediation","language":"","text":"The Importance of KYC RemediationByPrince, Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 27, 2023•5mins ReadUnderstanding KYC Remediation and its SignificanceKYC Remediation Process: Steps and Best PracticesBenefits of KYC Remediation for BusinessesKYC Remediation and Legal ComplianceKYC Refresh vs. Remediation: What’s the Difference?Data Remediation Definition: Why Data Quality is Crucial for KYC RemediationAchieving KYC Remediation with YouverifyFinal Thoughts: Staying KYC Compliant is Key to Business SuccessShare this postAs a business owner, you are probably aware of KYC compliance and its importance. KYC, or Know Your Customer, is a process used by businesses to verify the identity of their customers to detect and prevent potential fraudulent activities. However, simply being KYC-compliant is not enough. KYC remediation is also crucial to ensure that your business stays compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. In this article, we will discuss the importance of KYC remediation, its process, benefits, legal compliance, and the difference between KYC refresh and remediation. We will also provide tips on how to stay KYC compliant and how KYC remediation services can help your business. Understanding KYC Remediation and its Significance KYC remediation is the procedure of reviewing and bringing up-to-date customer information and documentation to guarantee compliance with the latest regulations and best practices. Some of the important points to note about KYC remediation include: It helps businesses identify and verify the customer’s identity, assess the risk associated with the customer, and document the findings. It helps you to identify and prevent potential fraudulent activities, such as money laundering, terrorist financing, and identity theft.KYC remediation is significant because it helps your business to maintain its reputation and credibility. KYC remediation also helps you to avoid legal and financial consequences associated with non-compliance. It can result in fines, legal actions, loss of reputation, and even loss of business.KYC Remediation Process: Steps and Best Practices KYC remediation involves several steps and best practices to ensure its effectiveness. The following are the common steps involved in the KYC remediation process: 1. Identify the customers who require remediation This involves reviewing your customer database and identifying the customers whose information and documentation require updating. 2. Collect the necessary information and documentation This involves requesting the necessary information and documentation from the customers, such as identity documents, address proof, and business information. 3. Verify the information and documentation This involves verifying the authenticity of the information and documentation provided by the customers, such as conducting background checks and verifying the identity of the customers. 4. Assess the risk associated with the customers This involves assessing the risk associated with the customers based on their location, business type, and other factors. 5. Document the findings This involves documenting the findings of the remediation process, such as updating the customer database and creating a remediation report. The following are some best practices to ensure the effectiveness of the KYC remediation process: 1. Define a clear KYC remediation policy and procedure This involves defining a clear policy and procedure for KYC remediation that outlines the roles and responsibilities of the stakeholders involved in the process. 2. Use technology to automate the KYC remediation process This involves using technology to automate the KYC remediation process, such as using software to verify the authenticity of the information and documentation provided by the customers. 3. Train your employees on KYC remediation This involves training your employees on KYC remediation to ensure that they understand the importance of the process, the best practices, and the latest regulations. Benefits of KYC Remediation for Businesses KYC remediation offers several benefits for businesses, such as: 1. Compliance with the latest regulations and best practices KYC remediation helps businesses to stay compliant with the latest regulations and best practices, which can help them to avoid legal and financial consequences associated with non-compliance. 2. Prevention of financial crimes KYC remediation helps businesses to prevent financial crimes, such as money laundering, terrorist financing, and identity theft, which can damage their reputation and credibility. 3. Improved customer experience KYC remediation helps businesses to improve the customer experience by ensuring that their information is secure and up-to-date. 4. Increased operational efficiency KYC remediation helps businesses to increase operational efficiency by automating the KYC remediation process and reducing the manual effort required. KYC Remediation and Legal Compliance Legal compliance is a crucial aspect of KYC remediation. Businesses are required to comply with the latest regulations and best practices to avoid legal and financial consequences. The following are the legal consequences of non-compliance with KYC regulations: 1. Fines: Businesses that fail to comply with KYC regulations can face fines imposed by regulatory authorities. 2. Legal actions: Businesses that fail to comply with KYC regulations can face legal actions, such as lawsuits and criminal charges. 3. Loss of reputation: Non-compliance with KYC regulations can damage the reputation and credibility of businesses, which can result in a loss of customers and revenue. KYC Refresh vs. Remediation: What’s the Difference? KYC refresh and remediation are often used interchangeably, but they refer to different processes. KYC refresh involves updating the customer information and documentation periodically, such as annually or bi-annually. It is a proactive approach to ensure that customer information is up-to-date and compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. KYC remediation, on the other hand, involves reviewing and updating the customer information and documentation when there is a trigger event, such as a change in the customer’s profile or a change in the regulatory requirements. It is a reactive approach to ensure that the customer information is compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. Data Remediation Definition: Why Data Quality is Crucial for KYC Remediation Data remediation is the process of identifying and resolving data quality issues to ensure that the data is accurate, complete, and consistent. Data quality is crucial for KYC remediation because it ensures that the information and documentation provided by the customers are authentic and reliable. Poor data quality can result in inaccurate customer information, which can lead to non-compliance and potentially fraudulent activities. Achieving KYC Remediation with Youverify KYC remediation services can help your business to stay compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. Youverify offers the following KYC remediation services: 1. Expertise: Youverify has the expertise and experience to ensure that your business stays compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. 2. Technology: Youverify uses technology to automate the KYC remediation process, which can reduce the manual effort required and increase operational efficiency. 3. Flexibility: Youverify's KYC remediation services offer flexible solutions that can be customized to meet the specific needs of your business. 4. Cost-effective: Youverify's KYC remediation services can be cost-effective compared to hiring an in-house team to perform the remediation process. Final Thoughts: Staying KYC Compliant is Key to Business Success In conclusion, KYC remediation is crucial to ensure that your business stays compliant with the latest regulations and best practices. It helps you to prevent potential fraudulent activities, maintain your reputation and credibility, and avoid legal and financial consequences associated with non-compliance. KYC remediation involves several steps and best practices, such as identifying the customers who require remediation, collecting and verifying the necessary information and documentation, and assessing the risk associated with the customers. Businesses can benefit from KYC remediation by staying compliant with the latest regulations and best practices, preventing financial crimes, improving the customer experience, and increasing operational efficiency. Compliance tips for businesses include defining a clear KYC policy and procedure, using technology to automate the KYC process, training your employees on KYC compliance, and conducting regular audits to ensure compliance. Remember, staying KYC compliant is key to business success! Speak to an expert at Youverify for more information on KYC remediation services and how we can help your business stay compliant. Request a demo today to get started. Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"The Importance of KYC Remediation","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/importance-of-kyc-remediation","id":587},{"content_hash":"016c137a4307ff81bb543dbbecfbe0ee9d6031ab3eb8c9f5df0727dd9ac4634c","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:59.280741+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/lookups/","language":"","text":"PostgreSQL specific lookups¶ Trigram similarity¶ trigram_similar¶ The trigram_similar lookup allows you to perform trigram lookups, measuring the number of trigrams (three consecutive characters) shared, using a dedicated PostgreSQL extension. A trigram lookup is given an expression and returns results that have a similarity measurement greater than the current similarity threshold. To use it, add 'django.contrib.postgres' in your INSTALLED_APPS and activate the pg_trgm extension on PostgreSQL. You can install the extension using the TrigramExtension migration operation. The trigram_similar lookup can be used on CharField and TextField: >>> City.objects.filter(name__trigram_similar=\"Middlesborough\") <QuerySet [<City: Middlesbrough>]> trigram_word_similar¶ The trigram_word_similar lookup allows you to perform trigram word similarity lookups using a dedicated PostgreSQL extension. It can be approximately understood as measuring the greatest number of trigrams shared between the parameter and any substring of the field. A trigram word lookup is given an expression and returns results that have a word similarity measurement greater than the current similarity threshold. To use it, add 'django.contrib.postgres' in your INSTALLED_APPS and activate the pg_trgm extension on PostgreSQL. You can install the extension using the TrigramExtension migration operation. The trigram_word_similar lookup can be used on CharField and TextField: >>> Sentence.objects.filter(name__trigram_word_similar=\"Middlesborough\") <QuerySet [<Sentence: Gumby rides on the path of Middlesbrough>]> trigram_strict_word_similar¶ Similar to trigram_word_similar, except that it forces extent boundaries to match word boundaries. To use it, add 'django.contrib.postgres' in your INSTALLED_APPS and activate the pg_trgm extension on PostgreSQL. You can install the extension using the TrigramExtension migration operation. The trigram_strict_word_similar lookup can be used on CharField and TextField. Unaccent¶ The unaccent lookup allows you to perform accent-insensitive lookups using a dedicated PostgreSQL extension. This lookup is implemented using Transform, so it can be chained with other lookup functions. To use it, you need to add 'django.contrib.postgres' in your INSTALLED_APPS and activate the unaccent extension on PostgreSQL. The UnaccentExtension migration operation is available if you want to perform this activation using migrations. The unaccent lookup can be used on CharField and TextField: >>> City.objects.filter(name__unaccent=\"México\") <QuerySet [<City: Mexico>]> >>> User.objects.filter(first_name__unaccent__startswith=\"Jerem\") <QuerySet [<User: Jeremy>, <User: Jérémy>, <User: Jérémie>, <User: Jeremie>]> Warning unaccent lookups should perform fine in most use cases. However, queries using this filter will generally perform full table scans, which can be slow on large tables. In those cases, using dedicated full text indexing tools might be appropriate.","title":"PostgreSQL specific lookups¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/lookups/","id":588},{"content_hash":"ad949fd50204c8c4341157ba7301054903f42bcd4af9955406f90be08fa22538","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:59.157960+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/une-conference-de-la-confiance-rallierait-une-forte-majorite-au-palais-de-chaillot-il-reste-a-faire-de-cette-suggestion-un-projet-concret-de-resolution_3140703_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Le vœu formé hier par M. Vincent Auriol de voir, dans le cadre des Nations unies, \" les hommes illustres vers qui convergent tant de regards anxieux... prendre entre eux un contact humain \" risque fort de ne pas être exaucé. Pourtant de nombreux délégués ont accueilli avec faveur la péroraison du discours présidentiel. Un projet de résolution invitant les chefs des quatre puissances mondiales à se réunir à Paris aurait incontestablement rallié une forte majorité de suffrages. Quel peuple d'autre part réprouverait-il a priori une \" conférence de la confiance \"? A Washington tous les journaux ont reproduit en première page les suggestions du président de la République, faisant ainsi ressortit l'intérêt qu'y attache l'opinion publique américaine. En Grande-Bretagne le libéral Manchester Guardian constate que \" M. Vincent Auriol n'a rien dit que la vérité en déclarant que le monde entier saluerait avec joie une telle rencontre \". Mais les milieux officiels intéressés se montrent bien plus réservés que la grande masse des délégués. Whitehall a beau considérer avec sympathie le souhait du président de la République, il n'en reste pas moins qu'un porte-parole du Foreign Office précise que \" cette proposition doit être attentivement étudiée \". M. Joseph Short, directeur des services de presse de la Maison Blanche, a déclaré de son côté que le président Truman n'avait pas officiellement été mis au courant des suggestions formulées par M. Vincent Auriol. A vrai dire on paraît sceptique aux États-Unis sur l'utilité d'une rencontre des Quatre Grands tant que l'on continue de se battre en Corée. A Paris l'entourage du secrétaire d'État se montre plus nuancé dans ses avis. On reconnaît volontiers que le président de la République s'est fait en termes très délicats l'interprète d'un désir unanime, mais on se garde de tout commentaire. Il semble difficile, souligne-t-on dans ces milieux, d'exprimer autre chose qu'un simple sentiment. Constitutionnellement en effet les pouvoirs d'un chef d'État français ne sauraient se comparer à ceux du président des États-Unis. On ne saurait donc officiellement prendre position au sujet de ses propos. Les Américains, en d'autres termes, auraient plus catégoriquement donné leur opinion si une invitation en règle avait été lancée par le gouvernement français. Formalistes à l'égal sinon plus que leurs adversaires de guerre froide, les Russes partagent cet avis. M. Andrei Vychinski n'a-t-il pas de son côté laissé entendre que le maréchal Staline pour prendre la question en considération devait en être saisi par les dirigeants des puissances occidentales ? Or l'A.F.P. rapporte que selon les milieux français informés \" il serait prématuré de croire que le gouvernement français formulera dans les jours qui viennent une proposition précise dans le sens du discours du président Auriol \". Ainsi, dans les capitales intéressées, le protocole vient-il au secours d'une diplomatie hésitante. Il vous reste 35.56% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"UNE CONFÉRENCE DE LA CONFIANCE rallierait une forte majorité AU PALAIS DE CHAILLOT Il reste à faire de cette \" suggestion \" un projet concret de résolution","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/une-conference-de-la-confiance-rallierait-une-forte-majorite-au-palais-de-chaillot-il-reste-a-faire-de-cette-suggestion-un-projet-concret-de-resolution_3140703_1819218.html","id":589},{"content_hash":"962dcc7301756bcdb0427f8ead9339abe2413c18cf7c4eef2267c58da4dd1545","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:59.103653+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/videogames-for-humans-20288","language":"","text":"Videogames for humans Published: November 13, 2013 6:07pm EST Brendan Keogh, RMIT University Author Brendan Keogh PhD Candidate, Game Studies, RMIT University Partners RMIT University provides funding as a strategic partner of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.5uq5e7j9f https://theconversation.com/videogames-for-humans-20288 https://theconversation.com/videogames-for-humans-20288 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article merritt kopas’s Forest Ambassador curates ‘videogames for human beings’. http://forestambassador.com/ For a lot of people, videogames are a very particular thing: a graphically advanced spectacle of guns and cars for old boys and young men, played on an Xbox or a Playstation console, or a powerful gaming PC, requiring nimble dexterity to control a dozen buttons at once. For anyone paying even remote attention to videogames, this is obviously an incredibly simplistic and reductive stereotype. There are indie games like Minecraft, Facebook games like Candy Crush Saga, mobile games like Angry Birds, and portable games like Pokémon, all of which fall beyond that ‘core gamer’ stereotype. But the fact that the stereotype persists remains unsurprising when you consider just which games get the most attention. Those games with the largest budgets (for both development and marketing) do often fit the stereotype. This doesn’t mean they are inherently ‘bad’, but they are certainly crafted to fit a certain mold. And, inevitably, these are the games that dominate discourses around games: they are the most visible and the most talked about. People are interested in the games that are advertised the most, and journalists are going to write about the games people are interested in. I don’t absolve myself from such an accusation—in the short life of this column I’ve already written about Grand Theft Auto and Call of Duty, two of the most heavily advertised franchises in any medium. So, despite the sheer plurality of games being created and played, the stereotypically “core” persists. And when there is a core, there are inevitably margins. If some games are getting most of the attention, then some games are getting less. And so the stereotype is strengthened, and so more games are advertised at the perceived core, and the cycle continues. There are, however, people and projects working to expand the scope of the games we play and talk about, and, most importantly, expanding just who “we” means when we talk about the people who play videogames. In particular, I find merritt kopas’s project Forest Ambassador fascinating and exciting. This isn’t a website trying to promote ‘indie’ games to the same gamers already playing games, but trying to promote a medium to those that have traditionally felt excluded. Games must fulfill four criteria for kopas to feature them on Forest Ambassador: they must be free, they must demand relatively little investment of time, they require little familiarity with game conventions, and they require no specialised equipment. This inevitably means that most ‘core’ games are excluded by default, and the spotlight can focus on those worthwhile games that are often ignored. The videogames Forest Ambassador shares are not for ‘core gamers’ but for human beings, as kopas herself has said in an essay on the website’s goals. Sitting someone who has never played videogames before in front of a ‘serious’ blockbuster game like Bioshock Infinite won’t prove how powerful games are; it will just reinforce how complicated and inaccessible games are to anyone who has not played games for fifteen years. Sit them in front of any of the games curated by Forest Ambassador, however, and you might just have helped them take their first steps into an exciting medium full of potential. (Forest Ambassador can be found at forestambassador.com. If you find the project interesting, you can support it through its Patreon page.) Previous post Published: November 4, 2013 A tale of two trailers Next post Published: November 21, 2013 Generational differences","title":"Videogames for humans","url":"https://theconversation.com/videogames-for-humans-20288","id":590},{"content_hash":"072baa5684494f5f9f61dcc4591692467ae5e6a15ed37fa29661e7a841100c66","domain":"reallygoodemails.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:59.043206+00:00","final_url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/environment-landing-page","language":"","text":"Environment Landing Page | Page template - RGE StudioNow you have zero excuses to NOT design really good emails. Introducing RGE StudioRead more Find inspirationAll categoriesBrowse by typePopular right nowBehavioral Enhancement Inaugural Industry Miscellaneous Promotional Punctual Seasonal Templates BehavioralAbandoned CartComment NotificationCurated ContentCustomer AppreciationCustomer ServiceDiscoveryEngagementFeedback and Survey and PollFollow-UpLoyalty / RewardsPost Purchase / Recent PurchaseProduct RecommendationsReferralRetention / Re-engagementReview and TestimonialSummaryThank YouUnsubscribeCurated by humans since 2014.Browse all categoriesPopular right nowCreate emailsProductWhat is RGE StudioThe email builder that gets you.FeaturesEverything you need to make email happen.Product tourSnoop around at your own pace.Works withEmail & page templatesStart from something really good.IntegrationsYour favorite ESP or CRM can come too.Salesforce ecosystemCreate in Studio, send from Salesforce.MCPBuild emails from your AI tools.SolutionsHigher educationBetter email for busy campus teams.NonprofitsMore mission, less busywork.IntegrationPerfect for Salesforce usersThe perfect addition to your Salesforce setup? An email editor that actually works.Learn moreSOC 2 Type II · ISO 27001 · DPF · AWS PartnerPricingTalk to usResourcesLearnBlogTips, trends, and teardowns.AcademyGet better at email. For free.Help centerStuck with RGE Studio? Start here.ConnectVirtual eventsCome learn stuff with email people.UnspamFor folks who hate boring email conferences.Feedback FridayReal emails, honest critique.NewsletterReally good stuff, in your inbox.ToolsAI prompt librarySmart starting points for your emails.Email signature generatorLook pro in every sign-off.Report2026 RGE Email SurveyFresh data on how email teams actually work: email got faster, but not for everyone.Read the report300,000 email pros learn and hang out here.Next webinarsJoin the newsletterPricingTalk to SalesBack to the template catalogREALLY GOOD EMAILSAboutSchoolFeedback FridayEventsContactCode of ConductEmail signature generatorPrompt libraryRGE STUDIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"Environment Landing Page | Page template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/environment-landing-page","id":591},{"content_hash":"6d97003675491be9b87bcb12acb5ed2907b7bef40984fc44793997d11c0f4b25","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:58.544035+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/astrology/horoscope/daily-astrology-news-alert-9-october-2026-libra-moon-and-mercury-saturn-tension-make-it-a-day-for-serious-talks/articleshow/134785153.cms","language":"","text":"Daily Astrology News Alert, 9 October 2026: Libra Moon and Mercury-Saturn Tension Make It a Day for Serious Talks - The Times of IndiaEditionININUSGCCEnglishEnglishहिन्दीमराठीಕನ್ನಡதமிழ்বাংলাമലയാളംతెలుగుગુજરાતીWeatherSign InTOIAstrologyHoroscopeSun Sign PredictionsCompatibility CalculatorYearly Horoscope 2026Chinese HoroscopeNumerologyNumerology PredictionsPlanets TransitsGemstones Vastu & feng shuikundali dasha & RemediesReligionMonthly HoroscopeOthersToday's ePaperLiveNewsAstrology NewsHoroscope NewsDaily Astrology News Alert, 9 October 2026: Libra Moon and Mercury-Saturn Tension Make It a Day for Serious TalksTrendingHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayHoroscope TodayAries Horoscope TodayDaily Career HoroscopeDaily Nadi HoroscopeTaurus Horoscope TodayGemini Horoscope TodayDaily Astrology News Alert, 9 October 2026: Libra Moon and Mercury-Saturn Tension Make It a Day for Serious TalksNeeraj Dhankher / TOI Astrology / Oct 09, 2026, 09:30 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint On Friday, October 9, 2026, with the Moon in Libra, the atmosphere favors fairness in tough conversations. However, Mercury's challenging alignment with Saturn emphasizes the necessity for clear communication. This tension may lead individuals to face pressure from multiple angles, forcing them to make tough decisions while remaining honest. As partners navigate discussions on roles and expectations, open dialogue becomes crucial to prevent misunderstandings in both personal and work relationships. Friday 9 October 2026 asks for straight answers. The Moon sits in Libra, the sign of fairness and balance. Mercury in Scorpio runs into tension with Saturn in Pisces. That mix is serious. Nobody is in the mood for jokes when a hard subject is on the table.Libra Moon: Fair Play, PleaseLibra hates a scene. Under this Moon, most of us want a pleasant tone and a quiet life. So we nod along even when we disagree. Today that habit may backfire, because a nod can be taken as a yes.Think of a friend who keeps asking for lifts. You say \"no problem\" every time and quietly seethe. Libra wants harmony, but real harmony needs honesty too.Mercury and Saturn: Say It Once, Say It ClearlyMercury speaks, and Saturn sets limits. When they clash, words come out slowly and carry weight. A chat may feel heavy. Silences may stretch a little too long. That is fine.Not a dramatic one. Just a plain sentence like \"I can't take that on this month.\" Saturn respects clear rules. And it rewards people who stick to them.Difficult decisions may land on your desk too. Sleep on anything that feels rushed. But don't delay forever, because Saturn dislikes dithering.Signs Under PressureLibra feels the Moon, so you may be pulled between two sides again. Choose one and explain why. Scorpio hosts Mercury, which sharpens your words, though Saturn urges you to keep them fair. Pisces holds Saturn and may feel worn down by duty. Aries, opposite Libra, could grow impatient with slow talks. Capricorn works well with this energy and may finally get a firm answer at work. Cancer might find a family request hard to refuse. Gemini and Aquarius should watch for mixed signals.Love, Career and MoneyLove: A partner may want to talk about time, space or chores. Listen first. Say what you need without dragging in old faults. Single people may spot a red flag early and choose to walk away. That is wisdom, not bad luck.Career: A boss or client may set a stricter rule. Ask questions until you know exactly what is expected. If you are overloaded, say so with numbers, such as hours or tasks. A calm email beats a grumpy sigh at your desk.Money: Loans, rent and shared bills need a fair split. Ask for the terms in writing. Don't agree to lend money if you would resent it later. A small no today saves a big row next month.Get the latest astrology news and horoscope updates. Download the TOI App.About the AuthorNeeraj DhankherAcharya Neeraj Dhankher is a Vedic Astrologer. He is the Founder and CEO of Astro Zindagi. He has been providing Astrological counsel to individuals, corporations, and businesses for more than 29 years. His mission is to empower people through astrological guidance.Read MoreEnd of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaHot PicksCBSE Date SheetGeorgia Medical Degree RowAparna JhaStock market crashH-1B ProgramRBI Repo Rate HikeNana PatekarWeather tomorrowUS Marines in JapanAmazon PrimeTop TrendingAssam Dancer MurderCristiano RonaldoJaime Chapman BoyfriendJerry JonesUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestSimone BilesCJP Chappal ProtestAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTrending StoriesIn AstrologyEntire WebsiteLove Horoscope Today: Daily love horoscope offers guidance; for all 12 zodiac signsAmavasya Daan On 10 October: Moon sign guide to what to donate; timings and tips9 October 2026 Forecast: Numerologist maps 12-month journey; focus on career, loveLucky Numbers And Colours: Libra Moon links with Number 2; forecast shares timingsMoon Enters Libra: Daily lucky numbers and colours shared; guide all 12 zodiac signsBhagyank Vs Namank: Birth date or name lead; guide to reading both togetherPisces Horoscope Today: Family joy, honest love and mindful work; carry water, be sensibleFacing Change With Steadiness: Krishna’s dhira stays calm; small anchors build balanceOctober 09, 2026 Horoscope: Zodiac guide to health and money; tips on travel and familyAquarius Horoscope: Stuck money returns for natives; pause before reacting todayNeighbors Challenge Estate Plans: Kelce’s Ohio fence fight widens; sewer, wildlife fears growBypoll Election Results 2026 Live Updates: Bypoll counting today for five assembly seats, one Lok Sabha seat in AssamKay Adams’ Reactions Go Viral: Clip eclipses Gronk’s Chargers take; fans ask what happenedHurricane Milton Relief Donations: Brady, Swift fuel $6.1m aid drive; app campaign matches giftsFaith Guides Wedding Plans: Gracie Hunt leans on Christian counseling; calls engagement a vulnerable timeBeauty Retail Tie-Up Deepens: Charlotte Tilbury renews Dallas Cowboys Cheerleaders deal; Amazon adds content-led shoppingCurry-Buffett Charity Auction: Lunch date pulls in $9 million; funds two family charitiesCpkc Stadium Expansion Funding: Kansas City Council clears $235 million; aims to attract World CupsBrady Raiders Stake Soars: NFL legend’s $220 million bet multiplies; franchise valuation hits $13 billionKansas City Giving Machines Launch: Kelce family fronts holiday donations; campaign backs 9 charitiesTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTired of too many ads?go ad free nowDaily PuzzlesConnectThe Perfect TracePlay Now Loop The LoopPuzzle CircuitPlay Now Daily CrosswordSpecial puzzlePlay Now Explore More PuzzlesExplore Every CornerAcross The GlobeTravis Kelce Ohio EstateBypoll Election Result 2026Pat CumminsTom Brady DonationDonna KelceTom Brady InvestmentPatrick 