a GandhiMad Sandhu MurderTN Dementia CasesWeather ForecastDelhi Airport Drone SeizedGulzar Singh CaseJaipur Live in MurderCJPJeff BezosJamie DimonChatGPT Teen RiskHubSpot LayoffsClaude Haiku 5.5Red Teaming AI AgentMark Zuckerberg BiohubBudget SmartPhone MarketAmazon LayoffsJeff Bezos MarsFired OpenAI ResearchersPopular CategoriesHeadlinesSports NewsBusiness NewsIndia NewsWorld NewsInternational SportsHealthBreaking NewsTravelAstrologyDeorhiTOI Young LeadersTOI Home Decor AwardsPower Creator Awards 2026The Times of India EcopreneurGold Rate TodayWeather TodaySilver Price TodayDelhi Weather TodayPetrol Price TodayDiesel Price TodayCNG Price TodayLPG Price TodayPetrol Price MumbaiFIFA 2026Is Bank Open TodayFIFA 2026 ScheduleIs Bank Open TomorrowPublic Holidays in JuneBank Holidays in JuneTrending Videos'You've Done No Wrong': Putin Openly Backs Iran In War Against U.S, Then Makes This Big Offer'GET OUT OF MY CITY': New York EXPLODES In Revolt Against ICE After Trump's Fed Agents Shoot ManEyewitness Video Captures Chaos After Ice Shooting In New YorkExplosions Rock Iraqi Kurdistan; Iran's Shahed-136 Drone Blitz Targets Kurdish Separatists In ErbilChina Russia Could Hit U.S_ Hegseth Faces Nuclear Reality On Cam; Iran War EXPOSES Trump _ Watch‘Trump's Reckless War!’: Hakeem Jeffries Blasts Hegseth Amid LA-San Diego 'Bombing' RowON CAM: Saudi Jet BLOWN TO PIECES! First Images Emerge After Houthis Strike Riyadh Airport‘Rusty, Battered & Broken!’: USS Lincoln ‘Scarred’ By Iran War; New Visuals Shock Families | WatchTrump Readies MASSIVE Iran Attack? Pentagon Prepares For Strikes On Nuclear SitesNidal Hasan Execution: Pete Hegseth Orders Ford Hood Shooter's 'Firing Squad' Live Streaming‘Why The Silence On Oct 7?’: Abdul El-Sayed Walks Away From Reporter; Then Rips TrumpA Very Merry Breakup Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​ : Elizabeth Guest and Timm Sharp Starrer A Very Merry Breakup Official Trailer​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​‘Not Playing This Game’: Marco Rubio Unloads On Reporter Over Kimberly Guilfoyle’s $100,000 BribeMusk's Team Trump Return? POTUS Resets Ties After Public Epstein Feud, Calls Him 'Modern Edison'Royal GP Murder Shock: Husband’s ‘Burglar’ Account Emerges After Hannah Cain Found DeadZohran Mamdani Grilled Over Oct. 7 Post; Reporter Asks: ‘Will You Apologize To Netanyahu?’‘Canada-EU Deal Done’: Mark Carney Slams Trump's Tariff Move In New PresserChrista HELPED Executioners? Pike Lawyers' SHOCKER Adds BIG Twist To Failed Execution MysteryLatest NewsHockey India polls set for Dec 5 amid growing row over state-unit electionsAsian Para Games send-off marred by protests over selection processGarba the traditional way: 500 college students revive the spirit of the festivalPsychology says children who ask you to repeat a silly word again and again may be enjoying more than the jokeWhen Bhadran wanted Nana Patekar to replace Mammootty in 'Iyer the Great'; why its Hindi remake never happened; the late actor was also considered for Mohanlal's 'Udayon'India show grit but Russia’s class shines through in 4-0 defeat8 IT firms including TCS, Infosys suspended from US green card programme: How does it hit Indian professionals?EXCLUSIVE | ‘In reality, it really isn’t’: Asian Games gold medallist Pranavi Urs on golf’s ‘rich man’s sport’ imageLove Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: What the stars say about your love lifeUS plans to livestream Fort Hood shooter’s death; first public execution in 90 yearsAt 6 ft 5 in and 6 ft 6 in, India’s racquet-sport giants face different battles'Unlikely to affect workforce strategy': TCS on Trump's green card suspension programme, plans 15,000 hiresWWE Money in the Bank 2026: A look back at 6 winners who cashed in MITB briefcase on the same-nightWatch: 2 Congress leaders slap each other during ‘vote theft’ protest in FaridabadBengaluru’s tech belt hits a roadblock: Deep potholes, dust and sewage turn daily commutes into a crawl‘I just like to be the first in everything’: Pranavi Urs sets sights on Olympic medalSuriya’s 'Scene' first single ‘Aaththi Iva Yaarraa’ out now, Nazriya steals the spotlight in Jithu Madhavan’s Diwali releaseLouis Pasteur transformed his family’s modest Arbois house into a private laboratory; the preserved home has just gained a new science exhibitionCopyright © 2026 Bennett, Coleman & Co. Ltd. All rights reserved. For reprint rights: Times Syndication ServiceFollow Us On","title":"Daily Astrology News Alert, 9 October 2026: Libra Moon and Mercury-Saturn Tension Make It a Day for Serious Talks","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/astrology/horoscope/daily-astrology-news-alert-9-october-2026-libra-moon-and-mercury-saturn-tension-make-it-a-day-for-serious-talks/articleshow/134785153.cms","id":592},{"content_hash":"c1ec4e34236cb8070e0ee8874cd06c0536172b0f416309ab2171f9dbfeccf674","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:57.972611+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/les-britanniques-continueraient-de-s-abstenir-sur-la-question-de-la-representation-chinoise_2072567_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives Les Britanniques continueraient de s'abstenir sur la question de la représentation chinoise Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"Les Britanniques continueraient de s'abstenir sur la question de la représentation chinoise","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/les-britanniques-continueraient-de-s-abstenir-sur-la-question-de-la-representation-chinoise_2072567_1819218.html","id":593},{"content_hash":"3ee9d1c39f18087726062dd1951cdadc41bf95a3f11f9014a1639f9ba77d2b15","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:57.898949+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/politics-a-peep-and-a-pull-20680","language":"","text":"Politics, a Peep and a Pull Published: November 24, 2013 8:48am EST Lauren Rosewarne, The University of Melbourne Author Lauren Rosewarne Senior Lecturer, The University of Melbourne Partners University of Melbourne provides funding as a founding partner of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.nagh9spqw https://theconversation.com/politics-a-peep-and-a-pull-20680 https://theconversation.com/politics-a-peep-and-a-pull-20680 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Source: Sydney Morning Herald (24 November, 2011) There are lots of ugly reasons behind discrimination. One of the less hideous and less predictable ones is hiring/coupling-up with/living near people just like us. As progressive as we might believe ourselves, most of our time is spent with people like us. Who share our values, our attitudes. Those who kinda even look like us. I, for example, truly don’t know a soul who’d have voted for Tony Abbott. Equally, most of those I associate with are oblivious to taboos; to the notion that there are things we’re just not supposed to talk about. This latter point perpetually intrigues me. For most of this year I’ve been writing a book about masturbation. The extent to which I talk about it - in turn, compelling friends and family to promptly shake off their squeamishness - might trick me into thinking that everybody is pretty much okay about it. Everybody however, is not okay about it. My book - it’ll be out next year - is about masturbation in the media. Hundreds of fictional examples paint a very clear picture of widespread cultural anxieties. Masturbation, apparently, is embarrassing and disgusting and getting caught doing it is the most hideous calamity imaginable. Sure, there are the odd sexy exceptions, but generally the act is portrayed as a revolting, icky shudder-fest. Paradoxically, of course. Because in real life most of us do it, revel in it, and scarcely do we feel much guilt. The screen’s story however, is quite different. As though this year’s Woolworths vibrator brouhaha wasn’t enough of a wank, now we have the Indonesian newspaper Rakyat Merdeka getting in on the demonising. Australian television equally so: reports over the weekend repeatedly described the image as depicting a “lewd” act. So in brief, we’ve got a cartoon Abbott, posed in his near-nudie glory, peeping on the Indonesians and self-pleasuring. Far from a celebration of masturbation, far from an acknowledgement that this is a normal act that nearly everybody enjoys (and feels scarcely any compunction about), here is the PM - like so many of the screen’s masturbating deviants and lowlifes before him - getting his loathsome rocks off to the “salacious” state secrets of our neighbour. Sure, it’s just a crude peeping tom metaphor. Sure, it’s just a cartoon. And sure, it’s just about one country shaming another. But it’s masturbation that is the tool. It’s masturbation that proves the sucker punch. It’s masturbation that enables Abbott to be portrayed as filthy and deprave. It’s about the spotlighting of the one act that - even well into the twenty-first century - we’re still encouraged to find gross, offensive and condemnable. Spying is portrayed as akin to peeping, phone-tapping to snow-dropping and espionage as the kind of porn that only the sleaziest scumbag could get off on. A clever image, a crass image, and one that can only work in a world where autoeroticism still chafes. Previous post Published: November 20, 2013 Mockery, Mimicry and Grandpas Gone Wild Next post Published: December 1, 2013 Like a lightbulb, babe","title":"Politics, a Peep and a Pull","url":"https://theconversation.com/politics-a-peep-and-a-pull-20680","id":594},{"content_hash":"d2e41ff959e5e66cb6c9504d5940078e47724856a51b6ea25c6eaf9394b95aa8","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:57.744771+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/oyo-osun-customs-intercepts/","language":"","text":"Oyo/Osun Customs Intercepts Arms, Explosives, Cannabis in N4.1bn Smuggling Bust By Prnigeria - September 20, 2026 Oyo/Osun Customs Intercepts Arms, Explosives, Cannabis in N4.1bn Smuggling Bust ccccccc The Acting Customs Area Controller of the Command, Deputy Comptroller Wale Adewole, disclosed this on Thursday, September 17, 2026, during a press briefing at the Command’s Operational Headquarters in Ibadan. Adewole said the seizures formed part of the Command’s sustained anti-smuggling operations aimed at protecting national security, public safety and the nation’s economy. Other security-sensitive items intercepted included 573 Nigeria Police Force crests and 92 yards of police camouflage uniform fabric, raising concerns over the potential use of such materials by criminal elements to impersonate law enforcement personnel. “We will continue to pay particular attention to items that have implications for national security and public safety, while ensuring that legitimate businesses and law-abiding members of the public are not unduly impeded,” Adewole said. Read Also: Zakari Mohammed: Kwara Must Rise to Full Potential as ADC Candidate Demands Public Debate WSCIJ Hails PRNigeria for Sustaining Young Communication Fellowship Beyond Grant Funding The People Who Made My PRNigeria Fellowship Memorable By Adegbite Anuoluwapo Rafat The Command also seized 2,012 bags of 50kg foreign parboiled rice, 550 bags of 25kg foreign parboiled rice, 2,440 wraps of Cannabis sativa, 1,150 used tyres, 17 used vehicles and 450 kegs of Premium Motor Spirit, equivalent to 5,000 litres. Additional seizures comprised 10 containers of corrosive mercury, 105 bales and 28 sacks of used clothing, as well as 48 packs of Tramadol. The Acting Controller stressed the importance of intelligence-driven enforcement and collaboration with sister security agencies in curbing smuggling and preventing the movement of illicit goods across the country. Meanwhile, the Oyo/Osun Area Command generated N65.66 billion in revenue between January and August 2026, representing a 13.88 per cent increase over the N57.66 billion collected during the corresponding period in 2025. Adewole attributed the revenue growth to improved compliance, enhanced stakeholder engagement and trade facilitation initiatives. He reaffirmed the Command’s commitment to sustaining the fight against smuggling, protecting national security and creating an enabling environment for legitimate trade. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSCannabisIntercepts Arms & ExplosivesN4.1bn Smuggling BustNigeria Customs Service Previous articleMatawalle Urges African Defence Chiefs to Unite Against Terrorism, Emerging Security Threats Prnigeria","title":"Oyo/Osun Customs Intercepts Arms, Explosives, Cannabis in N4.1bn Smuggling Bust","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/oyo-osun-customs-intercepts/","id":595},{"content_hash":"ea4aa44ff12f92fbdd912980d20e68ade47108b40a3174ef93d255f5800fc765","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:57.214709+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/what-to-do-after-receiving-inheritance.html","language":"","text":"Q&A: How can I make the most of my inheritance? Most heirs face many complex decisions — and potential tax consequences. These insights can help you avoid common mistakes and make more informed choices. July 15, 2026 Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog “Don’t wait until the decisions are urgent. Discuss in advance how the gift could help you pursue your goals.” — Jason Albano, managing director, wealth strategies executive with Bank of America Private Bank AN INHERITANCE CAN SEEM LIKE a mixed blessing, coming as it often does after the loss of a loved one. At such an emotional time, it can be difficult to think through what you need to do to manage the gift. But even if your parents decide to give you your inheritance or a portion of it while they’re still living — as parents increasingly prefer to do — you’ll likely have many questions, says Jason Albano, managing director, wealth strategies executive with Bank of America Private Bank. If you expect to receive an inheritance at some point in your life, your financial advisor can provide useful guidance, he notes. “Don’t wait until the decisions are urgent. Discuss in advance potential tax consequences with your advisor and personal tax professional and how the gift could help you pursue your goals,” Albano adds. You might also want to talk with your parents about their hopes for your financial future — even how they might be planning to structure their future gift to you. Read the insights below for a better sense of how to prepare for the many issues heirs can face. How do I begin a conversation with my parents about their estate plans? A: It’s not necessary to ask how much money your parents might leave you. “Start the conversation by asking about their values and what wealth means to them,” suggests Merrill Lynch Wealth Management Advisor Todd Silaika. That can lead to productive conversations about the role an inheritance could play in your financial future and what your parents’ expectations might be. This sort of conversation also provides you with the opportunity to share your preferences and priorities. For instance, if there’s a family business or even a family vacation home and you have no interest in owning either, be upfront about that. “When everyone understands the expectations, the outcomes tend to be better for everyone,” Silaika notes. I just found out I’m receiving an inheritance. How quickly do I need to make decisions? A: You’ll likely have some time before you receive the funds. Depending on the complexity of the estate, the probate process, if applicable, generally takes at least six months to a year. And that’s usually for the best, says Private Wealth Advisor Cheryl Smith. Too often, beneficiaries make large purchases or sweeping decisions they later regret. Even paying off debt right away may not be in your best interests, says Albano. “Does it make sense to pay off a mortgage at a rate below 3%? You might do better than a 3% return elsewhere,” he notes. Instead, consider setting up a meeting with your advisor to discuss your competing goals and how you could manage your newfound wealth to help you pursue them. Albano suggests looking at four buckets — spending needs, short-term goals, long-term goals and philanthropy. “How you allocate your inheritance to these buckets depends on your situation,” he says. If you have young children, a portion could go toward college costs in the long-term bucket. But if you’re looking at renovating your home or relocating, you’ll probably want to put those funds into a short-term bucket and make sure they’re easily available. What if my inheritance isn’t in cash? A: Most inheritances aren’t, says Albano. You might be named the beneficiary of a retirement account — or you could inherit the family home, for instance. If you’ve inherited a tax-deferred account like an IRA or a 401(k) account and you’re an eligible designated beneficiary1 or designated beneficiary, you may have as long as 10 years after the death of the original account owner to fully liquidate the account (depending on your age relative to the original account owner and certain other circumstances). The rules are complex, and distributions have tax implications. You should consult your tax professional regarding your specific situation. You’ll also be responsible for taking required minimum distributions (RMDs) as provided in the federal tax code. Assets in taxable accounts, by contrast, can be either liquidated or left in the account indefinitely. Again, you’ll want to meet with a tax professional to decide your best strategy. “Things can get more complicated when converting real assets, such as a family home or business, into cash, especially when multiple beneficiaries are involved,” notes Albano. If there are disputes over a will, it could take years for the issues to be resolved. In the best-case scenario, parents have worked with their advisor and estate attorney to construct a will or trust in such a way that its provisions account for the beneficiaries’ desires — for instance, which child wants to keep the family home or work in the family business. How could my inheritance affect my taxes? A: For tax purposes, an inheritance generally isn’t considered income, but there are some exceptions. Typically, the estate will pay any estate tax owed, with the beneficiaries receiving assets from the estate free of income taxes (see exception for retirement assets in the chart below). As a beneficiary, if you later sell or earn income from inherited assets, there may be income tax consequences. And if you inherit certain tax-deferred accounts like a traditional IRA or 401(k) account, you’ll pay taxes on your withdrawals, including RMDs, as ordinary income at whatever your tax rate is. Also, some states have an inheritance tax. Show text version Hide text version What’s my tax liability? It all depends on the kind of assets you inherit Cash and brokerage accounts assets other than cash are subject to income taxes if sold later for more than their value was at the time of your loved one’s death. Real estate and other tangible property assets are subject to income taxes if sold later for more than their value was at the time of your loved one’s death. Pre-tax and tax-deductible retirement accounts such as traditional IRAs and 401(k)s, you pay your federal ordinary income tax rate on withdrawals; in many cases, the account must be emptied within 10 years of the original account owner’s death. When it comes to taxable accounts and other assets like real estate, there’s the possibility you’ll owe no income tax because the cost basis of the asset gets stepped up (or down) to the current fair market value upon its owner’s death, thereby wiping out any taxable capital gains on appreciated assets, says Albano. Keep in mind that if you inherit an asset that provides income — say, a trust that pays out annually — it may put you in a higher marginal tax bracket. Could my inheritance require changes to my own estate plan? A: Whenever your net worth changes, or you have a significant life event, you should take the time to review your estate plan and make appropriate changes, says Albano. You may need to consider how to protect certain newly acquired assets — for instance, a family home or business that’s been passed down to you that you want someday to go to your children. “Ask yourself, if something happens to you, how will your assets flow to the people you care about?” he adds. A trust might be one way to accomplish that aim. Then talk to your advisor about how you might want to use your inheritance to further your own legacy — and help make your beneficiaries’ futures more secure. Where to start Start a conversation with parents about the role an inheritance could play in your financial future and what your parents’ expectations might beInstead of paying off any debt, consider meeting with your advisor to discuss how you could manage your newfound wealth with regard to spending needs, short-term goals, long-term goals and philanthropyConsult your tax professional if you are named the beneficiary of a retirement account or inherit the family home since most inheritances aren’t in cashAn inheritance could affect your tax liability based on the kind of assets you inheritReview your estate plan and talk to your advisor to make appropriate changes to help protect certain newly acquired assets Discover more with Merrill Work one-on-one with a Merrill advisor for more insights and personalized guidance. Connect with us today. Loading... Have us contact you Contact me Try Merrill Advisor Match Match with advisors Phone Loading... Email Loading... View your advisor ¹ An eligible designated beneficiary (EDB) is a surviving spouse, disabled or chronically ill individual, an individual who is not more than 10 years younger than the decedent, or a minor child of the account owner. Distribution rules differ based on the date of death, whether the beneficiary is an EDB and whether the original account owner died before or after their beginning date for required minimum distributions. Trust, fiduciary, and investment management services are provided by Bank of America Private Bank, a division of Bank of America, N.A. and its agents, Member FDIC, and a wholly owned subsidiary of Bank of America Corporation. The BullPen Timely insights to stay ahead of the curve September 9, 2026 Today's Markets Market briefs August 18, 2026 Financial Planning Planning for college: Investment accounts to help you pay for education July 21, 2026 Financial Planning Charitable giving unlocked for business owners under the OBBBA July 17, 2026 Financial Planning HSAs explained: The top benefits of health savings accounts July 15, 2026 Retirement Ways to replace your salary in retirement Related Articles 5 top reasons to turn to a financial advisor now 5 surprising ways to protect your family legacy — now and for generations 3 ways trusts can help — even if you’re not rich","title":"Q&A: How can I make the most of my inheritance?","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/what-to-do-after-receiving-inheritance.html","id":596},{"content_hash":"4c61a69695c0d936be86dc0512ab45f44f2730aefd67e69eb51c266a183589c4","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:56.747773+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-guillaumat-remplace-m-dautry-au-commissariat-a-l-energie-atomique_2073297_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Le conseil des ministres qui s'est réuni ce matin à l'Élysée a été essentiellement consacré à l'examen de la situation internationale, et notamment à la déclaration tripartie relative au contrôle des armements, qu'il a approuvé. Le texte de la déclaration ne sera rendu public que lorsqu'il aura été communiqué aux autres nations intéressées. Le conseil a également étudié l'attitude des représentants de ta France à la session de l'O.N.U. Les cérémonies du 11 novembre M. Emmanuel Temple, ministre des anciens combattants et victimes de la guerre, a fait connaître à ses collègues, qui l'ont approuvé, le programme des cérémonies du 11 novembre, auxquelles tous les membres du gouvernement assisteront, auprès du président de la République. L'augmentation des bourses de l'enseignement supérieur M. André Marie, ministre de l'éducation nationale, a fait une communication relative à l'augmentation nécessaire du nombre des bourses de l'enseignement supérieur et à la majoration de leur taux. Une prochaine réunion interministérielle fixera les crédits qui seront attribués au budget de l'éducation nationale à cet effet. Le relèvement serait de 50 % au minimum par rapport au taux actuel. L'accord n'est cependant pas définitivement réalisé entre les ministres de l'éducation nationale et du budget. Diminution de 1 franc par kilo de gros pain Sur rapport de M. Hugues, secrétaire d'Etat aux affaires économiques, le conseil des ministres a décidé de porter à 81 % le taux de blutage, afin de permettre ainsi une baisse de 1 franc par kilo de gros pain, qui aurait effet à partir du 1er décembre. Le taux de blutage, qui venait d'être relevé de 78 à 80, vient d'être porté à 81 car le taux de 80 ne permettait de diminuer le prix du pain que de 70 centimes. Les importations de blé envisagées seront plus ou moins importantes selon le résultat de la collecte. Les chiffres déclarés font apparaître un déficit, mais il est possible qu'il soit inférieur à la réalité. Il vous reste 26.98% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"M. GUILLAUMAT REMPLACE M. DAUTRY au commissariat à l'énergie atomique","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-guillaumat-remplace-m-dautry-au-commissariat-a-l-energie-atomique_2073297_1819218.html","id":597},{"content_hash":"5b61c5a19d41dfab439766d3b45aa47418924bc2b85416969143622d7d65bd80","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:56.698141+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/asias-uneasy-alliances-21380","language":"","text":"Asia’s uneasy alliances Published: December 11, 2013 3:39am EST Mark Beeson, Murdoch University Author Mark Beeson Professor of International Politics, Murdoch University Partners Murdoch University provides funding as a member of The Conversation AU. View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.hjq76d79d https://theconversation.com/asias-uneasy-alliances-21380 https://theconversation.com/asias-uneasy-alliances-21380 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article EPA China’s decision to declare a maritime air defence zone over disputed waters in the East China Sea in late November, together with the more recent response of Japan and South Korea, is both alarming and rather surprising. This is, after all, a region that has been essentially conflict-free for decades, despite containing some of the world’s most seemingly combustible flashpoints and deep-seated rivalries. The sudden deterioration in the security environment is shattering some of fundamental assumptions about the basis of regional security. To get a sense of how important and unexpected all this is, it is helpful to place in historical context. The institutional architecture that has kept the region remarkably stable for decades was established in the aftermath of World War Two. True, there were major wars in Vietnam and Korea, but within the non-communist camp, at least, peace prevailed. One of the key reasons the so-called “capitalist peace” endured was not because making money proved more attractive than making war, but rather because the grand bargain was actually imposed. Despite not being part of the region, the United States enjoyed an unprecedented dominance in East Asia and effectively determined the post-war regional order. Two aspects of the East Asian region at this time were crucial. First, although one tends to think of the Cold War as largely being about the containment of communism, it is important to remember that in East Asia it was also about containing Japan. The US had a particular interest in ensuring that Japanese militarism was eradicated. Japan’s so-called “peace constitution” and the subsequent focus on economic development ensured this happened. EPA/Hitoshi Maeshiro While China’s economic rise seizes the attention of the world these days, it is important to remember that it was Japan that pioneered Asian-style, state-led economic development. It was also Japan that become the cornerstone of the entire economic and strategic order that allowed the region to take-off in a way that astounded the world. The second critical aspect of the post-war order was the series of formal strategic alliances between the US and key regional states like Japan, the Philippines and Australia. Not only did these relationships underpin the security of the region, but they effectively marginalised those countries that were not part of the capitalist camp. China’s economic expansion really gathered pace after the Cold War ended, when economic integration became a truly regional business. These events are not merely historical curiosities. On the contrary, the unravelling of this long-standing regional order is at the centre of the current rising tensions. Not only are a number of countries still carrying a good deal of historical baggage from this period in the form of lingering animosities and unresolved territorial claims, but the very foundations of the old order are coming apart. Paradoxically enough, the creation of the conditions in which East Asia could prosper has also created formidable competitors for the US as first Japan and now China challenge and undermine its economic pre-eminence. The basis of American power and independence of action is being eroded as it becomes increasingly reliant on the East Asian region to underwrite its rapidly deteriorating fiscal position. The transformation of the region’s economic order is also having an impact on the security situation. The most visible manifestation of this possibility is China’s growing military capability and its more assertive foreign policy. While China may understandably have become the primary focus of attention in a region still nervous about the Middle Kingdom’s re-emergence as the most powerful economic and strategic force in East Asia, Japan remains a potentially pivotal actor, too. Despite the recent “rebalancing” toward East Asia, the US has long urged Japan to do more “burden sharing” when it comes to underwriting regional security. It may finally be getting its wish as politically born-again premier Shinzo Abe tries to make Japan a “normal” country. Yet urging Japan to abandon its rather admirable antipathy to war was arguably not wise at the best of times. At a time when the chances of accidental or intentional conflict with its old foe cannot be discounted, it looks foolhardy. The risk for the US and the rest of the region is that the very strategic architecture that seemed to provide certainty for so long may now become a source of great danger. On his recent trip to the region, US vice president Joe Biden was at pains to assure friend and potential foe alike that America would honour its security agreement with Japan and defend it in any conflict with China. It is not impossible that this pledge may be put to the test. If the US fails to act on Japan’s behalf its credibility as an alliance partner and security guarantor will be destroyed. EPA/Lintao Zhang We can only hope that such fears are groundless and fanciful. But as many observers have pointed out, much the same was said about European powers before World War One, when growing economic interdependence seemed to make war unthinkable. Tragically, however, a series of alliance relationships were triggered by the entirely random, unexpected assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand. The rest, as they say, is history. It is not impossible the world faces a similar dilemma. It is safe to assume that nobody in Beijing, Tokyo or Washington thinks that a major inter-state conflict is a good idea, much less ‘winnable’. And yet they risk being sucked into a European-style catastrophe by accident or miscalculation. Alliances are valuable as long as they deter risky or expansionist behaviour. When they fail to do so they may actually make conflicts significantly worse. The best hope for long-term stability in East Asia is one in which regional powers play a bigger part. Japan and China must not only settle their own differences, they must cooperate to underwrite regional security. The ASEAN Regional Forum provides a ready-made, albeit underutilised and under-performing potential vehicle in which joint leadership could be exercised. While this may look an unlikely prospect at present, history reminds us of the importance of making the effort. (An earlier version of this article appeared in the Straits Times) Previous post Published: December 2, 2013 Japan could be a force for stability Next post Published: December 12, 2013 The end of the national interest","title":"Asia’s uneasy alliances","url":"https://theconversation.com/asias-uneasy-alliances-21380","id":598},{"content_hash":"2881723acc8e6d34206ef6d20116e7cd76abd0b8ceadfc874e9ef98536afb5d7","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:56.097272+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-07/woolworths-increases-fuel-levy-surcharge/107238986","language":"","text":"Check-out pain fears as Woolworths increases freight fuel levy surchargeHBy Hanan DervisevicTopic:SupermarketsPosted Wed 7 Oct 2026 at 3:56pmWed 7 Oct 2026 at 3:56pmWed 7 Oct 2026 at 3:56pmWoolworths has increased its freight fuel levy again amid high diesel costs. (Supplied: Woolworths)abc.net.au/news/woolworths-increases-fuel-levy-surcharge/107238986Link copiedShareShare articleWoolworths has increased the fuel levy charged through its logistics network to food and grocery suppliers to almost triple its pre-Iran war level as diesel costs continue to climb.The fuel levy surcharges have increased for metro and regional areas, affecting some of the country's largest food and grocery manufacturers.This includes Primary Connect, Woolworths' logistics arm, which transports goods from suppliers to distribution centres and stores across the country.The business manages freight for more than 1,000 grocery producers, including brands such as Sanitarium, PepsiCo, Ingham's and Arnott's.The fuel levy for metro areas recently rose from 17.47 per cent to 19.88 per cent.In regional Australia, the levy increased from 44.20 per cent to 50.30 per cent.University of Sydney supply chain management professor Rico Merkert said the increases were not unusual when diesel prices rose sharply.\"Fuel levies are commonly used in freight markets to manage fuel-price volatility,\" Professor Merkert said.\"Many transport operators use surcharges that can be adjusted periodically rather than renegotiating contracts every time fuel costs move.\"If diesel costs rise sharply, it is economically reasonable for freight providers and retailers to increase fuel surcharges to recover those additional operating costs.\"Rico Merkert says transport costs are only one component of the final retail price at the supermarket. (Supplied)Prior to the Iran war, the fuel levy Woolworths charged in metro areas was 7.28 per cent.Within weeks of the war, it had risen to 12.47 per cent.The regional fuel levy has jumped by more than 172 per cent since March, when it was 18.44 per cent.\"We operate a large distribution network that includes independently owned and operated transport providers, and we are committed to supporting our transport partners, just as other industry players are doing,\" a Woolworths spokesperson said.\"To ensure they can keep their trucks on the road and get products on shelves in stores, we increased our review of the fuel levy to twice monthly in March to reflect the challenges they still face.\"Consumers likely to feel impactExperts believe the increase in freight costs are likely to be shared across the supply chain.\"My prediction is that consumers and suppliers will get the worst end of the deal of the fuel surcharge increase,\" said Medo Pournader, senior lecturer of supply chain management at the University of Melbourne.University of Sydney professor Ben Fahimnia, who is also a specialist on the issue, said continually pushing rising logistics costs back onto suppliers is not a sustainable solution.\"They have absorbed some of the costs initially and have tried to find savings elsewhere, but there is a limit to what businesses can absorb,\" Professor Fahimnia said.\"Someone ultimately has to pay, and, at these fuel levy rates, that will be Australian consumers at the check-out.\"While Professor Merkert agrees, he believes the effect is unlikely to be one-for-one.\"Higher fuel levies can eventually contribute to higher retail prices and hence inflation, but freight is only one element of the final shelf price, so consumers would not necessarily see a direct or proportional increase at the check-out,\" he said.Rising fuel prices have played a significant part in Australia's inflation rate, which recently jumped to 4 per cent in August.Transport was the second-largest contributor to the annual inflation figure as a result of higher automotive fuel prices.Inflation lower than expectedThe odds of the Reserve Bank lifting interest rates in November have fallen sharply after the latest consumer price index (CPI) came in lower than expected, with headline inflation up 4 per cent in the year to August.On a monthly basis, automotive fuel prices rose 14.8 per cent in August, compared with a rise of 7.5 per cent in July. In a press conference after last week's monetary policy decision, Reserve Bank governor Michele Bullock warned businesses were preparing to pass on higher fuel costs if necessary.\"The longer it [the war] lasts, if businesses were thinking, \"Oh, look, it'll come off, I won't pass on costs,\" the longer it goes, the more likely it is that businesses will just try to pass through the cost increases,\" Ms Bullock said.Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GooglePromotionTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerPopular nowHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicTopic:HomicideMcLaren driver clocked allegedly doing 259km/h on WA highway Topic:Traffic OffencesRelated topicsAustraliaConsumer GoodsPetrol PricesSupermarketsTransport IndustryTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Check-out pain fears as Woolworths increases freight fuel levy surcharge","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-07/woolworths-increases-fuel-levy-surcharge/107238986","id":599},{"content_hash":"502be15b8b1723f86a70c859364cd1b91ad4bf51e207dfe7b7f211726b532f28","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:55.675068+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/indexes/","language":"","text":"PostgreSQL specific model indexes¶ The following are PostgreSQL specific indexes available from the django.contrib.postgres.indexes module. BloomIndex¶ class BloomIndex(*expressions, length=None, columns=(), **options)[source]¶ Creates a bloom index. To use this index access you need to activate the bloom extension on PostgreSQL. You can install it using the BloomExtension migration operation. Provide an integer number of bits from 1 to 4096 to the length parameter to specify the length of each index entry. PostgreSQL’s default is 80. The columns argument takes a tuple or list of up to 32 values that are integer number of bits from 1 to 4095. BrinIndex¶ class BrinIndex(*expressions, autosummarize=None, pages_per_range=None, **options)[source]¶ Creates a BRIN index. Set the autosummarize parameter to True to enable automatic summarization to be performed by autovacuum. The pages_per_range argument takes a positive integer. BTreeIndex¶ class BTreeIndex(*expressions, fillfactor=None, deduplicate_items=None, **options)[source]¶ Creates a B-Tree index. Provide an integer value from 10 to 100 to the fillfactor parameter to tune how packed the index pages will be. PostgreSQL’s default is 90. Provide a boolean value to the deduplicate_items parameter to control whether deduplication is enabled. PostgreSQL enables deduplication by default. GinIndex¶ class GinIndex(*expressions, fastupdate=None, gin_pending_list_limit=None, **options)[source]¶ Creates a gin index. To use this index on data types not in the built-in operator classes, you need to activate the btree_gin extension on PostgreSQL. You can install it using the BtreeGinExtension migration operation. Set the fastupdate parameter to False to disable the GIN Fast Update Technique that’s enabled by default in PostgreSQL. Provide an integer number of kilobytes to the gin_pending_list_limit parameter to tune the maximum size of the GIN pending list which is used when fastupdate is enabled. GistIndex¶ class GistIndex(*expressions, buffering=None, fillfactor=None, **options)[source]¶ Creates a GiST index. These indexes are automatically created on spatial fields with spatial_index=True. They’re also useful on other types, such as HStoreField or the range fields. To use this index on data types not in the built-in gist operator classes, you need to activate the btree_gist extension on PostgreSQL. You can install it using the BtreeGistExtension migration operation. Set the buffering parameter to True or False to manually enable or disable buffering build of the index. Provide an integer value from 10 to 100 to the fillfactor parameter to tune how packed the index pages will be. PostgreSQL’s default is 90. HashIndex¶ class HashIndex(*expressions, fillfactor=None, **options)[source]¶ Creates a hash index. Provide an integer value from 10 to 100 to the fillfactor parameter to tune how packed the index pages will be. PostgreSQL’s default is 90. SpGistIndex¶ class SpGistIndex(*expressions, fillfactor=None, **options)[source]¶ Creates an SP-GiST index. Provide an integer value from 10 to 100 to the fillfactor parameter to tune how packed the index pages will be. PostgreSQL’s default is 90. OpClass() expressions¶ class OpClass(expression, name)[source]¶ An OpClass() expression represents the expression with a custom operator class that can be used to define functional indexes, functional unique constraints, or exclusion constraints. To use it, you need to add 'django.contrib.postgres' in your INSTALLED_APPS. Set the name parameter to the name of the operator class. For example: Index( OpClass(Lower(\"username\"), name=\"varchar_pattern_ops\"), name=\"lower_username_idx\", ) creates an index on Lower('username') using varchar_pattern_ops. UniqueConstraint( OpClass(Upper(\"description\"), name=\"text_pattern_ops\"), name=\"upper_description_unique\", ) creates a unique constraint on Upper('description') using text_pattern_ops. ExclusionConstraint( name=\"exclude_overlapping_ops\", expressions=[ (OpClass(\"circle\", name=\"circle_ops\"), RangeOperators.OVERLAPS), ], ) creates an exclusion constraint on circle using circle_ops.","title":"PostgreSQL specific model indexes¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/indexes/","id":600},{"content_hash":"275239f910ce145c8440546cab999b6675679b2e87d2662b363a3357f3a42388","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:55.603209+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-churchill-tend-la-main-a-l-opposition-pour-faire-face-a-la-menace-de-banqueroute_2073207_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Après que le lord-chancelier eut ouvert la session du nouveau Parlement en donnant lecture du discours du trône, le cabinet conservateur a abordé sa première bataille devant les Communes. Les lourdes tâches de ces jours-ci n'avaient nullement abattu M. Churchill, qui, tout en déployant une très grande vigueur, a su résister aux provocations comme aux railleries de ses adversaires. Vêtu d'une redingote d'un modèle que l'on n'avait pas vu à Westminster depuis l'époque de Gladstone, le premier ministre n'a pas craint d'annoncer des mesures impopulaires. Mais il a surtout tendu la main à l'opposition, car les sombres nuages de l'inflation constituent à ses yeux un péril extrêmement grave. En attendant l'exposé de M. Butler il a indiqué que le déficit de la balance des paiements atteindrait 500 à 600 millions de livres l'année prochaine, et que les pertes d'or et de devises pour la zone sterling dans son ensemble pourraient être encore plus importantes. Aussi M. Churchill estime-t-il que le Parlement et le peuple britanniques doivent manifester tout de suite leur ferme intention do remettre de l'ordre dans leurs finances afin de rétablir le crédit de la monnaie dans le reste du monde. Maximum de prudence L'exposé de la situation financière n'était pas l'unique mauvaise nouvelle apportée par M. Churchill. Les travaillistes se sont amusés de voir que le nouveau gouvernement répète aujourd'hui les plaidoyers de l'ancien ministre de l'alimentation. M. Churchill a en effet indiqué que la ration de viande est dangereusement compromise, et il n'a annoncé aucune amélioration dans l'avenir immédiat. De même pour le charbon les perspectives seraient extrêmement sombres. Le discours du trône et les explications supplémentaires de M. Churchill montrent avant tout que le nouveau gouvernement agira avec le maximum de prudence pour ne pas provoquer de chocs inutiles avec l'opposition et pour apaiser les inquiétudes éventuelles du monde ouvrier. L'industrie de l'acier sera bien dénationalisée, mais il est clair qu'on ne saurait revenir purement et simplement à l'ancien état de choses. En fait une grande partie des entreprises demeureront sans doute sous le contrôle de l'État. De même en ce qui concerne les transports routiers M. Churchill a fait des promesses beaucoup moins nettes qu'au cours de la campagne électorale. Les projets actuels prévoient simplement une plus grande liberté pour les camionneurs privés. Enfin et surtout le gouvernement conservateur renonce à rétablir immédiatement le vote universitaire, qui se serait heurté à une résistance acharnée de la part de l'opposition. Il vous reste 45.17% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"M. CHURCHILL tend la main à l'opposition pour faire face à la menace de banqueroute","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-churchill-tend-la-main-a-l-opposition-pour-faire-face-a-la-menace-de-banqueroute_2073207_1819218.html","id":601},{"content_hash":"76ece9860a80fbff20bee2c681902a348f56623c541933850a0cebf318a2f850","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:55.588324+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/topics/greater-toronto-area/","language":"","text":"Greater Toronto Area - The Globe and Mail HomeCanadaBusinessInvestingLifeOpinionWorldPoliticsPersonal FinanceCultureSportsMoreMoreLatest in MoreReal EstateDriveWatchlistFor YouEventsFind clarity in the chaos. Subscribe now and save 70%SUBSCRIBE FROM $0.99/WEEKDigital accessDigital + home deliveryGreater Toronto AreaLatest articlesJuly 31, 2024Father, son facing terror charges allegedly planning violent Toronto attack: RCMP1 min readJuly 17, 2024Toronto cleans up after storm as Trudeau calls for better infrastructure 2 min readApril 14, 2024Why is Montreal a stolen car export hub? Jurisdiction limits and size, officials say5 min readApril 5, 2024A winter camping road trip in a Toyota Prius Prime to see if it meets all of our needs5 min readApril 3, 2024Police, border services seize 598 stolen vehicles at Montreal port, most from Ontario3 min readApril 3, 2024Early spring storm brings heavy rain, high winds and snow to parts of Ontario1 min readApril 3, 2024Greater Toronto home sales down in March but competition pushing prices up, real estate board says3 min readMarch 29, 2024Program to improve health care for marginalized populations puts Black physicians in racialized communities 3 min readMarch 28, 2024Ontario minimum wage to increase Oct. 1 to $17.20 an hour1 min readMarch 28, 2024Calgary homeowners reap the rewards of a rising market4 min readMarch 27, 2024Toronto police recover 48 stolen vehicles amid ongoing rise in auto theft 2 min readMarch 22, 2024OPP, Montreal police nab 34 in Project Volcano, aimed at Ontario car theft suspects 2 min readMarch 22, 2024Snowfall warning issued for Greater Toronto Area, Hamilton, parts of eastern Ontario1 min readMarch 21, 2024Friend remembers Canadian killed in Mexico robbery2 min readAugust 5, 2021Interest in mortgage trust funds heats up along with the housing market5 min readApril 14, 2021the listingCondo construction hammered by rapid rise in costs5 min readMarch 1, 2021Police arrest 16 in Toronto-area vehicle fraud financing ring that scammed $2.85-million1 min readFebruary 9, 2021opinionToo many Canadians are going to work sick. Why? Because they’ve got no other choiceThe Editorial Board3 min readJuly 27, 2020New condo sales in GTA in second quarter drop to lowest level since Great Recession 3 min readJuly 1, 2020the next moveFirst-time buyers rush to Toronto market as new mortgage rules set to take effectCarolyn Ireland5 min readJune 29, 2020Doug Ford unveils task force for towing industry4 min readJune 16, 2020New Ontario development proposal would lock in years of sprawl, critics say 3 min readMay 27, 2020Local Ontario health officials urge regional approach to reopening4 min readMarch 13, 2020Volunteers help expand ride-service charity in GTA 2 min readPrev123NextTerms & ConditionsCommunity GuidelinesPrivacy PolicyDisclaimerSkip footer navigationSubscribeDigital + Home DeliveryDigital AccessGlobe2GoThe New York TimesGlobe Email NewslettersGift SubscriptionBusiness servicesAdvertise with UsGroup SubscriptionsGlobe CampusDataStoreGlobe Event CentreLeadership InstituteContact usAddress and Phone NumberStandards EditorStaffSecureDropSubmit an articleReader servicesAccount SettingsCustomer SupportSubscriptionsMember BenefitsPrivacy SettingsAbout usCompany InformationWork at The GlobeAccessibilityEditorial Code of ConductSustainabilityLicensing & PermissionsElection Advertising RegistryModern Slavery ReportAI GuidelinesSubscribeDigital + Home DeliveryDigital AccessGlobe2GoThe New York TimesGlobe Email NewslettersGift SubscriptionBusiness servicesAdvertise with UsGroup SubscriptionsGlobe CampusDataStoreGlobe Event CentreLeadership InstituteContact usAddress and Phone NumberStandards EditorStaffSecureDropSubmit an articleReader servicesAccount SettingsCustomer SupportSubscriptionsMember BenefitsPrivacy SettingsAbout usCompany InformationWork at The GlobeAccessibilityEditorial Code of ConductSustainabilityLicensing & PermissionsElection Advertising RegistryModern Slavery ReportAI GuidelinesJump to footer navigation© Copyright 2026 The Globe and Mail Inc. All rights reserved.351 King Street East, Suite 1600, Toronto, ON Canada, M5A 0N1Andrew Saunders, President and CEO","title":"Greater Toronto Area","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/topics/greater-toronto-area/","id":602},{"content_hash":"ddc8654ff4d4334cb4809c45d5b02812fd8106e7b8b6346f28bf99aab41e044f","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:55.502907+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/global-warming-no-sweat-for-heat-resistant-corals-11519","language":"","text":"Global warming no sweat for heat‑resistant corals Published: January 8, 2013 11:50pm EST https://theconversation.com/global-warming-no-sweat-for-heat-resistant-corals-11519 https://theconversation.com/global-warming-no-sweat-for-heat-resistant-corals-11519 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Corals have genetic resistance to warming oceans, research from Stanford University has found. The study provides hope for conserving coral reefs. Comparing heat-resistant and heat-sensitive corals, the researchers found under stress both corals switch on genes for damage control. However heat-resistant corals “front-load” these genes - switching them on even under normal conditions. The study proposes heat-resistant corals could be mapped and prioritised for conservation. Read more at Stanford University Source Stanford University Tags Climate change, Corals, Global warming","title":"Global warming no sweat for heat‑resistant corals","url":"https://theconversation.com/global-warming-no-sweat-for-heat-resistant-corals-11519","id":603},{"content_hash":"79bc088ff2bb9b6937a2e6dcf2283786c090c205a7ba589e54d3c28855994aef","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:55.443745+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-pkyc-perpetual-know-your-customer","language":"","text":"What is Perpetual KYC? All About Perpetual Customer Due DiligenceByPriscilla Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 22, 2023•5mins ReadUnderstanding Perpetual KYC (pKYC)What is pKYC?What are the Pillars of pKYC?What are the Benefits or Advantages Of pKYC?Final ThoughtsShare this postPerpetual KYC (more popularly referred to as pKYC) is a child of necessity that was birthed in response to one of the growing concerns that stem from the use of traditional Know Your Customer (KYC) processes. The key components of KYC are customer verification and authentication, customer due diligence and ongoing transaction monitoring. The advent of pKYC was necessitated by the lacuna discovered in conventional KYC. pKYC is therefore an evolution of traditional KYC risk monitoring. Just like KYC, pKYC exists to prevent money laundering and other financial crimes. Understanding Perpetual KYC (pKYC) As it becomes easier day by day to carry out financial transactions over the internet due to advancements in technology, fraudulent financial activities (such as money laundering and terrorist financing) have also been on the rise. This has made it quite necessary for businesses in the regulated industries (especially banks and other financial institutions) to preempt and prevent fraudulent customers who are constantly devising new means of speedily carrying out illegal financial activities without being caught. Aside from the risk of reputational damage to these financial institutions, they also have to abide by the requirement to keep up to date and be compliant with new laws and regulations which are constantly being churned out by their regulatory authorities. Failure to do so would result in fines and penalties which will undoubtedly result in a loss of business profit and revenue. In conventional KYC, risk monitoring is usually conducted at certain fixed periodic cycles or timeframes. Sometimes, between one risk assessment review and another, a lot of damage has been done through fraudulent financial activities. It is for this problem that the evolution from conventional KYC to pKYC became highly expedient. The fast pace of change in the regulatory compliance landscape makes it hard for compliance teams to keep using periodic automated monitoring. Read Also: Anti-Money Laundering (AML) Fines of 2021 - Key Takeaways & Insights to see instances where businesses in regulated industries have incurred financial losses in the form of fines due to noncompliance with regulatory requirements. What is pKYC? pKYC refers to the ongoing process through which financial organisations constantly gather and update customer information in real-time with the aim of using it for risk management. With perpetual KYC, businesses can foresee potential customer risks before they can cause any damage and either opt to cut ties or contain the impact depending on the situation. What are the Pillars of pKYC? One element that lies at the very heart of pKYC is fluctuating risk levels. The risk profile of a customer can take a radical shift at the twinkle of an eye, requiring the prompt attention of the Compliance team and some immediate preventive action. Between one scheduled periodic review of the customer risk profile and another, a customer that was considered a low risk at the point of onboarding could metamorphose into a high-risk customer due to some suspicious transactions or political exposure. Hence the need for perpetual KYC (pKYC). Some of the key components of perpetual KYC include: 1. Precise Customer Profile To effectively conduct pKYC, financial institutions require huge amounts of data. By handling such volumes of data and using them to provide structure and context to the KYC process, financial institutions can build and maintain precise customer profiles for all their customers. With such robust and precise customer profiles in place, it becomes easier to zoom in on and monitor any customer in real-time or near real-time when there is a prompt or trigger of suspicious financial activity. 2. Initiation of Prompt Reviews And Investigations Prompt risk assessment reviews and investigations are vital aspects of pKYC. Instead of the Compliance team spreading itself thin due to lots of work backlog during periodic risk assessments, pKYC enables the initiation of prompt risk investigations and reviews at any time whatsoever, not having to wait for the next cycle of review. This makes financial institutions fully armed and ready for compliance functions in a way that greatly limits risk and prevents fraudulent financial activities. 3. Updated Risk Assessment With pKYC, as soon as there is an event or transaction that triggers a risk assessment or investigation, a review is conducted immediately. This makes it possible for financial institutions to have updated risk assessments for customers as often as necessary, instead of the periodic risk assessments under traditional KYC which often provide obsolete results that end up being remedial rather than preventive. Recommended - What is Periodic KYC Refresh? What are the Benefits or Advantages Of pKYC? Some of the advantages of pKYC include a. It gives a holistic view of a customer's risk profile over time Due to continuous monitoring over the lifespan of the business relationship, a wider and more complete picture of each customer's risk profile is available at all times and not only when periodic risk assessments are conducted. This provides compliance teams with sufficient information to take swift actions and make emergency decisions where some fraudulent financial activities seem to be brewing. b. Prompt and timely assessments of customer risk Under the conventional KYC regime, the risk profiles of customers are assessed and determined first at the point of onboarding. The next time such an assessment might be conducted could be after 2 or 3 years. But with pKYC, risk assessment reviews for a customer are conducted as often as necessary, without waiting for the next scheduled period of risk assessment. c. Flexibility in operation PKYC allows for a shift from manual periodic risk assessment reviews to an event-triggered review option. d. Automatic review of data from multiple sources PKYC relies heavily on data from multiple sources such as government watchlists, sanctions and blacklists. With access to these data sources that are frequently updated, financial institutions can automatically review and analyse information about any customer at any given time. This automatic review makes it easier to assess and update risk profiles. Final Thoughts It has become clear that conventional KYC processes cannot keep up with the demands of fluctuating risk levels and the constantly evolving and speedy methods of financial crimes being deployed. It is therefore through pKYC and its preemptive features that regulatory compliance teams and business organisations can keep pace with the demands in the compliance landscape in our world of super-fast technological change and billions of online financial transactions. Join 100+ leading companies that use Youverify to conduct their KYC exercises and to perform compliance functions and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today.Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"What is Perpetual KYC? All About Perpetual Customer Due Diligence","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/what-is-pkyc-perpetual-know-your-customer","id":604},{"content_hash":"50ef00a158aca1dcd70c07e0ef4d30f767998aff48e9f0576c4a20fa74cf3786","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:54.939720+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-11/asic-hollard-insurance-home-storm-damage-claim-victoria/105166306","language":"","text":"Victorian couple forced to demolish storm-hit home after delays from insurer, ASIC allegesTopic:Consumer ProtectionPosted Fri 11 Apr 2025 at 2:37pmFri 11 Apr 2025 at 2:37pmFri 11 Apr 2025 at 2:37pm, updated Fri 11 Apr 2025 at 4:52pmFri 11 Apr 2025 at 4:52pmFri 11 Apr 2025 at 4:52pmASIC has taken legal action against Hollard Insurance over the company's handling of a claim related to storm damage. (Supplied: Danny Evans. file photo)In short:ASIC is suing Holland Insurance over delays in settling a claim over storm damage lodged by a regional Victorian couple.The corporate watchdog alleges Hollard relied on non-expert opinion when it rejected the claim.Hollard says it's co-operating with ASIC in the matter.abc.net.au/news/asic-hollard-insurance-home-storm-damage-claim-victoria/105166306Link copiedShareShare articleAustralia's corporate watchdog alleges one of the country's biggest insurers took nearly three-and-a-half years to resolve a claim over storm damage to a property in regional Victoria.The Australian Securities and Investment Commission (ASIC) is suing Hollard Insurance, accusing it of breaching its \"duty of utmost good faith\" in its handling of the claim.A regional Victorian couple lodged a claim two days after suffering major storm damage to their home.In late October 2021, major storms battered the state, resulting in thousands of calls for help to the State Emergency Service and leaving more than half a million properties without power.ASIC said Hollard — Australia's fifth largest general insurer — deliberated for 18 months before rejecting the claim in April 2023.Insurer caused 'prolonged harm' to couple, watchdog allegesThe watchdog claims Hollard relied on \"a non-expert opinion over prior expert reports\" on the cause of the damage to the property and offered the couple a small cash settlement, which was rejected.ASIC also alleged Hollard delayed decisions over repairs and took nine months to initiate an inspection by a structural engineer and delayed providing temporary accommodation to the couple.ASIC Deputy Chair Sarah Court says the couple's home is now uninhabitable. (ABC News: Billy Cooper)ASIC alleges the delays, poor communication and ignoring of expert advice \"exposed the couple to unnecessary and prolonged harm\" and breached Hollard's duty of good faith in handling the claim.\"In the end, because of the excessive delays by Hollard and the failure to undertake make-safe works, the home has become so riddled with moisture, mould and decay that it is uninhabitable and now needs to be demolished and rebuilt,\" ASIC deputy chair Sarah Court said.\"The egregious delay highlights ASIC's concern about the significant rise in general insurance complaints reported to AFCA since the 2022 floods, particularly those involving claim delays.\"The consequences of Hollard's actions have caused significant harm to the policyholders who remain without their own home three and a half years later.\"ASIC is seeking a civil penalty under the Insurance Contracts Act.A spokesperson for Hollard Insurance said the insurer acknowledged the Federal Court action.\"We support ASIC's work in driving positive outcomes for consumers and are cooperating with ASIC in this matter,\" the spokesperson said.\"As this is an ongoing legal matter, we are unable to provide further comment.\"Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerPopular nowHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicTopic:HomicideMcLaren driver clocked allegedly doing 259km/h on WA highway Topic:Traffic OffencesRelated topicsConsumer ProtectionInsuranceVICTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Victorian couple forced to demolish storm-hit home after delays from insurer, ASIC alleges","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-04-11/asic-hollard-insurance-home-storm-damage-claim-victoria/105166306","id":605},{"content_hash":"cbbed5af3171c993b1288c3cabbff9bdee83ad6a107e420f089f78ef0408633b","domain":"www.theguardian.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:54.792859+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/12/us-canada-border-tariffs-trade-war","language":"","text":"Tensions simmer on US-Canada border for two towns relying on each otherCanadians ‘angry’ at Trump for suggesting Canada should become 51st state, says mayor, as US businesses see sales fall amid trade warRachel Leingang in International Falls, MinnesotaSat 12 Sep 2026 06.00 EDTLast modified on Sun 13 Sep 2026 04.11 EDTSharePrefer the Guardian on GoogleLike many Canadians, Tom Reid isn’t crossing the US-Canada border as often these days. But he needed a haircut.The woman who cuts his hair lives in International Falls, Minnesota – her “sister is married to my wife’s cousin”, he explained – and so Reid, whose duty free shop in Fort Frances, Ontario, has to travel to her.That’s how it goes for the residents of the two towns, divided by the Rainy River and an international border. They’re longtime friends and, in many cases, family. People cross the border frequently to shop, dine, go bowling, take their kids to sports and visit each other.But Trump’s trade war against the US’s northern neighbor, and his derogatory comments about their country, has Canadians – known for their politeness – angry. Furious, actually. They’re shopping less at US businesses, and, in an unusual show of patriotism, encouraging consumers to “buy Canadian instead”.Reid is buying Canadian more and more, he said at the register in his shop, with the Canadian flag on the wall behind him. “I don’t have that much choice, eh?” he said.American business owners have seen declines in Canadian sales, while grappling with the uncertainty and costs associated with tariffs on their products. With tourist season winding down, and the leaves starting to change into yellows and oranges, the two towns are quieter.Canada’s retaliatory tariffs went into force this week, though no one who lived in the towns had seen that directly reflected in prices yet. It was the latest heated battle between the two countries in the last nearly two years.Canada’s retaliatory US tariffs take effect as trade dispute growsRead moreThe mayors of the two towns call each other friends, and signed a joint declaration in 2025 affirming that friendship after Trump retook the White House. The flags of Minnesota, Ontario, both towns, and both countries fly outside the town offices in Fort Frances. But there’s only so much – and really, nothing – that a local official can do to blunt the economic effects of slipshod tariffs put in place by one man, more than a thousand miles away in Washington.Hoping for the bestMost Canadians recognize this, according to Andrew Hallikas, the mayor of Fort Frances. They like Americans, and Fort Frances residents can relate to Minnesotans a lot: they share similar interests, in the outdoors and in community, he said. But they’re “irate” at Trump – for bullying their country, for going against an ally – and for suggesting Canada should become the 51st US state.“You cannot imagine how angry that makes us,” Hallikas said. “Please quote me on this. I cannot make this clear enough: we will never become the 51st state. We consider ourselves to probably be the best country in the world in which to live, and we’re quite content to remain that way, thank you very much.”For two towns that rely on each other, the tension of the trade war simmers underneath the surface. They all want it to end. They don’t know how, or when, it will.View image in fullscreen Photograph: Education Images/Universal Images Group/Getty ImagesAs the Guardian interviewed the mayor of International Falls at a coffee shop on the town’s main drag, a Fort Frances resident interjected angrily to tell the mayor that Canadians are “so pissed off” at Trump for how he’s attacked their country and that International Falls needed Canadians to survive. Their town was suffering, and their businesses losing longtime customers from Canada, because of it, he said.“I’m originally from the Falls, but I’m an American living in Canada,” the man said. “I’m scared to put that American flag on my shirt, what he’s done.”Residents, and the leaders of the towns, are worried about how they will rebuild the relationship between their two countries if this continues for much longer. Businesses will find other suppliers. Shoppers will replace their favorites with homegrown options. The sense of nationalism hardens.“We just hope for the best at this point,” said the International Falls mayor, Drake Dill.Grant Hauschild, a Democratic Minnesota state senator who represents 65% of the state’s border with Canada, said he’s heard from businesses that have lost up to 30% of their revenue from Canadians. With people already struggling with inflation and high costs, he said a hit like that goes from “a concern to devastation”.“My hope is, of course, that the relationship goes back to normal once we pass these chaotic times,” Hauschild said. “I think that’s what really scares me most … Even if people want to go back to the way things were, they might have unfortunately kind of moved on – and moved beyond – the relationship.”Two towns, one communityTo cross the Rainy River into either side, people use a toll bridge, with the cost for an average car now $10. They present documents to customs agents on whichever side they’re entering, and answer questions about their purpose in town. People who cross regularly recognize the agents and ask after their families.The main parts of each respective town sit on either side of the crossing, with both towns initially growing around paper mills. Fort Frances’ mill has since closed; International Falls’ is still running. Fort Frances is larger than International Falls, and because Canada is more sparse, Fort Frances serves as a regional hub. Its hospital will soon be getting an MRI machine – a huge boon for its aging population.The shops on either side carry gifts from both countries, catering to tourists who fly in to fish, mostly for walleye, and largely on the Canadian side. In a Fort Frances store, kids books called “Little Minnesota” and “Little Canada” sat right beside each other on a shelf. In International Falls, shelves of souvenir cups carried designs for International Falls and Canada.View image in fullscreenFort Frances, Ontario, on 8 August 2021. Photograph: Jacob Boomsma/Getty Images/iStockphotoWhile Canadian flags line the shop windows and fly throughout downtown Fort Frances, Hallikas said this is not a reflection of increased patriotism, but just a border town welcoming visitors to their country. The two towns are “neighbors by geography, but we are friends by choice”, he said.“The bridge is over 100 years old, and we’ve been going back and forth it for over 100 years,” Hallikas said. “Before that, there was a ferry. But our friendship is still going to exist when Trump is long gone.”Dill, the International Falls mayor, owned a business and lived in Canada for 11 years. Many of his friends are Canadian. This fall, he will go up to an island in Canada to meet up with friends.“There’s no politics in that. There’s no thought of exchange rate, or trade war, or tariffs. It’s just – we’re friends,” he said. “But then you come home and you talk and you watch the news. … I would be really disappointed if it gets to a point where we can’t work through it, and there’s a fundamental change that will hurt International Falls significantly.”Residents on both sides have favorite products they grab whenever they cross. For Heather Johnson, executive director of the Fort Frances chamber of commerce, it’s Top the Tater, an iconic Minnesota chive and onion dip. She also checks prices on cheeses, and peruses the wider variety of pastas for sale in US stores. Johnson bowls weekly in a league in International Falls, made up of both Americans and Canadians.Leanne Guba, a Canadian who owns Hometown Hobby and Crafts in International Falls, crosses back into her home country once or twice a month, usually walking across the bridge, to buy ketchup chips, chocolate bars and Dad’s brand oatmeal chocolate chip cookies.American liquor is no longer on the shelves at government-run Ontario liquor stores, by order of Premier Doug Ford. The push to buy Canadian is “pretty well elbows-up”, Johnson said. “I’m drinking Canadian red wine now.”‘Mr T took care of that’Reid, the duty-free shop owner, said the tourist season was about average for him, with no noticeable decline in sales. He has seen some change in his customers.A couple of years ago, they’d come in with the signature red Maga (Make America Great Again) hats. Last year, not as many. This year, he didn’t see any red hats, and customers would tell him they didn’t vote for Trump. They’ll ask about the missing American bourbon from his shelves, and he’ll tell them, “Mr T took care of that”.View image in fullscreenThe Canadian prime minister, Mark Carney, and Donald Trump at the White House on 7 October 2025. Photograph: Adrian Wyld/The Canadian Press/APHe used to cross the border to go to a show, or the grocery store, or Menard’s, a hardware store, but not anymore. His daughter and her husband used to cross weekly for a date night and shopping, but no longer. People over here are “really upset” with Trump, he said. It has renewed his sense of Canadian pride, but “I’ve always had good pride”.Johnson, whose kids are now grown, said: “I’m glad that my kids grew up knowing both countries, and they know that there really isn’t a difference in people.”Across the border in International Falls, craft store owner Guba said she was seeing fewer Canadians, for several reasons. They’re dealing with rising costs, too, and the exchange rate is unfavorable. Some of it is politics, she said. Canadian regulars are coming less frequently.“They’re definitely being a little more picky about when they come over or being more careful about what they buy,” she said.The decline, while dealing with unpredictable tariffs on goods she brings in from overseas, has had an impact on her business. She’s adapted by seeking out new suppliers, bringing in new merchandise lines, and trying to reduce costs. She has a few companies she orders from in Canada – including a toy brand and a puzzle company – and expects the new tariffs will likely increase costs on those products.People are upset about the political dynamics, but they don’t tend to vent their political frustrations openly, she said. There may be the odd joke here and there, but not usually full-blown political debate. “We tend to try to stay friendly,” she said.The latest tourist season was “decent”, she said. “Overall, we’re still surviving … You can’t really control it.”‘We remain neutral’It’s tough to gauge the full impact of declining Canadian spending in the border town, though anecdotally, people are noticing it. The International Falls chamber of commerce would not comment on the impact of the tariffs on the town, or the deteriorating relationship between the two countries.“We remain neutral on political matters so that we can best serve our diverse membership, which includes individuals and organizations with a wide range of views. Because of that, the Chamber does not have an official comment on the political issues surrounding the US-Canada relationship,” the chamber said in an email.Isaac Johnson, who owns the Coffee Landing café in International Falls and lives above it with his wife, said he still sees a lot of Canadians who come over to grab espresso and breakfast. He loves his Canadian customers, including the couple he watched meet up at the coffee shop from across the border. They later married.View image in fullscreenRainy River in International Falls Minnesota with a view across the river to Ontario Canada on 28 July 2018 Photograph: Don & Melinda Crawford/UIG/Getty Images/Collection Mix: Subjects RFThe locals are caught in the middle of political games, said Johnson, who tries to just focus on himself, his family, and his faith. But people do get emotional about it at times, and “if you definitely bring up politics, for sure,” he said – just as the man who confronted Dill, the mayor, did.The man, who didn’t share his name or want to have a longer conversation with a reporter, said he’s noticed far fewer Canadians around town, nothing like it used to be. He described Canadians as “very pissed off” and said snowbirds were selling their houses in the US. International Falls voters went for Trump in 2024.These broader issues, like the trade war, “pit us against each other”, Johnson said, noting that the man got mad at the mayor “over a guy that’s making billions off us all”.“I love the Canadians, I love Americans, and I love them coming over here and enjoying what we have here, and we can go and enjoy what they have,” Johnson said.Dill, the town’s mayor, said Canadians are “definitely” not coming to International Falls as much as they were, though he thinks it has more to do with the exchange rate. Still, he said later, “we need all the Canadian commerce we can get, and I would like to think they feel the same way”.Local officials are in a tough position. They have no control over the tariffs, though with their communities at the center of the impact, they are expected to answer questions and concerns over levies. They have no direct line to the Trump administration, if that would even make a difference. And their communities are suffering.Hope springs eternalHauschild, the Minnesota state senator, said he meets regularly with the Canadian consulate in St Paul, and reiterates his belief in a strong relationship with Canada. But as a state lawmaker, he has little to no control over any of it, beyond using his bully pulpit.Hallikas, the Fort Frances mayor, said most Canadians have “no rancor for individual Americans” and treat them kindly when they visit, largely avoiding talking about Trump or American politics. Americans will have to work to undo the damage Trump has done to the relationship between the two countries, and that will take time, even after the trade war simmers down or ends, he said.“But we’ll still be friends with the folks in Minnesota and International Falls,” Hallikas said.Dill said he doesn’t often run into the frustrations directed at him by the man in the coffee shop, though it happens. He doesn’t have any way he can directly fix the issue.“The US elected our current president and he has his own agenda and I don’t see that I can stop it,” he said. “I don’t want it to affect us locally. We’ll see. Hope springs eternal. I mean, I thought that everything was going along fine, but apparently it wasn’t.”The uncertainty of it all is “frustrating”, and he’s most curious about where it all lands, when a truce could be signed so that businesses can have a sense of comfort in planning for their futures. The two countries are engaged in a heated dispute – or a “donnybrook,” as they would say in Canada, noted Dill.He likened the current situation to two longtime best friends, who hunted and fished and vacationed together for years – until one of them said, you’ve been taking advantage of me for years.“That’s like driving a nail in a fence,” he said, “and then you pull it out, and even if the fence still stands, there’s a hole where the nail was.”Explore more on these topicsTrump tariffsDonald TrumpTariffsInternational tradeCanadaAmericasGlobal economyfeaturesShareReuse this content","title":"Tensions simmer on US-Canada border for two towns relying on each other","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/12/us-canada-border-tariffs-trade-war","id":606},{"content_hash":"97d4842cef100feef948d1e70778adaf879b015f00a5948c63de79f77b66b8c3","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:54.550850+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/etimes/trending/fssai-has-a-warning-about-those-tiny-fruit-stickers-heres-what-you-should-do-before-eating-fruits/articleshow/134787914.cms","language":"","text":"FSSAI has a warning about those tiny fruit stickers: Here’s what you should do before eating fruits - The Times of IndiaSign InTOITOIhomelifestylerelationshiptrendingHealth+styleparentingsoul searchbeautybooksrecipesfood newstravelhome & gardeneventsspotlightNewsetimeslifestyletrendingFSSAI has a warning about those tiny fruit stickers: Here’s what you should do before eating fruitsTrendingBaby NamesChildren PsychologySamantha Ruth PrabhuShammi NarangMakeup TipsVietnam Cambodia TourKarl DeisserothRam VilasOscar Wilde QuoteSharad Navratri 2026Baby NamesChildren PsychologySamantha Ruth PrabhuShammi NarangMakeup TipsVietnam Cambodia TourKarl DeisserothRam VilasOscar Wilde QuoteSharad Navratri 2026Baby NamesChildren PsychologySamantha Ruth PrabhuShammi NarangMakeup TipsVietnam Cambodia TourKarl DeisserothRam VilasOscar Wilde QuoteSharad Navratri 2026FSSAI has a warning about those tiny fruit stickers: Here’s what you should do before eating fruitsTrending Desk / etimes.in / Oct 08, 2026, 16:26 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint You pick up an apple, peel off the little sticker, give it a good wash and take a bite. Seems perfectly fine, right?Well, there’s one extra step India’s food safety regulator wants you to follow.The Food Safety and Standards Authority of India (FSSAI) has advised consumers not to simply remove stickers from fruits and vegetables and wash the produce. Instead, it says the skin or the portion where the sticker was placed should be peeled or cut away before eating.The reason? The adhesive used to stick those tiny labels to the produce can leave residue behind.So, what should you actually do?It's pretty simple.If there's a sticker on your apple, orange, avocado or any other fruit, remove it first. Then peel the skin or cut away the small patch where the sticker was attached.You don't need to throw away the fruit. And there's no need to panic if you've already eaten a stickered fruit after simply washing it.The advice is essentially about taking an extra precaution and avoiding direct consumption of any residue that may be left behind.Why are fruits covered in stickers anyway?Those little labels are something most of us barely notice, especially when doing our weekly grocery shopping.But they're there for a reason. Fruit stickers can carry codes used by retailers to identify the produce and its price. Depending on the product and retailer, they may also provide other information about the fruit.What they aren't, however, is edible packaging.So even if the sticker comes off easily, it's better not to assume that the surface underneath is completely free of adhesive.Is washing the fruit not enough?Washing your fruits and vegetables is still important. But in the case of produce that has had a sticker stuck directly onto its skin, FSSAI's advice is to go one step further.Remove the sticker and then peel or cut away the area where it was stuck.For something like an apple, that's easy enough. If you're eating the fruit without peeling it, simply cutting off that small patch is another option.Why the sticker maybe harmful?Don't panic.If you've ever discovered halfway through eating an apple that the tiny sticker has somehow vanished, you're certainly not aloneDo you need to throw the fruit away?No.A sticker doesn't mean the entire fruit is unsafe. Once you've removed the label, simply peel or cut off the area where it was attached.And yes, you should still wash the fruit properly before eating it and that's because the glue in the sticker maybe harmful for your health.The next time you're about to bite into that supermarket apple, take a second look at the sticker. Peel it off, remove the small stickered patch and then eat the fruit.It's a tiny extra step. But when it comes to something you eat, it doesn't hurt to be a little careful.Get the latest movie news, reviews, and celebrity updates. Download the TOI App.End of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaTop TrendingUtah Jazz InjuryTravis Kelce Garage BeerOdell Beckham JrUPI MDR ChargesUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestCBSE DatesheetNana Patekar DeathAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTrending StoriesIn SectionEntire WebsiteCookie Recipe Funds Lunches: Donna Kelce shares family favorite on mugs; fundraiser clears lunch debtDHS proposes major OPT fee for foreign students, citing US worker protection and visa fraudAparna Jha and team win national lunar habitat challenge with an AI-powered concept for future astronautsCJP founder Abhijeet Dipke accuses PM Modi of 'murdering democracy, vote theft'Palm Beach estate bought for $9M sells for $25.4MPrince William’s foundation funds Cornwall homes for 16 people facing homelessnessDr. Phil lists Beverly Hills mansion bought for $29.8M in 2010 for $67.5MNebraska farmer leases land for wind turbine, earns up to $12,500 a year against $600 f