& page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"Education Class | Email template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/education-class","id":608},{"content_hash":"fa64aa384b2ad691d4c94d288a53927cc576d8ea37c3021badb5d6b944a60ef2","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:54.375599+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/londres-accueille-avec-sympathie-la-suggestion-du-president-de-la-republique_2072109_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Londres, 7 novembre. - L'impression prévaut ici que le discours de M. Vincent Auriol a été inspiré par le désir bien connu de M. Churchiil, qui voudrait ajouter à tous ses titres de gloire celui de \" faiseur de paix \". Encore ne faudrait-il pas oublier qu'en défendant l'idée d'un tête-à-tête avec Staline le premier ministre britannique a toujours adopté une position un peu \" en flèche \" par rapport à son propre parti. Hier même il a cru nécessaire de souligner que les vues de M. Eden sont identiques aux siennes. Mais cette assurance un peu trop générale n'a pas dissipé toutes les rumeurs suivant lesquelles le Foreign Secretary éprouverait un peu moins d'enthousiasme pour la \" diplomatie personnelle \". Dans tous les cas il resterait à persuader M. Acheson et le président Truman lui-même. L'expérience est d'ailleurs là pour modérer les ambitions. L'affaire de Berlin par exemple a démontré aux Britanniques qu'il ne suffit pas de \" bavarder avec l'oncle Joe \" pour aboutir à un résultat satisfaisant et précis. Aussi le but d'une éventuelle rencontre serait-il de créer \" une nouvelle atmosphère et un climat de camaraderie humaine entre l'Est et l'Ouest \". Bien qu'hier aux Communes il n'ait pas donné de précisions. M. Churchill songerait surtout, semble-t-il, à dissiper la méfiance ainsi que les illusions des chefs du Kremlin. Beaucoup d'Anglais sont persuadés que les craintes manifestées par les Soviets sont souvent sincères, et qu'une conversation \" d'homme à homme \" permettrait peut-être de faire comprendre à Moscou que les nations atlantiques n'ont pas de buts agressifs, qu'elles ne sont pas non plus menacées d'un effondrement économique, et qu'enfin le refus des Russes de coopérer à la construction de la paix aurait pour inévitable résultat de renforcer encore la coalition des pays démocratiques. Staline accepterait-il une invitation ? Les Anglais savent évidemment que le chef soviétique n'a jamais jusqu'ici envisagé un voyage hors de son pays. S'il changeait d'avis, ce geste constituerait déjà un revirement significatif. En ce sens on croit donc, comme M. Auriol, que le monde accueillerait une rencontre des Grands avec allégresse. Le Manchester Guardian va jusqu'à écrire aujourd'hui que les Parisiens recevraient le leader soviétique avec une chaleur au moins égale à celle dont le général de Gaulle avait bénéficié le jour de la libération. Il vous reste 16.77% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"Londres accueille avec sympathie la suggestion du président de la République","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/londres-accueille-avec-sympathie-la-suggestion-du-president-de-la-republique_2072109_1819218.html","id":609},{"content_hash":"7b348279de463ad916ff31a86d553667655ce8170b7be75f25a0ecee67d25214","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:54.299908+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/global-warming-driving-up-monthly-heat-records-11610","language":"","text":"Global warming driving up monthly heat records Published: January 14, 2013 9:15pm EST https://theconversation.com/global-warming-driving-up-monthly-heat-records-11610 https://theconversation.com/global-warming-driving-up-monthly-heat-records-11610 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Monthly temperature extremes have become much more frequent, measurements from around the world indicate. On average, there are now five times as many record-breaking hot months worldwide than could be expected without long-term global warming, shows a study now published in Climatic Change. In parts of Europe, Africa and southern Asia the number of monthly records has increased even by a factor of ten. 80% of observed monthly records would not have occurred without human influence on climate, concludes the authors-team of the Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research (PIK) and the Complutense University of Madrid. If global warming continues, the study projects that the number of new monthly records will be 12 times as high in 30 years as it would be without climate change. Read more at Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research Source Potsdam Institute for Climate Impact Research Tags Climate change, Heat, Global warming, Heat wave","title":"Global warming driving up monthly heat records","url":"https://theconversation.com/global-warming-driving-up-monthly-heat-records-11610","id":610},{"content_hash":"d68f8aaaf6e0aa1d54bc161f5d1f2405a85383abdd2f8e04de3d21c82edb5da9","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:53.685295+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/fct-police-arrest-major-raid/","language":"","text":"FCT Police Arrest 300 in Major Raid on Crime Flashpoints, Recover Knives, Toy Gun, Cannabis By Prnigeria - September 20, 2026 FCT Police Arrests 300 Criminals FCT Police Arrest 300 in Major Raid on Crime Flashpoints, Recover Knives, Toy Gun, Cannabis The Federal Capital Territory (FCT) Police Command has arrested approximately 300 persons during a coordinated raid on identified crime flashpoints, shanties and uncompleted buildings across the Territory. The operation, conducted on Saturday, September 19, 2026, also led to the recovery of knives, a toy gun, cannabis and other illicit drugs from suspected criminal hideouts. The Command’s Police Public Relations Officer, SP Josephine Adeh, disclosed this in a statement issued on Saturday, adding that the operation was part of ongoing efforts to prevent crime, disrupt criminal activities and enhance the safety and security of residents. According to her, the arrested persons are undergoing thorough screening and profiling to establish their identities and determine possible involvement in criminal activities. She said those found to have committed offences would be charged to court in accordance with the law, while persons with no case to answer would be released after due screening. Read Also: Zakari Mohammed: Kwara Must Rise to Full Potential as ADC Candidate Demands Public Debate WSCIJ Hails PRNigeria for Sustaining Young Communication Fellowship Beyond Grant Funding The People Who Made My PRNigeria Fellowship Memorable By Adegbite Anuoluwapo Rafat The Commissioner of Police, FCT Command, CP Ahmed Muhammed Sanusi, reiterated the Command’s commitment to sustained intelligence-led raids and proactive policing operations, particularly at locations identified as potential hideouts for criminals. Sanusi also cautioned youths and other residents against the use and abuse of illicit drugs, warning that substance abuse could impair judgment, fuel violent and criminal behaviour, and expose individuals and communities to serious security risks. He urged persons struggling with drug use to seek appropriate counselling and rehabilitation support rather than allow substance abuse to lead them into criminal activities. The commissioner further directed police officers participating in such operations to maintain professionalism and operate strictly within the law. He warned officers against using raids and arrests as avenues for extortion, intimidation, harassment or unlawful demands for money, stressing that personnel found engaging in such misconduct would face appropriate disciplinary action. The Command called on residents to continue providing credible and timely information on suspicious activities and criminal hideouts within their communities. Members of the public requiring emergency assistance or wishing to provide security-related information can contact the FCT Police Command on 08032003913. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSArrest 300 in Major RaidCannabisFCT Police CommandRecover KnivesToy Gun Previous articleOyo/Osun Customs Intercepts Arms, Explosives, Cannabis in N4.1bn Smuggling Bust Prnigeria","title":"FCT Police Arrest 300 in Major Raid on Crime Flashpoints, Recover Knives, Toy Gun, Cannabis","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/20/fct-police-arrest-major-raid/","id":611},{"content_hash":"0f322bd96eab01fdba895cd8bd6b0637a1fde75ffb99aeae085ad769cd0614aa","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:53.448802+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/financing-a-home-renovation.html","language":"","text":"Financing a home renovation Making a house your home can be exciting but may also mean taking on potentially costly renovations. The good news is that there are a range of funding options that can help finance a home renovation, from savings to flexible borrowing solutions. While using cash on hand, or even selling investments or other assets, may be a good way to meet the cost of some important expenses, those may not always be the best solution for your situation. Consider borrowing against your eligible investments to fund major expenses such as a luxury purchase or a down payment on a commercial real estate property. Prudent use of credit can help enhance and further strengthen your balance sheet. Loan options to consider Borrowing to cover renovation expenses can help you preserve cash and avoid disrupting your financial strategies. There are multiple ways to borrow, two potential choices include a Home Equity Lines of Credit (HELOC) and Loan Management Accounts® (LMA® account), a demand line of credit from Bank of America, N.A. Both offer purchasing flexibility, up to your available credit limit, and have generally lower rates than unsecured borrowing options. Your Merrill Advisor can discuss the financing solutions that may be available to you, including these two types of loans, to determine which one may be a good fit for your cash flow needs, time horizon, risk tolerance and other important factors. Bank of America® Home Equity Line of Credit (HELOC) Bank of America® Loan Management Account® (LMA® account) Allows you to borrow against the available equity in your home, using the home as collateral.Provides a revolving credit line with on-demand access to cash, up to your available credit limit.Special rate discounts available.No application fees, no closing costs (on lines of credit up to $1,000,000) and no annual fees.Access available credit through Bank of America Online Banking, by phone or check, or by visiting a financial center.The interest you pay may be tax deductible.2 Allows you to borrow against accounts of eligible investments, using the assets as collateral.1Access credit through variable or fixed-rate advances and standby letters of credit.Flexible terms and repayment options, subject to required collateral maintenance requirements.No application or annual fees.Access funds through no-access-fee checks, free Fedwire or checking account transfers.Funds can generally be accessed within one day of approval. Learn more Your Merrill Advisor can help evaluate your home renovation financing options and choose the best fit. Note that securities-based financing involves special risks and is not for everyone. Risks of Borrowing LMA Securities-based financing involves special risks. You should review the LMA Loan Agreement and related documents and disclosures carefully and consult with your own independent tax and legal advisors before borrowing. A decline in the value of your collateral assets may require you to provide additional funds or securities to avoid a collateral maintenance call. You can lose more funds than are held in the collateral account. The LMA account is a full-recourse loan and you will be liable for any deficiency.The Bank can force the sale or other liquidation of any securities or other investment property in the collateral account and, unless otherwise required by law, can do so without first contacting you.You are not entitled to choose which securities in the collateral account are liquidated or sold.The Bank can change its collateral maintenance requirement at any time without notice to you.You are not entitled to an extension of time to satisfy the Bank’s collateral maintenance requirement.There may be adverse tax or other consequences to you if securities are sold or otherwise liquidated by the Bank.The LMA account is an uncommitted facility, although loans to individuals and trusts may be committed in an amount not to exceed $100,000. The Bank may demand full or partial repayment at any time and any commitment may be immediately terminated.For fixed-rate advances and term loans, principal payments made in advance of the end of the applicable fixed-rate period, whether voluntarily or involuntarily, (due to demand or liquidation by the Bank,) may be subject to a substantial breakage fee as determined by the Bank.Some restrictions on the use of LMA account proceeds may apply under the terms of the loan documents and applicable laws and regulations. HELOC As a variable-rate loan, interest rates and payments can change based upon The Wall Street Journal Prime Rate (“Prime”). Prime may change at any time and is subject to change without notice.If you select the interest-only option during the initial draw period, at the end of this period, you will still owe the original amount borrowed, and the monthly payment will increase significantly and may result in “payment shock” – even if interest rates stay the same.A HELOC takes a security interest in your home, so a default on the HELOC could result in the loss of your home.HELOC funds may not be used to purchase, carry or trade securities or repay debt incurred to purchase, carry or trade securities. 1 The Loan Management Account (LMA account) is a demand line of credit provided by Bank of America, N.A., Member FDIC. Equal Opportunity Lender. The LMA account requires a brokerage account at Merrill Lynch, Pierce, Fenner & Smith Incorporated and sufficient eligible collateral to support a minimum credit facility size of $100,000. All securities are subject to credit approval and Bank of America, N.A. may change its collateral maintenance requirements at any time. Securities-based financing involves special risks and is not for everyone. When considering a securities-based loan, consideration should be given to individual requirements, portfolio composition and risk tolerance, as well as capital gains, portfolio performance expectations and investment time horizon. The securities or other assets in any collateral account may be sold to meet a collateral call without notice to the client, the client is not entitled to an extension of time on the collateral call and the client is not entitled to choose which securities or other assets will be sold. The client can lose more funds than deposited in such collateral account. The LMA account is uncommitted and Bank of America, N.A. may demand full repayment at any time. A complete description of the loan terms can be found within the LMA account agreement. Clients should consult their own independent tax and legal advisors. Some restrictions may apply to purpose loans and not all managed accounts are eligible as collateral. All applications for LMA accounts are subject to approval by Bank of America, N.A. 2 Please consult your tax advisor regarding interest deductibility For fixed-rate and term advances, principal payments made prior to the due date will be subject to a breakage fee. Before taking out any mortgage or line of credit, borrowers should consult their tax advisor to understand the implications of each of their options. Banking, mortgage and home equity products offered by Bank of America, N.A., and affiliated banks, Members FDIC and wholly owned subsidiaries of Bank of America Corporation. Equal Housing Lender. Credit and collateral are subject to approval. Terms and conditions apply. This is not a commitment to lend. Programs, rates, terms and conditions are subject to change without notice. Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Switch to Accessibility Friendly View Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog How we can help Explore what it’s like to work with a Merrill Financial Advisor Start here More for you More for you Watch this next: “The Big Shift: New Market Forces and Ways to Prepare” Connect Find out how our advisors can help Sign up Get monthly insights from our Perspectives newsletter Related Articles","title":"Financing a home renovation","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/financing-a-home-renovation.html","id":612},{"content_hash":"fee0ce8d83a263647c623022a48c40577eb6359a3e03e21882c1ff9c2e11f393","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:53.373723+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/topics/canada-us-trade-war/","language":"","text":"Canada-U.S. Trade War - The Globe and Mail HomeCanadaBusinessInvestingLifeOpinionWorldPoliticsPersonal FinanceCultureSportsMoreMoreLatest in MoreReal EstateDriveWatchlistFor YouEventsFind clarity in the chaos. Subscribe now and save 70%SUBSCRIBE FROM $0.99/WEEKDigital accessDigital + home deliveryCanada-U.S. Trade War The escalating trade war between Canada and the U.S. continues to impact vital national industries, including autos, steel, aluminum, dairy, energy and agriculture. Following the imposition of Canada’s retaliatory tariffs on $28-billion in U.S. products on Sept. 8, President Donald Trump announced a ban on imports of Canadian products including alcohol, whey protein, molasses and some dairy products, as well as new levies on additional items, set to take effect Sept. 29. Last month, trade negotiations collapsed between the two countries, triggering Mr. Trump’s new 50-per-cent tariffs on Aug. 21, and Prime Minister Mark Carney’s resulting countertariffs. These measures have upended the long-standing relationship between the two countries, raising questions for everyday Canadians about economic stability, inflation and job security. Explore The Globe’s most up-to-date reporting and analysis on the Canada-U.S. trade war, covering everything from tariff announcements and negotiations, to Canada’s response, to how the dispute is affecting businesses and consumers on both sides of the border. The latestnewsletterBusiness Brief: Stelco and the forever trade war4 min readA border town on the front lines of the trade war1 min readU.S. trade deficit increases in August on record imports3 min readTrade surplus widens to $4.2-billion in August as exporters rush to beat U.S. tariffs3 min readToronto home sales fall for second straight month 2 min readListen | The Decibel on the trade warHow small businesses are handling the trade warPodcastSeptember 1, 2026At its core, the Canada-U.S. trade dispute is about autosPodcastAugust 27, 2026Canada hits U.S. with retaliatory tariffsPodcastAugust 26, 2026Carney’s tricky trade balancing actPodcastAugust 20, 2026More storiesYesterdaynewsletterBusiness Brief: Stelco and the forever trade war4 min readYesterdayA border town on the front lines of the trade war1 min readOctober 6, 2026U.S. trade deficit increases in August on record imports3 min readOctober 6, 2026Trade surplus widens to $4.2-billion in August as exporters rush to beat U.S. tariffs3 min readOctober 6, 2026Toronto home sales fall for second straight month 2 min readOctober 5, 2026Canada’s services PMI shows sector shrinking for fourth straight month in September2 min readOctober 4, 2026Uncertainty hangs over future of suspended Chinese tariffs on Canadian farm products, seafood5 min readOctober 3, 2026Carney’s first-ever project of national interest, Stelco cites tariffs for layoffs and finance executives bet on prediction markets: Must-read business and investing stories 3 min readOctober 3, 2026In an Ontario steel town, the trade war and local elections spark conversations about change5 min readOctober 2, 2026Niagara Region thanks Buffalo in open letter1 min readOctober 1, 2026Cleveland-Cliffs CEO questions logic of Ottawa potentially suing over Stelco layoffs4 min readOctober 1, 2026International Trade Minister meets with Greer for first time since August4 min readOctober 1, 2026Why does CBC Gem keep buying American during a trade war?J. Kelly Nestruck4 min readOctober 1, 2026The booze war within the trade war1 min readOctober 1, 2026Why Canada’s dollar-for-dollar countertariffs come with significant relief for importers6 min readSeptember 30, 2026G20 Steel forum calls for more tariffs, fewer subsidies to fight excess capacity3 min readSeptember 30, 2026opinionThe trade war is no excuse for flights of economic nationalist fancyAndrew Coyne4 min readSeptember 29, 2026opinionOttawa must not abandon electric vehicles to align with WashingtonChris Severson-Baker and Adam Thorn4 min readSeptember 28, 2026Trump expects Canada to cave in trade war as Stelco cites tariffs for layoffs5 min readSeptember 26, 2026As tariffs hit art, Indigenous artists face frustrating roadblocks and a sputtering art market4 min readPrev123NextTerms & ConditionsCommunity GuidelinesPrivacy PolicyDisclaimerSkip footer navigationSubscribeDigital + Home DeliveryDigital AccessGlobe2GoThe New York TimesGlobe Email NewslettersGift SubscriptionBusiness servicesAdvertise with UsGroup SubscriptionsGlobe CampusDataStoreGlobe Event CentreLeadership InstituteContact usAddress and Phone NumberStandards EditorStaffSecureDropSubmit an articleReader servicesAccount SettingsCustomer SupportSubscriptionsMember BenefitsPrivacy SettingsAbout usCompany InformationWork at The GlobeAccessibilityEditorial Code of ConductSustainabilityLicensing & PermissionsElection Advertising RegistryModern Slavery ReportAI GuidelinesSubscribeDigital + Home DeliveryDigital AccessGlobe2GoThe New York TimesGlobe Email NewslettersGift SubscriptionBusiness servicesAdvertise with UsGroup SubscriptionsGlobe CampusDataStoreGlobe Event CentreLeadership InstituteContact usAddress and Phone NumberStandards EditorStaffSecureDropSubmit an articleReader servicesAccount SettingsCustomer SupportSubscriptionsMember BenefitsPrivacy SettingsAbout usCompany InformationWork at The GlobeAccessibilityEditorial Code of ConductSustainabilityLicensing & PermissionsElection Advertising RegistryModern Slavery ReportAI GuidelinesJump to footer navigation© Copyright 2026 The Globe and Mail Inc. All rights reserved.351 King Street East, Suite 1600, Toronto, ON Canada, M5A 0N1Andrew Saunders, President and CEO","title":"Canada-U.S. Trade War","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/topics/canada-us-trade-war/","id":613},{"content_hash":"72b570d0dd096d7ba339534869f79b278f399e61fcf922248266a13d58106541","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:53.201315+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/augmentation-de-2-du-prix-du-gaz-en-province_2072926_1819218.html","language":"","text":"AUGMENTATION DE 2 % DU PRIX DU GAZ EN PROVINCE Le \" Journal officiel \" du 7 novembre publie la valeur de l'Index gazier correspondant aux conditions économiques du troisième trimestre 1951 : elle atteint le point 127.6, sur la base 100 au troisième trimestre 1950, contre 125 au deuxième trimestre.Cette valeur, déclare le \" Journal officiel \", est applicable à partir du 1er novembre 1951. A partir de cette date toutes les collectivités qui ont accepté le système de l'Index verront donc les tarifs majorée de 2 %. C'est le cas de ta plus grande partie de la province. Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"AUGMENTATION DE 2 % DU PRIX DU GAZ EN PROVINCE","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/augmentation-de-2-du-prix-du-gaz-en-province_2072926_1819218.html","id":614},{"content_hash":"78cbe25c8ccce786bf5215b4fba3370150ab42da3dfb8b0b81abe25f8f8754ea","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:53.091945+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/healthy-weight-gain-during-pregnancy-12162","language":"","text":"Healthy weight gain during pregnancy Published: February 11, 2013 9:52pm EST https://theconversation.com/healthy-weight-gain-during-pregnancy-12162 https://theconversation.com/healthy-weight-gain-during-pregnancy-12162 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article A healthy diet and not eating for two during pregnancy is vital to limit excess weight gain in both women and their babies, according to new research. The study found 74% of women pregnant for the first time gained an excessive amount of weight throughout their pregnancies. Significant weight gain in pregnancy lead to infants being large at birth and increased the number of caesarean deliveries in labour. Read more at University of Auckland, Waipapa Taumata Rau Source University of Auckland, Waipapa Taumata Rau Tags Pregnancy, Infant health, Weight gain","title":"Healthy weight gain during pregnancy","url":"https://theconversation.com/healthy-weight-gain-during-pregnancy-12162","id":615},{"content_hash":"39230320791d507e9e8aa35faff001ac99aebcb17dc0d28523b95af051ff0939","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:53.083059+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/article-toronto-home-sales-fall-in-september-declining-for-second-straight/","language":"","text":"CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountToronto home sales fell 5.2 per cent in September, declining for the second straight month as higher mortgage rates and economic uncertainty weighed on prospective buyers. There were 5,174 sales last month after removing seasonal influences, according to the Toronto Regional Real Estate Board. Before August’s drop in purchases, there had been five months of increasing sales.“We started to see sales ebb as we moved into fall,” Jason Mercer, the board’s chief information officer, said in an interview. Mr. Mercer attributed the reversal to the worsening trade dispute with the U.S., the prolonged war in the Middle East and rising borrowing costs.Although mortgage rates did not immediately increase when Canada walked away from trade talks with the U.S. in August, home loans are starting to become more expensive. The five-year fixed mortgage rate from national lenders averaged 4.87 per cent in early October, according to data from Wowa.ca, a personal finance website that tracks mortgage rates. That compares to 4.62 per cent in mid-August, prior to the collapse of the trade talks. The last time monthly sales dropped this much was in February, when the number of transactions declined by 5.2 per cent.Home values also fell for the second straight month. The home price index, which is the industry’s preferred measure, was $924,600 in September, according to the board. That was a month-over-month decrease of 0.5 per cent, after a 0.2-per-cent fall in August. Compared to last September, the home price index was down 4.8 per cent. Condos continue to lose the most value, falling 6.7 per cent year over year. Overall, condos in the city of Toronto did not lose as much value as the areas to the west and north of the city. In the Peel and York regions, the home price index for condos fell more than 8 per cent year over year. Mr. Mercer said prospective homebuyers want to take advantage of the lower prices. But he said in a press release that they need confidence that “their employment situation will remain solid and inflation will not put pressure on borrowing costs over the long term.” Last month, the number of homeowners that put their properties up for sale declined. New listings fell 3.8 per cent month over month.","title":"Toronto home sales fall for second straight month","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/business/article-toronto-home-sales-fall-in-september-declining-for-second-straight/","id":616},{"content_hash":"36340703b426af8943e838a3800d9079dffa790f711028d8076ce227a6578a00","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:52.642514+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/adelaide-miller/102482222","language":"","text":"Adelaide Miller - ABC NewsSkip to main contentSkip to news navigation, settings and searchExplore the ABC More from ABCClose menuABC ABC News ABC iview ABC listen Kids & Students Indigenous Information & ServicesEditorial PoliciesRead our editorial guiding principlesFollow the ABCnewsiviewlistenLoadingABC NewsJust InFor YouPoliticsWorldBusinessAnalysisSportLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMoreSearchNews HomeJust InFor YouAnalysisRuralWatch LiveHealthIndigenousPoliticsScienceDeep TimeElectionsWorldEnvironmentInvestigationsBusinessLocal newsQuizzesSportAFLNRLFootballTennisCricketNetballLifestyleWellbeingRelationships & FamilyFood & RecipesPersonal FinanceHome & GardenEntertainmentTV & MoviesBooksMusicPop CultureArtsFacebookYouTubeInstagramTwitterEmergencyBackstoryNewsletters中文新闻BERITA BAHASA INDONESIATOK PISIN Appearance (BETA) Follow system settings Select an optionAlways lightAlways darkFollow system settingsABC iViewABC ListenAdelaide MillerAdelaide Miller is an award-winning journalist and a national business reporter with the ABC's business reporting team. Previously, she was a researcher and reporter for ABC Investigations and has also worked at Background Briefing. Adelaide can be securely contacted at miller.adelaide@proton.me or at AdelaideMiller.01 on Signal.Contact Adelaide MillerLatest by Adelaide MillerHollard insurance fined $2m for handling of claim that destroyed homeThe Federal Court blasts Hollard's handling of an insurance claim which left a regional Victorian destroyed and uninhabitable.Topic:InsuranceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 1:48pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 1:48pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 1:48pmASX rises for third straight day, Nasdaq hits new record high — as it happenedThe Australian share market ended its day higher, driven by gains across banks, miners and real estate stocks.The Nasdaq hit a record high after Wall Street's tech stocks surged, while the euro has fallen to an almost two-year low against the Australian dollar.Topic:Stock MarketTue 6 Oct 2026 at 4:52pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 4:52pmTue 6 Oct 2026 at 4:52pmGovernment accused of 'staggering double standard' as ATO bans credit card paymentsSurcharge bans are supposed to save consumers money but retailers say they're being forced to put prices up and are finding ways around the reforms.Topic:Business and Industry RegulationFri 2 Oct 2026 at 9:23amFri 2 Oct 2026 at 9:23amFri 2 Oct 2026 at 9:23amRBA hikes rates, pointing to Middle East war and AI boom — as it happenedThe RBA delivers its fourth rate hike for 2026, bringing interest rates to the highest level since November 2011.Topic:Stock MarketTue 29 Sep 2026 at 7:03pmTue 29 Sep 2026 at 7:03pmTue 29 Sep 2026 at 7:03pmCredit card rewards programs face major shake-upCredit card rewards programs are being overhauled, with banks cutting points, changing fees and reducing some of the perks attached to rewards cards.Mon 28 Sep 2026 at 5:59pmMon 28 Sep 2026 at 5:59pmMon 28 Sep 2026 at 5:59pmHas Audio Duration: 4 minutes 14 seconds.4m 14sCustomers could still pay as surcharges endSome of the biggest payment changes in years take effect this week - with credit card surcharges to be banned from Thursday. While that might sound good - there are concerns customers could end up shouldering some of the cost. Mon 28 Sep 2026 at 8:29amMon 28 Sep 2026 at 8:29amMon 28 Sep 2026 at 8:29amHas Audio Duration: 2 minutes 51 seconds.2m 51sBanks cut rewards programs, free travel insurance amid surcharge banSweeping reforms from the Reserve Bank start this week, as banks hike fees and slash rewards programs in preparation.Topic:BankingMon 28 Sep 2026 at 5:16pmMon 28 Sep 2026 at 5:16pmMon 28 Sep 2026 at 5:16pmAs it happened: Warning petrol price could soar above $2.70 per litreA leading economist has warned the price of unleaded petrol could be headed above $2.70/litre. Meanwhile, the share market closed the week in negative territory as a global bond sell-off deepened. Topic:Stock MarketFri 25 Sep 2026 at 4:54pmFri 25 Sep 2026 at 4:54pmFri 25 Sep 2026 at 4:54pm'Bond market riot' sends ASX and Wall Street lower — as it happenedThe Australian share market falls as the price of oil surges above $US100 a barrel. It comes after steep losses on Wall Street as tech and AI stocks continue to be sold off. The struggling property developer Bathla is expected to cease construction across all of its sites.Topic:Stock MarketThu 24 Sep 2026 at 5:08pmThu 24 Sep 2026 at 5:08pmThu 24 Sep 2026 at 5:08pmNAB customers back online after experiencing phone and internet banking outagesNAB has experienced a digital outage across multiple platforms including its mobile app, internet banking and phone services.Topic:BankingWed 23 Sep 2026 at 5:09pmWed 23 Sep 2026 at 5:09pmWed 23 Sep 2026 at 5:09pmRBA more likely to raise rates as US Federal Reserve lifts to 4pcMarkets are pricing in a nearly 90 per cent chance of Australia's reserve bank lifting interest rates in September following the US Federal Reserve's decision to hike rates.Topic:Stock MarketThu 17 Sep 2026 at 5:06pmThu 17 Sep 2026 at 5:06pmThu 17 Sep 2026 at 5:06pmHow to avoid the 'worst kind of bullying' if hit with a debt collection noticeThis year has been a perfect storm for financial stress.Topic:ExplainerThu 17 Sep 2026 at 9:53amThu 17 Sep 2026 at 9:53amThu 17 Sep 2026 at 9:53amConsumer watchdog investigates debt collectorsDebt collectors are a necessary part of the country's financial sector, ensuring companies are paid outstanding bills in a timely manner.Wed 16 Sep 2026 at 12:23pmWed 16 Sep 2026 at 12:23pmWed 16 Sep 2026 at 12:23pmHas Audio Duration: 4 minutes 3 seconds.4m 3sSuncorp's debt collector engaging in 'worst kind' of bullying, former ACCC chair saysInsurance giant Suncorp's debt collector spent months using AI to harass an 81-year-old woman into paying a claim she was not responsible for. Topic:InsuranceWed 16 Sep 2026 at 11:09amWed 16 Sep 2026 at 11:09amWed 16 Sep 2026 at 11:09amASX slides to three-month low as oil price stokes inflation fears — as it happenedThe Australian share market finished at its lowest level since mid-June as rising oil prices lifted inflation fears, while US 10-year bond yields hit their highest level since 2007.Topic:Stock MarketTue 15 Sep 2026 at 5:14pmTue 15 Sep 2026 at 5:14pmTue 15 Sep 2026 at 5:14pmASX drops, Nikkei plunges as oil prices and bond yields surge — as it happenedAustralian shares are lower after oil prices and treasury yields surge, weighing on Wall Street.Topic:Stock MarketFri 11 Sep 2026 at 5:00pmFri 11 Sep 2026 at 5:00pmFri 11 Sep 2026 at 5:00pmAus Post fee allegationsAs the country's main delivery carrier, Australia Post sends millions of parcels across the country every year.Thu 10 Sep 2026 at 9:23amThu 10 Sep 2026 at 9:23amThu 10 Sep 2026 at 9:23amHas Audio Duration: 3 minutes 27 seconds.3m 27s$32 billion wiped off ASX in worst trading day since June — as it happenedThe Australian share market ended lower for the third-straight session this week despite recovering from earlier losses that saw it shed as much as $50 billion.Topic:Stock MarketThu 10 Sep 2026 at 5:00pmThu 10 Sep 2026 at 5:00pmThu 10 Sep 2026 at 5:00pm'How is that fair': Small businesses fume over shock Australia Post feesSmall businesses are being hit with \"underpaid postage fees\", but Australia Post says its size and weight scanners are \"highly accurate\" and will not disclose how often its technology is wrong, raising questions about the legality of its additional charges.Topic:Small BusinessesThu 10 Sep 2026 at 4:59amThu 10 Sep 2026 at 4:59amThu 10 Sep 2026 at 4:59amMore banks forecast RBA September interest rate hike — as it happenedSeveral Australian banks and global investment banks are now forecasting the Reserve Bank will hike the cash rate again this month.Topic:Stock MarketFri 4 Sep 2026 at 5:09pmFri 4 Sep 2026 at 5:09pmFri 4 Sep 2026 at 5:09pmCorporate Travel Management shares plummet — as it happenedShares in a major travel services company used by parliamentarians and embroiled in a $100 million scandal, Corporate Travel Management, plunge more than 80 per cent.Topic:Stock MarketThu 3 Sep 2026 at 5:09pmThu 3 Sep 2026 at 5:09pmThu 3 Sep 2026 at 5:09pmOwners warned about accepting cash settlements on house insurance claimsThe corporate watchdog warns that insurers are offering cash settlements for home insurance claims that do not cover the full cost of work needed.Topic:InsuranceMon 31 Aug 2026 at 7:49amMon 31 Aug 2026 at 7:49amMon 31 Aug 2026 at 7:49amWhat happens to your brain when you run a marathon?As marathon running booms, researchers are uncovering why the sport can reshape the brain, build resilience and create a new sense of purpose.Topic:MarathonSun 30 Aug 2026 at 4:30amSun 30 Aug 2026 at 4:30amSun 30 Aug 2026 at 4:30amAustralian dollar highest since May – as it happenedThe Australian dollar has rallied today as traders factor in the potential for higher interest rates. Topic:Stock MarketFri 28 Aug 2026 at 5:28pmFri 28 Aug 2026 at 5:28pmFri 28 Aug 2026 at 5:28pmRBA 'quietly' reduced US dollar exposure, big banks tip rate hike — as it happenedThe Reserve Bank, it has been revealed, reduced its target share of US dollars in its foreign reserves portfolio by 10 percentage points in 2025.Topic:Stock MarketThu 27 Aug 2026 at 5:01pmThu 27 Aug 2026 at 5:01pmThu 27 Aug 2026 at 5:01pmLoad more stories aboveGot a news tip?If you have inside knowledge of a topic in the news, contact the ABC.Confidential Tip‑offIf you are a whistleblower, believe you have sensitive information that should be made public, or wish to protect your identity, find our more secure options.Confidential Tip‑offNot confidential? Contact ABC News and investigations.Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashWoman, 71, treated on 'cold floor' of Adelaide hospitalData centre operator Firmus pulls blockbuster ASX float after lacklustre demandMass evacuations, cars washed away in Chilean flooding disasterSydney councillor accuses Indigenous elder Mick Mundine of sexual assault'Beau L-C' rental car card among items found near bodies, jury hearsliveButters unveiled by Bulldogs after 'megadeal', Blakey makes his decisionFederal government backtracks on disaster funding splitUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicThese are the frontrunners for this year's Nobel Peace Prize Powderfinger confirm reunion, 16 years after breaking upBack to topABC NewsContact ABC NewsYour home of Australian stories, conversations and events that shape our nation.This service may include material from Agence France-Presse (AFP), APTN, Reuters, AAP, CNN and the BBC World Service which is copyright and cannot be reproduced.Follow ABC NewsSectionsABC News HomeJust InFor YouWatch Live NewsLive ABC NewsRadioPoliticsElectionsAustraliaWorldSportLive SportRuralBusinessAnalysisScienceHealthLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMore TopicsNews in LanguageChinese 中文Tok PisinBerita Bahasa IndonesiaRelatedABC News appLocal NewsABC EmergencyABC IndigenousABC Religion & EthicsABC AsiaABC PacificMusic & AudioABC Listen Hometriple jLive RadioMovies & TVABC iview HomeList of ABC ShowsLive TVTV GuideKids & StudentsBTNEducationHeywireTakeoverABC KidsGet the ABC News AM newsletter Get the latest ABC News delivered to your email inboxEmail addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. All ABC newslettersStay informed and connected with the ABC. Covering a wide range of topics and brands including News, iview, listen, and more.Browse all ABC newslettersABC homeInformation & ServicesEditorial PoliciesAccessibilityHelpContact UsCommercialCareersAbout the ABCPrivacy PolicyTerms of Use© 2026 ABCFollow the ABCWe acknowledge Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples as the First Australians and Traditional Custodians of the lands where we live, learn and work.","title":"Adelaide Miller","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/adelaide-miller/102482222","id":617},{"content_hash":"733013ddd6437ad38a2322b01d7c3024c838b20f93ee6f8dcfb61c53c3a0351e","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:52.318207+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/functions/","language":"","text":"PostgreSQL specific database functions¶ All of these functions are available from the django.contrib.postgres.functions module. RandomUUID¶ class RandomUUID[source]¶ Returns a version 4 UUID. Usage example: >>> from django.contrib.postgres.functions import RandomUUID >>> Article.objects.update(uuid=RandomUUID()) Use django.db.models.functions.UUID4 instead. django.db.models.functions.UUID4 is a cross-database version of this function. RandomUUID may be deprecated in the future. TransactionNow¶ class TransactionNow[source]¶ Returns the date and time on the database server that the current transaction started. If you are not in a transaction it will return the date and time of the current statement. This is a complement to django.db.models.functions.Now, which returns the date and time of the current statement. Note that only the outermost call to atomic() sets up a transaction and thus sets the time that TransactionNow() will return; nested calls create savepoints which do not affect the transaction time. Usage example: >>> from django.contrib.postgres.functions import TransactionNow >>> Article.objects.filter(published__lte=TransactionNow()) <QuerySet [<Article: How to Django>]>","title":"PostgreSQL specific database functions¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/functions/","id":618},{"content_hash":"4aae8725617fac17a42ccec7b4cc736af24e48b2e14746e3841a5ec7a0f9516c","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:52.091267+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/kyc-in-crypto","language":"","text":"KYC in Crypto - Why does it Really Matter? ByPriscilla Edited by Emmanuel AgwuMarch 21, 2023•5mins ReadWhat Are Cryptocurrency Exchanges?Is KYC Necessary In Crypto?What Are Some Benefits of KYC In Crypto?What Are The Challenges Of KYC In Crypto?How Does KYC Work In Crypto?ConclusionShare this postCryptocurrency, often referred to simply as crypto is a digital currency (as opposed to physical conventional money) that uses cryptography to secure transactions. There usually exists a central issuing and regulating authority for all physical fiat currencies, e.g. the Federal Reserves for the American Dollar. Cryptocurrency however has no such central issuing or regulating authority. It uses a decentralized system for the issuing of new units and the recording of transactions. With crypto, encryption is used to verify transactions. Since crypto is an asset that can be exchanged for conventional money, it is highly important to know the persons involved in crypto transactions. This is where cryptocurrency exchanges are saddled with the responsibility of conducting Know Your Customer (KYC) exercises for persons who make use of their platforms. KYC entails verifying the identity of customers, conducting some background checks on them and continuously monitoring their transactions to ensure that they are not involved in financial crimes such as money laundering, terrorist financing or trafficking in persons. Conducting KYC protects businesses and limits their exposure to criminal liability when a customer uses their platform for illegal activities. What Are Cryptocurrency Exchanges? A cryptocurrency exchange (sometimes known as Digital Currency Exchange) is an online platform where buyers and sellers of crypto meet to trade crypto. A cryptocurrency exchange is like a marketplace for the trading of crypto. Cryptocurrency exchanges provide an online platform and serve as intermediaries between the buyers and sellers of crypto on the platform provided. Is KYC Necessary In Crypto? Billions of dollars worth of crypto has been traded by millions of persons across the globe since the inception of cryptocurrency. By its nature of providing anonymity for traders, crypto is vulnerable to fraud and can be easily used to illegally move money around and commit serious financial crimes without leaving obvious digital footprints behind. This defeats the goal of Anti-Money Laundering (AML) laws and regulations. It is because of this that governments of different countries and their regulatory agencies have ensured that cryptocurrency exchanges adopt and conduct KYC processes for their customers. In fact, since cryptocurrency exchanges have been defined and categorized as Money Service Businesses (MSBs) by federal regulations, KYC has become mandatory for most crypto exchanges. The large volumes of financial transactions they process have made regulatory agencies put in place rules and regulations to guide the operations of cryptocurrency exchanges. What Are Some Benefits of KYC In Crypto? Some of the benefits of KYC in crypto include: 1. It Helps To Detect And Track Criminal Activities Anonymity is one privilege that crypto traders enjoy a lot. Cryptocurrency provides privacy for all the transactions of its traders. Persons have hidden under the cover of anonymity and privacy in crypto to perpetrate financial crimes. Without KYC in crypto, these financial crimes will not be easy to detect, track and report to the relevant authorities. If such should continue, the crypto industry will be marred by a lot of fraudulent financial activities. 2. It Paints The Cryptocurrency Industry In A Better Light People have had serious reservations about crypto, especially in its early days. One of the major concerns of people was the fact that cryptocurrency exchanges did not have sufficient information about the people that trade on their platforms, hence making the platforms prone to scams and hacks with little or no means of identifying culprits. But with the adoption of KYC in crypto, people have more confidence in the crypto industry, knowing that cryptocurrency exchanges now conduct KYC processes before allowing customers to trade on their platforms. This boosts transparency, builds user trust and paints the crypto industry in a better light. 3. Cryptocurrency Exchanges Are Held To A Higher Standard Of Compliance By ensuring that crypto exchanges comply with KYC requirements, some level of compliance and safety standards are placed on them. This makes cryptocurrency exchanges work towards living up to such expectations to avoid any penalties that may arise due to violation. Without KYC in crypto, cryptocurrency exchanges would be held to almost no standard of compliance at all. 4. Reduced Legal Risk For Cryptocurrency Exchanges Demonstrating their due diligence in KYC and AML practices can help crypto exchanges mitigate the risk of facing legal disputes or regulatory fines. Even when attempts at fraudulent activities have been made on their platforms, their exposure to criminal liability is greatly reduced, as long as they are compliant with KYC and AML regulations. This allows crypto companies to prioritize improving their conversion rates, making transactions more efficient, and adhering to evolving global regulations instead of spending time and resources on court cases, regulatory fines and penalties. 5. Provides More Protection To Crypto Traders The deployment of KYC in crypto has greatly reduced the potential for money laundering and other fraudulent financial activities capable of plaguing cryptocurrency exchanges. The introduction of KYC in crypto has therefore guaranteed more protection for traders on KYC-compliant cryptocurrency exchanges. In the event of any hacks that lead to loss of money, the exchanges can easily track persons involved based on the information and details obtained during KYC processes. What Are The Challenges Of KYC In Crypto? As beneficial as the deployment of KYC in crypto is, cryptocurrency exchanges are faced with certain challenges in the course of implementing KYC processes. Some of these challenges include: 1. Tendency of Violating Data Privacy Laws And Regulations As crypto exchanges collect and verify the information of their customers during onboarding, they need to be extremely careful in how they store, handle and process such information (especially Personally Identifiable Information) so as not to breach or violate existing data protection laws and regulations. 2. Reduced Anonymity In Cryptocurrency A lot of cryptocurrency traders have expressed their unease at the introduction of KYC in crypto. Most users believe the implementation of KYC in crypto has stripped the digital currency of its major feature of anonymity, which was what made it attractive to several users. These concerns have made some crypto enthusiasts shy away from the crypto industry. 3. The Complex And Cumbersome Nature Of KYC The different stages and components of KYC are not easy tasks that can be walked over. Crypto exchanges are having to brace up and look for ways to ensure they are not overwhelmed by the often complex and cumbersome requirements of KYC. Those who have adopted automation and the use of KYC solutions can easily surmount this challenge. For more on KYC Automation, read: Digital Customer Onboarding Process: Why You should Automate your Customer Onboarding Process How Does KYC Work In Crypto? Just like KYC conducted by banks and other financial institutions, the process for KYC in crypto can be broken down as follows: i. Cryptocurrency exchanges collect personally identifiable information (PII) of their customers such as name, date of birth, address and ID number at the point of the customer's registration with the crypto exchange. ii. The crypto exchanges request valid means of identification from the customers, especially government-issued IDs such as National Identity Cards, Driver's Licences or International Passport. iii. The cryptocurrency exchanges then compare the personally identifiable information (PII) collected during registration with the means of identification submitted by the customers. This is to confirm and verify the identities of customers. Recommended: Identity Verification Questions - Frequently Asked Questions On Identity Verification iv. The crypto exchanges conduct searches in official databases of Politically Exposed Persons, watchlists and sanctioned individuals to determine the individual risk profiles of customers and the likelihood of their involvement in financial crimes. v. The cryptocurrency exchanges continuously monitor the transactions of customers for suspicious and unusual transactions to report to the necessary regulatory authorities and help law enforcement agencies to track criminal financial activities. Conclusion As much as ensuring anonymity and protecting the privacy of crypto traders is at the core of cryptocurrency, verifying customers' identity, conducting due diligence and monitoring customers' transactions continually are crucial KYC requirements that must also be considered. When the requirements of KYC in crypto are adopted and upheld by cryptocurrency exchanges, their processes become more standard and safe. People would have more confidence in a crypto exchange that conducts KYC than in one that does not. See how 100+ leading companies use Youverify for KYC and AML screening of customers for compliance and real-time risk detection. Request a demo today.Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"KYC in Crypto - Why does it Really Matter?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/kyc-in-crypto","id":619},{"content_hash":"0e4e6c31eea4e94612ab45d0164c66e5ceea72f3033bbc9c1f67a0b92428f0c5","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:51.980395+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/un-independant-est-elu-au-second-poste-municipal-de-new-york_2072007_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives Un indépendant est élu au second poste municipal de New-York Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"Un indépendant est élu au second poste municipal de New-York","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/un-independant-est-elu-au-second-poste-municipal-de-new-york_2072007_1819218.html","id":620},{"content_hash":"a9a9e6e52ab9f091a42563c54259c0f9a913f4b27fd673d497d72e1a44432a21","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:51.908970+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/global-warming-affecting-predators-and-prey-in-the-poles-12295","language":"","text":"Global warming affecting predators and prey in the poles Published: February 18, 2013 7:58pm EST https://theconversation.com/global-warming-affecting-predators-and-prey-in-the-poles-12295 https://theconversation.com/global-warming-affecting-predators-and-prey-in-the-poles-12295 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article The biodiversity of Arctic and Antarctic regions is being affected in different ways by global warming, new research shows. Global warming affects the polar regions more acutely than other areas of the planet. In the Arctic this has been shown to affect larger predatory species, such as the polar bear and whales. This has caused a phenomenon known as a “trophic cascade”, which poses a threat to ecosystems due to changes to predator species affecting species lower in the food web. The Antarctic, which has a larger number of prey species, has seen animals lower in the food web affected, including a marked decrease in Antarctic krill numbers. Read more at The University of Western Australia Source The University of Western Australia Tags Antarctica, Arctic, Global warming","title":"Global warming affecting predators and prey in the poles","url":"https://theconversation.com/global-warming-affecting-predators-and-prey-in-the-poles-12295","id":621},{"content_hash":"3978bca6e03fd718e3e21599ba3eb31f620a33bb64789c39a19f02107dac54fd","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:51.145114+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-01/coffee-lovers-turn-to-home-brews-as-cost-of-living-bites/107204414","language":"","text":"Price hikes force more Australians to make coffee at home to save moneyBy Jessica RossABC Sunshine CoastTopic:Cost of LivingPosted Thu 1 Oct 2026 at 9:00amThu 1 Oct 2026 at 9:00amThu 1 Oct 2026 at 9:00amGlenn Jones is a chief coffee taster at a Queensland coffee processing plant. (ABC Sunshine Coast: Jessica Ross)In short:October 1 is International Coffee Day, which celebrates the global significance of the popular beverage.Nestlé Australia, which produces instant coffee, says there has been a surge in sales.What's next?Cost-of-living pressures are forcing more coffee lovers to change their spending habits amid rising cafe prices.abc.net.au/news/coffee-lovers-turn-to-home-brews-as-cost-of-living-bites/107204414Link copiedShareShare articleAt a Queensland coffee-processing factory, 10 people are employed to ensure every batch tastes exactly as it should.Each day they gather in groups of three or four around a rotating table that has a few sinks on the side so they can spit out the samples as they go.\"We do sample up to 100 cups per day, so you don't want to be drinking that much coffee on a standard day,\" quality assurance manager or \"chief taste-tester\" for Nestlé, Glenn Jones, said.Nestlé Australia's Gympie factory employs 10 tasters who sample the coffee every day. (ABC Sunshine Coast: Jessica Ross)The factory in Gympie, about two hours' drive north of Brisbane, has been producing instant coffee since 1997.Mr Jones said even though drinking the beverage was part of his job description, he also enjoyed it when he was off-duty.\"I need coffee before I come to work in the morning, then I taste it all day, and I'll have coffee on the weekends — I love coffee,\" he said.Glenn Jones still drinks coffee outside of work hours. (ABC Sunshine Coast: Jessica Ross)A national obsessionMr Jones is not alone in his obsession, with an estimated three-quarters of Australians consuming coffee regularly.Data shows more than 5 million Australians visit a cafe daily for their caffeine hit.Martin Brown says cappuccino sachets have been the \"fastest-growing part of the market\" in the past five years. (Supplied.)But Martin Brown, general manager of Nestlé's coffee and dairy business, said the company's sales figures showed a shift back to home consumption.\"[Australia has] one of the most sophisticated coffee cultures in the world, but we also have a cost-of-living challenge at the moment,\" Mr Brown said.\"A lot of households are having to make choices and find smarter ways to buy things.\"He said cappuccino sachets had been the \"fastest-growing part of the market\" in the past five years, but there was also a shift back to more \"traditional\" soluble instant coffee.\"A cup of Blend 43 will cost you 12 to 15 cents a cup … it's super convenient and it's value for money and none of that goes out of fashion,\" he said.Nestlé Australia's Gympie processing plant produces about 20 million cups of coffee each day. (ABC Sunshine Coast: Jessica Ross)Coffee price pinchThe latest Australian Bureau of Statistics data showed food and non-alcoholic beverage prices rose by 3 per cent in the 12 months leading up to August this year, with meals out and takeaway prices up 4.5 per cent.The average price of a cafe-bought flat white in Australia is now about $6 a cup, despite warnings it could soar to double digits this year.University students (from left) Elora Hemmer, Charlotte Cairns and Holly Durbidge have cut back on buying coffee out. (ABC Sunshine Coast: Jessica Ross)On Queensland's Sunshine Coast, university student Charlotte Cairns said it was even more expensive to buy a cold coffee.\"I feel like when a coffee is $5 it's like, 'Oh, that's alright',\" Ms Cairns said.\"Whereas when it's like $9 dollars just for an iced latte it's like: 'OK, this is strictly a treat, I can't be doing this tomorrow as well.'\"Deann Mayfield said she and her partner had started to limit the amount of coffee they bought out.\"It's very expensive. We have one special coffee a day, so we try not to double up,\" Ms Mayfield said.Deann Mayfield says she feels for cafe owners, who were also dealing with rising costs. (ABC Sunshine Coast: Jessica Ross)A challenging environmentCoffee bean prices reached record highs last year, forcing importers and roasters to raise their prices, which was ultimately passed on to customers.While green bean prices have dropped in 2026, they remain three times higher than in 2021.More than five million Australians visit a cafe or coffee shop every day. (ABC Sunshine Coast: Jessica Ross)Australian Specialty Coffee Association president Bruno Maiolo said the drop had not offset the increase in \"all of the other costs\", including rent, electricity, milk and wages.\"We have a one-in-two failure rate [for coffee businesses],\" Mr Maiolo said.\"People are going broke, not because they're necessarily bad operators; it's because they cannot keep the margins that are required to stay in business.\"They're running on a shoestring.\"Bruno Maiolo says it is a challenging time to be in the cafe business. (Supplied)He said consumers in Australia had much cheaper coffee than in European countries, despite the fact that it was more expensive to make here.\"We have a phobia of putting the price up because we're all scared that everyone will stop drinking coffee and they'll go somewhere else,\" he said.\"No-one can absorb price increases forever.\"Simon O'Brien has made a conscious decision to offer $5 coffees to keep costs down for customers. (ABC Sunshine Coast: Jessica Ross)Simon O'Brien runs a coffee shop by the beach at Alexandra Headland and said he was able to still charge $5 for a cup due to the regular foot traffic.\"We've got coffee that people keep coming back for, and it's not so much about making a massive profit on each cup,\" Mr O'Brien said.\"It's the throughput over time and keeping that customer for as long as we can.\"ABC Sunshine — local news in your inboxGet our local newsletter, delivered free each WednesdayEmail addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. Share optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerRelated storiesThink $5.50 is too much for a flat white? Actually it's too cheap, and our world-famous cafes are paying the priceTopic:AnalysisCoffee prices up to $12 needed to make industry viable, supplier saysTopic:Cost of Living Australian coffee in demand as international prices soarTopic:Tea and Coffee GrowingRelated topicsAlexandra HeadlandCost of LivingFood and DrinkGympieMaroochydoreTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Price hikes force more Australians to make coffee at home to save money","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-01/coffee-lovers-turn-to-home-brews-as-cost-of-living-bites/107204414","id":622},{"content_hash":"1d00457172294e51aef31e3b96bd27bbb672ed2ea851f6234c776d3326d3944a","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:51.037740+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/etimes/trending/train-main-thodi-masti-kar-lete-hai-kya-hi-ho-jayega-woman-confronts-youngsters-over-train-noise-internet-divided/articleshow/134790904.cms","language":"","text":"'Train main thodi masti kar lete hai kya hi ho jayega’: Woman confronts youngsters over train noise, internet divided | - The Times of IndiaSign InTOITOIhomelifestylerelationshiptrendingHealth+styleparentingsoul searchbeautybooksrecipesfood newstravelhome & gardeneventsspotlightNewsetimeslifestyletrending'Train main thodi masti kar lete hai kya hi ho jayega’: Woman confronts youngsters over train noise, internet dividedTrendingBaby NamesVibhor JainChildren PsychologyIndiGo Flight Lightning StrikeLeo Tolstoy QuoteYellow Pillow StainsBen AffleckKarl DeisserothRam VilasSharad Navratri 2026Baby NamesVibhor JainChildren PsychologyIndiGo Flight Lightning StrikeLeo Tolstoy QuoteYellow Pillow StainsBen AffleckKarl DeisserothRam VilasSharad Navratri 2026Baby NamesVibhor JainChildren PsychologyIndiGo Flight Lightning StrikeLeo Tolstoy QuoteYellow Pillow StainsBen AffleckKarl DeisserothRam VilasSharad Navratri 2026'Train main thodi masti kar lete hai kya hi ho jayega’: Woman confronts youngsters over train noise, internet dividedTOI Trending Desk / etimes.in / Oct 08, 2026, 18:17 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint Woman confronts youngsters over train noise, internet divided A train journey with friends can easily turn lively, but the same energy may not feel enjoyable to everyone sharing the coach. A video from a recent train journey has sparked an online debate over where people should draw the line between having fun and disturbing fellow passengers.The discussion began after a group of youngsters was confronted by a woman in the same coach. While some viewers felt the group was simply enjoying their time together, others said passengers travelling around them also deserved some peace.Woman confronts youngsters in trainThe video, shared on Instagram by Saachi, shows a group of youngsters inside a train coach. They were seen chanting “Ganpati Bappa Morya\" and “Har Har Mahadev\" while travelling together.A woman in the coach then confronted the group about their behaviour. She claimed that she had already asked them several times to stop making noise.“You are literally uneducated, literally, been telling you repeatedly, you are doing the same thing and disturbing the entire coach,\" she said.She then questioned their conduct, asking, “What manners do you have?\"The woman also warned the youngsters that she would complain about them and seek action.“I am going to complain very, very strong, that you have to get down,\" she said.During the exchange, she also referred to an earlier interaction with police, where the group was seemingly asked not to make noise.‘Train main thodi masti kar lete hai’The video itself appeared to have been shared with a light-hearted take on the incident. Text written over the clip said, “Train main thodi masti kar lete hai kya hi ho jayega (Let’s have a little fun on the train, what could possibly go wrong?)\"It was followed by, “Lo hogaya (Well it happened).\"The post soon drew different opinions from viewers, with people debating whether the group had crossed a line.Internet divided over train behaviourSome users defended the youngsters and felt the woman had reacted too strongly.“The lady was just jealous because she didn’t have the friend group like yours,\" one person wrote.Others disagreed and said loud behaviour can become particularly disruptive during a night journey.“But actually at night it disturbs a lot. We had a group of our college sitting right beside our seats and they literally shouted like hell, although we were all going for the same trip.\"Another commenter said that passengers travelling with children, babies and elderly people should also be considered.“It’s not funny, it’s public transport, kids, babies, elders who want to sleep had to go through this nonsense. Get your own bus if you want to do this.\"‘Train hai fish market nahi’One user also strongly supported the woman and said they would have complained as well.“Aunty k jagah mai vi hota to complain he krta train hai fish market ni jaha jb mnn chillane lgo aur tmlog ka v shi h ek to glti kro fir proud feel kro usme he,\" the commenter wrote.The reactions have left viewers divided over a simple question of public etiquette. Some see the incident as friends enjoying a journey together, while others believe that shared spaces require people to keep their behaviour from affecting those around them.Disclaimer: The information in this article is based on the social media post and comments mentioned above. The views expressed by social media users are their own and have not been independently verified. They do not necessarily reflect the views of Times of India.Thumb image: InstagramGet the latest movie news, reviews, and celebrity updates. Download the TOI App.End of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaTop TrendingDonna KelcePatrick Mahomes MotherSam BealAsim MunirUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestSimone BilesCJP Chappal ProtestAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTrending StoriesIn SectionEntire WebsiteNeighbors Challenge Estate Plans: Kelce’s Ohio fence fight widens; sewer, wildlife fears growCookie Recipe Funds Lunches: Donna Kelce shares family favorite on mugs; fundraiser clears lunch debtLauren Kyle Builds Business Empire: business coach reshapes her mindset; five-venture empire followsOld Leak Resurfaces Online: Gia Duddy’s new NFL romance revives 2024 video; fans revisit scandalMcGregor Donations Top $5m: whiskey deal helps fund first responders; hospitals and kids benefitWhy Travis Kelce's supposed farewell season is starting to look like a record chaseMahomes' Father Cleared To Travel: Texas judge allows trips to Chiefs games; Cincinnati bid deniedCPKC Stadium Expansion: Kansas City backs $185m plan to boost KC Current; Riverfront district growsHeart & Serve Pickleball Tournament: Randi Mahomes leads Variety KC fundraiser; over $150,000 raisedKelces Boost Philly Youth: ch