rationPerfect for Salesforce usersThe perfect addition to your Salesforce setup? An email editor that actually works.Learn moreSOC 2 Type II · ISO 27001 · DPF · AWS PartnerPricingTalk to usResourcesLearnBlogTips, trends, and teardowns.AcademyGet better at email. For free.Help centerStuck with RGE Studio? Start here.ConnectVirtual eventsCome learn stuff with email people.UnspamFor folks who hate boring email conferences.Feedback FridayReal emails, honest critique.NewsletterReally good stuff, in your inbox.ToolsAI prompt librarySmart starting points for your emails.Email signature generatorLook pro in every sign-off.Report2026 RGE Email SurveyFresh data on how email teams actually work: email got faster, but not for everyone.Read the report300,000 email pros learn and hang out here.Next webinarsJoin the newsletterPricingTalk to SalesBack to the template catalogREALLY GOOD EMAILSAboutSchoolFeedback FridayEventsContactCode of ConductEmail signature generatorPrompt libraryRGE STUDIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"Business Link in Bio | Page template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/business-link-in-bio","id":642},{"content_hash":"40e951352431d204848deb56409028ff2307471e37b7ca7880b3e5ae5bacd909","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:47.098085+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/environmental-factors-affect-sharks-dive-13531","language":"","text":"Environmental factors affect sharks’ dive Published: April 16, 2013 12:04am EDT https://theconversation.com/environmental-factors-affect-sharks-dive-13531 https://theconversation.com/environmental-factors-affect-sharks-dive-13531 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article The diving behaviour of sharks has been shown to be affected by the moon, water temperature and time of day. Research on 40 grey reef sharks found that they tended to stay in deep waters on nights with a full moon, while rising to shallow waters during new moon periods. Water temperatures were also seen to have an effect, with the sharks diving to an average of 35 metres during winter and 60 metres in spring. Time of day also affected diving patterns, with the sharks diving deeper during the morning and rising to the surface during the afternoon. The data has given researchers a greater understanding of shark behavioural patterns and it is hoped that it will aid in conservation efforts by improving fishing methods in order to avoid sharks. Read more at The University of Western Australia Source The University of Western Australia Tags Moon, Sharks, Marine conservation, Temperature","title":"Environmental factors affect    sharks’ dive","url":"https://theconversation.com/environmental-factors-affect-sharks-dive-13531","id":643},{"content_hash":"6fb5ac2a68f69f9e08c56d83e1e94a03d9dc290500984e5f8da46535a060afbd","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:46.619381+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/10/01/ophk-troops-kill-terrorists/","language":"","text":"OPHK Troops Kill 16 Terrorists, Recover Firearms in Adamawa By Prnigeria - October 1, 2026 OPHK Troops Kill Terrorists OPHK Troops Kill 16 Terrorists, Recover Firearms in Adamawa Troops of the Joint Task Force, North East, Operation HADIN KAI (OPHK), have neutralised at least 16 terrorists and destroyed another terrorist concentration during separate operations in Adamawa State. The operations, conducted between September 30 and October 1, 2026, also led to the recovery of several arms and ammunition, while troops repelled coordinated attacks on military formations in Garkida and Garaha. The Theatre Command, in a statement issued on Thursday by the Acting Military Information Officer, Headquarters JTF North East, Capt. Mohammed Goni, said the first major encounter occurred in the Boima general area of Gombi Local Government Area. According to the statement, troops conducted a coordinated ambush against a terrorist enclave in the area, resulting in the neutralisation of 16 terrorists. It said troops subsequently exploited the engagement site and recovered three AK-47 rifles, two pump-action rifles, one Dane gun, six AK-47 magazines and a belt of pump-action cartridges. The statement added that the Air Component of OPHK also carried out a precision air strike after an Intelligence, Surveillance and Reconnaissance platform detected about 16 terrorists gathered alongside 15 motorcycles beneath a tree canopy in Sector 4. It said the target was identified and acquired before the air interdiction was conducted at about 5:30am on October 1. Read Also: Navy Rescues Two Kidnap Victims, Recovers Stolen Engines in Akwa Ibom Nigeria Enters Age of Prosperity: FULL TEXT of President Tinubu’s 66th Independence Day Anniversary Speech Emir of Zazzau Backs NAHCON’s Reform Drive, Calls for Sustained Excellence in Hajj Administration “Subsequent Battle Damage Assessment confirmed the successful destruction of the target, while few surviving elements scattered in disarray,” the statement said. Meanwhile, troops deployed to 226 Battalion in Garkida and 232 Battalion in Garaha repelled coordinated attacks launched by insurgents against their positions. The military said the troops responded with overwhelming firepower, forcing the attackers to abort their mission and withdraw. However, three soldiers were killed during the fierce engagements, while wounded personnel were evacuated and are receiving medical treatment in stable condition. The Theatre Command said follow-up exploitation and mop-up operations were ongoing across the affected areas. It attributed the latest operational successes partly to the ongoing restructuring of Headquarters Theatre Command, which it said was aimed at strengthening joint integration, improving operational synergy and ensuring greater inclusiveness of relevant stakeholders in the counter-terrorism and counter-insurgency campaign. The command said the renewed approach was already producing operational gains through improved coordination among land, air and other supporting components. It reaffirmed its commitment to sustaining offensive operations against terrorists and leveraging intelligence, technology and joint capabilities to deny hostile groups freedom of action across the North-East. The Theatre Command also called on members of the public to remain vigilant and provide timely and credible information to security agencies as military operations continue across the region. By PRNigeria VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSKill 16 TerroristsOperation Hadin KaiRecover Firearms Previous articlePHOTO: Troops Rescue 5 Abducted Women, Recover Rustled Livestock in Katsina, Sokoto Prnigeria","title":"OPHK Troops Kill 16 Terrorists, Recover Firearms in Adamawa","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/10/01/ophk-troops-kill-terrorists/","id":644},{"content_hash":"306ab089335f2ff74b76dfb4964206fb63936b4044c1a2c69136f94d80595e74","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:46.423363+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2007-09-28/mystery-over-deep-space-radio-burst/683378","language":"","text":"Mystery over deep space radio burstTopic:SpacePosted Fri 28 Sep 2007 at 8:00amFri 28 Sep 2007 at 8:00amFri 28 Sep 2007 at 8:00am, updated Fri 28 Sep 2007 at 9:29amFri 28 Sep 2007 at 9:29amFri 28 Sep 2007 at 9:29amWhat's the frequency?: The Parkes radio telescope picked up the sudden radio burst (NASA)abc.net.au/news/mystery-over-deep-space-radio-burst/683378Link copiedShareShare articleAstronomers who stumbled upon a powerful burst of radio waves say they have never seen anything like it before, and it could offer a new way to search for colliding stars or dying black holes.Australian and US scientists were searching for pulsars - a type of rotating compacted neutron star that sends out rhythmic pulses of radiation - when they spotted the giant radio signal.It was extremely brief but very strong, and appears to have come from about 3 billion light years away - a light year being the distance light travels in a year, or about 10 trillion kilometres.\"This burst appears to have originated from the distant universe and may have been produced by an exotic event such as the collision of two neutron stars or the death throes of an evaporating black hole,\" said Duncan Lorimer of West Virginia University and the National Radio Astronomy Observatory.Writing in the journal Science, Mr Lorimer and colleagues said they were looking at old scans done by the Parkes radio telescope in New South Wales when they spotted the burst.The burst appears to have lasted 5 milliseconds and may be the radio fingerprint of a single event such as a supernova or the collision of black holes, the astronomers said.\"This burst represents an entirely new astronomical phenomenon,\" Matthew Bailes of Melbourne's Swinburne University said in a statement.Maura McLaughlin of West Virginia University said the event was probably not rare.\"We think there are probably many of these bursts every day that we are just not detecting because we don't have the right kind of surveys of the sky looking for them,\" Ms McLaughlin said in a telephone interview.While satellites are detecting x-ray and gamma-ray bursts with regular surveys of large portions of the sky, radiotelescopes generally focus on a very narrow field, she said.\"We are really not sure [what it is],\" Ms McLaughlin said.\"We think it has got to be some sort of catastrophic event happening in another galaxy - like two stars colliding and merging or maybe a black hole. Something kind of exotic,\" she said.It is, however, unlikely to be the extraterrestrial equivalent of a radio or television broadcast.\"It's much too bright. There is no way any civilisation that we could possibly think of could create a thing so incredibly powerful,\" she said.- ReutersShare optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GoogleTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerPopular nowHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicTopic:HomicideMcLaren driver clocked allegedly doing 259km/h on WA highway Topic:Traffic OffencesRelated topicsAustraliaScience and TechnologySpaceUnited StatesTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Mystery over deep space radio burst","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2007-09-28/mystery-over-deep-space-radio-burst/683378","id":645},{"content_hash":"3adf6975f24757c919feb66685b9f30a6c78d4670ff0286977b931714ac14c15","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:46.380959+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/tax-planning-tips-for-athletes-and-entertainers.html","language":"","text":"Seven tax planning tips for professional athletes and entertainers Professional athletes and celebrity entertainers appear to lead glamorous lives — traveling first-class to play or perform for adoring fans, staying in luxury hotels and earning tremendous incomes. March 16, 2026 Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog What's less glamorous are the many different tax laws that accompany non-traditional incomes that professional athletes and entertainers earn. The complexities of these tax rules can be especially problematic early on in an athlete or entertainer's career before they have even had a chance to hire a team of qualified tax professionals. \"Professional athletes are often quite young,\" says Mitch Drossman, head of National Wealth Strategies in the Chief Investment Office for Merrill and Bank of America Private Bank. \"They come into money quickly, and this can result in a lot of taxes very quickly.\" If athletes and entertainers (and others working in those fields, such as coaches, trainers, directors and agents) don't have a sophisticated wealth advisor in place, including tax advisory, there are significant risks, according to Michael Duffy, Managing Director and Strategic Wealth Advisor for Merrill Lynch Wealth Management and Head of Art Planning for Merrill. \"They can easily rack up surprise tax bills, incur penalties and interest, create extensive debt, damage their personal brand and wind up in legal jeopardy simply by being unaware of applicable tax laws,\" Duffy explains. Early-career individuals may not have any experience with most state income tax laws, such as the \"jock taxes\" that can be accrued when working outside of the individual's home state; tracking multiple income streams and understanding the tax rates and tax laws for each; and understanding how to support their favorite causes without running afoul of the laws that govern charitable giving. While the majority of high-net-worth individuals will hire an accountant or tax attorney to prepare their various tax returns, every earner should have a baseline understanding of the tax rules. At Merrill, we have extensive experience collaborating with high-net-worth athletes and entertainers, as well as their managers, agents, CPAs and tax attorneys, to assist these professionals with tax planning.1 Here we've compiled a list of the top points for athletes, entertainers and other non-traditional earners to keep in mind, highlighting some important nuances to consider. Tax planning and compliance does not have to be a second job where it consumes an already busy schedule. But it is important and can help one plan and act accordingly. \"Professional athletes are often quite young. They come into money quickly, and this can result in a lot of taxes very quickly.\" — Mitch Drossman, Managing Director Head of National Wealth Strategies, Chief Investment Office, Merrill and Bank of America Private Bank Get organized and pay on time The number one thing to keep in mind is to make sure to file tax returns and make tax payments on time to avoid penalties and interest. Most athletes' and entertainers' incomes will vary considerably from year to year, which can complicate the task of making estimated quarterly income tax payments. Careful planning and budgeting can help mitigate years where earnings are considerably lower. They will also need to put in place a system to collect and track the information that will enable their CPA to accurately calculate what tax is owed. Understand the jock tax Many states require athletes and entertainers who perform in their state to file a state income tax return and pay a portion of their earnings, known as a \"jock tax,\" to that state, even though the athlete or entertainer doesn't live there. Rates and filing requirements vary from state to state — a few states don't require income tax at all, and some states only require income tax on athletes who are residents of states that also enforce a jock tax on visiting athletes and entertainers. On top of the state income tax, some cities may charge a municipal tax (in addition to state income tax) on athletes for games played in certain venues. One popular history attributes the origin of the jock tax to 1991, when the Los Angeles Lakers lost the NBA finals to the Chicago Bulls. In what some interpreted as a desire for retribution, California charged the visiting Chicago Bulls' athletes income tax, and in response Illinois began enforcing income tax on visiting California athletes.2 Frequent complaints about the jock tax are that these taxes unfairly target athletes and entertainers because they place an undue compliance burden on them. For instance, it's not uncommon for a National Football League player to file up to a dozen non-resident state income tax returns. Careful tax planning can help athletes and entertainers avoid surprise tax bills from places they've traveled to for work and keep them in compliance with the various taxing authorities. \"They can easily rack up surprise tax bills, incur penalties and interest, create extensive debt, damage their personal brand and wind up in legal jeopardy simply by being unaware of applicable tax laws.\" — Michael Duffy, Managing Director Strategic Wealth Advisor & Head of Art Planning Merrill Lynch Wealth Management Clearly establish your domicile A domicile is the place one treats as their permanent home. Many athletes and entertainers can save millions in taxes by choosing to reside in a state that does not have an income tax, such as Florida, Texas or Washington. Living in a low or no income tax state can also reduce taxes because signing bonuses are not subject to the jock tax and are only taxed in the state where the athlete or entertainer resides. However, proving that the athlete or entertainer resides in that state can be complicated if they travel frequently for work or spend time in multiple homes they own in different states. Proving official domicile often means being clear about where one's primary residence is — where they vote, where they are registered to vote, the state where a driver’s license is issued, how many days are spent in the state, where one goes to maintain religious or secular community-based social affiliations and where their children attend school. A knowledgeable advisor can help athletes and entertainers navigate the more complicated aspects of the domicile equation. Multiple revenue streams, endorsements, appearance fees One of the primary ways that athletes and entertainers differ from other earners in other sectors is that their income almost always comes through a variety of different channels. They may make a base salary, while also enjoying signing and performance bonuses, appearance fees, sponsorship deals, prize money, royalties and back-end compensation. There can potentially be different rules and regulations that govern tax rates and deductible expenses for each of these sources of income. For example, when most people sell property that they create, any gain is taxed as ordinary income. However, when a songwriter sells a song or part or all of their songwriting catalog, proceeds can be taxed under the more favorable capital gains rate if the appropriate election is made. Understanding the tax ramifications of different sources of income is critical to an athlete’s or entertainer’s financial management and well-being. The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (2017) Prior to the passage of this law, professional athletes and entertainers were able to make significant deductions for expenses such as agent fees, union dues and payments made to trainers in the offseason. The new tax code eliminated many of these deductions until 2026. One strategy athletes and entertainers have pursued to mitigate this change is to form what's called a loan-out company, where the individual becomes an employee of their own company and which may take the form of an LLC or an S-Corp. They may also do this as a liability shield against certain creditors. If you'd like to learn more about this strategy and its potential benefits, we encourage you to talk with a knowledgeable financial advisor. \"When someone considers philanthropy, they may have their specific ideas about how they want to do it, and their approach doesn't always align with their tax goals.\" — Dina Friedman, Managing Director, Private Wealth Strategist & Co-Head of the Strategic Wealth Advisory Group, Merrill Lynch Wealth Management Philanthropy After reaching certain professional goals, athletes and entertainers may want to give back to their community through charitable giving. One popular path is to leverage their public profile and ability to inspire others by setting up a charitable foundation. Another avenue for giving is to donate time and effort to a cause. It's important to note that there are no income deductions allowed for donating time, so this approach does not confer tax benefits. \"Philanthropy often plays a big role in tax planning,\" notes Dina Friedman, Managing Director and Co-Head of the Strategic Wealth Advisory Group at Merrill Lynch Wealth Management. \"When someone considers philanthropy, they may have their specific ideas about how they want to do it, and their approach doesn't always align with their tax goals. It then becomes a question of 'What is it you're trying to accomplish as a philanthropist?' Some clients have a philanthropic goal in mind and are not concerned about achieving tax savings, so we can structure their giving strategy to achieve their stated goals. Whereas, if they say, 'I want to give money to charity, but I also want to make sure I optimize any available tax deductions,' that might lead to a different solution set.” \"While it may be common for athletes and entertainers to set up foundations, it's tremendously important that they assistance from a certified professional,\" Friedman points out. Additionally, individuals may want to hire family members or friends to work at the foundation. But setting up a charitable foundation is not a simple task, and there are a number of laws and rules that govern how foundations are structured, funded and managed, including rules for paying reasonable compensation and the necessity to avoid conflicts of interests between oneself, family members and the charitable entity. A well-intentioned foundation that doesn't carefully adhere to the rules can attract scrutiny and could end up hurting their image and even the causes that they're trying to benefit. For these reasons, it's critical to consult knowledgeable advisors when setting up a foundation or other philanthropic entities. Understand estate tax and the role of life insurance in planning for eventual estate tax payments Athletes and entertainers who've achieved great wealth often want to ensure the next generation of their family will benefit from this wealth. Understanding how gift and estate taxes work and planning for those tax considerations is key to any robust tax planning process. In particular, these individuals may have created valuable intellectual property, such as a singer who owns copyright on their song catalog. If, in their estate plan, they bequeath those songs to a relative the estate may not have the cash needed to pay the estate tax and the estate may be forced to sell. Or, that relative can have those copyrights stripped from them down the road under Federal Copyright laws. \"This is where a strategic life insurance policy can be a viable solution. Life insurance benefits are paid out in cash. It provides the beneficiaries of the deceased person with liquidity to pay estate tax bills,\" offers Friedman. Athletes, entertainers and other high-net-worth non-traditional earners need to have a basic understanding of their tax responsibilities. Their support team, including managers, tax attorneys and financial advisors, should help them get organized, track expenses and potential deductions, give back to their communities and plan for future generations. We understand that just as no two athletes or entertainers are alike, your investment strategy and financial wellness will be unique to you. We will recommend a strategy that is appropriate for you and your life goals. And when your goals change, we will work with you to modify your strategy to fit your new plans. Merrill offers comprehensive services with an understanding of the unique challenges of professionals in the sports and entertainment industries. Personalized investment strategies that address unpredictable and/or large sporadic income streams Tax minimization strategies Manage everyday cash and access liquidity — help with planning for your income and cash flow needs, including expense management and budgeting Help figuring out multiple income streams, compensation for use of name, image and likeness, early in career — even while in college Access to Bank of America Loans and mortgages — realize borrowing power to fund your needs, from buying a first home to funding unexpected opportunities (i.e., vacation home, securities-based lending, customized lending, custom mortgages) Plan for a fulfilling retirement and help with understanding league-sponsored pension plan characteristics and league-specific 401(k) benefits Give back to loved ones and community — our Private Wealth Services group will work with you to determine the legacy you want to create for yourself, your family and chosen causes Preserve income and assets — thoughtful planning and insurance solutions that can help protect the things you care most about — your family, business and assets Legal services referrals Discover more with Merrill Work one-on-one with a Merrill advisor for more insights and personalized guidance. Connect with us today. Loading... Have us contact you Contact me Try Merrill Advisor Match Match with advisors Phone Loading... Email Loading... View your advisor 1 Merrill, its affiliates, and financial advisors do not provide legal, tax, or accounting advice. You should consult your legal and/or tax advisors before making any financial decisions. 2 Nick Overbay, A Uniform Application of the Jock Tax: The Need for Congressional Action, 27 Marq. Sports L. Rev. 217 (2016) Available at: http://scholarship.law.marquette.edu/sportslaw/vol27/iss1/9 The BullPen Timely insights to stay ahead of the curve September 9, 2026 Today's Markets Market briefs August 18, 2026 Financial Planning Planning for college: Investment accounts to help you pay for education July 21, 2026 Financial Planning Charitable giving unlocked for business owners under the OBBBA July 17, 2026 Financial Planning HSAs explained: The top benefits of health savings accounts July 15, 2026 Retirement Ways to replace your salary in retirement Related Articles Sports & Entertainment Professionals Stepping It Up: Wealth Planning for Athletes and Entertainers Financial Education Matters: A Conversation with Darren Woodson","title":"Seven tax planning tips for professional athletes and entertainers","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/tax-planning-tips-for-athletes-and-entertainers.html","id":646},{"content_hash":"d2b187fd3cd906fcb492e666aee678ef493b1e78ceba3502d7bf5079cd403012","domain":"healthline.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:46.213615+00:00","final_url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/depression/cyclothymia","language":"","text":"Cyclothymia, or cyclothymic disorder, is a mild mood disorder with symptoms similar to bipolar II disorder. But the two differ in intensity. Treatment can help manage the symptoms.Both cyclothymia and bipolar disorder cause emotional ups and downs, from manic highs to depressive lows. Cyclothymia is characterized by fluctuating low-level depressive symptoms along with periods of mild mania (hypomania). Symptoms must be present for at least two years before a diagnosis of cyclothymia may be made (one year in children). These changes in mood tend to occur in cycles, reaching highs and lows. In between these highs and lows, you may feel like your mood is stable.The main difference between the two disorders is intensity. The mood swings associated with cyclothymia are not as extreme as those that come with bipolar disorder: Those with bipolar disorder experience intense symptoms that meet clinically criteria for the diagnoses of mania and major depression, while those with cyclothymia experience milder âups and down,â described as hypomania and mild depression. If left untreated, cyclothymia can increase your risk of developing bipolar disorder. The condition usually develops in adolescence. People with the disorder often appear to function normally, although they may seem âmoodyâ or âdifficultâ to others. People will often not seek treatment because the mood swings do not seem severe. People with cyclothymia may occasionally even be hyper-productive.According to the most recent Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-V), cyclothymia is distinguished from bipolar disorder because it lacks the full criteria of major depression, mania, or a mixed episode disorder. However, some people with cyclothymia will develop bipolar I or bipolar II disorder later in life. What Are the Symptoms of Cyclothymia?People with cyclothymia usually experience many weeks of low-level depression followed by an episode of mild mania that lasts several days.Depressive symptoms of cyclothymia may include:irritabilityaggressivenessinsomnia or hypersomnia (sleeping too much)changes in appetiteweight loss or gainfatigue or low energylow sexual desire and functionfeelings of hopelessness, worthlessness, or guiltinattentiveness, lack of concentration, or forgetfulnessunexplained physical symptomsThe manic symptoms of cyclothymia may include:extremely high self-esteemexcessive talking or speaking very quickly, sometimes so fast others have trouble following what the person is sayingracing thoughts (muddled and disorganized)lack of focusrestlessness and hyperactivityincreased anxietygoing for days with little or no sleep (without feeling tired)argumentativehypersexualityreckless or impulsive behaviorSome patients experience âmixed periods,â in which a combination of both manic and depressed symptoms occur within a very short time â one followed immediately by the other. How Is Cyclothymia Diagnosed?Researchers are not sure what causes or triggers symptoms of cyclothymia. The condition is, however, known to run in families.A person does not have cyclothymia if they feel symptom-free for more than two months. To distinguish cyclothymia from regular moodiness, your doctor will compare your symptoms to the following clinical criteria: many periods of elevated mood (hypomania) and depression for at least two years (one year in children and teens) occurring at least half of the timeperiods of stable moods lasting less than two monthssymptoms that socially impact your daily life â at school, work, etc.symptoms that donât meet the criteria for bipolar disorder, major depression or another mental disordersymptoms not caused by substance abuse or another medical conditionYour doctor will discuss your symptoms and medical history with you. He/she may also ask you questions about your use of drugs or alcohol.Lab tests may also be performed to rule out other medical conditions that may be causing the symptoms. What Are the Treatments for Cyclothymia?Cyclothymia is a chronic condition that will require lifelong treatment. If you stop taking medications â even during periods of remission â your symptoms will return.Because cyclothymia may develop into bipolar disorder, it is crucial that you receive appropriate treatments. Alcohol and drug use may increase your symptoms as well.The main types of medications used to treat cyclothymia include:mood stabilizers such as lithiumanti-seizure medications (also known as anticonvulsants) include divalproex sodium (Depakote), lamotrigine (Lamictal), and valproic acid (Depakene)atypical antipsychotic medications such as olanzapine (Zyprexa), quetiapine (Seroquel) and risperidone (Risperdal) may help patients who donât respond to anti-seizure medicationsanti-anxiety medications such as benzodiazepineantidepressants should only be used in conjunction with a mood stabilizer as they may cause potentially harmful manic episodes when taken on their ownPsychotherapy is considered a vital part of the treatment of cyclothymia. The two primary types of psychotherapy used to treat cyclothymia are cognitive behavior therapy and well-being therapy. Cognitive behavioral therapy focuses on identifying negative or unhealthy beliefs and behaviors and replacing them with positive or healthy ones. It may also help you manage stress and develop coping techniques. Well-being therapy focuses on improving overall quality of life rather than fixing specific psychological symptoms. One recent clinical study found that a combination of cognitive behavioral therapy and well-being therapy bring significant improvements to the lives of patients with cyclothymia. Other types of therapy that may benefit patients include talk, family, or group therapy.Online therapy optionsRead our review of the best online therapy options to find the right fit for you. What Is the Outlook for Cyclothymia?There is no cure for cyclothymia, but there are treatments that will help you manage your symptoms. Your doctor will help you create a treatment plan that will most likely include a combination of medication and therapy. It can be tempting to stop taking your medication or attending therapy sessions during episodes of hypomania. But itâs very important that you stick with your treatment plan.","title":"Cyclothymia","url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/depression/cyclothymia","id":647},{"content_hash":"179c774c1553173f6370ac6829813c401bc9aaecd10005dc3121e94b4213713e","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:45.974072+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/l-opinion-de-la-presse-parisienne_2072953_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Le rêve passe? Le discours de M. Vincent Auriol retient toute l'attention de nos confrères du matin. Le président de la République \" a dit tout haut ce que pensent tous les hommes de tous les pays \", estime M. Massip, dans le Figaro. Il a traduit \" le sentiment unanime du peuple français \", ajoute M. Fourrier dans Libération, \" Paroles émouvantes \", dit Ce mutin. Mais si tout cela n'était qu'un rêve ? Franc-Tireur se veut lucide et interroge les \" réalités \". Une mise au point du Quai d'Orsay a indiqué que les paroles prononcées par le président de la République ne comportaient pas de suite pratique. \" Pourquoi faut-il, demande M. Ronsac, que des consignes tenues de haut aient donné l'impression qu'on cherchait à réduire la portée du discours Auriol en le ramenant à un souhait platonique partagé par l'ensemble du peuple français ?... \" Même si c'est la plus commune illusion de croire que la sagesse s'impose toute seule et que la raison triomphe par sa propre vertu, le discours présidentiel prend sa portée et trouve sa valeur exacte dans les limites et les possibilités de l'O.N.U. Et M. Robert Verdier, du Populaire, constate : \" Les années qui viennent de s'écouler ont montré que si l'Organisation internationale avait survécu à tous les orages et accompli malgré toutes les difficultés une œuvre fort utile, notamment dans ses organismes spécialisés, elle ne pouvait cependant remplir pleinement sa tâche aussi longtemps que le désaccord entre les grandes puissances persisterait. C'est donc aux \" Grands \" qu'il appartient, dans une première phase, de créer les conditions nécessaires à une action plus efficace de l'Organisation des Nations unies. \" M. Raymond Aron le dit plus nettement encore dans le Figaro : \" Les Occidentaux attendent beaucoup de l'actuelle session, parce qu'ils comptent sur la reprise du dialogue. Mais les monologues alternés, chacun chargé de propagande et d'invectives, sont à peine la caricature d'un dialogue. Qu'on la loue ou qu'on la blâme, on aurait tort de surestimer l'importance d'une organisation qui reflète le monde bien plus qu'elle ne le dirige. \" Il vous reste 51.85% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"L'opinion de la presse parisienne","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/l-opinion-de-la-presse-parisienne_2072953_1819218.html","id":648},{"content_hash":"7618d0da4ffc508b8f4c9fa7d043065be577f89813a69f706c09f689b58069d5","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:45.906754+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/healthy-older-workers-can-boost-the-economy-13652","language":"","text":"Healthy older workers can boost the economy Published: April 21, 2013 10:42pm EDT https://theconversation.com/healthy-older-workers-can-boost-the-economy-13652 https://theconversation.com/healthy-older-workers-can-boost-the-economy-13652 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article New research from Monash University has found Australia’s employment and real GDP could increase significantly if older workers managed their health better. The research looked at the relationship between health and workforce participation to analyse the effects of chronic disease. Dr George Verikios of the Centre of Policy Studies found significant economic benefits for improved health in older workers. “We found health improvements for 10 per cent of the unhealthiest older workers (those aged 49-60) could have strong effects on the country’s economy. \"We estimate employment could rise by 0.13 per cent and real Gross Domestic Product (GDP) by 0.1 per cent over the period 2011-2030. Similar improvements in health status for younger workers (those aged 29-38) lead to much smaller effects with employment forecast to rise by 0.01 per cent and real GDP by 0.008 per cent.” Read more at Monash University Source Monash University","title":"Healthy older workers can boost the economy","url":"https://theconversation.com/healthy-older-workers-can-boost-the-economy-13652","id":649},{"content_hash":"00d353c1a9788780182b1431d6d2639d5d66ea15c12eddd9b5779b7f55cfe6aa","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:44.734057+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/la-mort-d-alphonse-de-chateaubriant_2072593_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Nous avons reçu la lettre suivante : Je lis dans votre numéro daté 30 octobre sous le titre général \" Alphonse de Châteaubriant est mort au Tyrol \" une note signée R. G. que tout autorise à attribuer à votre chroniqueur littéraire, M. Robert Coiplet. Cette note contient d'une part un certain nombre d'erreurs de fait, d'autre part une appréciation si contraire à l'évidence que je me crois, ne serait-ce que par égard à la simple vérité, le devoir de relever. Sur le premier point, qui touche à la biographie d'Alphonse de Châteaubriant, la thèse que M. Coiplet avance doit être corrigée par les précisions suivantes : Mon père n'a pas passé son enfance dans un château médiéval, mais dans la propriété de son père, 7, avenue du Calvaire, à Nantes. Au lycée il a eu entre autres professeurs M. Edouard Herriot. Il n'est pas né en 1882 mais en 1877. Il ne débuta pas par Monsieur des Lourdinés, publié en 1910 et non en 1911, mais avec Instantanés aux Pays-Bas. Mon père n'a jamais eu le prix de littérature de l'Académie, mais pour la Brière le Grand Prix du roman. Entre la Réponse du Seigneur et la Gerbe de forces votre collaborateur omet de citer deux livres de caractère purement littéraire, la Meute et les Pas ont chanté. Ces précisions, entre autres, semblent de nature à infirmer la thèse de votre collaborateur qu'il résume en une phrase : \" Alphonse de Châteaubriant n'était pas un écrivain. \" Sur cette appréciation et sur la preuve que M. Coiplet en trouve dans la Brière, il nous suffirait sans doute d'en appeler aux quelque cinq cent mille lecteurs que le livre compte dans le public de langue française, sans compter les pays de langue anglaise, allemande, russe, espagnole, italienne, portugaise, roumaine, etc. Mais s'il s'agit d'opposer la compétence de M. Coiplet à d'autres, nous citerons seulement les témoignages de Romain Rolland, d'Albert Thibaudet, d'Edmond Jaloux, de Lucien Descaves, de Henry de Régnier et les 1700 articles de presse parus au moment de la publication de cet ouvrage. Il vous reste 27.59% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"La mort d'Alphonse de Châteaubriant","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/la-mort-d-alphonse-de-chateaubriant_2072593_1819218.html","id":650},{"content_hash":"0876ce621ba5ee48f7fbc0b40b3637b5a1f8e87560b6ef02002b74457b700803","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:44.692198+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/video-games-can-help-reverse-cognitive-decline-13902","language":"","text":"Video games can help reverse cognitive decline Published: May 2, 2013 2:28am EDT https://theconversation.com/video-games-can-help-reverse-cognitive-decline-13902 https://theconversation.com/video-games-can-help-reverse-cognitive-decline-13902 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Researchers at the University of Iowa had groups of people aged 50 to 64 years old and those over 65 play a video game named Road Tour. The game involves identifying a vehicle and matching it was a road sign. Researchers found that, a year after testing, those who played the video game for ten hours gained at least three years of cognitive improvement. Playing the game an extra four hours added another year worth of improvement. Read more at University of Iowa Source University of Iowa Tags Cognitive decline, Video games","title":"Video games can help reverse cognitive decline","url":"https://theconversation.com/video-games-can-help-reverse-cognitive-decline-13902","id":651},{"content_hash":"898f0b90862d9a177f398afd8f5fa7816e7906136c61fb9a28704f7633091461","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:44.469133+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/kyc-aml-compliance-for-user-onboarding","language":"","text":"Step-by-Step Guide to KYC/AML Compliance For Onboarding in the USByTemitope LawalJune 16, 2024•5mins ReadWhat are KYC AML Compliance Regulations in User OnboardingWho Needs to Comply with KYC/AML Regulations?What is the Role of FinCEN in KYC/AML Compliance?Step-by-Step Guide to KYC/AML Compliance in User Onboarding in the United StatesBest Practices for KYC/AML Compliance for User OnboardingConclusionShare this post Today, user onboarding is all about speed and convenience. Customers expect a frictionless sign-up process. But for businesses in the financial sector, striking a balance between user onboarding and regulatory compliance can seem difficult. Know Your Customer (KYC) and Anti-Money Laundering (AML) regulations ensure it is not a playground for money launderers and terrorist financiers.Money laundering, estimated at €715 billion to €1.87 trillion annually (2-5% of global GDP), poses a significant threat to the global economy, according to the UNODC.The US is a prime target for these illicit activities. To combat this threat, KYC AML regulations play a crucial role in safeguarding the financial system and protecting legitimate businesses.This article will equip US businesses requiring digital identification services with the knowledge and tools for KYC AML compliance when onboarding their new customers and employees. We'll go into the regulations, explore the role of key players, and provide a step-by-step approach to achieve a compliant and user-friendly onboarding process. What are KYC AML Compliance Regulations in User Onboarding KYC AML stands for Know Your Customer/Anti-Money Laundering. These regulations are designed to prevent financial crime by requiring businesses to verify the identities of their customers and monitor their transactions for suspicious activity.The fight against financial crimes like money laundering and terrorism financing is a global effort. The US has established a powerful framework through the Money Laundering Control Act of 1986 and its participation in the Financial Action Task Force (FATF). These regulations aim to prevent criminals from using the financial system for illicit activities. A guide to KYC AML compliance will help you understand how to implement these regulations in user onboarding. Who Needs to Comply with KYC/AML Regulations?A wide range of financial institutions in the US are subject to KYC anti-money laundering regulations. Here are some key players who need to be compliant:• Banks: This includes traditional banks, credit unions, and online banking platforms.• Money Service Businesses (MSBs): These businesses provide services like money transfers, check cashing, and currency exchange.• Insurance Companies: Insurance providers are required to implement KYC AML procedures to prevent fraudulent insurance claims.• Brokers/Dealers in Securities: These entities dealing with securities like stocks and bonds need to comply with KYC AML regulations.• Domestic and Foreign Financial Institutions with US Operations: Even foreign financial institutions with a presence in the US must adhere to KYC/AML rules. What is the Role of FinCEN in KYC/AML Compliance?The Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), a bureau under the US Department of the Treasury, is the main regulatory body overseeing AML compliance. FinCEN plays an essential role in the following ways:• Issuing Regulations: They establish and enforce Anti-Money Laundering Counter-Terrorism Financing (AML/CFT) regulations for financial institutions.• Collecting Data: FinCEN collects Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) and Currency Transaction Reports (CTRs) filed by financial institutions, acting as a repository of financial intelligence for law enforcement.• Enforcing Compliance: FinCEN investigates and penalizes institutions found to be non-compliant with AML regulations.Further reading: A Comprehensive Guide to AML Compliance Reporting Step-by-Step Guide to KYC/AML Compliance in User Onboarding in the United StatesNow that we understand the context, let's dig into the practical steps for achieving KYC AML compliance in your user onboarding process: 1. Establish Policies and Procedures:• Develop a risk-based AML program tailored to your specific business model and the risks associated with your customer base and industry.• The program should outline your approach to customer identification, verification, risk assessment, transaction monitoring, and reporting.• Consider seeking guidance from legal professionals or AML compliance consultants to ensure your program aligns with the latest regulations. 2. Customer Identification Program (CIP):Implement a Customer Identification Program (CIP) to collect essential information from your customers. This includes:Full NameDate of BirthAddressGovernment-issued ID number (e.g., Social Security Number) 3. Document Verification:Verify the collected customer information with reliable documentation. This may involve obtaining and verifying:Government-issued photo IDs (driver's license, passport)Documents confirming your addressAlso, consider using digital verification providers like Youverify to streamline this process. Youverify utilizes advanced document verification technology and secure data connections to ensure the authenticity of submitted documents, saving you time and resources. 4. Risk Assessment:Customer type (individual, corporation)Geographic location (higher risk jurisdictions)Source of fundsTransaction history 5. Ongoing Monitoring:Continuously monitor customer transactions for suspicious activities that could indicate money laundering or terrorist financing. Red flags to watch for include:Transactions exceeding expected activity levels and lacking a demonstrable economic justificationTransactions involving high-risk countries or individualsUnusual activity patterns compared to established customer behaviour 6. Reporting:File Suspicious Activity Reports (SARs) with FinCEN when you suspect illegal activity. SARs are crucial for law enforcement agencies to investigate potential financial crimes.Additionally, certain cash transactions exceeding a specific threshold require filing Currency Transaction Reports (CTRs) with FinCEN.Following the above KYC?AML compliance for user onboarding can be easier when organizations make use of the best digital customer onboarding software. Best Practices for KYC/AML Compliance for User Onboarding 1. Streamlining the KYC process is key to maintaining a user-friendly onboarding experience that fosters high conversion rates while adhering to regulations. Here are some additional tips:2. Simplify the Process: Aim for a user-friendly onboarding experience. Leverage digital solutions like Youverify to automate document verification and minimize friction for your customers.3. Stay Updated: The regulatory outlook is constantly developing. Regularly review and update your AML program to ensure compliance with the latest FinCEN guidelines.4. Invest in Technology: Consider utilizing digital verification providers like Youverify. Youverify offers a suite of KYC/AML tools that streamline customer onboarding, enhance data security, and free up your resources to focus on core business activities. Youverify's solutions are built with compliance in mind, ensuring your adherence to KYC AML regulations. ConclusionKYC/AML compliance might seem frightening at first, but with the right approach, it doesn't have to be a hindrance. By following the steps outlined above and embracing technological advancements like Youverify's digital verification solutions, you can achieve a flawless onboarding process that meets regulatory requirements and provides a positive user experience. A compliant and secure onboarding process is about safeguarding your business, protecting your customers, and building a foundation for sustainable growth.Ready to simplify your KYC/AML compliance and simplify your user onboarding process? Visit Youverify today! We offer a range of digital verification solutions custom-fit to meet the specific needs of your business. Let Youverify help you achieve effortless compliance and focus on what matters most to growing your business. Related ArticlesKnow Your Customer (KYC)Emmanuel Agwu•April 26, 2022What is KYC in Finance?Read MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Temitope Lawal•November 2, 2022CBN Tiered KYC Requirements: What Banks and Fintechs Must Verify at Each TierRead MoreKnow Your Customer (KYC)Lola, Edited by Emmanuel Agwu•April 25, 2023Why is Negative News Screening (NNS) Important?Read More","title":"Step-by-Step Guide to KYC/AML Compliance For Onboarding in the US","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/kyc-aml-compliance-for-user-onboarding","id":652},{"content_hash":"fdcb7e52c91236d154502d6bc39dac4b1bb6d325006ec96535564295ef01446d","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:44.112613+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/fields/","language":"","text":"PostgreSQL specific model fields¶ All of these fields are available from the django.contrib.postgres.fields module. Indexing these fields¶ Index and Field.db_index both create a B-tree index, which isn’t particularly helpful when querying complex data types. Indexes such as GinIndex and GistIndex are better suited, though the index choice is dependent on the queries that you’re using. Generally, GiST may be a good choice for the range fields and HStoreField, and GIN may be helpful for ArrayField. ArrayField¶ class ArrayField(base_field, size=None, **options)¶ A field for storing lists of data. Most field types can be used, and you pass another field instance as the base_field. You may also specify a size. ArrayField can be nested to store multi-dimensional arrays. If you give the field a default, ensure it’s a callable such as list (for an empty default) or a callable that returns a list (such as a function). Incorrectly using default=[] creates a mutable default that is shared between all instances of ArrayField. base_field¶ This is a required argument. Specifies the underlying data type and behavior for the array. It should be an instance of a subclass of Field. For example, it could be an IntegerField or a CharField. Most field types are permitted, with the exception of those handling relational data (ForeignKey, OneToOneField and ManyToManyField) and file fields (FileField and ImageField). It is possible to nest array fields - you can specify an instance of ArrayField as the base_field. For example: from django.contrib.postgres.fields import ArrayField from django.db import models class ChessBoard(models.Model): board = ArrayField( ArrayField( models.CharField(max_length=10, blank=True), size=8, ), size=8, ) Transformation of values between the database and the model, validation of data and configuration, and serialization are all delegated to the underlying base field. size¶ This is an optional argument. If passed, the array will have a maximum size as specified. This will be passed to the database, although PostgreSQL at present does not enforce the restriction. Note When nesting ArrayField, whether you use the size parameter or not, PostgreSQL requires that the arrays are rectangular: from django.contrib.postgres.fields import ArrayField from django.db import models class Board(models.Model): pieces = ArrayField(ArrayField(models.IntegerField())) # Valid Board( pieces=[ [2, 3], [2, 1], ] ) # Not valid Board( pieces=[ [2, 3], [2], ] ) If irregular shapes are required, then the underlying field should be made nullable and the values padded with None. Querying ArrayField¶ There are a number of custom lookups and transforms for ArrayField. We will use the following example model: from django.contrib.postgres.fields import ArrayField from django.db import models class Post(models.Model): name = models.CharField(max_length=200) tags = ArrayField(models.CharField(max_length=200), blank=True) def __str__(self): return self.name contains¶ The contains lookup is overridden on ArrayField. The returned objects will be those where the values passed are a subset of the data. It uses the SQL operator @>. For example: >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"First post\", tags=[\"thoughts\", \"django\"]) >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"Second post\", tags=[\"thoughts\"]) >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"Third post\", tags=[\"tutorial\", \"django\"]) >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__contains=[\"thoughts\"]) <QuerySet [<Post: First post>, <Post: Second post>]> >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__contains=[\"django\"]) <QuerySet [<Post: First post>, <Post: Third post>]> >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__contains=[\"django\", \"thoughts\"]) <QuerySet [<Post: First post>]> contained_by¶ This is the inverse of the contains lookup - the objects returned will be those where the data is a subset of the values passed. It uses the SQL operator <@. For example: >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"First post\", tags=[\"thoughts\", \"django\"]) >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"Second post\", tags=[\"thoughts\"]) >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"Third post\", tags=[\"tutorial\", \"django\"]) >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__contained_by=[\"thoughts\", \"django\"]) <QuerySet [<Post: First post>, <Post: Second post>]> >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__contained_by=[\"thoughts\", \"django\", \"tutorial\"]) <QuerySet [<Post: First post>, <Post: Second post>, <Post: Third post>]> overlap¶ Returns objects where the data shares any results with the values passed. Uses the SQL operator &&. For example: >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"First post\", tags=[\"thoughts\", \"django\"]) >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"Second post\", tags=[\"thoughts\", \"tutorial\"]) >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"Third post\", tags=[\"tutorial\", \"django\"]) >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__overlap=[\"thoughts\"]) <QuerySet [<Post: First post>, <Post: Second post>]> >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__overlap=[\"thoughts\", \"tutorial\"]) <QuerySet [<Post: First post>, <Post: Second post>, <Post: Third post>]> >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__overlap=Post.objects.values_list(\"tags\")) <QuerySet [<Post: First post>, <Post: Second post>, <Post: Third post>]> len¶ Returns the length of the array. The lookups available afterward are those available for IntegerField. For example: >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"First post\", tags=[\"thoughts\", \"django\"]) >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"Second post\", tags=[\"thoughts\"]) >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__len=1) <QuerySet [<Post: Second post>]> Index transforms¶ Index transforms index into the array. Any non-negative integer can be used. There are no errors if it exceeds the size of the array. The lookups available after the transform are those from the base_field. For example: >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"First post\", tags=[\"thoughts\", \"django\"]) >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"Second post\", tags=[\"thoughts\"]) >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__0=\"thoughts\") <QuerySet [<Post: First post>, <Post: Second post>]> >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__1__iexact=\"Django\") <QuerySet [<Post: First post>]> >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__276=\"javascript\") <QuerySet []> Note PostgreSQL uses 1-based indexing for array fields when writing raw SQL. However these indexes and those used in slices use 0-based indexing to be consistent with Python. Slice transforms¶ Slice transforms take a slice of the array. Any two non-negative integers can be used, separated by a single underscore. The lookups available after the transform do not change. For example: >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"First post\", tags=[\"thoughts\", \"django\"]) >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"Second post\", tags=[\"thoughts\"]) >>> Post.objects.create(name=\"Third post\", tags=[\"django\", \"python\", \"thoughts\"]) >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__0_1=[\"thoughts\"]) <QuerySet [<Post: First post>, <Post: Second post>]> >>> Post.objects.filter(tags__0_2__contains=[\"thoughts\"]) <QuerySet [<Post: First post>, <Post: Second post>]> Note PostgreSQL uses 1-based indexing for array fields when writing raw SQL. However these slices and those used in indexes use 0-based indexing to be consistent with Python. Multidimensional arrays with indexes and slices PostgreSQL has some rather esoteric behavior when using indexes and slices on multidimensional arrays. It will always work to use indexes to reach down to the final underlying data, but most other slices behave strangely at the database level and cannot be supported in a logical, consistent fashion by Django. HStoreField¶ class HStoreField(**options)¶ A field for storing key-value pairs. The Python data type used is a dict. Keys must be strings, and values may be either strings or nulls (None in Python). To use this field, you’ll need to: Add 'django.contrib.postgres' in your INSTALLED_APPS. Set up the hstore extension in PostgreSQL. You’ll see an error like can't adapt type 'dict' if you skip the first step, or type \"hstore\" does not exist if you skip the second. Note On occasions it may be useful to require or restrict the keys which are valid for a given field. This can be done using the KeysValidator. Querying HStoreField¶ In addition to the ability to query by key, there are a number of custom lookups available for HStoreField. We will use the following example model: from django.contrib.postgres.fields import HStoreField from django.db import models class Dog(models.Model): name = models.CharField(max_length=200) data = HStoreField() def __str__(self): return self.name Key lookups¶ To query based on a given key, you can use that key as the lookup name: >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Rufus\", data={\"breed\": \"labrador\"}) >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Meg\", data={\"breed\": \"collie\"}) >>> Dog.objects.filter(data__breed=\"collie\") <QuerySet [<Dog: Meg>]> You can chain other lookups after key lookups: >>> Dog.objects.filter(data__breed__contains=\"l\") <QuerySet [<Dog: Rufus>, <Dog: Meg>]> or use F() expressions to annotate a key value. For example: >>> from django.db.models import F >>> rufus = Dog.objects.annotate(breed=F(\"data__breed\"))[0] >>> rufus.breed 'labrador' If the key you wish to query by clashes with the name of another lookup, you need to use the hstorefield.contains lookup instead. Note Key transforms can also be chained with: contains, icontains, endswith, iendswith, iexact, regex, iregex, startswith, and istartswith lookups. Warning Since any string could be a key in a hstore value, any lookup other than those listed below will be interpreted as a key lookup. No errors are raised. Be extra careful for typing mistakes, and always check your queries work as you intend. contains¶ The contains lookup is overridden on HStoreField. The returned objects are those where the given dict of key-value pairs are all contained in the field. It uses the SQL operator @>. For example: >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Rufus\", data={\"breed\": \"labrador\", \"owner\": \"Bob\"}) >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Meg\", data={\"breed\": \"collie\", \"owner\": \"Bob\"}) >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Fred\", data={}) >>> Dog.objects.filter(data__contains={\"owner\": \"Bob\"}) <QuerySet [<Dog: Rufus>, <Dog: Meg>]> >>> Dog.objects.filter(data__contains={\"breed\": \"collie\"}) <QuerySet [<Dog: Meg>]> contained_by¶ This is the inverse of the contains lookup - the objects returned will be those where the key-value pairs on the object are a subset of those in the value passed. It uses the SQL operator <@. For example: >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Rufus\", data={\"breed\": \"labrador\", \"owner\": \"Bob\"}) >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Meg\", data={\"breed\": \"collie\", \"owner\": \"Bob\"}) >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Fred\", data={}) >>> Dog.objects.filter(data__contained_by={\"breed\": \"collie\", \"owner\": \"Bob\"}) <QuerySet [<Dog: Meg>, <Dog: Fred>]> >>> Dog.objects.filter(data__contained_by={\"breed\": \"collie\"}) <QuerySet [<Dog: Fred>]> has_key¶ Returns objects where the given key is in the data. Uses the SQL operator ?. For example: >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Rufus\", data={\"breed\": \"labrador\"}) >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Meg\", data={\"breed\": \"collie\", \"owner\": \"Bob\"}) >>> Dog.objects.filter(data__has_key=\"owner\") <QuerySet [<Dog: Meg>]> has_any_keys¶ Returns objects where any of the given keys are in the data. Uses the SQL operator ?|. For example: >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Rufus\", data={\"breed\": \"labrador\"}) >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Meg\", data={\"owner\": \"Bob\"}) >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Fred\", data={}) >>> Dog.objects.filter(data__has_any_keys=[\"owner\", \"breed\"]) <QuerySet [<Dog: Rufus>, <Dog: Meg>]> has_keys¶ Returns objects where all of the given keys are in the data. Uses the SQL operator ?&. For example: >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Rufus\", data={}) >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Meg\", data={\"breed\": \"collie\", \"owner\": \"Bob\"}) >>> Dog.objects.filter(data__has_keys=[\"breed\", \"owner\"]) <QuerySet [<Dog: Meg>]> keys¶ Returns objects where the array of keys is the given value. Note that the order is not guaranteed to be reliable, so this transform is mainly useful for using in conjunction with lookups on ArrayField. Uses the SQL function akeys(). For example: >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Rufus\", data={\"toy\": \"bone\"}) >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Meg\", data={\"breed\": \"collie\", \"owner\": \"Bob\"}) >>> Dog.objects.filter(data__keys__overlap=[\"breed\", \"toy\"]) <QuerySet [<Dog: Rufus>, <Dog: Meg>]> values¶ Returns objects where the array of values is the given value. Note that the order is not guaranteed to be reliable, so this transform is mainly useful for using in conjunction with lookups on ArrayField. Uses the SQL function avals(). For example: >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Rufus\", data={\"breed\": \"labrador\"}) >>> Dog.objects.create(name=\"Meg\", data={\"breed\": \"collie\", \"owner\": \"Bob\"}) >>> Dog.objects.filter(data__values__contains=[\"collie\"]) <QuerySet [<Dog: Meg>]> Range Fields¶ There are five range field types, corresponding to the built-in range types in PostgreSQL. These fields are used to store a range of values; for example the start and end timestamps of an event, or the range of ages an activity is suitable for. All of the range fields translate to psycopg Range objects in Python, but also accept tuples as input if no bounds information is necessary. The default is lower bound included, upper bound excluded, that is [) (see the PostgreSQL documentation for details about different bounds). The default bounds can be changed for non-discrete range fields (DateTimeRangeField and DecimalRangeField) by using the default_bounds argument. PostgreSQL normalizes a range with no points to the empty range A range with equal values specified for an included lower bound and an excluded upper bound, such as Range(datetime.date(2005, 6, 21), datetime.date(2005, 6, 21)) or [4, 4), has no points. PostgreSQL will normalize the value to empty when saving to the database, and the original bound values will be lost. See the PostgreSQL documentation for details. IntegerRangeField¶ class IntegerRangeField(**options)¶ Stores a range of integers. Based on an IntegerField. Represented by an int4range in the database and a django.db.backends.postgresql.psycopg_any.NumericRange in Python. Regardless of the bounds specified when saving the data, PostgreSQL always returns a range in a canonical form that includes the lower bound and excludes the upper bound, that is [). BigIntegerRangeField¶ class BigIntegerRangeField(**options)¶ Stores a range of large integers. Based on a BigIntegerField. Represented by an int8range in the database and a django.db.backends.postgresql.psycopg_any.NumericRange in Python. Regardless of the bounds specified when saving the data, PostgreSQL always returns a range in a canonical form that includes the lower bound and excludes the upper bound, that is [). DecimalRangeField¶ class DecimalRangeField(default_bounds='[)', **options)¶ Stores a range of floating point values. Based on a DecimalField. Represented by a numrange in the database and a django.db.backends.postgresql.psycopg_any.NumericRange in Python. default_bounds¶ Optional. The value of bounds for list and tuple inputs. The default is lower bound included, upper bound excluded, that is [) (see the PostgreSQL documentation for details about different bounds). default_bounds is not used for django.db.backends.postgresql.psycopg_any.NumericRange inputs. DateTimeRangeField¶ class DateTimeRangeField(default_bounds='[)', **options)¶ Stores a range of timestamps. Based on a DateTimeField. Represented by a tstzrange in the database and a django.db.backends.postgresql.psycopg_any.DateTimeTZRange in Python. default_bounds¶ Optional. The value of bounds for list and tuple inputs. The default is lower bound included, upper bound excluded, that is [) (see the PostgreSQL documentation for details about different bounds). default_bounds is not used for django.db.backends.postgresql.psycopg_any.DateTimeTZRange inputs. DateRangeField¶ class DateRangeField(**options)¶ Stores a range of dates. Based on a DateField. Represented by a daterange in the database and a django.db.backends.postgresql.psycopg_any.DateRange in Python. Regardless of the bounds specified when saving the data, PostgreSQL always returns a range in a canonical form that includes the lower bound and excludes the upper bound, that is [). Querying Range Fields¶ There are a number of custom lookups and transforms for range fields. They are available on all the above fields, but we will use the following example model: from django.contrib.postgres.fields import IntegerRangeField from django.db import models class Event(models.Model): name = models.CharField(max_length=200) ages = IntegerRangeField() start = models.DateTimeField() def __str__(self): return self.name We will also use the following example objects: >>> import datetime >>> from django.utils import timezone >>> now = timezone.now() >>> Event.objects.create(name=\"Soft play\", ages=(0, 10), start=now) >>> Event.objects.create( ... name=\"Pub trip\", ages=(21, None), start=now - datetime.timedelta(days=1) ... ) and NumericRange: >>> from django.db.backends.postgresql.psycopg_any import NumericRange Containment functions¶ As with other PostgreSQL fields, there are three standard containment operators: contains, contained_by and overlap, using the SQL operators @>, <@, and && respectively. contains¶ >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__contains=NumericRange(4, 5)) <QuerySet [<Event: Soft play>]> contained_by¶ >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__contained_by=NumericRange(0, 15)) <QuerySet [<Event: Soft play>]> The contained_by lookup is also available on the non-range field types: SmallAutoField, AutoField, BigAutoField, SmallIntegerField, IntegerField, BigIntegerField, DecimalField, FloatField, DateField, and DateTimeField. For example: >>> from django.db.backends.postgresql.psycopg_any import DateTimeTZRange >>> Event.objects.filter( ... start__contained_by=DateTimeTZRange( ... timezone.now() - datetime.timedelta(hours=1), ... timezone.now() + datetime.timedelta(hours=1), ... ), ... ) <QuerySet [<Event: Soft play>]> overlap¶ >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__overlap=NumericRange(8, 12)) <QuerySet [<Event: Soft play>]> Comparison functions¶ Range fields support the standard lookups: lt, gt, lte and gte. These are not particularly helpful - they compare the lower bounds first and then the upper bounds only if necessary. This is also the strategy used to order by a range field. It is better to use the specific range comparison operators. fully_lt¶ The returned ranges are strictly less than the passed range. In other words, all the points in the returned range are less than all those in the passed range. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__fully_lt=NumericRange(11, 15)) <QuerySet [<Event: Soft play>]> fully_gt¶ The returned ranges are strictly greater than the passed range. In other words, the all the points in the returned range are greater than all those in the passed range. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__fully_gt=NumericRange(11, 15)) <QuerySet [<Event: Pub trip>]> not_lt¶ The returned ranges do not contain any points less than the passed range, that is the lower bound of the returned range is at least the lower bound of the passed range. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__not_lt=NumericRange(0, 15)) <QuerySet [<Event: Soft play>, <Event: Pub trip>]> not_gt¶ The returned ranges do not contain any points greater than the passed range, that is the upper bound of the returned range is at most the upper bound of the passed range. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__not_gt=NumericRange(3, 10)) <QuerySet [<Event: Soft play>]> adjacent_to¶ The returned ranges share a bound with the passed range. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__adjacent_to=NumericRange(10, 21)) <QuerySet [<Event: Soft play>, <Event: Pub trip>]> Querying using the bounds¶ Range fields support several extra lookups. startswith¶ Returned objects have the given lower bound. Can be chained to valid lookups for the base field. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__startswith=21) <QuerySet [<Event: Pub trip>]> endswith¶ Returned objects have the given upper bound. Can be chained to valid lookups for the base field. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__endswith=10) <QuerySet [<Event: Soft play>]> isempty¶ Returned objects are empty ranges. Can be chained to valid lookups for a BooleanField. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__isempty=True) <QuerySet []> lower_inc¶ Returns objects that have inclusive or exclusive lower bounds, depending on the boolean value passed. Can be chained to valid lookups for a BooleanField. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__lower_inc=True) <QuerySet [<Event: Soft play>, <Event: Pub trip>]> lower_inf¶ Returns objects that have unbounded (infinite) or bounded lower bound, depending on the boolean value passed. Can be chained to valid lookups for a BooleanField. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__lower_inf=True) <QuerySet []> upper_inc¶ Returns objects that have inclusive or exclusive upper bounds, depending on the boolean value passed. Can be chained to valid lookups for a BooleanField. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__upper_inc=True) <QuerySet []> upper_inf¶ Returns objects that have unbounded (infinite) or bounded upper bound, depending on the boolean value passed. Can be chained to valid lookups for a BooleanField. >>> Event.objects.filter(ages__upper_inf=True) <QuerySet [<Event: Pub trip>]> Defining your own range types¶ PostgreSQL allows the definition of custom range types. Django’s model and form field implementations use base classes below, and psycopg provides a register_range() to allow use of custom range types. class RangeField(**options)¶ Base class for model range fields. base_field¶ The model field class to use. range_type¶ The range type to use. form_field¶ The form field class to use. Should be a subclass of django.contrib.postgres.forms.BaseRangeField. class django.contrib.postgres.forms.BaseRangeField¶ Base class for form range fields. base_field¶ The form field to use. range_type¶ The range type to use. Range operators¶ class RangeOperators¶ PostgreSQL provides a set of SQL operators that can be used together with the range data types (see the PostgreSQL documentation for the full details of range operators). This class is meant as a convenient method to avoid typos. The operator names overlap with the names of corresponding lookups. class RangeOperators: EQUAL = \"=\" NOT_EQUAL = \"<>\" CONTAINS = \"@>\" CONTAINED_BY = \"<@\" OVERLAPS = \"&&\" FULLY_LT = \"<<\" FULLY_GT = \">>\" NOT_LT = \"&>\" NOT_GT = \"&<\" ADJACENT_TO = \"-|-\" RangeBoundary() expressions¶ class RangeBoundary(inclusive_lower=True, inclusive_upper=False)¶ inclusive_lower¶ If True (default), the lower bound is inclusive '[', otherwise it’s exclusive '('. inclusive_upper¶ If False (default), the upper bound is exclusive ')', otherwise it’s inclusive ']'. A RangeBoundary() expression represents the range boundaries. It can be used with a custom range functions that expected boundaries, for example to define ExclusionConstraint. See the PostgreSQL documentation for the full details.","title":"PostgreSQL specific model fields¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/fields/","id":653},{"content_hash":"8ed5e8f8429f93a2f50b9704f8b5b9431494ff62e8cfe5383ea00c02d6a6ec3e","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:43.804796+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/etimes/trending/indian-railways-or-blinkit-passengers-diaper-request-for-6-month-old-gets-a-quick-response-internet-reacts/articleshow/134804310.cms","language":"","text":"Indian Railways or Blinkit? Passenger’s diaper request for 6-month-old gets a quick response, internet reacts | - The Times of IndiaSign InTOITOIhomelifestylerelationshiptrendingHealth+styleparentingsoul searchbeautybooksrecipesfood newstravelhome & gardeneventsspotlightNewsetimeslifestyletrendingIndian Railways or Blinkit? Passenger’s diaper request for 6-month-old gets a quick response, internet reactsTrendingBaby NamesVibhor JainChildren PsychologyIndiGo Flight Lightning StrikeLeo Tolstoy QuoteYellow Pillow StainsBen AffleckKarl DeisserothRam VilasSharad Navratri 2026Baby NamesVibhor JainChildren PsychologyIndiGo Flight Lightning StrikeLeo Tolstoy QuoteYellow Pillow StainsBen AffleckKarl DeisserothRam VilasSharad Navratri 2026Baby NamesVibhor JainChildren PsychologyIndiGo Flight Lightning StrikeLeo Tolstoy QuoteYellow Pillow StainsBen AffleckKarl DeisserothRam VilasSharad Navratri 2026Indian Railways or Blinkit? Passenger’s diaper request for 6-month-old gets a quick response, internet reactsTrending Desk / etimes.in / Oct 09, 2026, 07:56 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint Running out of diapers while travelling with a baby can be stressful, especially when you're on a train and don't have a shop nearby. But one passenger's experience with Indian Railways has caught the internet's attention after a request for diapers was reportedly sorted within minutes.The passenger, Debiprasad, shared the incident on X, explaining that his family had run out of small-sized diapers for their six-month-old baby during the journey. He raised a complaint through RailMadad at 10:07 am. Within 10 minutes, he received calls from RailMadad and the Pantry Car Manager, and the required diapers were arranged at the train's next stoppage.‘This is what transformation looks like’Sharing the experience online, Debiprasad praised the quick response and coordination between the railway staff and the grievance redressal service.“This is what transformation looks like,” he wrote, before explaining how the request was handled. The passenger said the response was prompt, with the diapers arranged at the next stoppage.He described the incident as an example of how digital grievance redressal can make a difference to passengers during train journeys.“From digital grievance redressal to faster, accountable passenger services, this is a very different railway experience. Complaint → Response → Solution,” he added.Internet reacts: ‘Indian Railways as Blinkit?’The post drew mixed reactions online. While several users praised the railway staff for stepping in to help a family travelling with a young baby, others questioned whether passengers should expect the service to arrange essentials they had forgotten to pack.“Truly transformative,” one user commented.“Most people don’t even know these facilities exist. Super,” another wrote.A third user praised the effort, saying, “This service is unavailable anywhere in the world. Using our resources-availability of labour (& providing them with gainful employment) that benefits service receivers -absolutely stellar. Nobody needs such help more than parents of young babies and the elderly.”But not everyone was convinced. Some users felt that carrying enough diapers and other baby essentials was the parents' responsibility.“Basic planning for essentials like that should be non-negotiable. Did you pay for the diapers? If not pls pay for it promptly,” one comment read.Another user questioned why the request had been raised as a complaint, writing, “Uhm, what is to ‘complaint’ about it? Was it their responsibility to have it if you forget it? I do appreciate that they got it for you at the next stop but this is your poor planning. Still don’t get why ‘complaint’. You could’ve ordered via blinkit, Gaya isn’t a small village.”One person summed up the debate with a pointed remark: “You made Indian railways as a Blinkit service.”The incident has sparked a wider conversation about passenger service and preparedness. For some, the quick response showed how a digital grievance system can help travellers in an unexpected situation. For others, it raised a question about where railway assistance ends and personal responsibility begins.Get the latest movie news, reviews, and celebrity updates. Download the TOI App.End of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaTop TrendingAssam Dancer MurderCristiano RonaldoJaime Chapman BoyfriendJerry JonesUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestSimone BilesCJP Chappal ProtestAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTrending StoriesIn SectionEntire WebsiteNeighbors Challenge Estate Plans: Kelce’s Ohio fence fight widens; sewer, wildlife fears growBypoll Election Results 2026 Live Updates: Bypoll counting today for five assembly seats, one Lok Sabha seat in AssamKay Adams’ Reactions Go Viral: Clip eclipses Gronk’s Chargers take; fans ask what happenedHurricane Milton Relief Donations: Brady, Swift fuel $6.1m aid drive; app campaign matches giftsFaith Guides Wedding Plans: Gracie Hunt leans on Christian counseling; calls engagement a vulnerable timeBeauty Retail Tie-Up Deepens: Charlotte Tilbury renews Dallas Cowboys Cheerleaders deal; Amazon adds content-led shoppingCurry-Buffett Charity Auction: Lunch date pulls in $9 million; funds two family charitiesCpkc Stadium Expansion Funding: Kansas City Council clears $235 million; aims to attract World CupsBrady Raiders Stake Soars: NFL legend’s $220 million bet multiplies; franchise valuation hits $13 billionKansas City Giving Machines Launch: Kelce family fronts holiday donations; campaign backs 9 charitiesNeighbors Challenge Estate Plans: Kelce’s Ohio fence fight widens; sewer, wildlife fears growBypoll Election Results 2026 Live Updates: Bypoll counting today for five assembly seats, one Lok Sabha seat in AssamKay Adams’ Reactions Go Viral: Clip eclipses Gronk’s Chargers take; fans ask what happenedHurricane Milton Relief Donations: Brady, Swift fuel $6.1m aid drive; app campaign matches giftsFaith Guides Wedding Plans: Gracie Hunt leans on Christian counseling; calls engagement a vulnerable timeBeauty Retail Tie-Up Deepens: Charlotte Tilbury renews Dallas Cowboys Cheerleaders deal; Amazon adds content-led shoppingCurry-Buffett Charity Auction: Lunch date pulls in $9 million; funds two family charitiesCpkc Stadium Expansion Funding: Kansas City Council clears $235 million; aims to attract World CupsBrady Raiders Stake Soars: NFL legend’s $220 million bet multiplies; franchise valuation hits $13 billionKansas City Giving Machines Launch: Kelce family fronts holiday donations; campaign backs 9 charitiesTired of too many ads?go ad free nowDaily PuzzlesConnectThe Perfect TracePlay Now Loop The LoopPuzzle CircuitPlay Now Daily CrosswordSpecial puzzlePlay Now Explore More PuzzlesMORE FROM ETIMESetimesLatest Movies 2026Best Bollywood Movies 2026Hollywood MovieTamil Movies 2026Telugu Movies 2026Malayalam Movies 2026Latest Kannada MoviesMarathi MoviesBengali Movies 2026Top Rated Movies 2026Living and entertainmentETimesTravel DestinationsCricbuzz.comTVLifestyleNewspaper SubscriptionFood NewsTimes PrimeWhats HotServicesCouponDuniaTechGigTimesJobsBollywood NewsTimes MobileGadgetsWork with UsColombiaHot on the WebNana PatekarNana Patekar Passes AwayShraddha KapoorLeo Tolstoy QuoteRajpal YadavVirat KohliGene HackmanZendayaDrishyam 4Halle BerrySalman KhanNana Patekar Last RitesDrishyam 3 Box Office CollectionNana Patekar WifeTravis BarkerColin FarrellAvengers: DoomsdayRanabaali TrailerDolly PartonHanuman Ansh CollectionEllen DeGeneresDionne WarwickThe Paradise Movie ReviewHanuman Ansh Movie ReviewSanjay DuttSonu NigamBen AffleckBaby NamesVietnam Cambodia TourHoroscope TodayIndia Powerus AgreementAir Force DayEnemy PropertyCumulative Matrimonial ConductApple iPhone CompensationVoltas AC CompensationIAF 94th AnniversaryFPV DronesChina US F-35 Stealth FighterChang'E-6 SoilRarest Rattlesnake BreedCaptions For Instagram PostsTrending TopicsLatest MoviesBollywood MoviesHollywood MoviesTamil Movies 2026Telugu Movies 2026Malayalam Movies 2026Kannada MoviesMarathi MoviesBengali Movies 2026Top Rated Movies 2026Best Hindi MoviesBest English MoviesBest Telugu MoviesBest Tamil MoviesBest Malayalam MoviesBest Kannada MoviesBest Bengali MoviesUpcoming Hindi MoviesBest Movies Of All TimeBest Hindi Movies of All TimeLatest English MoviesLatest Malayalam MoviesEnglish TV NewsTamil TV NewsTelugu TV NewsMalayalam TV NewsKannada TV NewsMovie ReviewsBhojpuri Cinema NewsGujarati Cinema NewsPopular CategoriesViral NewsK Pop NewsWeb Series NewsAnime NewsUpcoming English MoviesUpcoming Tamil MoviesUpcoming Telugu MoviesUpcoming Malayalam MoviesUpcoming Kannada MoviesFashion TipsTravel NewsEntertainment NewsBollywood NewsTollywood NewsKollywood NewsMollywood NewsFood NewsLatest Hindi MoviesLatest Tamil MoviesParenting TipsHome RemediesWeight LossBeauty TipsParenting TipsHindi VideosHindi Video SongsBhojpuri Music VideosLatest Telugu MoviesBhojpuri Music VideoHindi TV NewsLatest NewsRamayana release date advanced in India? Ranbir Kapoor and Yash starrer ditches Diwali release to debut worldwide on November 5 : Reports5 WWE superstars unlikely to win the Money in the Bank 2026 ladder matchCJP alleges Rajasthan police detained Deepak Baliyan, drove him 100km before releasing himThe hidden tax of unpredictability: What a Patna commute reveals about the economyMarvel unveils first logo for live-action spy thriller 'Zodiac'; series to release in 2028Britain rebuilt 3.5 miles of the A46 using 17,432 tonnes of material from the old road; the project cut its carbon footprint by 23%Silambarasan shares 'Arasan' set picture with ‘Peace Over Everything’ note, dismisses Vetri Maaran film shooting rumoursHockey India polls set for Dec 5 amid growing row over state-unit elections'Cyberpunk 2077' movie in development : 'Transformers' producer Lorenzo di Bonaventura attachedAsian Para Games send-off marred by protests over selection processGarba the traditional way: 500 college students revive the spirit of the festivalPsychology says children who ask you to repeat a silly word again and again may be enjoying more than the jokeWhen Bhadran wanted Nana Patekar to replace Mammootty in 'Iyer the Great'; why its Hindi remake never happened; the late actor was also considered for Mohanlal's 'Udayon'India show grit but Russia’s class shines through in 4-0 defeat8 IT firms including TCS, Infosys suspended from US green card programme: How does it hit Indian professionals?EXCLUSIVE | ‘In reality, it really isn’t’: Asian Games gold medallist Pranavi Urs on golf’s ‘rich man’s sport’ imageLove Horoscope Today, October 09, 2026: What the stars say about your love lifeUS plans to livestream Fort Hood shooter’s death; first public execution in 90 yearsCopyright © 2026 Bennett, Coleman & Co. Ltd. All rights reserved. For reprint rights: Times Syndication ServiceFollow us on","title":"Indian Railways or Blinkit? Passenger’s diaper request for 6-month-old gets a quick response, internet reacts","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/etimes/trending/indian-railways-or-blinkit-passengers-diaper-request-for-6-month-old-gets-a-quick-response-internet-reacts/articleshow/134804310.cms","id":654},{"content_hash":"e0f5539c82ed654b0c0d221549a7ae54535b6b90150806d2ad34f576ea547305","domain":"healthline.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:43.757739+00:00","final_url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/depression/ect-treatment-for-depression","language":"","text":"ECT can seem like something out of science fiction, but it actually has decades of studies to show how effective it can be in reducing depression symptoms. Depression is one of the most common mental health conditions across the globe, affecting tens of millions of people in the United States alone. Depression isnât just a mental health condition â itâs also a significant source of disability for many people who have it.When someone has severe depression, it can be difficult to find treatment options that work or work quickly. However, thereâs one option that has proven to be a quick and effective treatment, even in people with treatment-resistant depression: electroshock therapy (ECT).Below, weâll explore the role of ECT treatment for depression, including some of the benefits, side effects, and other important information you need to know about this treatment option.Does ECT work for depression?Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT), also known as electroshock therapy, is a medical procedure used to treat certain mental health conditions, including depression.During an ECT session, mild electrical currents travel to the brain through small electrodes placed on different areas of the scalp. As these currents stimulate the brain, they cause a temporary change in the brainâs activity â in other words, a seizure.Because this procedure happens under general anesthesia, the person is not aware of whatâs happening and does not experience any pain or discomfort.Although electroconvulsive therapy may seem like a drastic option, this procedure is extremely effective for depression. In fact, decades of research have shown that ECT remains one of the most effective treatment options for people with major depression.One research review published in 2022 explored the safety and effectiveness of ketamine versus ECT for treating major depressive disorder (MDD). According to the results, which included six clinical trials spanning 340 participants, ECT was more effective than ketamine at improving depressive symptoms. However, the researchers noted that both treatment options had their own advantages and disadvantages.Another large research review from 2023 also investigated the effectiveness of ECT in adults with MDD. Results of the analysis showed that ECT used alone and alongside antidepressants was effective at reducing relapse and recurrence of MDD.Researchers still arenât entirely sure exactly why ECT is so effective for reducing depressive symptoms. However, some of the more popular theories involve changes in neurotransmitters, hormones, brain anatomy, and brain cell regeneration, to name a few.What are the side effects of an ECT treatment?Electroconvulsive therapy may be an effective treatment option for people with severe depression, but as with any medical procedure, there are risks.Some of the most commonly reported side effects of ECT include:dry mouthnauseaheadachemuscle achesconfusionmemory changesamnesiaMany of the symptoms mentioned above are typically mild and usually only last for a few hours after treatment. However, short-term and long-term memory loss and changes may last for several months for some people.Other rare but serious side effects of ECT include prolonged seizures, heart rate changes, and other effects from anesthesia or medications used during the procedure. Although extremely rare, the risk of death from ECT or ECT complications is roughly .01%.How long do the effects of ECT last?When you live with depression, the goal of treatment is to improve your symptoms and make it easier for you to function in your day-to-day life.Some of the most common treatment options for depression â like therapy and medication â can take weeks or months to start working. However, one of the major benefits of ECT for depression is that itâs a quick-acting treatment with long lasting clinical effects.According to research, the effects of ECT can last upward of a few years, although the results vary depending on the type of ECT, number of sessions, and other factors.How much does ECT treatment cost?ECT therapy for depression generally includes multiple sessions per week that take place over the course of several weeks.Sessions occur 2-3 times a week, and most people start to see improvements in as little as six sessions of ECT. However, people with more severe symptoms or other recurring mood disorders may need more sessions or choose to continue their ECT for longer.Since ECT requires multiple sessions, one potential downside of this treatment option for depression is the cost. One 2018 study found that average out-of-pocket treatment costs for ECT ranged from $300 to upward of $1,000 per session.With that said, because ECT has a long history of use as an effective treatment, most major insurance plans â as well as Medicaid and Medicare â may cover ECT for depression.TakeawayElectroconvulsive therapy (ECT) is a medical procedure that involves the use of electrical stimulation to relieve the symptoms of depression and other mood disorders. ECT is one of the most effective treatments for depression, especially for symptoms that havenât responded to other treatments.If youâre interested in trying ECT for severe or treatment-resistant depression, consider reaching out to your doctor to learn more about your options.","title":"Your Guide to ECT Treatment for Depression","url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/depression/ect-treatment-for-depression","id":655},{"content_hash":"cc0091466625e0a4ea9661cb0773fcba95da80e8be20e61f5aa3ce35c7d2ebd7","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:43.564521+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/l-armee-europeenne-comprendrait-quarante-trois-groupements-nationaux_2073368_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives L'armée européenne comprendrait quarante-trois \" groupements nationaux \" Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"L'armée européenne comprendrait quarante-trois \" groupements nationaux \"","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/l-armee-europeenne-comprendrait-quarante-trois-groupements-nationaux_2073368_1819218.html","id":656},{"content_hash":"61887c4de2c92441931d11d990e19afe80290ab4399234b057348773d8fa54a6","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:43.500401+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/global-warming-range-narrowed-14752","language":"","text":"Global warming range narrowed Published: May 28, 2013 10:37pm EDT https://theconversation.com/global-warming-range-narrowed-14752 https://theconversation.com/global-warming-range-narrowed-14752 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Research from the University of Melbourne and Victoria University has generated more reliable global warming estimates at 2100. Researchers used a new method combining carbon dioxide observations and global temperature variations with simple climate model simulations to project future global warming. Exceeding six degrees of warming is now believed to be unlikely, while exceeding two degrees is very likely with the current emissions rate. Read more at University of Melbourne and Victoria University Source University of Melbourne and Victoria University Tags Climate change, Carbon emissions, Global warming","title":"Global warming range narrowed","url":"https://theconversation.com/global-warming-range-narrowed-14752","id":657},{"content_hash":"a2d0262831e09a640383cc0b9083bb0d43479f16e268c63f09cdef7a7161a8b8","domain":"reallygoodemails.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:43.373864+00:00","final_url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/asian-restaurant","language":"","text":"Asian Restaurant | Email template - RGE StudioNow you have zero excuses to NOT design really good emails. Introducing RGE StudioRead more Find inspirationAll categoriesBrowse by typePopular right nowBehavioral Enhancement Inaugural Industry Miscellaneous Promotional Punctual Seasonal Templates BehavioralAbandoned CartComment NotificationCurated ContentCustomer AppreciationCustomer ServiceDiscoveryEngagementFeedback and Survey and PollFollow-UpLoyalty / RewardsPost Purchase / Recent PurchaseProduct RecommendationsReferralRetention / Re-engagementReview and TestimonialSummaryThank YouUnsubscribeCurated by humans since 2014.Browse all categoriesPopular right nowCreate emailsProductWhat is RGE StudioThe email builder that gets you.FeaturesEverything you need to make email happen.Product tourSnoop around at your own pace.Works withEmail & page templatesStart from something really good.IntegrationsYour favorite ESP or CRM can come too.Salesforce ecosystemCreate in Studio, send from Salesforce.MCPBuild emails from your AI tools.SolutionsHigher educationBetter email for busy campus teams.NonprofitsMore mission, less busywork.IntegrationPerfect for Salesforce usersThe perfect addition to your Salesforce setup? An email editor that actually works.Learn moreSOC 2 Type II · ISO 27001 · DPF · AWS PartnerPricingTalk to usResourcesLearnBlogTips, trends, and teardowns.AcademyGet better at email. For free.Help centerStuck with RGE Studio? Start here.ConnectVirtual eventsCome learn stuff with email people.UnspamFor folks who hate boring email conferences.Feedback FridayReal emails, honest critique.NewsletterReally good stuff, in your inbox.ToolsAI prompt librarySmart starting points for your emails.Email signature generatorLook pro in every sign-off.Report2026 RGE Email SurveyFresh data on how email teams actually work: email got faster, but not for everyone.Read the report300,000 email pros learn and hang out here.Next webinarsJoin the newsletterPricingTalk to SalesBack to the template catalogREALLY GOOD EMAILSAboutSchoolFeedback FridayEventsContactCode of ConductEmail signature generatorPrompt libraryRGE STUDIOProduct TourEmail & page templatesWebinarsHelp center SOLUTIONSHigher EducationNonprofitsFEATURESFeaturesMCP INTEGRATIONSIntegrationsSalesforce Marketing CloudSalesforce Account Engagement PardotSlateHubSpotSUBSCRIBE TO OUR NEWSLETTERGet weekly inspiration in your inbox.By subscribing you agree with our Privacy Policy Thank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. Really Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.©2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCA Cookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice Can we be honest?We want your email address. Let us send you the best emails designs and links twice a week.Name*Email*I’m okay with getting emails and having that activity tracked to improve my experience, and I agree to the Privacy PolicyThank you! Your submission has been received!Oops! Something went wrong while submitting the form. DisclaimerWe’ve updated our Privacy Policy. We encourage you to review it here.Legal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"Asian Restaurant | Email template - RGE Studio","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/template/asian-restaurant","id":658},{"content_hash":"7f42bae300e59436eb51707de76f17a6f562c0fba75659b62b577d29c916e3d5","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:43.095924+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/jacinta-bowler/103583808","language":"","text":"Jacinta Bowler - ABC NewsSkip to main contentSkip to news navigation, settings and searchExplore the ABC More from ABCClose menuABC ABC News ABC iview ABC listen Kids & Students Indigenous Information & ServicesEditorial PoliciesRead our editorial guiding principlesFollow the ABCnewsiviewlistenLoadingABC NewsJust InFor YouPoliticsWorldBusinessAnalysisSportLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMoreSearchNews HomeJust InFor YouAnalysisRuralWatch LiveHealthIndigenousPoliticsScienceDeep TimeElectionsWorldEnvironmentInvestigationsBusinessLocal newsQuizzesSportAFLNRLFootballTennisCricketNetballLifestyleWellbeingRelationships & FamilyFood & RecipesPersonal FinanceHome & GardenEntertainmentTV & MoviesBooksMusicPop CultureArtsFacebookYouTubeInstagramTwitterEmergencyBackstoryNewsletters中文新闻BERITA BAHASA INDONESIATOK PISIN Appearance (BETA) Follow system settings Select an optionAlways lightAlways darkFollow system settingsABC iViewABC ListenJacinta BowlerJacinta Bowler is the ABC science reporter. They have previously hosted Science Friction series Dead and Alive on Radio National, and co-hosted the previous series, Artemis Explained. Their work has been published in the Best Australian Science Writing anthology for the last 5 years.Latest by Jacinta BowlerRadio telescope detects 10 billion-year-old signal from early universeA newly discovered fast radio burst from 3 billion years after the big bang is among the most mysterious objects in astronomy.Topic:SpaceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 9:53amFri 9 Oct 2026 at 9:53amFri 9 Oct 2026 at 9:53amAre we in the Anthropocene Epoch? Scientists can't decideWhile the Anthropocene is a term most of us are well aware of, it is not an official geological epoch. Now a group of scientists has reignited the push to get it formally recognised. Topic:GeologyFri 2 Oct 2026 at 4:30amFri 2 Oct 2026 at 4:30amFri 2 Oct 2026 at 4:30amPilots, aircrew face higher radiation-related cancer death rates, study findsWhile the study of death records across 500 occupations is US based, experts say the finding could be similar in Australia and suggests more radiation monitoring of airline crew is needed.Topic:Air Transport IndustryWed 30 Sep 2026 at 11:26amWed 30 Sep 2026 at 11:26amWed 30 Sep 2026 at 11:26amFeather discovered in fossilised dinosaur poo is first of its kindA well-preserved feather discovered inside fossilised dinosaur faeces could help explain why ancestors of modern birds survived the mass extinction.Topic:DinosaursFri 11 Sep 2026 at 7:49amFri 11 Sep 2026 at 7:49amFri 11 Sep 2026 at 7:49am'Uproar' unleashed after OpenAI says it solved major maths mysteryThe subject of the controversy may be extremely complex, but experts say it boils down to simple issues of trust and ethics. Topic:ExplainerThu 10 Sep 2026 at 3:31pmThu 10 Sep 2026 at 3:31pmThu 10 Sep 2026 at 3:31pmVicious friends and buried undies win at 2026 Ig Nobel prizesEach year, the Ig Nobel prizes celebrate research that makes you laugh, then makes you think. This year's winners include an \"underwear index\", a cumbersome kissing definition and why we want friends that will happily stab someone in the eye.Topic:Science AwardsFri 4 Sep 2026 at 7:35amFri 4 Sep 2026 at 7:35amFri 4 Sep 2026 at 7:35am'This could be it': Dark matter detector finds hint of unknown particleA single flash of energy spotted by the world's most sensitive dark matter detector has scientists excited they may have found hints of the elusive substance that permeates the universe. Topic:PhysicsWed 2 Sep 2026 at 12:00amWed 2 Sep 2026 at 12:00amWed 2 Sep 2026 at 12:00am'Pretty overwhelming': The 23 species in Australia hit by bird flu so farBird flu has quickly spread around Australia. Hidden in the numbers are more than 20 distinct species of birds and mammals that have fallen to the disease. Topic:ExplainerMon 31 Aug 2026 at 9:19amMon 31 Aug 2026 at 9:19amMon 31 Aug 2026 at 9:19amFlannel flower voted Australia's favourite wildflower after 70,000 votes cast — as it happenedThe country's top 10 wildflowers have been revealed, with the flannel flower crowned the winner in a tightly contested battle of the blooms.Topic:Native SpeciesFri 21 Aug 2026 at 2:24pmFri 21 Aug 2026 at 2:24pmFri 21 Aug 2026 at 2:24pmKeen to plant native flowers in your garden? We asked an expert for tipsWe often think that native plants thrive on little water and poor soil, but according to our expert it takes a bit more TLC than that, at least to get them started.Topic:ExplainerThu 20 Aug 2026 at 4:30amThu 20 Aug 2026 at 4:30amThu 20 Aug 2026 at 4:30amArmy of volunteers races to save one of Australia's rarest orchidsIt takes years of work, dozens of volunteers, picky pollinators and a special fungus to save Australia's endangered orchids. Here's why people drive from all over the country to do it anyway.Topic:BotanySun 9 Aug 2026 at 4:30amSun 9 Aug 2026 at 4:30amSun 9 Aug 2026 at 4:30amAustralian councils swap grass for native wildflower meadowsYou may have spotted more native plants popping up in your local park or on the side of the road. It's part of a push to create hardier, more beautiful council plantings across Australia.Topic:PlantsFri 7 Aug 2026 at 10:00amFri 7 Aug 2026 at 10:00amFri 7 Aug 2026 at 10:00amGovernments have spent billions on quantum computers. Has AI usurped them?More than half a century after the first computers were built, Australia is aiming to be a global innovator in quantum computing. But can it compete with the rise of AI?Topic:Quantum TechnologySun 2 Aug 2026 at 5:00amSun 2 Aug 2026 at 5:00amSun 2 Aug 2026 at 5:00amGrass 'skeletons' hint at 25,000-year-old Indigenous fire ritual in caveEvidence of ancient burnt grasses unearthed in a Victorian cave suggest the old ancestors of the GunaiKurnai people used the cave for fire rituals during the Ice Age.Topic:Indigenous CultureThu 23 Jul 2026 at 9:48amThu 23 Jul 2026 at 9:48amThu 23 Jul 2026 at 9:48am'Best place to look for life': Atmosphere discovered on Earth-like planetThe search for life beyond Earth has marked a new discovery — evidence of an atmosphere on an Earth-like planet beyond our Solar System with modelling suggesting it may be a water world.Topic:SpaceFri 17 Jul 2026 at 4:00amFri 17 Jul 2026 at 4:00amFri 17 Jul 2026 at 4:00amQ-Day is coming and it might break the entire internetThe countdown is on to Q-Day, when an algorithm written more than 30 years ago could run on a quantum computer and compromise the security of data on the internet. How are we preparing? Topic:Cyber SecurityThu 16 Jul 2026 at 8:52amThu 16 Jul 2026 at 8:52amThu 16 Jul 2026 at 8:52amAustralia's huntsman fastest spider on record, new study suggestsA species of huntsman spider found in Queensland is the fastest mover out of more than 250 spider species analysed in a new study.Topic:ArachnidsMon 13 Jul 2026 at 9:43amMon 13 Jul 2026 at 9:43amMon 13 Jul 2026 at 9:43amCan vaccines stop deadly bird flu from spreading?With bird flu arriving on our shores, governments, wildlife groups and agricultural organisations are springing into action. But can vaccines limit the economic and environmental damage of the deadly influenza?Topic:ExplainerTue 11 Aug 2026 at 11:59amTue 11 Aug 2026 at 11:59amTue 11 Aug 2026 at 11:59amRichard Scolyer remembered by peers for contributions to cancer treatmentBefore being diagnosed with cancer, the 2024 Australian of the Year was a leading melanoma researcher, developing better therapies for those with the disease.Topic:CancerTue 9 Jun 2026 at 4:57amTue 9 Jun 2026 at 4:57amTue 9 Jun 2026 at 4:57amWhy is there no vaccine for this Ebola outbreak?The latest Ebola outbreak is caused by a strain of virus discovered nearly two decades ago, yet there is no vaccine. So what does this mean for people affected? And can vaccines be fast-tracked?Topic:ExplainerWed 20 May 2026 at 5:20amWed 20 May 2026 at 5:20amWed 20 May 2026 at 5:20amThe 'plunk' sound of rain makes seeds sprout faster, scientists findScientists say they have the first direct evidence that seeds can sense the sound of rain, and will sprout faster when they do.Topic:PlantsThu 23 Apr 2026 at 4:46pmThu 23 Apr 2026 at 4:46pmThu 23 Apr 2026 at 4:46pmAstronauts hoisted to recovery ship after 'perfect bullseye' landing — as it happenedThe crew on board the Orion has returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean in a perfect '\"bullseye\". Read back over the day's event in our Artemis II blog.Topic:SpaceSat 11 Apr 2026 at 5:25pmSat 11 Apr 2026 at 5:25pmSat 11 Apr 2026 at 5:25pmViews from the Artemis II mission are absolutely 'unreal', astronaut says — as it happenedThe Artemis II astronauts have travelled further from Earth than any humans before, completing a historic fly-by of the Moon.Topic:Science and TechnologyTue 7 Apr 2026 at 2:37pmTue 7 Apr 2026 at 2:37pmTue 7 Apr 2026 at 2:37pmThis moon mission is different. They have toilets nowMore than 50 years ago, the Apollo missions took humanity to the Moon. Now with Artemis, there's a new generation. What has changed — and not changed — between then and now might surprise you.Topic:SpaceWed 15 Apr 2026 at 3:14pmWed 15 Apr 2026 at 3:14pmWed 15 Apr 2026 at 3:14pmLook back on the launch of NASA's first Moon mission in half a centuryFour astronauts are on board the Orion capsule, orbiting Earth as humanity seeks to return to the Moon for the first time in 54 years. Take a look back at how the day's events unfolded.Topic:SpaceThu 2 Apr 2026 at 1:45pmThu 2 Apr 2026 at 1:45pmThu 2 Apr 2026 at 1:45pmLoad more stories aboveGot a news tip?If you have inside knowledge of a topic in the news, contact the ABC.Confidential Tip‑offIf you are a whistleblower, believe you have sensitive information that should be made public, or wish to protect your identity, find our more secure options.Confidential Tip‑offNot confidential? Contact ABC News and investigations.Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashWoman, 71, treated on 'cold floor' of Adelaide hospitalData centre operator Firmus pulls blockbuster ASX float after lacklustre demandMass evacuations, cars washed away in Chilean flooding disasterSydney councillor accuses Indigenous elder Mick Mundine of sexual assault'Beau L-C' rental car card among items found near bodies, jury hearsliveButters unveiled by Bulldogs after 'megadeal', Blakey makes his decisionFederal government backtracks on disaster funding splitUS army's first firing squad execution since WW2 to be publicThese are the frontrunners for this year's Nobel Peace Prize Powderfinger confirm reunion, 16 years after breaking upBack to topABC NewsContact ABC NewsYour home of Australian stories, conversations and events that shape our nation.This service may include material from Agence France-Presse (AFP), APTN, Reuters, AAP, CNN and the BBC World Service which is copyright and cannot be reproduced.Follow ABC NewsSectionsABC News HomeJust InFor YouWatch Live NewsLive ABC NewsRadioPoliticsElectionsAustraliaWorldSportLive SportRuralBusinessAnalysisScienceHealthLifestyleEntertainmentQuizzesMore TopicsNews in LanguageChinese 中文Tok PisinBerita Bahasa IndonesiaRelatedABC News appLocal NewsABC EmergencyABC IndigenousABC Religion & EthicsABC AsiaABC PacificMusic & AudioABC Listen Hometriple jLive RadioMovies & TVABC iview HomeList of ABC ShowsLive TVTV GuideKids & StudentsBTNEducationHeywireTakeoverABC KidsGet the ABC News AM newsletter Get the latest ABC News delivered to your email inboxEmail addressSubscribeYour information is being handled in accordance with the ABC Privacy Collection Statement. All ABC newslettersStay informed and connected with the ABC. Covering a wide range of topics and brands including News, iview, listen, and more.Browse all ABC newslettersABC homeInformation & ServicesEditorial PoliciesAccessibilityHelpContact UsCommercialCareersAbout the ABCPrivacy PolicyTerms of Use© 2026 ABCFollow the ABCWe acknowledge Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples as the First Australians and Traditional Custodians of the lands where we live, learn and work.","title":"Jacinta Bowler","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/jacinta-bowler/103583808","id":659},{"content_hash":"b46fd952708442d4639c3cb735acf398eff858162a70d05395361bbef6c4b3ac","domain":"healthline.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:42.727966+00:00","final_url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/mania-vs-hypomania","language":"","text":"Mania causes atypically high levels of physical and mental energy in people with bipolar disorder. Hypomania causes these same symptoms, but theyâre not as intense or long lasting.What is the difference between mania and hypomania?Mania and hypomania are symptoms that can occur in people with bipolar disorder. They can also occur in people who do not have bipolar disorder.What is mania?Mania is more than just having extra energy. Itâs a mood disturbance that makes you atypically energized, both physically and mentally, for a week or more. Mania can be severe enough to require require hospitalization.Mania can occur in people with bipolar I disorder. In most bipolar I diagnoses, manic episodes can alternate with periods of depression.However, people with bipolar I disorder donât always have depressive episodes. Itâs very common for an individual to have more typical moods in between episodes.What is hypomania?Hypomania is a milder form of mania. If youâre experiencing hypomania, your energy level is higher than usual, but itâs not as extreme as in mania. It may only last for a few days.Hypomania symptoms are different than your typical activity levels and behaviour so other people may notice that difference.According to experts, in most cases hypomania does not require hospitalisation.People with bipolar II disorder may experience hypomania that alternates with depression, or they may have more typical emotional states in between each.What are the signs of hypomania and mania?The main differences between mania and hypomania are the intensity of the symptoms and how long those symptoms last. Symptoms of mania and hypomaniaWhile they vary in intensity, most mania and hypomania symptoms are the same. The key symptoms include:having higher-than-typical energy levelsbeing restless or unable to sit stillhaving a decreased need for sleephaving increased self-esteem, confidence, or grandiositybeing more talkativehaving a racing mind or having lots of new ideas and planshaving decreased inhibitionshaving increased sexual desireengaging in potentially harmful behaviors that may be unusual for you, such as having impulsive sex, gambling with life savings, or going on big spending spreesThe slight differences between mania and hypomania include: ManiaHypomaniaYour behavior is so extreme that you are unable to maintain regular activities.People might notice changes, but you may still be able to maintain regular activities.Delusions or hallucinations may occur.Typically, delusions and hallucinations do not occur.Feelings of invincibility are common.Taking actions that may be harmful to you or others is common.You may feel âdetachedâ from reality.You may feel easily distracted.During a manic or hypomanic phase, you may not be able to recognize these changes in yourself.The more severe symptoms of maniaUnlike hypomanic episodes, manic episodes can lead to serious consequences. When the mania subsides, you may experience remorse or depression for your actions during the episode.With mania, you may also have a break from reality. Psychotic symptoms can include:visual or auditory hallucinationsdelusional thoughtsparanoid thoughtsWhat are the causes and risk factors?Mania and hypomania are common symptoms of bipolar disorder. However, these symptoms can also be due to:sleep deprivationmedicationalcohol usedrug useThe exact cause of bipolar disorder is unclear. Research has identified some environmental risk factors, but it could also be genetic, meaning that family history may play a role. There isnât enough research to establish a relationship between environmental factors and bipolar disorder, so more studies are needed.Youâre at an increased risk of mania or hypomania if youâve already had an episode. Your risk may also increase if you have bipolar disorder and cannot take medications as your doctor prescribes.Can mania or hypomania be prevented?Mania and hypomania, as well as bipolar disorder, are not preventable. However, you can take steps to lessen the effects of an episode. Maintaining your support systems and working with a healthcare professional like a mental health professional are two methods that can help lessen the possibility of an episode and manage one if it occurs. Itâs important to stick with your treatment plan if you have one. Taking your medications as prescribed and keeping an open line of communication with your doctor are key. Working together, you and your doctor can create the best treatment plan to manage your symptoms and improve your quality of life.âBipolar mania can be a scary thing to experience. But the good news is that bipolar disorder is treatable. Since receiving my diagnosis, Iâve found the right medication and the right dosage so that day-to-day life is totally normal.ââ Mara RobinsonHow are they diagnosed?If you believe you may be dealing with symptoms of mania, hypomania, or bipolar disorder itself, itâs a good idea to talk with a healthcare professional. During your appointment, your doctor will request your medical history and do a physical exam.Itâs important that you tell your doctor about all the prescription and over-the-counter medications and supplements you take, as well as any illegal drugs you may have taken.If your doctor suspects you are dealing with mania or hypomania, they will likely refer you to a mental health professional for an actual diagnosis. Diagnosing mania and hypomania can be complicated. For instance, you may not be aware of some symptoms or how long youâve been having them.Also, if you have depression, itâs important to share your complete medical history to help your doctor in making an official diagnosis.In addition, other health conditions can cause mania and hypomania. Diagnosing maniaIn most cases, symptoms must last at least a week for your doctor to diagnose them as mania. However, if your symptoms are so severe that youâre hospitalized, a diagnosis can be made even if the symptoms last for a shorter time.Diagnosing hypomaniaYour symptoms must persist for at least 4 consecutive days for your doctor to diagnose hypomania.How are hypomania and mania treated?To treat mania and hypomania, your doctor may prescribe psychotherapy and medication. The medication can include mood stabilizers and antipsychotics.You may need to try several different medications before your doctor discovers the right combination to treat your symptoms effectively.Itâs important that you take your medication as your doctor prescribes. Even if you have side effects from the drugs, it can be dangerous to stop taking your medication without your doctorâs supervision. If you have problems with side effects, talk with your doctor. They may be able to switch you to a different kind of treatment that isnât so difficult on your body.For hypomania, itâs often possible to manage symptoms without medication. Healthy lifestyle habits can help, such as:maintaining a balanced, nutritious dietgetting physical activity every day keeping a regular sleep schedule and prioritizing restkeeping a journal that notes any mood changes or changes pointed out by loved onesCoping with mania and hypomaniaThese tips can help you cope with mania and hypomania:Learning about your condition: You can manage mania and hypomania. Learning to recognize personal triggers can help avoid them.Keeping a mood diary: By charting your moods, you might be able to spot early warning signs. With your doctorâs help, you may also be able to prevent an episode from worsening. For instance, by learning to spot the early warning signs of a manic episode, you can manage it well.Staying in treatment: If you have bipolar disorder, treatment is key. Getting your family involved in therapy might be a good idea as they can help you stay in treatment.Watching for suicidal thoughts: If you have thoughts of self-harm, speak with a trusted friend or family member immediately or contact a suicide prevention hotline.Reaching out to others for help: Asking for help is a strength, not a weakness. Talk with loved ones about your situation and how they can best help you succeed. Look up support groups for individuals living with bipolar disorder. The more support you have, the less alone you may feel.Help is out thereIf you or someone you know is in crisis and considering suicide or self-harm, please seek support:Call or text the 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline at 988.Text HOME to the Crisis Textline at 741741. Not in the United States? Find a helpline in your country with Befrienders Worldwide.Call 911 or your local emergency services number if you feel safe to do so.If youâre calling on behalf of someone else, stay with them until help arrives. You may remove weapons or substances that can cause harm if you can do so safely.If you are not in the same household, stay on the phone with them until help arrives.Frequently asked questionsIs hypomania more severe than mania?No hypomania is milder than mania. Symptoms of mania are much more intense than those of hypomania and can last for a week or more.What are the 3 types of mania?Mania can lead to euphoria, psychosis, or as hypomania. These are not clearly defined types, and a person can have overlapping symptoms.TakeawayMania and hypomania are two common symptoms of bipolar disorder, but they can also occur outside of it. Both mania and hypomania can cause a marked increase in energy, uplifted emotions, behaviors that may cause harm, and irritability.While the episode may feel good while itâs occurring, both conditions can cause an individual to do things they might regret later. Mania can be especially harmful if you do not manage it properly.Although these two mental health conditions share similar characteristics, the main differences between mania and hypomania are the severity and the length of an episode.If you believe you or someone you love is experiencing a manic or hypomanic episode, consider talking with a mental health professional as soon as possible.Medication, therapy, and support can help treat manic episodes. Sometimes, lifestyle changes can help manage hypomanic episodes instead of medication.","title":"What You Should Know About Mania vs. Hypomania","url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/mania-vs-hypomania","id":660},{"content_hash":"f02484b33bc20ebc9b8e7e54c74946666c4119eb6cb2c0b56bce2414b7156433","domain":"prnigeria.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:42.620510+00:00","final_url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/30/emir-zazzau-backs-nahcon/","language":"","text":"Emir of Zazzau Backs NAHCONâs Reform Drive, Calls for Sustained Excellence in Hajj Administration By Prnigeria - September 30, 2026 NAHCON Emir of Zazzau Backs NAHCONâs Reform Drive, Calls for Sustained Excellence in Hajj Administration In a significant endorsement of ongoing efforts to strengthen Nigeria’s Hajj administration, the Emir of Zazzau, His Royal Highness Ambassador Ahmed Nuhu Bamalli, has pledged the support of the traditional institution to the National Hajj Commission of Nigeria (NAHCON), while urging the commission to sustain its reform agenda and operational efficiency in the management of Hajj affairs. The revered royal father made the commitment during a courtesy visit to the Chairman and Chief Executive Officer of NAHCON, Ambassador Ismail Abba Yusuf, at the Commission’s headquarters in Abuja. The visit underscores the growing confidence of critical stakeholders in NAHCON’s ongoing transformation efforts and highlights the pivotal role traditional institutions continue to play in fostering public trust, pilgrim education, and community engagement across the country. Speaking during the meeting, the Emir commended NAHCON’s leadership for its determination to improve service delivery despite the enormous complexities associated with coordinating one of the world’s largest annual religious gatherings. He noted that the successful management of Hajj operations requires foresight, transparency, discipline, and meticulous planning, particularly in a country with one of the largest pilgrim contingents in Africa. Describing Hajj as a sacred obligation that deserves the highest standards of care and organization, the Emir emphasized that the welfare, safety, and spiritual fulfilment of Nigerian pilgrims must remain at the heart of every policy and operational decision. Read Also: Gombe PDP Suspends Mailantarki, Garry over Alleged Anti-Party Activities after Governorship Primary Nigeria @66: FG Pushes for Growth, Jobs, Improved Living Standards Military Records 367 Attacks, Kills 52 Terrorists in September “We are here to offer our prayers, counsel, and support to NAHCON. We also urge the Commission to sustain its drive toward structural efficiency, early preparations, and transparent communication to overcome recurring operational challenges,” the Emir stated. He further assured that the Zazzau Emirate and the wider traditional institution would continue to support initiatives aimed at sensitizing intending pilgrims and promoting compliance with Hajj guidelines, thereby contributing to a smoother and more successful pilgrimage experience. Responding, Ambassador Ismail Abba Yusuf expressed profound appreciation for the visit, describing it as both historic and encouraging for the Commission’s management and staff. He noted that the endorsement and counsel of respected traditional leaders provide valuable support for the Commission’s efforts to continually improve service delivery and deepen stakeholder engagement. The NAHCON Chairman briefed the Emir on a number of strategic initiatives being implemented by the Commission, including policy reforms, digital innovations, and partnerships designed to enhance operational efficiency, reduce costs, improve accommodation and feeding arrangements, and ensure a more seamless airlift process for pilgrims. Ambassador Yusuf reaffirmed the Commission’s commitment to building a modern, responsive, and pilgrim-centred Hajj administration system capable of meeting global best practices and the expectations of Nigerian pilgrims. He assured the Emir that the observations and recommendations made during the visit would receive due consideration as the Commission intensifies preparations for future Hajj operations. The visit is widely seen as a demonstration of the strong synergy between traditional leadership and public institutions in advancing reforms that directly impact the welfare of citizens, while reinforcing confidence in NAHCON’s mission to deliver a world-class Hajj experience for the Nigerian Muslim Ummah.This version adopts a stronger public relations angle by emphasizing stakeholder confidence, institutional endorsement, reform momentum, and the strategic significance of traditional leadership support. VISIT OUR OTHER WEBSITES PRNigeria.com EconomicConfidential.com PRNigeria.com/Hausa/ EmergencyDigest.com PoliticsDigest.ng TechDigest.ng HealthDigest.ng SpokesPersonsdigest.com TeensDigest.ng ArewaAgenda.com Hausa.ArewaAgenda.com YAShuaib.com TAGSEmir of ZazzauHajjHis Royal Highness Ambassador Ahmed Nuhu BamalliNAHCON Previous articleGombe PDP Suspends Mailantarki, Garry over Alleged Anti-Party Activities after Governorship Primary Prnigeria","title":"Emir of Zazzau Backs NAHCONâs Reform Drive, Calls for Sustained Excellence in Hajj Administration","url":"https://prnigeria.com/2026/09/30/emir-zazzau-backs-nahcon/","id":661},{"content_hash":"5e459ed35527814306e879e12c2fdf7bbd1c7ad586aa0acf492c74e2f3982c36","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:42.388588+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/l-assemblee-generale-aborde-l-examen-de-son-ordre-du-jour_2071981_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives L'assemblée générale aborde l'examen de son ordre du jour Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"L'assemblée générale aborde l'examen de son ordre du jour","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/l-assemblee-generale-aborde-l-examen-de-son-ordre-du-jour_2071981_1819218.html","id":662},{"content_hash":"a77b235190c5ce67cd04fa567f7a9f6b0bc5ada9c9f84da06dd92b4653f61589","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:42.379874+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/imposter-scams-are-on-the-rise.html","language":"","text":"Imposter scams are on the rise: Here’s how to manage the risks As criminals impersonate trusted figures and set up fraudulent websites, education is the best defense Key takeaways: Education is the best defense against increasingly common imposter scams in which scammers may impersonate Merrill financial advisors.If you receive an unexpected call, text or email from someone claiming to be a financial advisor — even your personal financial advisor — always verify the source using a known phone number, legitimate website, bill or statement.Fraudulent websites can often be identified by poor design quality, grammatical errors, misspellings and urgent requests for contact or other information.Be proactive by coordinating a mutually understood defense with your advisor. As our daily reliance on digital communication steadily increases, scammers are evolving their tactics to exploit the trust we’ve built for online and other financial services. Imposter scams are on the rise and are currently the most commonly reported fraud, with approximately 2.5 million complaints in 2024 according to the Federal Trade Commission. These types of scams cost victims more than $12.5 billion.1 Imposter scams typically begin with an anomalous email (commonly known as phishing), a phone call from a falsified number (vishing), an unsolicited text (smishing) or a social media message. The communications appear to be from trusted professionals such as financial advisors, lawyers or government officials — or family members or celebrities.2 To make them appear more authentic, the perpetrators may even pose as your personal Merrill advisor, or representatives of companies or charities you already have relationships with. By creating fraudulent websites using legitimate information they’ve harvested from online sources, these scammers lure clients and potential prospects into providing confidential information with the intention of committing financial fraud. Imposter scams are on the rise, and education is our best defense. Scammers are impersonating financial advisors by using their names, photos and other contact information to create fraudulent websites in order to steal your personal information. They may contact you by phone, text or email to try and trick you into providing your personal information. By spoofing legitimate phone numbers to call or text you, these requests can be very convincing. Posing as a trustworthy advisor, an imposter will send false emails containing malicious links or malware. Don’t reply to messages from unknown senders or click on their attachments. If someone contacts you claiming to be a financial advisor (from Merrill, Bank of America Private Bank, Consumer Investments or other brokerage firms) or other trusted professional, there are clues to help you tell if the contact isn’t legitimate. The name of the advisor is the website domain, and the site includes a photo of the financial advisor. Also watch for: information about their employment history, including prior employers’ CRD numbers and examinations. The site asks you to supply contact information. It may also have grammar errors, misspellings, awkward phrasing, and poor design quality, including low-resolution images and odd layouts. Scammers often try to elicit a strong emotional reaction to get you to fall for their schemes. Don't feel pressured to respond immediately — this is what they want you to do. Read more about scams by visiting our Security Center. 1 of 8 Warning signs to watch for Recognizing that imposter scams exist and are increasing in prevalence may be the best defense against them. But beyond awareness, there are some common red flags that can help you identify these scams before you fall victim, including: An urgent request to fill out a form with personal information that the advisor should already have if you’re a client or wouldn’t need if you’re a prospect.Poor website design quality, including odd layouts and low-resolution images or photos of the advisor.Grammatical errors, misspellings, awkward phrasing or misuse of investor terminology.A website domain that uses the name of the financial advisor rather than a reputable firm.A list of the advisor’s employees or examination history publicly on the site. Best practices to protect yourself and others Imposter scams are successful because of how much legitimate information scammers can mine from publicly available information on the web, enabling them to convincingly impersonate a professional or simulate a professional’s website. But by following these best practices, you can protect yourself and others: Verify all anomalous communications or requests for money or personal information by double-checking the sender information and by independently confirming the source using a verified phone number from an official website, bill or statement.Don’t rely on caller ID to determine if a caller is legitimate.Never send money to someone you don’t know, think you know or have only met online.Never give sensitive information, such as your Social Security number, over the phone or through a website unless you are sure of who you’re interacting with.Cut off contact at any point with someone you suspect is impersonating a professional. In addition, being proactive and coming up with a mutually understood defense against imposter scams with your advisor and professional contacts can be an effective way to decrease your chances of falling victim. For more information on this scam and others, visit the Merrill Security Center and read more about how to avoid scams. If you suspect you are a victim of fraud, contact us immediately at 1.800.MERRILL(637.7455) or speak to your financial advisor for further assistance. Immediately forward any suspicious emails or text messages that use Merrill or Bank of America's name to abuse@bofa.com and then delete it. Remember: Don’t transfer money as a result of an unexpected phone call or text. We will never ask you to send us personal information such as an account number, Social Security number or Tax ID over text, over email or online. Choose your advisor in a more personalized way All our advisors are committed to putting your needs and priorities first. Find some who match your personal preferences too. Loading... Try Advisor Match Answer a few questions Want us to contact you? Submit a request Phone Loading... Email Loading... View your advisor 1 Federal Trade Commission 2024 snapshot found at ftc.gov 2 “Imposter Scams,” AARP, March 30, 2022 Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Switch to Accessibility Friendly View Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog How we can help Explore what it’s like to work with a Merrill Financial Advisor Start here More for you More for you Explore more “Diverse Viewpoints” Connect Find out what it's like to work with a Merrill advisor Sign up Get monthly insights from our Perspectives newsletter Related Articles Help protect against online shopping scams Read more Stay ahead of student cyber scams Read more Be cyber secure: Dos and don’ts for your family Read more","title":"Imposter scams are on the rise: Here’s how to manage the risks","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/imposter-scams-are-on-the-rise.html","id":663},{"content_hash":"fcd3dbea854c442b285db122dfd833f99e7c9f80418a044b3bdb258bc554e2e7","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:42.302815+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/health-inequalities-in-uk-children-widening-14774","language":"","text":"Health inequalities in UK children widening Published: May 29, 2013 9:49pm EDT https://theconversation.com/health-inequalities-in-uk-children-widening-14774 https://theconversation.com/health-inequalities-in-uk-children-widening-14774 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Health inequalities in British children have widened over ten years, according to a study of young people between 1999 and 2009. Health inequality refers to the gap in health outcomes between groups on opposite ends of the socioeconomic spectrum – the wealthy compared to the less affluent. Researchers anticipate that child poverty is the UK will continue to rise. Read more at UCL Source UCL Tags Health inequality","title":"Health inequalities in UK children widening","url":"https://theconversation.com/health-inequalities-in-uk-children-widening-14774","id":664},{"content_hash":"47c1512bf276821110fcf00608ab17ee0d509a2f6e4e059e1f94a3e2e4a19050","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:41.147437+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/un-senateur-voudrait-limiter-la-vitesse-des-automobiles-a-90-kilometres-a-l-heure_2073105_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives UN SÉNATEUR VOUDRAIT LIMITER LA VITESSE DES AUTOMOBILES A 90 KILOMÈTRES A L'HEURE Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"UN SÉNATEUR VOUDRAIT LIMITER LA VITESSE DES AUTOMOBILES A 90 KILOMÈTRES A L'HEURE","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/un-senateur-voudrait-limiter-la-vitesse-des-automobiles-a-90-kilometres-a-l-heure_2073105_1819218.html","id":665},{"content_hash":"b4eadeba153066adf7e5fd14d0fb9911d2d07e88ee006242631201ef264a0ab4","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:41.132116+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/canada/article-alleged-nato-spy-more-federal-departments-previously-reported-court/","language":"","text":"Open this photo in gallery:Prior to her internship at NATO, Claire (Biwei) Zhang worked in public-service roles, including at Statistics Canada.YVES HERMAN/Reuters CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountThe NATO intern arrested last month by Belgian authorities on espionage charges has a work history with the Canadian government that is more extensive than previously reported, according to employment records she filed in court. Biwei Zhang, a 33-year-old who has also used her English name, Claire Zhang, was charged with espionage while working for the North Atlantic Treaty Organization in the city of Mons. The Globe and Mail has previously reported that a source said the overseas posting was sponsored by Global Affairs Canada, and that the department was responsible for her security check. Documents filed in the Federal Court of Canada and obtained by The Globe chronicle Ms. Zhang’s account of her work history, which the records say dates back to 2017 and included stints at the National Research Council, Statistics Canada, and Employment and Social Development Canada (ESDC). She filed her employment history and an affidavit in court when she unsuccessfully appealed a ruling that she had committed fraud within the civil service when she wrote the same job exam twice for a single posting at the Canada Border Services Agency.The CBSA became aware of Ms. Zhang’s dual applications in 2019 when a third-party human-resources provider flagged them as a potential issue, the records show. She never worked for the border agency. Arrested NATO intern has history of fraud, was ordered to take ethics course“It was never my intention to create such an awkward and embarrassing situation for myself,” she wrote in an apology letter, which she also filed in court. “I extremely enjoyed my career and appreciated my professional experience that government of Canada brought to me.”Authorities in Belgium have not publicly named the country Ms. Zhang is accused of spying on behalf of, but two Canadian national-security sources have previously told The Globe that Belgian prosecutors have accused her of working for China.Since her arrest, Ottawa has faced questions about how she managed to pass the federal vetting process.At a Wednesday news conference in Toronto, in reply to a question about whether Canada’s screening processes need reform, Prime Minister Mark Carney said, “Any time you have a situation, you should take, you take stock and take lessons from this.”Mr. Carney said the probe into Ms. Zhang is “an active investigation,” which he said limits what he can say about it.“The only thing I would add cryptically is that Canadian officials have been involved from the start,” he added.China silent on allegations of NATO spying as Beijing extends Canada anti-dumping probeA senior government source said the Prime Minister was referring to the Canadian Security Intelligence Service. The source said that the spy agency was keeping the Prime Minister updated. The Globe is not naming the source because they were not authorized to discuss the matter publicly.CSIS declined to comment, citing in a statement that the matter is subject to a continuing investigation. Ms. Zhang wrote detailed accounts of her life in her job applications that were submitted as evidence in the court case. She lists an address in Toronto’s North York as her home and also listed an Ottawa address. She was hired for several government jobs, her affidavit says, as part of the federal student work experience program. English is her second language, she said in the filings. In her applications, she listed past volunteering experience for the Toronto International Film Festival and for an “election campaign for Liberal Party of Canada,” which she did not specify. The federal Liberal Party said in a statement that it had “no record of the individual in question holding any formal campaign role.”Arrested NATO intern accused by Belgium of spying for China, source saysAt the time of her application to the CBSA, she was completing an honours economics degree from York University. In a 2017 summer placement, she said in the filings, she worked as a junior data scientist at the federal National Research Council. Part of her work, she said, was to analyze ship movements in the port of Montreal. The next year, according to the documents, she studied “swine movement data” for Statistics Canada’s agricultural division before migrating to ESDC in early 2019 to do data analysis for programs such as Old Age Security and the Canada Pension Plan. She said her work there was devoted to developing “predictive models and other data-driven tools to ensure that the right Canadians are being paid the right benefit at the right time.” The NRC, Statistics Canada and ESDC all declined to comment, citing the continuing investigation. By 2019, the court filings show, Ms. Zhang had created seven profiles for the online job portal that is used by applicants to the federal public service. In her court submissions, she said this was a result of forgetting her login information, which forced her to create new login IDs when she applied to new jobs. She responded to a CBSA posting for a permanent staff position that paid up to $94,000 a year. The security agency said it was modernizing by hiring staff who “have a passion for analytics.” RCMP pledge to support Belgian investigation into alleged NATO spyMs. Zhang was among 1,200 candidates who applied for the job, but she submitted two applications, using different e-mails and calling herself “Biwei Zhang” in one application and “Zhang Biwei” in the other, the court filings show.When it came time to write the civil-service test, she wrote it twice – on March 14 and March 15, 2019. This was despite federal rules that say job candidates cannot write the same test twice within 180 days.After being alerted to her multiple applications, the Public Service Commission, a government watchdog for federal hiring, began investigating her and demanded a sit-down interview. In 2023, while the commission found that Ms. Zhang had not acted dishonestly in submitting two applications, it ruled that she had committed fraud in sitting for the examination twice and failing to confirm that fact when “asked directly” by the border agency. In 2024, a Federal Court judge ruled against Ms. Zhang, ending her appeal. The court records show that as early as 2019, investigators with the commission had sought interviews with her about the CBSA applications, and that her interview with the watchdog took place in December, 2020. However, Ms. Zhang was re-hired by Statistics Canada in May, 2020, through an external non-advertised appointment and worked there for a year, according to the PSC’s investigation that was contained in the court records. Although the record of employment filed in court is only current up to 2021, a LinkedIn account that The Globe has identified as belonging to Ms. Zhang says she worked at the European Space Agency in Italy in 2022, and later as a systems engineer at the Canadian Space Agency in Quebec. Colleen Bauman, a labour lawyer who acted for Ms. Zhang in the Federal Court appeal, said Wednesday that she could not comment on the case. “I have no information whatsoever,” she said, adding that she worked on Ms. Zhang’s case years ago.With a report from Ian Bailey","title":"NATO intern accused of spying reported working a range of Canadian government jobs","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/canada/article-alleged-nato-spy-more-federal-departments-previously-reported-court/","id":666},{"content_hash":"c17fa8e648980c14d78e4299fbb917b2108342ae5f4f676e92b5e3d0c4fa17fb","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:41.092132+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/chinas-air-quality-kills-15922","language":"","text":"China’s air quality kills Published: July 8, 2013 10:45pm EDT https://theconversation.com/chinas-air-quality-kills-15922 https://theconversation.com/chinas-air-quality-kills-15922 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article China’s high level of polluted air is costing lives, according to researchers at Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. Research found China’s lifespan expectancy rate lowers by three years at birth for each additional 100 micrograms of particulate matter per cubic metre in the atmosphere. China has one of the world’s highest rates of poor air quality in the world. Read more at Proceedings of National Academy of Sciences (PNAS) Source Proceedings of National Academy of Sciences (PNAS) Tags China, Air pollution, Air quality","title":"China’s air quality kills","url":"https://theconversation.com/chinas-air-quality-kills-15922","id":667},{"content_hash":"ffe900d50c0bd4dffd648552a1a8585e12770d80f98445eaca04ca972b0da495","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:40.932051+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/politics/article-arrested-nato-intern-fraud-history-ordered-to-take-ethics-course/","language":"","text":"Open this photo in gallery:Prior to her internship at NATO, Claire (Biwei) Zhang worked in public-service roles, including at Statistics Canada.YVES HERMAN/Reuters CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountA Canadian NATO intern arrested in Belgium on suspicion of spying for China was ordered by a federal watchdog to take a course in ethics after being found to have committed fraud in applying twice under two slightly different identities for a public service job. Claire (Biwei) Zhang, a Canadian of Chinese origin, worked in public service roles, including at Statistics Canada, before taking up the NATO internship at Supreme Headquarters Allied Powers Europe, known as SHAPE, in Mons near the French border.She was found to have acted dishonestly by submitting two applications from different e-mail addresses, with slightly different identifying names, for a role with the Canada Border Services Agency, according to federal court documents. The public-service watchdog, which found she had committed fraud, ordered that for a year she notify it in writing before accepting a position within the federal public service.The June, 2023 Public Service Commission report added that if she joined the federal public service within a year, she must also complete an ethics course followed by a discussion with her manager within two months of starting the job. Ms. Zhang has not made any public comment on the allegations since her detention, nor has ‌any lawyer ⁠acting on her behalf. Her identity was confirmed to The Globe and Mail by a national-security source. The source and a second national-security source said Belgian prosecutors are looking into allegations that she spied for China. The Globe is not identifying the sources, who were not authorized to speak publicly about the case.China silent on allegations of NATO spying as Beijing extends Canada anti-dumping probeOpposition MPs and experts on the public service are questioning how, after being found to have committed fraud, she was able to secure roles in public bodies both in Canada and abroad.Former Clerk of the Privy Council Michael Wernick said there should be a fresh look at the security screening process. He said, “it is a long-standing problem” in the Canadian public service that “people can slip between the cracks and move from one place to another despite transgressions of conduct and mismanagement.”Ms. Zhang sought judicial review of the commission’s ruling, with her lawyers arguing that the corrective measures were “disproportionately harsh.” But a federal court judge dismissed her appeal in 2024, court records show.The judgment refers to her having also submitted more than one application for a role at Statistics Canada. The agency had detected this and consolidated her applications, the federal court document said.Carter Mann, head of media relations at Statscan, said it “takes the integrity of its staffing and security processes very seriously and has measures in place to identify and address irregularities when they arise.”The investigation by the Public Service Commission concluded that Ms. Zhang had increased her chances over other candidates for the CBSA role, as in 2019 she had two attempts to pass the exam in 24 hours, the court documents show. The CBSA had spotted that her applications were virtually identical, despite being submitted on the same day from different e-mail addresses at the University of Montreal and York University.Ms. Zhang, who also worked at the Canadian Space Agency, was given security clearance by the federal government before starting the NATO internship, after vetting by the Canadian Security Intelligence Service, and checks by the RCMP into whether she had a criminal record. Ottawa vetted NATO intern arrested on suspicion of espionageMr. Wernick said he would like to see the National Security and Intelligence Committee of Parliamentarians (NSICOP), made up of MPs and senators who have top-secret security clearance, “take up the matter.”NSICOP should look into not just the specifics of this case “but a broader examination of the security screening apparatus, whether it is properly aligned and calibrated, and whether it is resourced and equipped to deal with the enormous caseload of personnel and contractors,” he said in an e-mail. NSICOP declined to comment.Ken Rasmussen, professor emeritus at the University of Regina’s Johnson Shoyama Graduate School of Public Policy, said the federal government needs to conduct a “fairly robust inquiry” into what happened with its screening processes. “They’re going to have to, if not name and blame, say what measures have been taken to … ensure this kind of thing can’t happen,” he said. “Even if there were no great national-security secrets being siphoned off, it’s still a very bad look for the government.”Conservative national-security critic Dane Lloyd said it was embarrassing for Canada that Ms. Zhang had been given security clearance by the federal government before starting her NATO internship, raising questions about how thorough Ottawa’s vetting process is.He said a finding of fraud by the public service watchdog should have been an indication that “maybe we should look more closely at this person.”He said Canadians would expect foreign nationals coming from countries that have a history of engaging in espionage to be subject to “a very high level of scrutiny.” China has said little about the case. China’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs hasn’t responded to requests for comment. A spokesperson for the Chinese embassy in Brussels dismissed the allegations as “unfounded, fabricated suspicions” from anonymous sources and said the embassy would not be commenting further.The Public Service Commission said it could not comment on individual cases. In a statement, it said it “is committed to protecting the integrity of the federal public service and the merit-based appointment system.”CBSA spokesperson Luke Reimer said in a statement: “The Canada Border Services Agency works diligently to ensure the integrity of all its activities, including staffing processes. This includes taking concrete measures to detect and address suspected fraud.”With a report from James Griffiths","title":"Arrested NATO intern has history of fraud, was ordered to take ethics course","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/politics/article-arrested-nato-intern-fraud-history-ordered-to-take-ethics-course/","id":668},{"content_hash":"37353ac03c19a506ee9f90226ec5f1084d6dd3d62bd66f809d313d53f4eabed2","domain":"docs.djangoproject.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:40.855930+00:00","final_url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/expressions/","language":"","text":"PostgreSQL specific query expressions¶ These expressions are available from the django.contrib.postgres.expressions module. ArraySubquery() expressions¶ class ArraySubquery(queryset)[source]¶ ArraySubquery is a Subquery that uses the PostgreSQL ARRAY constructor to build a list of values from the queryset, which must use QuerySet.values() to return only a single column. This class differs from ArrayAgg in the way that it does not act as an aggregate function and does not require an SQL GROUP BY clause to build the list of values. For example, if you want to annotate all related books to an author as JSON objects: >>> from django.db.models import OuterRef >>> from django.db.models.functions import JSONObject >>> from django.contrib.postgres.expressions import ArraySubquery >>> books = Book.objects.filter(author=OuterRef(\"pk\")).values( ... json=JSONObject(title=\"title\", pages=\"pages\") ... ) >>> author = Author.objects.annotate(books=ArraySubquery(books)).first() >>> author.books [{'title': 'Solaris', 'pages': 204}, {'title': 'The Cyberiad', 'pages': 295}]","title":"PostgreSQL specific query expressions¶","url":"https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/ref/contrib/postgres/expressions/","id":669},{"content_hash":"6859b71fe9db7f91a51111cca4d1c5a23bf0755d9ae8a34876421d1beb8219e9","domain":"blog.google","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:40.842821+00:00","final_url":"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/global-network/nevada-clean-energy-fund/","language":"","text":"We’re committing $10 million toward Nevada’s cleaner, more affordable energy future. Sep 15, 2026 Today, we’re announcing a $10 million Energy Impact Fund (EIF) to support energy innovation and affordability in Nevada. As part of this investment, we're providing $5 million to the Nevada Clean Energy Fund (NCEF) to help families within our data center communities lower their utility bills and improve home health and safety.These funds will go to support NCEF's Residential Energy Upgrade Program (RE-UP), which brings free weatherization and energy efficiency improvements to households who face financial barriers to home upgrades but don't qualify for existing assistance programs.Across our data center communities in North and South Nevada, these investments will reduce monthly energy bills and deliver meaningful public health and grid resiliency benefits for participating families and their surrounding communities — at no cost to Nevada’s residents. We’re proud to partner with NCEF today, and look forward to announcing our next grant later this year.","title":"We’re committing $10 million toward Nevada’s cleaner, more affordable energy future.","url":"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/global-network/nevada-clean-energy-fund/","id":670},{"content_hash":"867355b711e4f7d4eef32b3be99d1cf240c2989d49ae79aaa9fbb4d3aae1035e","domain":"ama.org","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:40.655538+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ama.org/2022/11/29/do-we-reveal-more-or-less-about-ourselves-when-interacting-with-voice-technology/","language":"","text":"Do We Reveal More or Less About Ourselves When Interacting with Voice Technology? 11.29.2022 Andrea Bonezzi, Johann Melzner and Tom Meyvis We live in an era where consumers constantly interact with technological devices connected to the internet, and whenever they do so, they reveal information about themselves. The rapid propagation of voice technology is shifting the way consumers interact with technological devices from typing to speaking, raising an important question: Do consumers reveal more or less information about themselves when they interact with technology orally rather than manually? In a new Journal of Marketing article, we answer this question by examining how voice technology influences what consumers disclose verbally (by voluntarily providing information through language) as well as nonverbally (by involuntarily revealing information through paralanguage and ambient sound). First, we offer a framework that unpacks the complexities of how voice technology can affect consumersâ likelihood to disclose information verbally. Our analysis suggests that speaking to technology can sometimes increase and sometimes decrease verbal disclosure. Our framework provides impetus to gauge when and how voice technology can make consumers more versus less likely to disclose information verbally. Second, we discuss how voice technology allows capturing nonverbal disclosures that are typically involuntary. When consumers speak to connected devices, vocal paralanguage (e.g., the sound of their voice or how something is said) and ambient sounds (e.g., sounds in the current environment and from activities) are inherently captured and reveal information about consumers. We provide an overview of consumer states (e.g., emotions, health conditions, current activities) and traits (habits, ethnicity, personality, identity) that can be inferred from such nonverbal disclosures. Of Interest to Marketers We provide practically relevant suggestions for marketers to aid them in counteracting processes that reduce consumersâ likelihood to disclose information verbally when speaking with connected devices. Additionally, we discuss how vocal paralanguage and ambient sound captured in oral interactions with connected devices can be used as novel data sources to improve targeting effectiveness, specificity, and context-awareness. Of Interest to Policy Makers We provide suggestions for improved consumer protection against mechanisms that may misleadingly increase consumersâ verbal disclosure likelihood when speaking to connected devices. Further, we discuss privacy challenges of collecting and using information inferred from vocal paralanguage and ambient sound inherently captured in oral interactions with technology in light of both U.S. and European privacy legislation. Our analysis suggests that voice technology can increase, but also decrease, disclosure. From our research, policy makers can gain a better understanding of how to regulate the collection and use of information disclosed to voice technology in the interest of consumer welfare. In particular, our analysis calls for higher privacy protections for information disclosed in oral interactions with technology. Read the full article. From: Johann Melzner, Andrea Bonezzi, and Tom Meyvis, âInformation Disclosure in the Era of Voice Technology,â Journal of Marketing. Go to the Journal of Marketing Related Articles Article Bad News? Send an AI. Good News? Send a Human Article Improving Health Insurance Selection and Costs [Best Strategies] Article Using Machine Learning and Natural Language Processing to Measure Consumer Reviews for Product Attribute Insights Academic Academic Research Consumer Behavior Customer Experience Marketing Technology Andrea Bonezzi Andrea Bonezzi is Associate Professor of Marketing, New York University, USA. Johann Melzner Johann Melzner is Assistant Professor of Marketing, University of Miami, USA. Tom Meyvis Tom Meyvis was Merchantsâ Council Professor of Retail Management and Consumer Behavior, New York University, USA.","title":"Do We Reveal More or Less About Ourselves When Interacting with Voice Technology?","url":"https://www.ama.org/2022/11/29/do-we-reveal-more-or-less-about-ourselves-when-interacting-with-voice-technology/","id":671},{"content_hash":"193d804e0c88a7c8bc4d2df9c637b40c836f97c00c5e18562f4173d33b14742f","domain":"youverify.co","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:40.635791+00:00","final_url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/identification-verification-authentication","language":"","text":"Identification vs Verification vs Authentication: What's the Difference?ByEmmanuel Agwu (Edited by Favour Praise)March 15, 2023•5mins ReadKey TakeawaysIdentification, verification, and authentication each serve a distinct role in the identity verification process, answering \"Who are you?\", \"Can you prove it?\", and \"Can you access this system?\" respectively.Identity verification combines document verification, biometric verification, and other trusted methods to confirm a user's identity, reduce identity fraud, and support KYC and AML compliance.Authentication protects verified users by requiring passwords, one-time passwords (OTPs), biometric authentication, or multi-factor authentication (MFA) before granting access to systems or accounts.Using identification, verification, and authentication together helps businesses strengthen customer onboarding, prevent fraud, protect sensitive information, and deliver secure digital identity management.","title":"Identification vs Verification vs Authentication: What's the Difference?","url":"https://youverify.co/en/blogs/identification-verification-authentication","id":672},{"content_hash":"18b4df4ad52336b194a814406aa17d6798ded7d8ec767a493751a313ccd4f65a","domain":"healthline.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:40.145907+00:00","final_url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/bipolar/bipolar-depression","language":"","text":"Key takeaways Bipolar depression involves depressive episodes as part of the mood cycles in bipolar disorder, differing from major depressive disorder, which doesnât include manic or hypomanic phases. Diagnosis of bipolar disorder requires identifying symptoms of mania or hypomania, with bipolar I needing a manic episode and bipolar II needing a hypomanic and a depressive episode. Effective management of bipolar depression includes long-term treatment plans with medications to stabilize mood and psychotherapy like cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) to change harmful thinking patterns. Bipolar disorder is a mental disorder characterized by significant shifts in mood, energy, and behavior. These mood shifts are often severe and debilitating and can make daily functioning very difficult.While mania is the primary feature of bipolar disorder, depression can also be a major aspect of the condition.But does everyone with bipolar disorder experience depression? Are depressive episodes in bipolar similar to other types of depression?What is bipolar depression?Bipolar depression refers to the depressive episodes that are part of the mood cycling in bipolar disorder. Evidence shows that bipolar depressive episodes tend to last longer than periods of mania or hypomania. Depressive episodes can last anywhere from 2 weeks to several months.During a bipolar depressive episode, you may experience the following symptoms:persistent feelings of sadness, worthlessness, or hopelessnesssleeping too little or too muchloss of interest or pleasure in activities that you once enjoyedchanges in appetite or weightmental or physical fatigue or sluggishnesspoor concentration or decisivenesssuicidal thoughtsWhatâs the difference between bipolar depression and major depressive disorder?Depressive symptoms in both bipolar disorder and major depressive disorder (MDD) are the same. According to the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, 5th edition, text revision (DSM-5-TR), you must experience five or more of the following symptoms for at least 2 weeks to be diagnosed with a major depressive episode:depressed mood (sad, hopeless, empty) most of the day, nearly every day â in kids or teens, this can look like irritabilityloss of interest or pleasure in most activitiessignificant changes (more than 5% in a month) in appetite or weightpsychomotor changes such as restlessness or agitation (severe enough to be noticed by others)feeling tired or fatigued or having low energy to complete taskssleep disturbances, such as insomnia or sleeping too mucha sense of worthlessness or delusional guiltpoor ability to think or concentrate, indecisivenessrecurrent thoughts of death, recurrent suicidal ideation without a specific plan, or a suicide attemptWhile depressive episodes are very common in bipolar I, theyâre not actually required for a diagnosis. A depressive episode is, however, required, for a diagnosis of bipolar II.In bipolar disorder, manic and depressive symptoms can also occur at the same time. This is known as âmixed features.â A mixed episode tends to feel like a low mood aggravated by lots of energy.Mixed features might look like the following:irritability or agitationunprovoked rageweeping spellsracing thoughtsinsomniaCan you have bipolar and be mostly depressed?Itâs possible for most of your mood episodes in bipolar disorder to be depressive. However, this is more common in bipolar II disorder.While a depressive episode isnât required for a bipolar I diagnosis (only mania), at least one depressive episode is required for a diagnosis of bipolar II (plus hypomania). Research shows that people with bipolar II tend to have more severe depressive episodes than those with bipolar I. In particular, depressive episodes in bipolar II are often more frequent, longer lasting, and debilitating. Bipolar disorder diagnosisAccording to the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, 5th edition (DSM-5), a diagnosis of bipolar I or II requires three (or more) of the following symptoms to be present (four if the mood is only irritable):inflated self-esteem or feelings of grandiositydecreased need for sleep (may feel rested after only a few hours of sleep)excessive talking or pressure to continue talkingracing thoughts or wild ideasengages in activities that have a high risk for painful consequences (uncontrolled buying sprees, imprudent business investments, or sexual carelessness)easily distractedexcessive focus on goal-oriented activities (either socially, at school/work, or sexually) or psychomotor agitationIn bipolar I, the manic episode must last at least 1 week and be present most of the day, nearly every day (or less if hospitalization is necessary).In bipolar II, hypomania (a less severe form of mania) must last at least 4 consecutive days and be present most of the day, nearly every day. Hypomania may feel like a great mood rather than a debilitating disorder. Bipolar II also requires at least one depressive episode.Treatment options for bipolar depressionBipolar disorder is a chronic disorder, and so it requires a long-term treatment plan, even when you arenât having noticeable symptoms.Medications for bipolar disorder can help stabilize your moods so that you can carry out your daily tasks with as few interruptions as possible.Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT), a type of psychotherapy, is also effective for people with bipolar disorder. CBT is a goal-oriented therapy that helps you identify and change false or harmful thinking patterns. Research shows that CBT can help people with bipolar disorder in a variety of ways:decrease mania severityimprove depressive symptomsdecrease relapse rateimprove psychosocial functioningBottom lineBipolar depression is a depressive episode that occurs as part of the mood cycling in bipolar disorder. Though very common, a depressive episode is not required for a bipolar I diagnosis. It is, however, part of the criteria for bipolar II.If you think youâre experiencing bipolar depression, youâre not alone. Consider reaching out to a mental health professional to get started on a treatment regimen.","title":"What Is Bipolar Depression?","url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/bipolar/bipolar-depression","id":673},{"content_hash":"3188f5fad14418acf3dea4cdc6e05a8fa864b83044ac5af854c9d55a5518c2a6","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:40.101304+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/le-conseil-economique-et-l-echelle-mobile_2071802_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives Le Conseil économique et l'échelle mobile Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"Le Conseil économique et l'échelle mobile","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/le-conseil-economique-et-l-echelle-mobile_2071802_1819218.html","id":674},{"content_hash":"8221edcb0cc356872bae5f69863964f6751e708691747124460f58c1c63d933f","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:39.890408+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/global-climate-models-signal-rainfall-insecurity-in-the-pilbara-16899","language":"","text":"Global climate models signal rainfall insecurity in the Pilbara Published: August 9, 2013 1:23am EDT https://theconversation.com/global-climate-models-signal-rainfall-insecurity-in-the-pilbara-16899 https://theconversation.com/global-climate-models-signal-rainfall-insecurity-in-the-pilbara-16899 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Recent global climate models (GCMs) indicate large uncertainty as to whether the recent wetting trend in most of the Pilbara will continue. Most projections indicate future rainfall is likely resemble the long term average and not increase – as has been experienced in the central and east Pilbara for the past few decades. The models’ median decrease in regional rainfall is projected to be 1% by 2030 and 2% by 2050 for the high emissions scenario. But there is likely to be significant regional variations across such a large area. At the same time, median potential evaporation rates are projected to rise by 3% by 2030 and by 6% by 2050, based on the long-term temperature trends simulated by the models for the high emissions scenario. Read more at CSIRO Source CSIRO Tags Climate change, Emissions, Water, Climate modelling, Global warming, Rainfall, Pilbara","title":"Global climate models signal rainfall insecurity in the Pilbara","url":"https://theconversation.com/global-climate-models-signal-rainfall-insecurity-in-the-pilbara-16899","id":675},{"content_hash":"d3f56340405abc740dde513e6f74d45cf6b1ac4d289515894260f293fbeffe32","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:39.513180+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/politics/article-canadian-intern-nato-biwei-zhang-spy-espionage/","language":"","text":"CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountThe Canadian intern charged with espionage at NATO headquarters in Belgium was found in April near the office of the alliance’s supreme commander after a fire alarm was pulled by a suspected accomplice, a Belgian source familiar with the case told The Globe and Mail. The intern’s unexpected appearance there triggered an investigation into her activities by the counterintelligence service of NATO’s Supreme Headquarters Allied Powers Europe, known as SHAPE. It reported its concerns to Belgian military intelligence. In July, she was arrested by Belgian police and faces espionage charges on suspicion of spying for a third country.What we know so far about the Canadian NATO intern accused of spying for ChinaBiwei Zhang (also known as Claire Zhang) is a former Canadian federal public servant who took an internship at SHAPE in Mons, Belgium, last year. Authorities in Belgium have not publicly named the country that Ms. Zhang is suspected of spying on behalf of, but two Canadian national-security sources have previously told The Globe and Mail that Belgian prosecutors have accused her of working on behalf of China.On April 21 this year, the intern, an IT specialist, was found near the building of U.S. General Alexus Grynkewich, the Supreme Allied Commander Europe, as an evacuation of SHAPE’s personnel took place after the triggering of a fire alarm. Ms. Zhang, a naturalized Canadian of Chinese origin, had already come to the attention of SHAPE’s counterintelligence service after asking colleagues questions about the consequences of security protocol breaches. The Belgian source told The Globe on Friday that the investigation into Ms. Zhang, who is in pretrial detention in Belgium, is probing whether she was aided by accomplices both inside and outside the North Atlantic Treaty Organization. The fire alarm is believed by investigators to have been triggered by someone other than Ms. Zhang, but they have not been identified, the source said. And on the day that it was triggered, according to the source, there was some suspicious activity in the access-control system of the building where the supreme NATO commander works. The Globe is not naming the Belgian source, who is not authorized publicly to discuss the investigation that led to Ms. Zhang’s arrest.They said that after she was arrested and incarcerated, a number of passwords to her social-media accounts were remotely changed. Her apartment in Mons was also broken into while she was behind bars, the source said, sparking questions about whether material that could relate to her case was being searched for.On July 23, the day before her arrest, Belgian officers searched both Ms. Zhang’s home in Belgium and place of work inside NATO’s command centre, with support from a computer crime unit and a forensic and scientific unit, the Belgian prosecutor’s office said after her arrest. A Belgian judge on Monday denied her appeal for release from prison. The Chamber of Indictments decided to extend her pretrial detention by a month. The Belgian source said that there is concern among those working on the investigation that if granted bail, Ms. Zhang could flee the country or try to remove or tamper with evidence material to her case. Ms. Zhang is being represented by highly rated Belgian lawyer Sven Mary. Mr. Mary has defended a number of notorious clients including Michel Lelièvre, an accomplice of Belgian serial killer Marc Dutroux, and Salah Abdeslam, the sole surviving member of the group that carried out the 2015 terrorist attacks in Paris in which 130 people were killed and more than 400 injured.Mr. Mary declined a request to comment. Belgium’s Federal Prosecutor’s Office, which is leading the investigation, said it could not comment on matters relating to the case, adding that it is difficult to predict how long the judicial investigation will take. “The investigation is still actively ongoing and is aimed at further establishing the relevant facts and circumstances,” said spokesperson Yasmina Vanoverschelde in a statement. “At present, there is therefore no indication as to when or whether the case may proceed to trial.”“Depending on the nature and complexity of the case, an investigation may take several months or, in some cases, several years,” she added.The Canadian Security Intelligence Service is working with Belgian authorities on the case. In a statement, CSIS spokesperson Eric Balsam said, “as the Prime Minister has shared, we are directly supporting our Belgian counterparts in support of the ongoing legal process in Brussels.” Some of those involved in the investigation were surprised, according to the source, that Ms. Zhang was given security clearance by the Canadian government before starting her internship at NATO headquarters in Mons, near the French border. They said her background would likely have raised a number of red flags with security experts in Belgium. In Canada’s Parliament last month, the Commons public safety and national security committee launched an inquiry into vetting of public officials, after MPs expressed concern about how Ms. Zhang was cleared by Ottawa to work at NATO headquarters. MPs launch probe into security screening after arrest of alleged NATO spyShe had previously held a number of federal jobs including at Statistics Canada and the Canadian Space Agency. But in 2023, Ms. Zhang was found to have committed fraud within the federal civil service when she wrote the same job exam twice for a single posting at the Canada Border Services Agency, applying under slightly different names and separate e-mail addresses. The public-service watchdog, which investigated her conduct, ruled that if she joined the federal public service within a year, she would have to complete an ethics course.Margaret McCuaig-Johnston, a senior fellow at the University of Ottawa and advisory-board member of the China Strategic Risks Institute, said it is possible that Ms. Zhang, who had “been in numerous and increasingly responsible positions already, did not get the screening depth that she might otherwise have had.” Since Mark Carney took office, she added in an e-mail, Canada’s security agencies “have been spread thin with budget cuts and many demands to screen new employees into sensitive positions in the PM’s and ministers’ offices as well as staff of newly created agencies.”","title":"Canadian intern was found near NATO supreme commander’s office, source says","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/politics/article-canadian-intern-nato-biwei-zhang-spy-espionage/","id":676},{"content_hash":"c61edb956b2060004c3b662da4c2855c7ba425abd50ac9d73b4b340cd3a1e28d","domain":"timesofindia.indiatimes.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:39.501038+00:00","final_url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/etimes/trending/no-sugar-no-flour-moms-unusual-bone-marrow-birthday-cake-for-babys-first-birthday-divides-the-internet-netizens-says-jesus-christ-someone-called-the-child-helpline/articleshow/134806962.cms","language":"","text":"No sugar, no flour! Mom’s unusual ‘bone marrow cake’ for baby's first birthday divides the internet, netizens says 'Jesus Christ, someone called the child helpline' - The Times of IndiaSign InTOITOIhomelifestylerelationshiptrendingHealth+styleparentingsoul searchbeautybooksrecipesfood newstravelhome & gardeneventsspotlightNewsetimeslifestyletrendingNo sugar, no flour! Mom’s unusual ‘bone marrow cake’ for baby's first birthday divides the internet, netizens says 'Jesus Christ, someone called the child helpline'TrendingBaby NamesVibhor JainChildren PsychologyIndiGo Flight Lightning StrikeLeo Tolstoy QuoteYellow Pillow StainsBen AffleckKarl DeisserothRam VilasSharad Navratri 2026Baby NamesVibhor JainChildren PsychologyIndiGo Flight Lightning StrikeLeo Tolstoy QuoteYellow Pillow StainsBen AffleckKarl DeisserothRam VilasSharad Navratri 2026Baby NamesVibhor JainChildren PsychologyIndiGo Flight Lightning StrikeLeo Tolstoy QuoteYellow Pillow StainsBen AffleckKarl DeisserothRam VilasSharad Navratri 2026No sugar, no flour! Mom’s unusual ‘bone marrow cake’ for baby's first birthday divides the internet, netizens says 'Jesus Christ, someone called the child helpline'Trending Desk / etimes.in / Oct 09, 2026, 09:57 ISTSaveCommentsShareAa+Text SizeSmallMediumLargePrint A baby's first birthday usually calls for a cake, a few pictures and plenty of mess. But one mother has taken a very different approach to the celebration, and the internet can't quite agree on it.Content creator Bridget Levine has gone viral after sharing a video of the birthday cake she made for her baby. Instead of the usual flour-and-sugar sponge, she used a block of watermelon and topped it with whipped bone marrow. Yes, bone marrow.The unusual combination has attracted millions of views, with some people praising her efforts to avoid refined sugar and others wondering why anyone would put bone marrow on a birthday cake.Watch the unusual birthday cakeIn the Instagram video, Levine shapes a piece of watermelon into a cake-like block before spreading whipped bone marrow over it. She then presents the creation to her baby, who appears to enjoy eating it.Levine explained that she wanted to make something more nourishing for her child's first birthday, rather than serve a conventional cake made with refined flour and sugar.“At this age, I want the foods I serve her to do more than just fill her up. I want them to provide nutrients her growing body actually needs,” she wrote in the caption.“No refined sugar. No refined flour. Just real, nutrient-dense food turned into a birthday cake she absolutely devoured.”Her choice of ingredients, however, has become the biggest talking point. While watermelon is a familiar fruit, bone marrow is hardly what most people expect to see on a birthday cake.‘Why bone marrow?’The reactions have been split. Some viewers felt the baby should be allowed to enjoy a traditional birthday cake, while others questioned the need to make the occasion so different.“I understand the benefits of bone marrow. I also understand the benefits of just having a little fucking cake on your birthday,” one person commented.Another viewer wasn't convinced by the combination either. “It looks nice, but genuinely why bone marrow? It would've made a little more sense for something like yoghurt or something else that's still healthy, but why Bone marrow? It seems like the type of cake you would make for a dog,” the comment read.But Levine also found support from people who agreed with her approach to feeding her child.“So many people triggered on here lol. The baby clearly loves it and won't remember this anyway. There are soooo many benefits to no processed sugar before 3 years old, and I'm sure these parents will compromise when the child is old enough to go to parties and have other cake,” one user wrote.Another defended the ingredients, arguing that watermelon contains fibre and that the fat in bone marrow could slow the absorption of fructose.The debate has since moved beyond the cake itself, with viewers weighing in on how parents should approach food during a child's early years. For some, the unusual recipe was a sensible way to make a birthday treat using whole foods. For others, it was an unnecessarily complicated alternative to a small slice of cake.Either way, Levine's birthday creation has got people talking. And while a watermelon-and-bone-marrow cake might not be everyone's idea of a celebration, it has certainly made for an unconventional first birthday.Get the latest movie news, reviews, and celebrity updates. Download the TOI App.End of ArticleFollow Us On Social MediaTop TrendingAssam Dancer MurderCJP ProtestJaime Chapman BoyfriendJerry JonesUS Iran WarINDIA Bloc ProtestBengaluru CJP ProtestSimone BilesCJP Chappal ProtestAEW Pac DeathTired of too many ads?go ad free nowTrending StoriesIn SectionEntire WebsiteNeighbors Challenge Estate Plans: Kelce’s Ohio fence fight widens; sewer, wildlife fears growBypoll Election Results 2026 Live Updates: Bypoll counting today for five assembly seats, one Lok Sabha seat in AssamIrvine Ranch Conservation Effort: Donald Bren preserves 57,500 acres; land stays open for future generationsEviction Approved In Long Foreclosure: magistrate orders Telerico out; buyback talks continueRs 2 crore deposit, no ITR, Rs 2.51 crore addition; ITAT quashes because reassessment is 5 days lateRichard Branson bought an island to impress a girl, later bought $15 million Moskito IslandWoman pays £80 for Dior bag, later discovers it's rare 2003 Koi Saddle Bag worth £7,870Mark Zuckerberg’s sister Arielle reveals secret DJ career, 37-year-old playing techno since 2018Kay Adams’ Reactions Go Viral: Clip eclipses Gronk’s Chargers take; fans ask what happenedHurricane Milton Relief Donations: Brady, Swift fuel $6.1m aid drive; app campaign matches giftsNeighbors Challenge Estate Plans: Kelce’s Ohio fence fight widens; sewer, wildlife fears growBypoll Election Results 2026 Live Updates: Bypoll counting today for five assembly seats, one Lok Sabha seat in AssamIrvine Ranch Conservation Effort: Donald Bren preserves 57,500 acres; land stays open for future generationsEviction Approved In Long Foreclosure: magistrate orders Telerico out; buyback talks continueRs 2 crore deposit, no ITR, Rs 2.51 crore addition; ITAT quashes because reassessment is 5 days lateRichard Branson bought an island to impress a girl, later bought $15 million Moskito IslandWoman pays £80 for Dior bag, later discovers it's rare 2003 Koi Saddle Bag worth £7,870Mark Zuckerberg’s sister Arielle reveals secret DJ career, 37-year-old playing techno since 2018Kay Adams’ Reactions Go Viral: Clip eclipses Gronk’s Chargers take; fans ask what happenedHurricane Milton Relief Donations: Brady, Swift fuel $6.1m aid drive; 