flies, license suspended'Egypt were robbed': NYC mayor Zohran Mamdani joins VAR row over Argentina's dramatic World Cup comebackMumbai gets relief from relentless rain, BEST buses, local trains operate smoothly; IMD warns of heavy rainfall in isolated areas'Days, weeks or months': US gearing up for prolonged military campaign against IranRajesh Sharma health update; Actor remains under observation after insect bite; Infection spreads to kneeMaharashtra rains: 3 killed, 800 evacuated in rain-related incidentsHe dropped out of school and became a billionaire, but one book made Nikhil Kamath rethink what success really meansWhy the Income Tax Department is opposing a same-sex couple's plea for spousal tax benefitsLauren Bennett's passing treated as suspected suicide as father says NHS \"failed to treat her appropriately\"'Lenin' first review: Akhil Akkineni roars back with blockbuster-worthy mass entertainerTraffic crawls as heavy rain batters Delhi-NCR; severe waterlogging reported in multiple areas; IMD issues 'red' alertFrom Rajasthan to Texas: Abhimanyu Lamba's pursuit of the American dream | Exclusive'They called a little while ago': Trump claims Iran wants to 'make deal badly' after fresh US strikesSnake bites 4 students in school hostel, 1 diesSunny Deol's new clean-shaven avatar fuels 'Ramayana' speculation; Fans CLAIM it's for Hanuman's role in Nitesh Tiwari's mythological dramaCopyright © 2026 Bennett, Coleman & Co. Ltd. All rights reserved. For reprint rights: Times Syndication ServiceFollow Us On","title":"Watch: IShowSpeed loses it as Cristiano Ronaldo scores his first goal of the 2026 World Cup","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us-streamers/watch-ishowspeed-loses-it-as-cristiano-ronaldo-scores-his-first-goal-of-the-2026-world-cup/articleshow/131949341.cms","id":756},{"content_hash":"f9f196d18cc3aa4145c4b5cfc8329abe7795570a70de4b508f72b72020de06bd","domain":"ml.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:26.185975+00:00","final_url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/healthcare-in-retirement.html","language":"","text":"5 questions to help plan for healthcare costs in retirement Even with Medicare, medical costs could put you at risk of outliving your savings. Are you prepared? “You could call healthcare the biggest retirement expense people fail to plan for. Many folks just assume Medicare is going to pay for everything but, in reality, it only covers about two-thirds of your costs.” — Ben Storey, director, Retirement Research & Insights, Bank of America Premiums, out-of-pocket expenses and critical services not covered by Medicare can deplete your retirement savings. In fact, a healthy 65-year-old couple who retired in 2023 will likely use nearly 70% of their lifetime Social Security benefits to cover their medical costs in retirement.1 Yet only about half of Americans surveyed by the Harris Poll2 said they understood how much they need to cover healthcare costs in retirement. “Retirees counting on Social Security to supplement their retirement income are often surprised to learn that most of it may need to be used for healthcare,” notes Storey. “Your financial advisor can help you estimate and develop a strategy to save for those costs.” Consider these five questions as you work together to develop a plan to help you cover all your healthcare expenses. Click on the links below to jump between questions. How much could medical expenses cost me in retirement? What does Medicare cover, and how much does it cost? What if I retire before I’m eligible for Medicare at age 65? What about my future long-term care needs? Are there other ways to prepare for healthcare costs in retirement? How much could medical expenses cost me in retirement? While Medicare will pay about two thirds of your medical costs, out-of-pocket healthcare expenses like premiums, copays and deductibles can add up. And the post-pandemic economy, with inflation at a 40-year high, has significantly impacted those costs. In fact, healthcare costs have been increasing at one-and-a-half to two times the rate of inflation.3 A 55-year old couple today can expect to pay more than $1 million for healthcare costs during their retirement.3 Medicare won't cover 1/3 of your medical expenses “Such a large amount may put you at risk of outliving your retirement savings,” says Storey. It most likely means that it is time to revisit what you have earmarked for healthcare expenses in retirement. Your advisor can walk you through what Medicare does — and doesn't — cover, estimate how much you may need to put aside for future healthcare costs and develop a plan to help you get there.” “As you estimate your potential costs, consider factors such as your current health, your family’s health history, where you'll retire (medical costs can vary across the country) and whether your employer offers retiree health coverage,” says Storey. Your advisor may recommend adjusting your financial plan to include a one-time lump sum investment or an increase in your regular investment contributions to cover these additional costs. Other changes might include taking on more risk in your portfolio or reprioritizing your goals. The approach you take will depend on your individual circumstances but just like investing for any goal, the sooner you start, the better your chances of being successful. What does Medicare cover, and how much does it cost? Medicare Parts A and B, known as Original Medicare, cover a portion of hospital stays and medical services — but not vision, hearing or dental care; prescription drugs; or medical care outside the U.S. So, you may want to add a drug plan (Medicare Part D) and a Medicare Supplement Insurance Policy, commonly known as Medigap, which can help cover out-of-pocket costs as long as you have Original Medicare. Medicare Advantage, or Part C, is a private insurance alternative that bundles much of that coverage but often restricts you to a limited network of providers. While Medicare Part A is typically premium-free, you will pay monthly premiums for Part B, some Part C Medicare Advantage plans, Part D prescription coverage and Medigap. 2025 Medicare Part A No Premium 2025 Medicare Part B $185.00 or higher monthly premium And while there is no premium for Medicare Part A, there is a $1,676 deductible in 2025 for each inpatient hospital benefit period, before Medicare starts to pay. And there is no limit to the number of benefit periods that can take place in a year. This means that you could pay the deductible amount many times if you have multiple hospital stays during a year.4 The monthly premium for Medicare Part B is $185 in 2025 or higher depending on your income. You’ll pay the higher premium if your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) is more than $106,000 (from your tax return from two years prior), if you file an individual tax return or are married and file separately or if your MAGI is more than $212,000, if you are married and file a joint tax return. The higher monthly premium costs range from $259 to $628.90 based on income.4 In addition, Medicare Part D premiums can also be impacted by income. The monthly cost, in addition to the plan premium, can range from $13.70 to $85.80.5 Medicare plans at a glance Medicare Plan Part A (Hospital insurance) Covers inpatient hospital, skilled nursing, home healthcare, hospice care Out-of-pocket costs: Deductibles, coinsurance and copays Medicare Plan Part B (Medical coverage) Covers doctors’ visits, outpatient care, other medical services Out-of-pocket costs: Monthly premiums, deductibles, coinsurance and copays Medicare Plan Part C (Medicare Advantage) Covers private alternative to Parts A, B and D, plus optional benefits; network limits common Out-of-pocket costs: Variable costs determined by insurer Medicare Plan Part D (Prescription drugs) Covers brand name and generic drugs Out-of-pocket costs: Monthly premiums, deductibles, coinsurance and copays Medicare Supplement Insurance (Medigap) Paired with Parts A and B to help with some of the costs those plans don't cover Out-of-pocket costs: Monthly premiums 1 of 5 What if I retire before I’m eligible for Medicare at age 65? Unless your employer offers retiree coverage, you’ll need to consider other health insurance options so that you don’t go without coverage until you are Medicare eligible. The cost of coverage should be fully explored among various resources and can vary widely. Your options include: Joining your partner’s plan if they are still working or taking on a new job that provides you with healthcare benefits.Sticking with your employer’s insurance plan under COBRA (the Consolidated Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act) for up to 18 months.Purchasing health insurance directly from a company or through an insurance broker or purchasing health insurance on the government-managed exchange.Purchasing a high-deductible health plan that meets federal tax standards permitting you to open a health savings account (HSA). What about my future long-term care needs? “Long-term care insurance can help improve the chances that your savings will last through your full retirement,” says Storey. Even with all the medical coverage available through Medicare, there’s one element that falls through the cracks — the cost of long-term care. “Savings can get eaten up pretty quickly,” notes Storey. And many people are unprepared to meet those costs. Nearly 70% chance of someone turning 65 today requiring some type of long-term care during their lifetime.6 Show text version Hide text version 28% of ages 50-64 have set aside money that could be used to pay for future living assistance expenses, compared to 48% for ages 65 and older.7 Purchasing insurance to cover the costs can help when you need to pay for a caregiver to keep you in your home or if you need to move to a long-term care facility. It can cost upwards of $100,000 per year for a private room in a nursing home.8 The annual long-term care insurance premium for a couple (both aged 65) with a benefit of $165,000 (increasing by 5% each year) was $9,675 in 2022.9 But if you haven’t purchased it by the time you’re in your 60s, the premiums on traditional long-term care policies may be out of reach and, after age 70, it may be harder to obtain coverage. Other options to consider include hybrid life insurance/long-term care policies, which are designed to provide long-term care coverage as well as cash value you could tap. In addition, some life insurance policies have a long-term care rider that can provide coverage. “The sooner you consider your needs, the more options you’ll have,” says Storey. Are there other ways to prepare for healthcare costs in retirement? You could self-fund by increasing the amount you save in your 401(k) or other retirement accounts. “But a health savings account (HSA), with its potential tax advantages,10 is a particularly powerful way to save for medical expenses,” says Storey. To qualify for an HSA, you must have a high-deductible health plan, and you can’t be covered by any other medical plan or be enrolled in Medicare. Opening an HSA could help you accumulate critical additional funds to pay for eligible expenses in retirement, including long-term care services and insurance premiums. Once you enroll in Medicare, you can no longer contribute to your HSA but you can use the funds you’ve saved to cover most out-of-pocket medical expenses and Medicare premiums (Medigap Supplement Insurance premiums are an exception). Either way, “the more you can earmark for healthcare as you save for retirement, the better,” Storey sums up. Choose your advisor in a more personalized way All our advisors are committed to putting your needs and priorities first. Find some who match your personal preferences too. Loading... Try Advisor Match Answer a few questions Want us to contact you? Submit a request Phone Loading... Email Loading... View your advisor 1 HealthView Services, “Medicare and Social Security COLAs: Putting the 2023 Numbers into Context,” October 2023. 2 The Nationwide Retirement Institute, “2022 Health Care Cost in Retirement Survey,” October 2022. 3 HealthView Services, “2022 Retirement Healthcare Costs Data Report,” March 2022. 4 Medicare. “Costs”. Accessed January 30, 2025. 5 Medicare. “Monthly premium for drug plans.” Accessed January 30, 2025. 6 U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, “How Much Care Will You Need?” 2020. 7 American Association for Long Term Care Insurance. 2022 long-term care insurance statistics data facts. Accessed January 30, 2024. 8 Kaiser Family Foundation. “The Affordability of Long-Term Care and Support Services: Findings from a KFF Survey.” November 2023. 9 Genworth, “Cost of Care Trends and Insights,” 2022. 10 You can receive tax-free distributions from your HSA to pay or be reimbursed for qualified medical expenses you incur after you establish the HSA. If you receive distributions for other reasons, the amount you withdraw will be subject to income tax and may be subject to an additional 20% tax, unless an exception applies. Any interest or earnings on the 411 assets in the account are tax-free. You may be able to claim a tax deduction for contributions you, or someone other than your employer, make to your HSA directly (not through payroll deductions). In addition, HSA contributions may reduce your state income taxes in certain states. Certain limits may apply to employees who are considered highly compensated key employees. Bank of America recommends you contact qualified tax or legal counsel before establishing an HSA. This material should be regarded as educational information on healthcare considerations and is not intended to provide specific healthcare advice. If you have questions regarding your particular healthcare situation, please contact your healthcare, legal or tax advisor. Long-term-care insurance coverage contains benefits, exclusions, limitations, eligibility requirements and specific terms and conditions under which the insurance coverage may be continued in force or discontinued. Not all insurance policies and types of coverage may be available in your state. Life insurance policy guarantees are backed by the claims-paying ability of the issuing insurance company. They are not backed by Bank of America, Merrill or its affiliates, nor does Bank of America, Merrill or its affiliates make any representations or guarantees regarding the claims-paying ability of the issuing insurance company. You can receive tax-free distributions from your HSA to pay or be reimbursed for qualified medical expenses you incur after you establish the HSA. If you receive distributions for other reasons, the amount you withdraw will be subject to income tax and may be subject to an additional 20% tax, unless an exception applies. Any interest or earnings on the 411 assets in the account are tax-free. You may be able to claim a tax deduction for contributions you, or someone other than your employer, make to your HSA directly (not through payroll deductions). In addition, HSA contributions may reduce your state income taxes in certain states. Certain limits may apply to employees who are considered highly compensated key employees. Bank of America recommends you contact qualified tax or legal counsel before establishing an HSA. Merrill, its affiliates, and financial advisors do not provide legal, tax, or accounting advice. You should consult your legal and/or tax advisors before making any financial decisions. This information should not be construed as investment advice and is subject to change. It is provided for informational purposes only and is not intended to be either a specific offer by Bank of America, Merrill or any affiliate to sell or provide, or a specific invitation for a consumer to apply for, any particular retail financial product or service that may be available Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Switch to Accessibility Friendly View Facebook opens a dialog X opens a dialog Linkedin opens a dialog Plan ahead for long-term care Being prepared for unexpected healthcare costs could be key to your retirement strategy. Explore your options More for you More for you Do you have a retirement spending plan? Connect Find out how our advisors can help Sign up Get monthly insights from our Perspectives newsletter Related Articles 3 steps to help your money last in retirement Read more Ways to replace your salary in retirement Read more","title":"5 questions to help plan for healthcare costs in retirement","url":"https://www.ml.com/articles/healthcare-in-retirement.html","id":757},{"content_hash":"75d4377847549cc8ac31e40fdbe94536a27a4262061930c180deb6d10a645fc6","domain":"healthline.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:25.747151+00:00","final_url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/hyperosmia","language":"","text":"Hyperosmia, or extreme smell sensitivity, can occur due to certain health conditions, including migraine, pregnancy, and Lyme disease, among others. Treating the underlying cause may help reduce symptoms.Hyperosmia is a heightened and hypersensitive sense of smell that has been associated with a number of medical conditions. Loss of smell is more common than hyperosmia. Outside of conditions that are known to cause this disorder, chronic hyperosmia can sometimes occur without any clear cause. SymptomsPeople with hyperosmia can experience strong discomfort and even illness from certain smells. Exposure to chemical odors like synthetic fragrances, perfumes, and cleaning products can trigger mild to severe discomfort. Even the scent of certain shampoos can be too much.Exposure to odors and toxic vapors that aggravate your hyperosmia may lead to anxiety and depression. Individual triggers and irritants vary from one person to the next. Complications and associated conditionsHyperosmia is sometimes caused by migraines. One study found that between 25 to 50 percent of the 50 patients in their study experienced some version of hyperosmia during their migraine attacks. 11 of the patients experienced hyperosmia before the actual migraine.Severe cases of hyperosmia can disrupt your life by causing anxiety and depression, especially if youâre unsure what smells might trigger the discomfort. This can be isolating because it may be difficult for you to attend certain events or go to certain places.CausesHyperosmia is associated with multiple conditions and can trigger a variety of symptoms. Some conditions associated with hyperosmia can cause the change in smell, and vice versa. Because of this, it may be difficult for you to determine whether your hyperosmia is a symptom of a larger disorder or the cause of it.PregnancyOne of the most common causes of hyperosmia is pregnancy. An early symptom of pregnancy is a heightened sense of smell. This can trigger headaches, nausea, and vomiting during first-trimester morning sickness. Itâs also associated with hyperemesis gravidarum, a severe form of morning sickness that can lead to hospitalization. Symptoms often fade as the pregnancy goes on, and typically go away after birth.MigrainesMigraine headaches can cause and be caused by hyperosmia. Heightened sensitivity to smells can happen between migraine episodes. Odor sensitivity can also trigger a migraine or make you more susceptible to having them.Lyme diseaseLyme disease is another illness that is associated with hyperosmia. In one study, 50% of Lyme disease patients experienced a heightened sense of smell. If you think you might have been exposed to Lyme disease, talk to your doctor about being tested.Autoimmune diseasesRecently, researchers have begun studying links between autoimmune diseases like Addisonâs disease. Hyperosmia is also a symptom of untreated adrenal insufficiency, which is a precursor to Addisonâs disease.Neurological conditionsSome neurologic conditions have also been linked to hyperosmia, including multiple sclerosis (MS), Parkinsonâs, Alzheimerâs, and epilepsy. Multiple sclerosis is known to affect senses like taste and smell. Loss of smell is most common in these conditions. With the exception of MS, people with these conditions may experience hyperosmia instead. In rare cases, neoplastic growths like polyps or tumors can occur intranasally or intracrannially. These may affect the olfactory nerve.Other possible causes of hyperosmia include:allergiessterile meningitisdiabetesCushing syndromeB-12 deficiencynutrient deficienciescertain prescription medicationThe condition (or predisposition to hyperosmia) may also be genetic. More research needs to be done into its causes and possible treatments. TreatmentIf you have hyperosmia, chewing peppermint gum can help until you can move away from the triggering smell.Successful long-term treatment of hyperosmia involves pinpointing and treating the underlying cause of the symptom. Treatment based on the root cause should alleviate your hypersensitivity to odors. Work with your doctor to determine the cause. If you donât already have a primary care doctor, the Healthline FindCare tool can help you find a physician in your area.If a growth like a polyp or tumor is causing hyperosmia, surgical removal may alleviate the symptoms. Migraine medications can help treat hyperosmia when migraines are the root cause. Migraine medications can also prevent migraines from occurring as a result of hyperosmia.Avoiding specific triggers when possible is valuable. Triggers are different for each person. Some people are triggered by certain foods. Others canât tolerate perfume or chemical smells.Itâs possible that your prescription medication could cause you to experience hyperosmia. If youâve experienced hyperosmia after starting a new prescription, you should ask your doctor about changing medications. OutlookIf youâre able to pinpoint and treat the underlying cause of your hyperosmia, your long-term outlook looks good. You should be able to make a full recovery.Hyperosmia can be difficult to treat when the underlying cause is difficult to find. In these cases, managing symptoms is the best approach until the cause is found.In the meantime, reduce or eliminate your exposure to irritant odors as much as possible. Try to track what types of smells give you the most trouble. If youâre experiencing symptoms of depression or anxiety as a result of the condition, make an appointment to see a counselor to help you cope.","title":"Hyperosmia","url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/hyperosmia","id":758},{"content_hash":"b4553da231a9a50db2ff6b9349cc924e145618aab113b7ae6eec93f04f6c7c61","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:25.715421+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-08/wallabies-all-blacks-joseph-aukuso-suaalii-bledisloe-cup/107242770","language":"","text":"Wallabies keep Joseph-Aukuso Suaalii on wing for Bledisloe Cup Test against All BlacksBy Luke PentonyTopic:Rugby UnionPosted Thu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:26pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:26pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 1:26pm, updated Thu 8 Oct 2026 at 2:08pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 2:08pmThu 8 Oct 2026 at 2:08pmJoseph-Aukuso Suaalii has been named on the wing for the Wallabies. (Getty Images: Kenta Harada)In short:The Wallabies will keep Joseph-Aukuso Suaalii on the wing for Saturday night's Bledisloe Cup Test at Eden Park.Isaac Henry is the only change to the Wallabies' starting XV after being included at outside centre.What's next?ABC Sport will live blog the Bledisloe Cup Test on Saturday night.abc.net.au/news/wallabies-all-blacks-joseph-aukuso-suaalii-bledisloe-cup/107242770Link copiedShareShare articleThe Wallabies have kept Joseph-Aukuso Suaalii on the wing for Saturday night's Bledisloe Cup Test at Auckland's Eden Park, with Isaac Henry named in the centres.Henry has been included at outside centre to partner Len Ikitau in the Wallabies' midfield against the All Blacks.Ikitau, who has recovered from an ankle injury, moves from outside to inside centre in the wake of Hunter Paisami's suspension.Wallabies upset Boks in PerthThe Wallabies manage to overcome four late yellow cards to defeat world champions South Africa 42-38 in a pulsating Test in Perth.Wallabies coach Les Kiss resisted the temptation to shift Suaalii from the wing back to the centres.He has shown faith in 27-year-old Henry, who he coached at the Queensland Reds.Kiss also handed Henry his Test debut two months ago, against Japan in Osaka.Henry, who will be playing his fifth Test, is the only change to the Wallabies' starting XV following their stunning 42-38 win over world champions South Africa in Perth late last month.The Wallabies made four changes on the reserves bench, opting for a 5-3 (forwards-back split).Hooker Josh Nasser, prop Massimo De Lutiis, back-rower Nick Champion de Crespigny and fly half Declan Meredith earned call-ups.De Lutiis and Champion de Crespigny are the respective replacements for Taniela Tupou and Tom Hooper, who have returned to their European clubs.Isaac Henry will wear the number 13 jersey against the All Blacks. (Getty Images: Paul Miller)\"Keeping consistency in the starting side gives us a solid foundation, while adding some freshness on the bench will provide energy late in the game,\" Kiss said in a statement.The Wallabies have enjoyed a six-match unbeaten streak after beginning the Test season with defeats to Ireland and France in the Nations Championship.Since Kiss took over from Joe Schmidt as coach, the Wallabies have recorded four wins and a draw.But the All Blacks pose an almighty challenge, especially at Eden Park.The Wallabies have lost 24 consecutive Tests against the All Blacks at the venue, with their most recent victory coming in 1986.They did, however, beat Wales at Eden Park in the third-place play-off at the 2011 Rugby World Cup.\"There aren't many tougher challenges than playing New Zealand at Eden Park, but it's one we're excited by and know that we will need to be at our best in all aspects of our game,\" Kiss said.Damian McKenzie will start at fly half for the All Blacks. (Getty Images: Fiona Goodall)Meanwhile, injury has forced the All Blacks to name Damian McKenzie at fly half as they look to maintain their stranglehold on the Bledisloe Cup.McKenzie will start at 10 after Ruben Love (ankle) and Richie Mo'unga (calf) were ruled out.Beauden Barrett will play fullback, with McKenzie having held down the role for all four Tests in the All Blacks' recent 3-1 series loss to the Springboks.All three Barrett brothers will start against the Wallabies, with ex-captain Scott named at lock and Jordie slotting in the backline at inside centre.The All Blacks have held the Bledisloe Cup since 2003.Wallabies squad (1-15): Angus Bell, Brandon Paenga-Amosa, Allan Alaalatoa, Jeremy Williams, Lukhan Salakaia-Loto, Rob Valetini, Fraser McReight, Harry Wilson (C), Ryan Lonergan, Ben Donaldson, Joseph-Aukuso Suaalii, Len Ikitau, Isaac Henry, Max Jorgensen, Tom WrightReplacements: Josh Nasser, Aidan Ross, Massimo De Lutiis, Lachlan Shaw, Nick Champion de Crespigny, Tate McDermott, Declan Meredith, Harry PotterAll Blacks squad (1-15): Ethan de Groot, Codie Taylor (C), Fletcher Newell, Scott Barrett, Josh Lord, Tupou Vaa'i, Peter Lakai, Wallace Sititi, Cam Roigard, Damian McKenzie, Leroy Carter, Jordie Barrett, Quinn Tupaea, Will Jordan, Beauden BarrettReplacements: Asafo Aumua, George Bower, Pasilio Tosi, Shannon Frizell, Anton Segner. Kyle Preston, Rieko Ioane, Josh MoorbyShare optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)See more ABC coverage in your search resultsMake ABC a preferred source in GooglePromotionSportButters unveiled by Bulldogs after 'megadeal', Blakey makes his decisionLIVEWallabies must 'walk the walk' if 40-year-old curse is to endAnalysis by Luke PentonyKiwi driver endures 42G impact from high-speed crash at BathurstTopic:Motor SportsThe mixed fortunes of Australia's last South African touring partyTopic:CricketPiastri hopes for better in Singapore as fading challenger promises 'all-out' attackTopic:Formula 1Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerRelated storiesWhy NRL grand final 'freak' is making Wallabies lick their lipsTopic:Rugby UnionWallabies reduced to 11 men in chaotic upset win over world champ BoksTopic:Rugby UnionRelated topicsAustraliaNew ZealandRugby UnionSportTop StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Wallabies keep Joseph-Aukuso Suaalii on wing for Bledisloe Cup Test against All Blacks","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-08/wallabies-all-blacks-joseph-aukuso-suaalii-bledisloe-cup/107242770","id":759},{"content_hash":"70bdbf40743d2f388b04da3e63cbb2e59c6056d626ca234681dbf323c6c651df","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:25.586975+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/culture/film-and-tv/film/article-behind-the-scenes-of-a-toronto-filmmakers-terry-fox-treasure-hunt/","language":"","text":"Open this photo in gallery:Sean Menard's documentary Run Terry Run draws on 96 reels of restored and largely unseen footage of Terry Fox.Run Terry Run/Supplied CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountIn the Canadian film world, there is a typical path to follow. If you’re lucky, your project gets accepted into a festival at home or abroad. If you’re really lucky, it’ll show in a handful of theatres. If you’re exceptionally lucky, that theatrical run will lead to a healthy long shelf-life on streaming and television. But most of the time, it is a constant fight for attention and eyeballs. And if you’re releasing a documentary instead of a feature narrative film? Well, good luck. Canadian director Sean Menard doesn’t believe much in chance – he’s more a do-it-yourself-or-die-trying kind of guy. In 2023, after securing a premiere at the SXSW Film Festival in Austin, Tex., for his MuchMusic documentary 299 Queen Street West, Menard skipped the theatrical-release playbook and rented out Toronto’s Roy Thomson Hall for the film’s hometown premiere (spreading the charges across three credit cards). Open this photo in gallery:Menard at the Roy Thomson Hall premiere of 299 Queen Street West in 2023.GEORGE PIMENTEL/SuppliedThat gambit led to what the director calls a successful road-show across the country that brought in $600,000 in ticket sales – an unusual method of self-distribution that Menard is looking to replicate with his new Terry Fox documentary, Run Terry Run. The film will have its world premiere at Roy Thomson Hall Nov. 10, featuring a live musical score performed by the Toronto Symphony Orchestra. Ahead of the premiere, Menard spoke with The Globe and Mail about Fox’s Marathon of Hope and his own long-distance run in the film industry. There have been several movies and documentaries about Terry Fox’s life. What did you feel was missing? I just never got a sense of who he really was, and why he was doing the race, straight from his perspective. I wanted to make a documentary that brought people back to 1980. The entire film is set then. There are no look-backs, no view from the modern day. And I had a feeling that there would be missing reels out there somewhere, because I was watching all this footage on YouTube and it was incredibly low, VHS quality that someone had put up from some other source. If I could track down any original film, I could digitize it into 4K and we’d have an image of a Canadian icon that no one’s ever seen before. You say this film features footage from 96 previously unseen reels, and unheard audio interviews. How did you track that down?I started just asking questions, with Terry’s family. It then became a treasure hunt for nine months, and I was eventually pointed in the direction of a storage facility, which ended up being five street lights from my house in Toronto. Who did the footage belong to?The Terry Fox Foundation had accumulated a bunch of this footage over the years, and were paying to store it. At some point, no one had the ability to view the reels any more, so nothing was properly labelled. It was me trying to piece it all together. Years ago, a filmmaker named John Simpson was the first to film Terry on the East Coast. He started gathering stuff, and the foundation had acquired his footage. But he’d say for years that the stuff was thrown out, so he was pretty shocked to find it existed.Open this photo in gallery:The entire film is set in 1980, during the Marathon of Hope.HO/The Canadian PressDoes John have any involvement with this project? I’ve spoken with him on the phone and invited him to the premiere. He’s coming, and he’s excited. But he’s very shocked it existed. I started digitizing the reels in February, then conducting interviews. But I only have four interviews in the film, just the people who were directly tied to the story. I had a long rough cut three or four months later. It was the fastest I’d ever gone through a story, because it’s a linear narrative. The MuchMusic film, that was 30 years’ worth of archives, so it was much longer. Why premiere this at Roy Thomson yourself, and not go the traditional route of a festival or a theatrical release? We’re calling it an “advance screening,” because we still want to be able to hold on to a festival opportunity for a premiere. I wanted to have something where ticket sales could go to the very thing that Terry was running for. I spoke to the Foundation, and they came in and handled it.So you’re not putting your own money into this, like the last time? Well, I put my own money into making the film. But not the money to screen it or distribute it. What was your takeaway from that first time, with the MuchMusic documentary? Did you make your money back? We did 15 concert halls in that tour, and the money I made off of that I put directly into funding this project. Distributors would tell me back then, “Oh, no one is going to pay $15 to see a documentary in theatres.” And I’d say they’re wrong, because people want to have that experience, especially if the subject matter lends itself to an emotional response. I didn’t set out to try and break the distribution model. I just tried to make my money back. I have to ask about the status of the MuchMusic film, because last I heard, it was set to stream on Crave and then it was pulled just before the release date. The word out there was that there were rights issues with the music used. I don’t know legally how much I can speak on it, other than to say the record labels and the rights holders have reached an agreement, and they’re just getting the necessary approvals from publishers and artists. There’s hope that it will come out by the end of the year. Run Terry Run screens Nov. 10 at Roy Thomson Hall in Toronto. More information at terryfox.org.This interview was condensed and edited.","title":"Behind the scenes of a Toronto filmmaker’s Terry Fox treasure hunt","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/culture/film-and-tv/film/article-behind-the-scenes-of-a-toronto-filmmakers-terry-fox-treasure-hunt/","id":760},{"content_hash":"b5254a47a233124594e8de793bdae416a0d5b53d914d505f996997f6eb6bd36c","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:25.558580+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-fleischmann-chef-de-la-production-de-guerre-americaine-vient-en-europe_2072048_1819218.html","language":"","text":"Cet article vous est offert Pour lire gratuitement cet article réservé aux abonnés, connectez-vous Se connecter Vous n'êtes pas inscrit sur Le Monde ? Inscrivez-vous gratuitement Archives Archives Archives M. Fleischmann chef de la production de guerre américaine vient en Europe Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"M. Fleischmann chef de la production de guerre américaine vient en Europe","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/m-fleischmann-chef-de-la-production-de-guerre-americaine-vient-en-europe_2072048_1819218.html","id":761},{"content_hash":"d7e7f7ee3619fc9e4fca4a4ffa8c4934b8cbdba1416483fbcf494f7de05a0ac9","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:25.495522+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/chinas-farms-pose-growing-antibiotic-resistance-risk-12144","language":"","text":"China’s farms pose growing antibiotic resistance risk Published: February 11, 2013 5:53pm EST Widespread and unmonitored use of antibiotics in commercial Chinese pig farms may pose a risk to human health worldwide, the study said. podchef, http://www.flickr.com/photos/86571141@N00 https://theconversation.com/chinas-farms-pose-growing-antibiotic-resistance-risk-12144 https://theconversation.com/chinas-farms-pose-growing-antibiotic-resistance-risk-12144 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Unchecked use of antibiotics in Chinese farms had led to widespread antibiotic resistance, a new study has found, with researchers warning the problem may spread worldwide through manure and fertiliser run-off. China is the world’s biggest producer and consumer of antibiotics in the world, with at least 46.1% being used in livestock, as much as four times the amount used by livestock industry in the US. Some bacteria have genes that make them resistant to antibiotics, while others can get such resistance from another bacteria. Overuse of antibiotics kills the germs with no resistance but grows the proportion of resistant bacteria in the environment. In a study published today in the journal PNAS, researchers from the Chinese Academy of Sciences and Michigan State University analysed manure from three large scale commercial pig farms in China, looking for antibiotic resistant genes (ARGs) in bacteria. They also tested a control sample of manure from pigs never fed antibiotics and soil from a pristine forest in Putian, China. “We detected 149 unique ARGs among all of the [commercial farm] samples, which is three times more types of ARGs than were found in the control samples,” the researchers said in their paper, warning that the antibiotic resistant genes could spread globally and pose a human health risk. “Microbes from manure, compost, or soil containing the ARGs are subject to dispersal via runoff into rivers, leaching to subsurface waters, air dispersal via dust, human travel, and distribution of agricultural products, including compost for gardening, which could expand a local contamination to regional and even global scales.” The researchers also found high levels of metals such as arsenic and copper in the commercial farm samples, and theorise that the metals may be contributing to the development and persistence of antibiotic resistance. “Policies and management tools to facilitate prudent use of antibiotics and heavy metals, including their combined use, in animal industries and animal waste management are needed,” the researchers said. A co-author of the study, graduate student Timothy Johnson from Michigan State University, said the researchers did not test for superbugs, which are germs that are resistant to a combination of antibiotics. “We do see that there is extensive and alarming enrichment for antibiotic resistance genes, but these genes may be spread out in many different bacteria so that individual bacteria may not be resistant to many antibiotics, or it may be the case that they are superbugs. With the data we have we just cannot say if they are superbugs or not,” he said. Professor Matthew Cooper from the University of Queensland’s Institute for Molecular Bioscience said this type of resistance evolution had been seen before but this paper quantified it in Chinese pigs for the first time. “If you look at the reports and many studies, there is a recognised and clear statistical link between the use of antibiotics as growth promoters and the incidence of superbugs in animals and their transfer to the human population,” said Professor Cooper, who was not involved in the study. “That drove the European Union to ban antibiotics as growth promoters, then more recently the US has begun to ban more and more classes of antibiotics and Australia have done the same.” The scale of the problem means that a collective approach is needed, he said. “Let’s take the hypothesis that a country was the only country in the world that used antibiotics – that doesn’t help us at all. We need some type of framework whereby all countries standardised the practice.” Professor Cooper called on authorities in China and India to bring in more transparent reporting measures for antibiotic use, saying that people should be very concerned by the results of this new study. “Animals get transported between countries, as do people. Infectious disease is no longer a national issue, it’s a global issue.” Superbugs vs Antibiotics Georgina Scambler, The Conversation, Sunanda Creagh, The Conversation, Matthew Cooper Authors Georgina Scambler Editor Sunanda Creagh Editor Interviewed Matthew Cooper Prof. Institute for Molecular Bioscience, The University of Queensland Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.4xshcvanq","title":"China’s farms pose growing antibiotic resistance risk","url":"https://theconversation.com/chinas-farms-pose-growing-antibiotic-resistance-risk-12144","id":762},{"content_hash":"3111f1c7a7740f1c50e80f5ee86acf6d41859f6195de67de58fc54d6aaccda76","domain":"healthline.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:24.914230+00:00","final_url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/photophobia","language":"","text":"You may experience sensitivity to light with health conditions that affect the brain or eyes. These can include migraine and corneal abrasion.Light sensitivity is a condition in which bright lights hurt your eyes. Another name for this condition is photophobia. Itâs a common symptom thatâs associated with several different conditions, ranging from minor irritations to serious medical emergencies.Mild cases make you squint in a brightly lit room or while outside. In more severe cases, this condition causes considerable pain when your eyes are exposed to almost any type of light. What causes photophobia?MigrainePhotophobia is a common symptom of migraine. Migraine causes severe headaches that can be triggered by a number of factors, including hormonal changes, foods, stress, and environmental changes. Other symptoms include throbbing in one part of your head, nausea, and vomiting.Itâs estimated that more than 10 percent of people around the world have migraine. They also occur more often in women than in men.Conditions That Affect the BrainLight sensitivity is commonly associated with a few serious conditions that affect the brain. These include:EncephalitisEncephalitis occurs when your brain is inflamed from a viral infection or other cause. Severe cases of it can be life-threatening.MeningitisMeningitis is a bacterial infection that causes inflammation of the membranes surrounding the brain and spinal cord. The bacterial form can lead to serious complications such as brain damage, hearing loss, seizures, and even death.Subarachnoid hemorrhageA subarachnoid hemorrhage occurs when you have bleeding between your brain and the surrounding layers of tissue. It can be fatal or lead to brain damage or a stroke.Conditions that affect the eyesPhotophobia is also common in several conditions that affect the eyes. These include:Corneal abrasionA corneal abrasion is an injury to the cornea, the outermost layer of the eye. This type of injury is common and can happen if you get sand, dirt, metal particles, or other substances in your eyes. This can lead to a serious condition called a corneal ulcer if the cornea becomes infected.ScleritisScleritis occurs when the white part of your eye becomes inflamed. About half of all cases are caused by diseases that affect the immune system, such as lupus. Other symptoms include eye pain, watery eyes, and blurred vision.ConjunctivitisAlso known as âpink eye,â conjunctivitis occurs when the layer of tissue that covers the white part of your eye becomes infected or inflamed. Itâs mostly caused by viruses, but it can also be caused by bacteria and allergies. Other symptoms include itching, redness, and eye pain.Dry eye syndromeDry eye occurs when your tear glands canât make enough tears or make poor-quality tears. It results in your eyes being excessively dry. Causes include age, environmental factors, certain medical conditions, and some medications. When to seek immediate careSome conditions that cause sensitivity to light are considered medical emergencies. If you have this symptom and any other symptoms associated with one of these conditions, you should seek immediate medical care.Corneal abrasionSymptoms include:blurry visionpain or burning in your eyerednessthe sensation that you have something in your eyeEncephalitisSymptoms include:severe headachefeverbeing difficult to arouseconfusionMeningitisSymptoms include:fever and chillssevere headachestiff necknausea and vomitingSubarachnoid hemorrhageSymptoms include:sudden and severe headache that feels worse toward the back of your headirritability and confusionreduced awarenessnumbness in parts of your body How to treat photophobiaHome careStaying out of sunlight and keeping the lights dimmed inside can help make photophobia less uncomfortable. Keeping your eyes closed or covering them with dark, tinted glasses can also provide relief.Medical treatmentConsult your doctor immediately if youâre experiencing severe light sensitivity. Your doctor will perform a physical examination as well as an eye exam. They may also ask questions about the frequency and severity of your symptoms to determine the cause.The type of treatment you need will depend on the underlying cause. Types of treatment include:medications and rest for migraineeye drops that reduce inflammation for scleritisantibiotics for conjunctivitisartificial tears for mild dry eye syndromeantibiotic eye drops for corneal abrasionsanti-inflammatory medications, bed rest, and fluids for mild cases of encephalitis (Severe cases require supportive care, such as breathing assistance.)antibiotics for bacterial meningitis (The viral form usually clears up on its own within 2 weeks.)surgery to remove excess blood and relieve pressure on your brain for subarachnoid hemorrhage Tips to prevent photophobiaWhile you may not be able to prevent light sensitivity, certain behaviors can help prevent some of the conditions that can cause photophobia, including the following:Try to avoid the triggers that cause you to have migraine attacks.Prevent conjunctivitis by practicing good hygiene, not touching your eyes, and not sharing eye makeup.Reduce your risk of getting meningitis by avoiding contact with people who are infected, washing your hands often, and getting immunized against bacterial meningitis.Help prevent encephalitis by washing your hands frequently.Getting vaccinations against encephalitis and avoiding exposure to mosquitoes and ticks can also help prevent encephalitis. OutlookLight sensitivities may be resolved, but you first need to see your doctor to help diagnose the exact cause of photophobia. Treating the underlying cause may help your symptoms.Talk to your doctor if youâre experiencing severe photophobia or for more suggestions to reduce your symptoms.","title":"What Causes Light Sensitivity?","url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/photophobia","id":763},{"content_hash":"31feaa410ae414c3769c5f60e6db98d87966f2343331ff0b105347841e7a209f","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:24.489016+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/la-construction-des-ensembles-d-habitation-se-heurte-a-des-difficultes-administratives-et-financieres_2072606_1819218.html","language":"","text":"La construction des \" ensembles \" d'habitation se heurte à des difficultés administratives et financières. Le Monde Publié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00, modifié le 08 novembre 1951 à 00h00 Temps de Lecture 1 min. Article réservé aux abonnés Lire plus tard Mettre Le Monde en favori sur Google Partager Partager sur Messenger Partager sur Facebook Envoyer par e-mail Partager sur Linkedin Copier le lien","title":"La construction des \" ensembles \" d'habitation se heurte à des difficultés administratives et financières.","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/la-construction-des-ensembles-d-habitation-se-heurte-a-des-difficultes-administratives-et-financieres_2072606_1819218.html","id":764},{"content_hash":"404a9e63af58dfa63822b041816a32326309ad275cb0df7cff6e8dbd09235606","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:24.307799+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/worlds-third-largest-asteroid-impact-zone-found-in-south-australia-12233","language":"","text":"World’s third largest asteroid impact zone found in South Australia Published: February 14, 2013 7:47pm EST The asteroid hit Earth up to 360m years ago, the study found. NASA https://theconversation.com/worlds-third-largest-asteroid-impact-zone-found-in-south-australia-12233 https://theconversation.com/worlds-third-largest-asteroid-impact-zone-found-in-south-australia-12233 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article An asteroid measuring up to 20km across hit South Australia up to 360 million years ago and left behind the one of the largest asteroid impact zones on Earth, according to new research published today. The impact zone in the East Warburton Basin was buried under nearly four kilometres of earth, said Dr Andrew Glikson, a visiting fellow to the Australian National University’s Planetary Science Institute and a co-author of the paper. “It’s significant because it’s so large. It’s the third largest impact terrain anywhere on Earth found to date,” Dr Glikson said. “It’s likely to be part of a particular cluster that was linked with a mass extinction event at that time.” Dr Glikson published his findings in a paper in the journal Tectonophysics, co-authored by ANU colleagues Dr John Fitzgerald and Dr Erdinc Saygin and by the University of Queensland’s Dr Tonguc Uysal. The team analysed quartz grains drawn from over 200 samples taken from far below the Earth’s surface and studied underground seismic anomalies. Dr Glikson said there was a chance that the asteroid that caused the impact zone actually split in two before it hit. “We are studying another anomaly in West Warburton that could well be its twin but we don’t know yet.” Dr Simon O'Toole, Research Astronomer at the Australian Astronomical Observatory, said the finding was very interesting. “It strengthens the case for the idea that the Chicxulub crater is connected to the mass extinction of the dinosaurs. We are starting to see more evidence that impact events caused mass extinction events,” said Dr O'Toole, who was not involved in the research. “Australia is a fantastic place for impact crater hunters because we have huge open space with nothing in it,” said Dr O'Toole, adding that the size of the new impact zone was very significant. “It’s huge. Most asteroid events are about 100m in diameter.” Another asteroid, dubbed 2012 DA14, will pass within 27,700 kilometres from Earth on Saturday, potentially passing communication satellites, but is unlikely to hit the planet. Asteroids Sunanda Creagh, The Conversation, Andrew Glikson, Simon O'Toole Author Sunanda Creagh Editor Interviewed Andrew Glikson Adjunct professor, UNSW Simon O'Toole Head of Research Data & Software, Macquarie University Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.y36yf5krh","title":"World’s third largest asteroid impact zone found in South Australia","url":"https://theconversation.com/worlds-third-largest-asteroid-impact-zone-found-in-south-australia-12233","id":765},{"content_hash":"55acbeafe9bad70304ee4fa7a4ff10f81943f4baeb00b00dc7cd1fe69213d200","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:24.022740+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-09-27/wallabies-vs-springboks-rugby-test-perth-live-blog-updates/107201492","language":"","text":"Wallabies reduced to 11 players in chaotic 42-38 upset win over SpringboksBy Luke PentonyTopic:Rugby UnionPosted Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:04pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:04pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:04pm, updated Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 11:56pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 11:56pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 11:56pmThe Wallabies celebrate with the Mandela Challenge Plate after defeating the Springboks. (Getty Images: Paul Kane)Skip to timelineabc.net.au/news/wallabies-vs-springboks-rugby-test-perth-live-blog-updates/107201492Link copiedShareShare articleThe Wallabies have desperately held on to defeat the world champion Springboks 42–38 in Perth after being reduced to 11 players during the final five minutes.In one of the most chaotic finishes in Test rugby history, the Wallabies were shown four yellow cards late in the match after comfortably leading 42–17.Captain Harry Wilson, Ryan Lonergan, Joseph-Aukuso Suaalii and Hunter Paisami were each sent to the sin-bin by referee Andrea Piardi.Wilson was shown a yellow card for a high shot on Malcolm Marx in the 69th minute, before the Springboks were awarded a penalty try due to an infringement from Lonergan.As Lonergan received his marching orders, the Wallabies' lead was cut to 42–24.They subsequently lost Suaalii for the remainder of the match after he committed an offside play.The Springboks capitalised on their numerical advantage to score a converted try through Morné van den Berg.Wallabies captain Harry Wilson (left) attempts to speak with referee Andrea Piardi. (Getty Images: Paul Kane)The Wallabies appeared to seal victory in the 76th minute when Tom Wright intercepted a pass from Springboks captain André Esterhuizen.Wright raced away to cross the goal line, but the try was denied as Paisami was ruled to have made contact with Sacha Feinberg-Mngomezulu's head in an earlier tackle.Paisami was given his yellow card, leaving the Wallabies with 11 players.The Springboks got within striking distance of the Wallabies on the scoreboard after Ox Nché scored a converted try in the 78th minute.The Wallabies' lead had been slashed to four points, but they managed to hold on and record a famous victory in front of a boisterous crowd of 54,678.Adding to the bizarre scenes during the final 10 minutes, Piardi was forced to speak with his fellow match officials via a mobile phone following a malfunction with their communications system.\"It was a crazy last 10 minutes,\" Wilson told the Nine Network after the match.\"But that first 70 minutes … the way we played, we implemented our game plan. We did exactly what we wanted.\"We stepped up in the physical battle and I'm so proud of the boys.\"The Wallabies survived an extraordinary finish in Perth. (Getty Images: Janelle St Pierre)Wilson was right to highlight the Wallabies' dominance until the flurry of late yellow cards.They led 24–17 at half-time and extended their advantage in the second term with tries to Wilson and Max Jorgensen.Lonergan helped build scoreboard pressure with two conversions and as many penalty goals.Len Ikitau, playing in his preferred outside centre position, impressed in the first half with two tries.His move back to the number 13 jersey meant Suaalii played on the wing, with the former NRL star handling the role superbly.The Springboks arrived in Australia following their recent victory in the four-Test series against the All Blacks.The physical and emotional impact of their win in the series, billed as Rugby's Greatest Rugby Rivalry, will be open to debate.They made 13 changes to the starting line-up that defeated the All Blacks 43–28 in Baltimore earlier this month.The triumph saw the Wallabies awarded the Mandela Challenge Plate for the first time since 2022, which was also the last occasion they beat the Springboks on Australian soil.They face the All Blacks in the first of two Bledisloe Cup Tests in Auckland on October 10.The return clash will be played in Sydney on October 17.Look back at how the action unfolded in our live blog.Submit a comment or question Log in to commentLive updatesCollapse all postsoffSortSort posts Latest Select an optionLatestOldestNew: FiltersChoose what information you see below by using filtersFilter PostsAll67Key Events19Highlights14Tries10Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:40pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:40pmThanks for your company!LBy Luke PentonyThat's where we are going to sign off for the evening — thanks for joining us!What a Test … what a finish!Congratulations to the Wallabies and well down to the Springboks for throwing all they had at the home side in the dying stages.We'll be back to blog the first Bledisloe Cup Test on October 10, so we hope you can come along for the ride.Have a good night!ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:28pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:28pmMaking sense of what just happenedLBy Luke PentonyWell … how do you sum up that finish?The Wallabies found enough in the tank to hold on, despite being reduced to 11 players late in the match.But as Les Kiss highlighted, the Wallabies had done enough to keep their noses in front before referee Andrea Piardi went to his pocket four times to produce a yellow card.Were all those yellows warranted?That's up for debate.Looking at the Springboks, they had made 13 changes to their starting XV following Rugby's Greatest Rivalry.But there is no such thing as a weak South African squad.It was a deserved victory for the Wallabies and they have much reason to be proud.They have next weekend off ahead of the back-to-back Bledisloe Cup Tests on October 10 (Auckland) and Sydney (October 17).ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:16pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:16pmA 'win is a win'LBy Luke PentonyWin is a win bizarre you might say but the new chapter has begun … onya - Milan ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:16pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:16pmKiss breaks down 'bizarre finish'LBy Luke PentonyHere's Wallabies coach Les Kiss, courtesy of Nine:I've got to give credit to my coaching team. They were calm up in the box, just trying to get the right message in the right way.In those moments, you've just got to hold your nerve and stay clear about what you want to do and I think they did that. A bizarre finish.But I don't want us to forget about the first 60 (minutes).I think it was a really important step up for us to take that game forward, control the game and really be brave and attack the game, as we would have liked to.ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:09pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:09pmOnya Les!LBy Luke PentonyWell done wallabies what a game Les Kiss i could kiss you have been waiting for so long to see the boys win against all the odds !- Wendy Taylor Qld ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:08pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:08pm'So special'LBy Luke PentonyJoseph-Aukuso Suaalii has attempted to sum up that bizarre finish in Perth.He also spoke with Nine:That was unreal.That was so special for our group. We've trained so hard over the last five, six weeks.For a performance like that, we had 11 players on the field.For us to really dig deep and have such a great challenge against the South African team … it's so special.ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:59pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:59pmIndeed, what was that?LBy Luke PentonyWhat was that - ChrisReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:58pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:58pm'Crazy last 10 minutes'LBy Luke PentonyWallabies captain Harry Wilson has just given his thoughts on his side's win, courtesy of Nine.It was a crazy last 10 minutes.But that first 70 minutes, the way we played, we implemented our game plan. We did exactly what we wanted.We stepped up in the physical battle and I'm so proud of the boys.It was always a challenge every time you take the field against the Springboks.ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:56pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:56pmBoks speak after full-timeLBy Luke PentonySpringboks captain André Esterhuizen has also spoken with Nine.I think it was a pretty intense game and quite quick for Test match. But they (Wallabies) capitalised on all our errors and I think discipline let us down, especially in that first half. We let them in too many times and that really hurt us.ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:54pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:54pmJorgensen speaks after frantic finishLBy Luke PentonyWallabies winger Max Jorgensen has spoken to the Nine Network after full-time:There's not even words to describe what happened just then.It's so out of the ordinary. Never seen anything like it.But so proud of the boys to put in that extra effort at the end, that serious care and fight that we always talk about down.We stuck in there and got the win.ReactReactKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:49pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:49pmWALLABIES WIN!!!LBy Luke PentonyThe Wallabies have downed the world champions. (Getty)WALLABIES HOLD ON!!THEY WIN 42-38!The Boks were on attack after the full-time siren had sounded.But they were penalised for an obstruction play, giving the Wallabies the chance to kick the ball into touch and end the match.What have we just witnessed?Skipper Harry Wilson came back on after the final try, giving the Wallabies one player back.But they still finished with 12 players after playing with 11 for much of the final five minutes.ReactReactKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:48pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:48pm80' Boks score!LBy Luke PentonyThe Springboks get over through Ox Nche and the conversion is made.The score is 42-38 to the Wallabies!ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:47pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:47pm80' Minute on the clock — are Wallabies home?LBy Luke PentonyThe Wallabies are defending on their line again!It's 15 on 11!Boks are awarded a penalty.ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:45pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:45pm77' Wallabies deny Boks tryLBy Luke PentonyBrilliant goal-line defence from the Wallabies holds out the Boks.Two minutes on the clock — can the Wallabies hold on?ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:40pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:40pm77' WALLABIES DOWN TO 11 PLAYERS!LBy Luke PentonyTom Wright, come on down!Against the run of play, Tom Wright scores an intercept try.Hang on …The TMO wants a look at an earlier tackle attempted by Hunter Paisami on Sacha Feinberg-Mngomezulu.ANOTHER YELLOW CARD!PAISAMI IS OFF THE FIELD!WALLABIES DOWN TO 11 PLAYERS!Naturally, Wright's try doesn't stand.ReactReactKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:39pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:39pm74' Boks score!LBy Luke PentonyMorne van ⁠den Berg goes over!The conversion is successful and the Wallabies' lead is reduced to 11 points, 42-31.The Wallabies are down to 12 players — can they hold on?ReactReactKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:33pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:33pm72' Wallabies down to 12 players!LBy Luke PentonyThe Wallabies are down to 12 players! Joseph-Aukuso Suaalii has now been shown a yellow card for an offside play made in defence!Boks winger Edwill ‌van der Merwe had earlier been denied a try in the right corner, but referee Andrea Piardi went back in the play to ping Suaali via a call from assistant referee Ben O'Keeffe.ReactReactKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:32pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:32pm71' Boks awarded penalty tryLBy Luke PentonyThe Wallabies are penalised at the scrum, with referee Andrea Piardi awarding the Springboks a penalty try.Ryan Lonergan is shown a yellow card for offside play.The Wallabies are down to 13 players!The Boks cut the home side's lead to 42-24.ReactReactSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:30pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:30pm70' Boks denied a tryLBy Luke PentonyThe Springboks crossed under the posts but are pinged for a knock-on.However, penalty advantage was being played and the Boks have another chance.They opt for another five-metre scrum in front of the sticks.ReactReactKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:28pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:28pm70' Wilson shown yellowLBy Luke PentonyBizarre scenes here …Referee Andrea Piardi is communicating with TMO Matteo Liperini via a mobile phone.The usual comms are down.They are checking contact made between Harry Wilson and Malcolm Marx.Wilson was attempting a tackle and he has been shown a yellow card!The Wallabies will finish the match with 14 players.ReactReactShow more postsTimelineThanks for your company!Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:40pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:40pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:40pmMaking sense of what just happenedSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:28pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:28pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:28pmA 'win is a win'Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:16pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:16pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:16pmKiss breaks down 'bizarre finish'Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:16pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:16pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:16pmOnya Les!Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:09pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:09pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:09pm'So special'Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:08pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:08pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 10:08pmIndeed, what was that?Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:59pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:59pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:59pm'Crazy last 10 minutes'Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:58pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:58pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:58pmBoks speak after full-timeSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:56pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:56pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:56pmJorgensen speaks after frantic finishSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:54pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:54pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:54pmWALLABIES WIN!!!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:49pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:49pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:49pm80' Boks score!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:48pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:48pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:48pm80' Minute on the clock — are Wallabies home?Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:47pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:47pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:47pm77' Wallabies deny Boks trySun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:45pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:45pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:45pm77' WALLABIES DOWN TO 11 PLAYERS!Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:40pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:40pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:40pm74' Boks score!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:39pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:39pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:39pm72' Wallabies down to 12 players!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:33pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:33pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:33pm71' Boks awarded penalty tryKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:32pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:32pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:32pm70' Boks denied a trySun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:30pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:30pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:30pm70' Wilson shown yellowKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:28pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:28pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:28pm69' Springboks eye off Wallabies' lineSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:24pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:24pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:24pm68' McReight ends his shiftSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:20pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:20pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:20pm67' Boks awarded penalty inside Wallabies' halfSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:19pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:19pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:19pm64' Jorgensen, what a try!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:14pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:14pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:14pm63' Wallabies make another changeSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:13pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:13pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:13pm60' Wilson scores this time!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:10pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:10pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:10pm59' Wallabies denied a trySun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:09pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:09pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:09pm58' Wallabies go closeSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:07pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:07pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:07pm54' Ikitau leaves field injuredSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:03pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:03pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:03pm53' Wallabies move further aheadKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:02pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:02pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 9:02pm48' Boks bring on Bomb SquadSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:57pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:57pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:57pm47' Wallabies extend leadKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:55pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:55pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:55pm45' McReight to the foreSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:53pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:53pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:53pm41' Wallabies go to their benchSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:50pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:50pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:50pmSecond half makes a startSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:47pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:47pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:47pmKeep in mind …Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:44pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:44pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:44pmNo Wharfie timeSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:41pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:41pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:41pmStat chatSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:41pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:41pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:41pmHT: Wallabies lead Springboks 24-17Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:33pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:33pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:33pm39' Wallabies score!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:30pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:30pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:30pm37' TMO comes into playSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:27pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:27pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:27pmHighlights aplentySun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:25pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:25pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:25pm35' Boks blow attacking chanceSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:23pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:23pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:23pm30' Boks reply!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:18pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:18pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:18pm27' Ikitau scores again!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:14pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:14pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:14pm24' Wallabies score!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:10pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:10pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:10pm23' Wallabies get penalty inside Boks' halfSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:09pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:09pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:09pm18' Wallabies cough up the ball againSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:07pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:07pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:07pm15' Wallabies their own worst enemiesSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:03pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:03pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:03pmWallabies' poor disciplineSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:01pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:01pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 8:01pm10' Boks score first try!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:57pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:57pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:57pm7' Lonergan levels for WallabiesKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:53pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:53pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:53pm4' Boks on the boardKey EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:50pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:50pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:50pm1' Breathtaking start from SuaaliiSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:49pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:49pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:49pmAnd we're away!Key EventSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:45pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:45pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:45pmNot long to goSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:40pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:40pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:40pmWho's in charge?Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:37pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:37pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:37pmTonight's venueSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:35pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:35pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:35pmBoks in formSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:34pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:34pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:34pmSuaalii on the wing — yea or nay?Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:31pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:31pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:31pmTale of the tapeSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:26pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:26pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:26pmEnjoy the match!Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:20pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:20pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:20pmICYMI: Wallaroos get the win against ScotlandSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:20pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:20pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:20pmAnd here are the BoksSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:16pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:16pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:16pmWho's in the Wallabies' line-up?Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:07pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:07pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:07pmGood evening!Sun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:04pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:04pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 7:04pmWallabies vs Springboks: live scoreSun 27 Sep 2026 at 5:30pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 5:30pmSun 27 Sep 2026 at 5:30pmShare optionsCopy linkFacebookX (formerly Twitter)Top StoriesVictorian language charity with $16m kitty has links to white nationalistsTopic:ExtremismFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFormer Bachelor Sam Wood to fight for bail application on domestic violence chargesTopic:Law, Crime and JusticeATO delays credit card ban as government backs down after business backlashTopic:Federal GovernmentHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerJust InFamilies of men killed on Mexico surf trip call for maximum sentencesTopic:CrimeFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmAmbulance ramping data casts doubt over LNP promise to reduce wait timesTopic:Ambulance ServiceFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:05pmHow the card surcharge ban could change our relationship with cashTopic:ExplainerFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:01pmThe fruit and vegetables to look out for this monthTopic:Food and DrinkFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmFri 9 Oct 2026 at 4:04pmMore Just InBack to top","title":"Wallabies reduced to 11 players in chaotic 42-38 upset win over Springboks","url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-09-27/wallabies-vs-springboks-rugby-test-perth-live-blog-updates/107201492","id":766},{"content_hash":"14314e393769bb0dcb6600b8f527ccf15670c55697ab7d25cc323d17d5aad2ba","domain":"healthline.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:23.337644+00:00","final_url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/vomiting-causes-treatment","language":"","text":"Vomiting â forcefully expelling whatâs in your stomach through your mouth â is your bodyâs way of getting rid of something harmful in the stomach. It may also be a response to irritation in the gut. Vomiting isnât a condition, but rather a symptom of other conditions. Some of these conditions are serious, but most arenât a cause for concern. Vomiting can be a one-time event, especially when itâs caused by eating or drinking something that doesnât settle right in the stomach. However, vomiting repeatedly can be a sign of an emergency or a serious underlying condition. Read on to learn the causes of vomiting in adults, babies, and pregnant women, how to treat it, and when itâs considered an emergency. Primary causes of vomitingThe most common causes of vomiting are different in adults, babies, and pregnant or menstruating women.Vomiting in adultsThe most common causes of vomiting in adults include:foodborne illnesses (food poisoning)indigestionbacterial or viral infections, like viral gastroenteritis, which is often referred to as a âstomach bugâmotion sicknesschemotherapymigraine headachesmedications, like antibiotics, morphine, or anesthesiaexcessive alcohol consumptionappendicitisacid reflux or GERDgallstonesanxietyintense painexposure to toxins, such as leadCrohnâs diseaseirritable bowel syndrome (IBS)concussionfood allergiesVomiting in babiesCommon causes of vomiting in babies include:viral gastroenteritisswallowing milk too quickly, which can be caused by the hole in the bottle teat being too largefood allergiesmilk intoleranceother types of infections, including urinary tract infections (UTIs), middle ear infections, pneumonia, or meningitisaccidentally ingesting a poisoncongenital pyloric stenosis: a condition present at birth in which the passage from the stomach to the bowel has narrowed so food canât pass through easilyintussusception: when the bowel telescopes in on itself resulting in a blockage â a medical emergencyVomiting when pregnantCauses of vomiting in pregnant women include:morning sicknessacid refluxfoodborne illnesses (food poisoning)migraine headachessensitivity to certain smells or tastesextreme morning sickness, known as hyperemesis gravidarum, which is caused by rising hormonesVomiting during menstruationHormone changes during menstruation can make you nauseous and make you throw up. Some women also experience migraine headaches during their periods, which can also cause vomiting. How to treat vomitingTreatment for vomiting depends on the underlying cause. Drinking plenty of water and sports drinks containing electrolytes can help prevent dehydration. In adultsConsider these home remedies:Eat small meals consisting of only light and plain foods (rice, bread, crackers or the BRAT diet).Sip clear liquids.Rest and avoid physical activity.Medications can be helpful:Over-the-counter (OTC) medications like Imodium and Pepto-Bismol may help suppress nausea and vomiting as you wait for your body to fight off an infectionDepending on the cause, a doctor may prescribe antiemetic drugs, like ondansetron (Zofran), granisetron, or promethazine.OTC antacids or other prescription medications can help treat the symptoms of acid reflux.Anti-anxiety medications can be prescribed if your vomiting is related to an anxiety condition.In babiesKeep your baby lying on their stomach or side to lessen the chances of inhaling vomit Make sure your baby consumes extra fluids, such as water, sugar water, oral rehydration solutions (Pedialyte) or gelatin; if your baby is still breastfeeding, continue to breastfeed often.Avoid solid foods.See a doctor if your baby refuses to eat or drink anything for more than a few hours.When pregnantPregnant women who have morning sickness or hyperemesis gravidarum may need to receive intravenous fluids if theyâre unable to keep down any fluids.More severe cases of hyperemesis gravidarum might require total parenteral nutrition given through an IV.A doctor may also prescribe antiemetics, such as promethazine, metoclopramide (Reglan), or droperidol (Inapsine), to help prevent nausea and vomiting. These medications can be given by mouth, IV, or suppositoryWhen to see a doctorAdults and babiesAdults and babies should see a doctor if they:are vomiting repeatedly for more than a day are unable to keep down any fluids have green colored vomit or the vomit contains bloodhave signs of severe dehydration, such as fatigue, dry mouth, excessive thirst, sunken eyes, fast heart rate, and little or no urine; in babies, signs of severe dehydration also include crying without producing tears and drowsinesshave lost significant weight since the vomiting beganare vomiting off and on for over a monthPregnant womenPregnant women should see a doctor if their nausea and vomiting makes it impossible to eat or drink or keep anything in the stomach.Medical emergenciesVomiting accompanied by the following symptoms should be treated as a medical emergency:severe chest painsudden and severe headacheshortness of breathblurred visionsudden stomach painstiff neck and high feverblood in the vomit Infants younger than 3 months who have a rectal fever of 100.4ÂºF (38ÂºC) or higher, with or without vomiting, should see a doctor.Prediction and preventionPredicting when you might vomitBefore you vomit, you may begin to feel nauseous. Nausea can be described as stomach discomfort and the sensation of your stomach churning. Young children may not be able to recognize nausea, but they may complain of a stomachache before they vomit. PreventionWhen you begin feeling nauseous, there are a few steps you can take to potentially stop yourself from actually vomiting. The following tips may help prevent vomiting before it starts:Take deep breaths.Drink ginger tea or eat fresh or candied ginger.Take an OTC medication to stop vomiting, such as Pepto-Bismol.If youâre prone to motion sickness, take an OTC antihistamine such as Dramamine.Suck on ice chips.If youâre prone to indigestion or acid reflux, avoid oily or spicy foods.Sit down or lie down with your head and back propped up.Vomiting caused by certain conditions may not always be possible to prevent. For example, consuming enough alcohol to cause a toxic level in your bloodstream will result in vomiting as your body attempts to return to a non-toxic level.Care and recovery after vomitingDrinking plenty of water and other liquids to replenish lost fluids is important after a bout of vomiting. Start slowly by sipping water or sucking on ice chips, then add in more clear liquids like sports drinks or juice. You can make your own rehydration solution using:1/2 teaspoon salt6 teaspoons sugar1 liter waterYou shouldnât have a big meal after you vomit. Begin with saltine crackers or plain rice or bread. You should also avoid foods that are difficult to digest, like:milkcheesecaffeinefatty or fried foodsspicy foodAfter you vomit, you should rinse your mouth with cool water to remove any stomach acid that could damage your teeth. Donât brush your teeth right after vomiting as this could cause damage to the already weakened enamel. Key takeawaysVomiting is a common symptom of many conditions. Most often, vomiting in both adults and babies is a result of an infection called gastroenteritis, indigestion, or food poisoning. However, there can be several other causes. In pregnant women, vomiting is often a sign of morning sickness. Vomiting can be concerning if a person shows signs of severe dehydration, or it accompanies chest pain, sudden and severe abdominal pain, a high fever, or a stiff neck. People whoâve recently had a head injury or are vomiting blood should see a doctor right away. If youâre experiencing vomiting, make sure to sip water and other clear fluids to prevent dehydration. Eat small meals when youâre able to, consisting of plain foods like crackers. If the vomiting doesnât subside in a few days, see a doctor.","title":"Causes of Vomiting and How to Treat in Adults, Babies, and When Pregnant","url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/vomiting-causes-treatment","id":767},{"content_hash":"77c06a0e849d1d0b36314ccb7b6cb1b97536b995240d2d82c1c68adf3760956c","domain":"www.theglobeandmail.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:23.196467+00:00","final_url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/culture/film-and-tv/film/article-terry-fox-documentary-sean-menard-canadian-stories/","language":"","text":"Open this photo in gallery:Sean Menard on the red carpet for Run Terry Run at the Toronto International Film Festival on Sept. 19. Menard worked closely with Darrell, Terry's brother, on the documentary.Laura Proctor/The Canadian Press CommentsShareSave for laterPlease log in to bookmark this story.Log InCreate an accountThere are no talking heads in Sean Menard’s documentary Run Terry Run about Terry Fox and the 1980 Marathon of Hope. Crafted almost entirely from reels of lost footage Menard discovered at the Terry Fox Foundation warehouse, the new film stays tight on the Canadian legend as he makes history, attempts to run across the country and fights a losing battle for his life. Menard, a 41-year-old filmmaker from Hamilton, says he took his storytelling cues from the big-budget Hollywood films that provided salvation from his difficult teens. “I had a challenging upbringing, food wasn’t always in the fridge, but you have these moments in the movie theatre where the world would disappear,” said Menard last month during an interview alongside Darrell Fox, Terry’s younger brother, at the Toronto International Film Festival.Terry Fox documentary Run Terry Run deftly humanizes a Canadian legend“A lot of docs pump you with information, but don’t leave time and space for the viewer to absorb their intended world. On Run Terry Run, I want to put the audience in the van as Darrell tries to support Terry and participate in the film with their own thoughts and dreams.” Darrell, who worked as Menard’s closest collaborator, says he was impressed with the filmmaker’s immersive style on his MuchMusic doc, 299 Queen Street West, which transported him back to his childhood in the 1980s. It was Darrell who ended up giving Menard the rights to make the Terry Fox Foundation-supported film. “I lived that time and loved Sean’s direction because it brought back everything about how Canada looked, and how it felt,” says Darrell. Now 65, he is still emotional when talking about his brother. “There was a need for a new documentary and I didn’t know what Sean’s theme was, but I liked him and there was this amazing breakthrough when we found all those lost reels.”Behind the scenes of a Toronto filmmaker’s Terry Fox treasure huntThe lost reels, which had been missed during the making of Steve Nash’s 2010 ESPN documentary on Fox, Into the Wind, included hours of outtakes from John Simpson’s 1981 CBC film Terry Fox: I Had a Dream. Watching the clips with fresh eyes after signing onto the project, Menard knew he had the opportunity to bring new life into Terry’s story.“We need to be bolder as Canadians in celebrating our heroes and our stories deserve to be up on the biggest screens,” says Menard, who used his own money to book cross-country screenings of 299 Queen Street West when that film failed to land a distributor and also ponied up to rent Toronto’s Roy Thomson Hall and hire the Toronto Symphony Orchestra to demonstrate the power of Run Terry Run to an audience including executives from Elevation Pictures, who ended up purchasing his film.“You have to find a way to get your films out there and I’m the complete underdog, but I’ll put up my house if that’s what it takes to tell our stories – it’s that important that Canadians, not Americans, get our stories to the finish line.” TIFF 2026: Terry Fox documentary Run Terry Run proves just how long a legacy can lastMenard’s urgency finds the perfect protagonist in Terry Fox and his film crescendos as the Marathon of Hope runs from Newfoundland through Nova Scotia, New Brunswick, Quebec and straight through Toronto to its final destination outside Thunder Bay. The footage, of Darrell putting a cake in his brother’s face on his 21st birthday, of Terry removing his stump and the sunburned kid stubbornly making his way in the rain, reveals a sensitive, indelible part of Canadian history. It’s a new way of telling an old story. “Terry’s story is a positive story as much as it’s an emotional story and I’m lucky to be Terry Fox’s brother, but it’s also a curse because I live with the loss of Terry every day,” says Darrell, sporting the same Marathon of Hope shirt he’ll wear for all of his promotional events. For Darrell, Run Terry Run is an opportunity to celebrate his big brother, a brother whose legacy has helped bring in more than $1-billion for cancer research and still goes strong today. Last month, more than four million Canadians participated in the Terry Fox Run across the country, bringing in more than $30-million dollars. “I think this film is a blessing because even Terry, knowing that he was going to die, believed everything happening to him happened for a purpose and the movie shows that,” he says. “It captures exactly what we were experiencing in 1980 in the van.” Run Terry Run opens in select theatres Oct. 9.","title":"‘We need to be bolder as Canadians in celebrating our heroes’: How a new Terry Fox doc unearths a legend","url":"https://www.theglobeandmail.com/culture/film-and-tv/film/article-terry-fox-documentary-sean-menard-canadian-stories/","id":768},{"content_hash":"97f58ca638da40f39ef650ee3f3aaea65f045813e446958641696800394dc45f","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:23.168855+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/la-nouvelle-aurore-master-cyrano_2072718_1819218.html","language":"","text":"L'auteur (cinégraphique) du Champion - je veux dire Mark Robson - a tourné en 1951 la manivelle d'une caméra devant un aveugle de la guerre terminée en 1945. \" That is the question \" se disent jour après jour les producteurs de cinéma furieusement occupés d'une part à suçoter et à crachoter des olives au Fouguet's, d'autre part à regretter de n'avoir pas tourné Marius. \" Nous ne pouvons plus intéresser nos spectateurs à des malheurs d'un autre âge. \" Eh bien, la Nouvelle Aurore est un film remarquable, infiniment supérieur aux Plus balles années de notre vie, de M. Wyler, qui sera considéré comme un navet sans épices par les juges d'un autre temps. La Nouvelle Aurore s'occupe de la rééducation d'un aveugle de guerre. Elle ne se contente pas de critiquer ceux qui l'ont blessé ; ce n'est pas d'un faux pamphlet, superficiel comme un article démagogique grasseyé dans un haut-parleur, qu'il s'agit ; le film a beau s'appeler la Nouvelle... Aurore, il ne s'est pas assuré la collaboration d'Henry Bénazet, non ! Nous avons là, au contraire, une fois encore, la preuve que la discrétion porte toujours ses fruits, que l'ellipse est une très bonne manière d'obliger à penser un spectateur de salle obscure, que le contrepoint peut assurer du succès son auteur s'il possède autant de talent que M. Noël Coward et que M. David Lean, metteur en scène de Brève rencontre. Je sais bien qu'il faudrait être aveugle pour ne pas verser quelques pleurs au spectacle, un soir sincère où Margot vous entête d'un parfum de violettes, devant le malheur d'un être privé de regard... et qui n'a plus pour lui que le parfum des violettes et le contact d'une quelconque Margot. Mais justement la Nouvelle Aurore n'a aucun des défauts du mélodrame où Margot va pleurer. Le G.I. qui en est le héros (Arthur Kennedy), excellent, demeure toujours sobre. La jeune femme qui lui prête un bras, puis lui ouvre les deux, Peggy Dow, ne paraît pas moins vraisemblable que le succès d'un cheval de course. Leurs comparses demeurent discrets. Il vous reste 28.34% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"\" La Nouvelle Aurore \" \" Master Cyrano \"","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/la-nouvelle-aurore-master-cyrano_2072718_1819218.html","id":769},{"content_hash":"d81ffa07a1ab432d8311e9a69ec30bb94dd7a0c23fa389203db280d466fb1def","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:23.094023+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/news-corp-targets-us-classrooms-with-low-cost-tablet-device-12690","language":"","text":"News Corp targets US classrooms with low cost tablet device Published: March 6, 2013 9:12pm EST New tablet devices are emerging in classrooms, but will they change the traditional learning process? Jose Picardo https://theconversation.com/news-corp-targets-us-classrooms-with-low-cost-tablet-device-12690 https://theconversation.com/news-corp-targets-us-classrooms-with-low-cost-tablet-device-12690 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article News Corporation’s Amplify education business has launched a US$299 tablet device in the US, taking on Apple in the education technology market. To purchase the tablet at the lowest cost price, schools must also purchase a two-year subscription to the company’s teaching software and pre-loaded content for US$99 a year, which includes teacher training and access to telephone support. The Android tablet operates an open platform, meaning it supports digital content created elsewhere. It comes preloaded with content from a range of organisations, including online education provider the Khan Academy. “This is more than just a tablet. It’s a complete learning solution organised around the school day,” said Stephen Smyth, president of Amplify’s Access division. News Corp is trying to muscle in on territory that Apple has been trying to corner for some time, said Dr Roland Sussex, Emeritus Professor at the University of Queensland’s Centre for Educational Innovation and Technology. “It’s a two tier thing, you buy the basic tablet and then you buy access to the educational content. They’ve preloaded it with some useful things which will allow the teacher to monitor the students activities.” Professor Sussex said while it was a good thing that the offering had a focus on blended learning, its ultimate utility for Australian teachers would depend on what sort of material people wrote for the platform and the licenses Amplify developed with education providers. “They will also look at price and this looks competitively priced, but more than that they will look at educational content,” he said. There is likely to be strong interest in the product from Australia as a result of the pricing, said Dr Gilly Salmon, Pro Vice-Chancellor of learning transformations at Swinburne University of Technology. “Many schools are already either using or experimenting with the use of tablets in the classroom, but the cost is restrictive, so the fact that these people are trying to get something cheap out there is relevant,” Dr Salmon said. The tablet is priced US$100 lower than the Apple iPad in the US market, however Dr Salmon said Australia was already paying more than other markets for text books, which was part of the reason why digital textbooks were attractive. “We need to tell News Corp and anyone else who’s interested: don’t push the price up for Australia.” Swinburne University is currently working on developing a digital set of teacher education programs, and Dr Salmon said the new class of teachers would have had their own education largely through mobile devices. “What really matters is it enables us to think very differently about the type of education we do. It provides a catalyst for looking at something that’s more mobile, more available, a different environment for children to learn, and this could be a tipping point.” However Mike Horsley, director of the Learning and Teaching Education Research Centre at Central Queensland University, said each online or digital platform and its associated materials required a different set of practices and teacher training. “From a schools perspective they know that if there’s a multiplicity of these things there has to be a training element when considering if to purchase and use them,” Professor Horsley said. He added that it was how tablets were used by teachers, as opposed to the tablets alone, that contributed to the learning process. “Apple have digital textbook tools for authors to write on iPads, and they’re marketing them as being superior because they have digital pedagogic features, but the reality is it’s quite difficult to design learning experiences around those sorts of things in a school.” News Corporation Android Online learning Tablet computing digital classroom Charis Palmer, The Conversation, Gilly Salmon, Mike Horsley, Roland Sussex Author Charis Palmer News Editor Interviewed Gilly Salmon Pro Vice-Chancellor (Learning Transformations), Swinburne University of Technology Mike Horsley Director, Learning and Teaching Education Research Centre, CQUniversity Australia Roland Sussex Professor Emeritus, The University of Queensland Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.mnnh477as","title":"News Corp targets US classrooms with low cost tablet device","url":"https://theconversation.com/news-corp-targets-us-classrooms-with-low-cost-tablet-device-12690","id":770},{"content_hash":"34e430f3372467a7224d407c1d1cd6f14e97e496c86a9c55349ad8cda5e45a94","domain":"www.abc.net.au","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:22.841169+00:00","final_url":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/topic/rugby-union","language":"","text":"Rugby Union - Topic - 