m waste charges linked to property tax bills in Gurgaon5,097 RTE seats vacant after 4th round allotmentUS city proclaims June 22 after BAPS swamiZen garden proposed at Gorwa UPHCHome guard stole huge liquor stock from police station, sold to bootleggerMan killed after altercation in Gir Somnath8 arrested in Patan mule bank accounts racketBodies of man, two-year-old daughter found in tankLion cub falls into residential’s society well in JunagadhGRD jawan held for blackmail, repeated rape of minor in KeshodPower shutdown in Trichy tomorrowMinister promises urgent action to revive Vattiyoorkavu National Games Shooting RangeRabid twist to Jafrabad motorcyclist’s ‘lion attack’ deathA conman who impersonated as cop cheated people claiming to recover stolen mobile arrestedFire safety drive: One coaching centre sealed, 17 served noticesAir Force officer cheated of ₹82L in solar business schemeIFSCA board clears global commodity trading platform at GIFT CityGujarat: SGST dept busts ₹128cr fake invoice racket, arrests father-son duoCopyright © 2026 Bennett, Coleman & Co. Ltd. All rights reserved. For reprint rights: Times Syndication ServiceFollow Us On","title":"Brendan Sorsby's NFL dream just got cancelled — Here's the letter that did it","url":"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/sports/nfl/news/brendan-sorsbys-nfl-dream-just-got-cancelled-heres-the-letter-that-did-it/articleshow/131949192.cms","id":780},{"content_hash":"e1e868290fbf22de158f4351e3bb8619d25aeaff40688f86641a2e9d1c57ca73","domain":"reallygoodemails.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:21.231020+00:00","final_url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/emails/newsletter-email-design-from-campaign-monitor","language":"","text":"Newsletter Email Design from Campaign Monitor from Campaign Monitor - Desktop Email View | Really Good EmailsNow you have zero excuses to NOT design really good emails. Introducing RGE StudioRead more Find inspirationAll categoriesBrowse by typePopular right nowBehavioral Enhancement Inaugural Industry Miscellaneous Promotional Punctual Seasonal Templates BehavioralAbandoned CartComment NotificationCurated ContentCustomer AppreciationCustomer ServiceDiscoveryEngagementFeedback and Survey and PollFollow-UpLoyalty / RewardsPost Purchase / Recent PurchaseProduct RecommendationsReferralRetention / Re-engagementReview and TestimonialSummaryThank YouUnsubscribeCurated by humans since 2014.Browse all categoriesPopular right nowCreate emailsProductWhat is RGE StudioThe email builder that gets you.FeaturesEverything you need to make email happen.Product tourSnoop around at your own pace.Works withEmail & page templatesStart from something really good.IntegrationsYour favorite ESP or CRM can come too.Salesforce ecosystemCreate in Studio, send from Salesforce.MCPBuild emails from your AI tools.SolutionsHigher educationBetter email for busy campus teams.NonprofitsMore mission, less busywork.IntegrationPerfect for Salesforce usersThe perfect addition to your Salesforce setup? An email editor that actually works.Learn moreSOC 2 Type II · ISO 27001 · DPF · AWS PartnerPricingTalk to usResourcesLearnBlogTips, trends, and teardowns.AcademyGet better at email. For free.Help centerStuck with RGE Studio? Start here.ConnectVirtual eventsCome learn stuff with email people.UnspamFor folks who hate boring email conferences.Feedback FridayReal emails, honest critique.NewsletterReally good stuff, in your inbox.ToolsAI prompt librarySmart starting points for your emails.Email signature generatorLook pro in every sign-off.Report2026 RGE Email SurveyFresh data on how email teams actually work: email got faster, but not for everyone.Read the report300,000 email pros learn and hang out here.Next webinarsJoin the newsletterPricingTalk to SalesHome/Newsletter Email Design from Campaign MonitorDesktopCodeTextInfoEditable templatesNEW!From:Campaign MonitorSubject:Newsletter Email Design from Campaign MonitorSave this email to start commentingSign up to Really Good Emails for freeSave this email to start commentingSign up to Really Good Emails for freeLegal and Privacy infoReally Good Emails is part of Beefree. It started as a volunteer project in 2014.© 2026 BEE Content Design, Inc. All rights reserved. Terms | Privacy & Cookie Policy | DMCACookies Preferences and Your Privacy Choice","title":"Newsletter Email Design from Campaign Monitor from Campaign Monitor - Desktop Email View | Really Good Emails","url":"https://reallygoodemails.com/emails/newsletter-email-design-from-campaign-monitor","id":781},{"content_hash":"e330cf9bb46d1f39958a38902195f0fb1d4ae7e52751155b489ee7d868bd0ec6","domain":"healthline.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:20.943673+00:00","final_url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/migraine/nausea-migraine","language":"","text":"Key takeaways Nausea is one of the most common symptoms of a migraine attack. Nausea and vomiting can occur before, during, or after a migraine. While the exact cause of migraine-related nausea is unknown, it may be related to the activation of the central nervous system or a delay in stomach emptying. Anti-nausea medications can help. Some people find relief from migraine-induced nausea through natural remedies, such as eating a piece of ginger or applying pressure to certain trigger points. Nausea is a common symptom of migraine. It can happen before, during, or after the onset of a migraine attack.Although nausea is common, people experience migraine differently. Nausea can occur at any point, and it may or may not lead to vomiting. Nausea can occur in people with different types of migraine, including migraine with aura and without aura. Many alternative treatments and prescription medications can help with migraine-related nausea. Does migraine cause nausea?Many people experience nausea before or during a migraine attack. In a 2019 study involving 6,045 people with migraine, about 65% reported experiencing nausea, light sensitivity, and sound sensitivity during migraine episodes. Around 28% said nausea was their most bothersome migraine symptom. The American Migraine Foundation says nausea may occur during two stages of an attack: prodrome and headache. Prodrome is the period before a migraine headache that can last from a few hours to a few days. The headache stage can last from 4 to 72 hours. Some people experience aura between the prodrome and headache stages. Some people may experience nausea in all stages of a migraine episode. A 2012 study found that 51% of 861 migraine attacks studied included nausea as part of the aura phase. Why does migraine cause nausea?Researchers donât know what causes migraine or nausea associated with migraine.One idea, outlined in a 2018 research review, is that migraine triggers, such as stress, activate the central nervous system (CNS) pathways. The CNS handles the bodyâs autonomic (involuntary) functions, such as heart rate and digestion. Nausea and vomiting indicate a change in the autonomic system function.Some people with migraine have gastroparesis, a condition in which there is a delay in emptying the stomach contents. Nausea and vomiting are common symptoms of gastroparesis.Nausea and migraine typesYou can get nausea with any type of migraine. The International Classification of Headache Disorders (ICHD-3) includes nausea as part of the diagnostic criteria for migraine without aura. It does not include nausea in the criteria of migraine with aura.According to ICHD-3, someone must have nausea and vomiting or light and sound sensitivity during a headache to receive a migraine without aura diagnosis.Nonetheless, many people who experience aura can have nausea during some or all attacks. How to find relief from nausea during a migraineIf you experience nausea during a migraine attack, you can take steps to feel better. You can get prescription medications from your doctor or use alternative or home therapies.AntiemeticsAntiemetics are medications to treat nausea and vomiting. A doctor may prescribe an antiemetic and another drug for you to take during a migraine attack.The American Academy of Family Physicians lists five antiemetics that may be appropriate as treatments for nausea associated with acute migraine:chlorpromazine droperidolmetoclopramideprochlorperazinepromethazine These medications are available in different formulations, like nasal spray, sublingual tablet, or liquid, that may help you keep them down during episodes of nausea and vomiting. AcupressureAcupressure is a technique involving a professional applying pressure to specific points on the body. Unlike acupuncture, acupressure doesnât use needles. Acupressure may help with nausea associated with migraine.GingerGinger is a common remedy for nausea. There is also evidence it helps with migraine-associated nausea and may help with other migraine symptoms.A small 2020 review that included three studies found ginger helped people with migraine to reduce pain within 2 hours. Study groups that took ginger had less vomiting and nausea than the control groups. AromatherapyAromatherapy is an alternative treatment that uses scents from plant extracts to enhance well-being. There is some evidence that peppermint essential oil may help with nausea.A small 2020 study involving 80 people receiving chemotherapy for cancer found peppermint oil may help reduce nausea and vomiting. Thirty-six of the 80 people placed a drop of peppermint oil on their upper lips three times a day for 5 days in addition to taking antiemetic drugs. They experienced less frequent nausea and vomiting than those who took the antiemetics alone. Preventing nausea with migraineYou may be able to prevent migraine-induced nausea by taking steps to reduce the frequency of migraine attacks.Lifestyle changes: These changes can involve following the SEEDS mnemonic, getting quality sleep, eating balanced meals at regular intervals, exercising regularly, keeping a headache diary, and managing stress, which all may help prevent or reduce migraine attacks.Avoiding triggers: After tracking your migraine attacks, you may get a better picture of your triggers. Avoiding them can help prevent or decrease migraine.Medications: Preventive migraine treatments available by prescription include beta-blockers, antidepressants, antiseizure medications, calcium channel blockers, and calcitonin gene-related peptide antagonists.Alternative treatments: Practices like yoga, relaxation training, biofeedback, and cognitive behavioral therapy may help reduce the number of attacks or prevent them.If nausea occurs late in your migraine attacks, you may want to try to take acute treatment medications early in the attack so you do not vomit the medication.Frequently asked questionsHere are some FAQs and answers about nausea and migraine.What happens if I throw up my migraine medication?If you throw up your migraine medication, it may not work well. Ask your doctor if it is safe for you to take another dose, as doctors have different opinions about how quickly the body absorbs medication. You can also get your medication in a way that is easier to take, such as a wafer or nasal spray.Can throwing up help stop a migraine attack?Experts do not recommend making yourself throw up without medical supervision. Although some academic literature, like this 2017 review, noted throwing up may help you feel better during a migraine episode, the support for this is from older research published in 2013.Do migraines make you dizzy and nauseous?Nausea is a common migraine symptom, but migraine episodes can also sometimes cause dizziness and vertigo. Vestibular migraine is a form of migraine that can cause frequent episodes of vertigo (the sensation that the world around you is spinning). Sensory changes are common in people with migraine aura or ocular migraine. Light and sound sensitivity, difficulty concentrating, and visual disturbances are all common migraine symptoms.TakeawayNausea is common with migraine attacks. It can happen at any stage of a migraine attack. Prescription antiemetics, ginger, peppermint oil, and acupressure may help with nausea during a migraine episode. Preventive treatment includes lifestyle changes, prescription medications, and avoiding triggers.","title":"Why You Puke (or Want to Puke) During a Migraine","url":"https://www.healthline.com/health/migraine/nausea-migraine","id":782},{"content_hash":"d90759500924be15f0a4cc22260f5d366f5906c98ab5bd64717696ccd8ea721f","domain":"www.lemonde.fr","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:20.797480+00:00","final_url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/la-coiffure-au-seuil-de-l-hiver_2072640_1819218.html","language":"","text":"LES lois de la mode sont formelles en matière de coiffure, et plus qu'une robe ou un manteau les cheveux des femmes sont assujettis à des servitudes, charmantes s'il en fut, mais formelles. Les cheveux sont de plus en plus courts, c'est un fait, et la tentative faite par les couturiers pour ressusciter nattes et chignons fut un feu de paille qui s'éteignit au seuil des salons de couture. La coiffure d'autre part se stylise, et le fameux \" coup de brosse \" donné tu hasard pour faire mousser des bouclettes folles, longues à peine de quelques centimètres, n'est plus de mise ! C'est un véritable tout de force, si l'on tient compte de la longueur des cheveux, que d'avoir pu réaliser une telle abondance et une telle variété de coiffures. Il y a du Directoire dans l'air... Mme Tallien et Mme Récamier ont inspiré les plus, récentes créations de nos maîtres coiffeurs... Entendons-nous ! Ce n'est là malgré tout qu'une inspiration lointaine qui respecte les grandes lignes de ces coiffures mais les transforme entièrement dans leurs détails. Le mouvement d'arrière en avant est typique. La ligne générale exige des cheveux courts, très courts, avec sur le dessus de la tête et sur les côtés des mèches dégradées, tandis que d'autres, séparées, tombent, légères, sur le front... La nuque n'est plus Jamais rasée, mais toujours très dégagée. Pourrière se refuse à être prisonnier du passé, et interprète avec cette maîtrise qui lui est personnelle les plus célèbres coiffures du Directoire... Très effilés, les cheveux ne dépassent jamais 4 à 6 centimètres de long. Les oppositions de teintes survivent à l'été, et souvent la nuque est plus foncée que le devant de la tête... Georgel, qui passa pour révolutionnaire il y à quelques saisons, pourrait prendre comme formule \" de plus en plus court \"... Ses coiffures à succès, à quelques modifications près, continuent à triompher, et si ce créateur consentit à imaginer en août pour les collections d'hiver des coiffures à nattes pour les mannequins de Fath ce ne fut là qu'un essai sans lendemain, car les intéressées ont toutes depuis lors fait couper leurs cheveux. Il vous reste 67% de cet article à lire. La suite est réservée aux abonnés.","title":"La coiffure au seuil de l'hiver","url":"https://www.lemonde.fr/archives/article/1951/11/08/la-coiffure-au-seuil-de-l-hiver_2072640_1819218.html","id":783},{"content_hash":"3623761b61719d7e06e6b2653dd3fef94c0bfe3570344a680619eef4502eeff0","domain":"www.theconversation.com","fetched_at":"2026-10-09T05:27:20.682655+00:00","final_url":"https://theconversation.com/global-poverty-is-shrinking-study-12877","language":"","text":"Global poverty is shrinking: study Published: March 18, 2013 12:52am EDT A new study says global poverty is on the way down. EPA/Yahya Arhab https://theconversation.com/global-poverty-is-shrinking-study-12877 https://theconversation.com/global-poverty-is-shrinking-study-12877 Link copied Share article Share article Copy link Email Bluesky Facebook WhatsApp Messenger LinkedIn X (Twitter) Print article Global poverty is declining and may be eradicated altogether in some countries in the next 20 years, a new study by the University of Oxford has found. The Oxford Poverty and Human Development Initiative analysed 22 countries using a Multidimensional Poverty Index, a collection of factors such as number of years of schooling, child mortality, nutrition, electricity and drinking water access, living standards, housing and asset ownership. The findings showed poverty levels had significantly dropped in 18 out of 22 countries. Bangaldesh, Rwanda and Nepal were the top performing countries, with the largest reductions in absolute poverty. “If the current pace of poverty reduction continues to the end, then half of the countries would eradicate [multidimensional] poverty within 20 years, 18 of the 22 within 41 years and the remaining four countries within 95 years,” according to the report. The study showed that a total of 1.6 billion people are living in multidimensional poverty – which looks at a range of factors driving poverty, rather than just income – and that 51% of the world’s poorest live in South Asia. Dr Paul Burke, an economic and development expert from the Arndt-Corden Department of Economics at Australian National University, said the study will “help us better understand the global poverty picture – where poverty is located, and in what form impoverishment takes.” “While income-based measures pick up a lot of what we need to know, when it comes to something as serious as poverty, more data is normally better,” he said. “The data allow countries and regions within countries to be benchmarked against one another over a number of criteria. If a country is struggling in one particular dimension of poverty, the country’s government and international donors might be able to better target this area,” said Dr Burke, who noted that the data released did not include countries from the Australia Pacific region. “The data complement what’s available from the World Bank, United Nations, and other sources. The provision of sub-national data is particularly useful, as differences within countries can be just as large as differences between countries.” But some experts have expressed concerns with the use of an aggregated multidimensional index to compare levels of poverty and development. “I am quite against the idea of constructing a single MPI index that collapses all dimensions of human development with equal weight or some arbitrary weighting schemes,” said Dr Sommarat Chantarat, from the Crawford School of Public Policy at the Australian National University. “As a development economist, we all realise that poverty is multifaceted, but it is unclear what we could learn and what policy implications might be from looking (and comparing) an aggregated MPI index.” Dr Chantarat said studies that compare MPIs across countries and regions could fail to indicate “the varying impacts of growth on households’ productive investment and the varying impacts of looming stochastic shocks (economics, climate, manmade and natural disasters) that could prevent some subpopulation from gaining from growth.” Dr Daniel Suryadarma, a development economics expert from the Arndt-Corden Department of Economics at the Australian National University’s Crawford School of Public Policy, also expressed concerns with the methodology of the study. “First, the number of dimensions included and the weight assigned to each dimension depend on the judgement of the researcher,” he said. “So, while they usually claim that their measure is more comprehensive than studies using a single measure of poverty - for example consumption poverty - the potential for researcher subjectivity to \"pollute” the results is actually higher.“ \"Second, the factor analysis basically produces a single measure of poverty and, from experience, I understand that the resulting measure is usually very highly correlated with consumption. Therefore, the advantage of calculating multidimensional poverty over consumption poverty is negligible.” Dr Suryadarma said while he had concerns for the study’s methodology, economic development may have reduced poverty in many areas. “I believe the results that poverty - however measured - has generally fallen in most countries,” he said. “The explanation is simple: economic growth, which is the most powerful poverty reduction tool.” Poverty MDG Katie Moore, The Conversation, Daniel Suryadarma, Paul Burke, Sommarat Chantarat Author Katie Moore Editor Interviewed Daniel Suryadarma Deputy Team Leader - RISE Programme in Indonesia, SMERU Research Institute Paul Burke Professor, Crawford School of Public Policy, Australian National University Sommarat Chantarat Research Fellow, Australian National University Partners View all partners DOI https://doi.org/10.64628/AA.cwckpfgtj","title":"Global poverty is shrinking: study","url":"https://theconversation.com/global-poverty-is-shrinking-study-12877","id":784}],"index":{"N":385,"avgdl":688.787012987013,"docLens":{"400":90,"401":343,"402":721,"403":595,"404":1587,"405":416,"406":131,"407":380,"408":840,"409":1039,"410":925,"411":1058,"412":343,"413":1515,"414":1113,"415":217,"416":131,"417":1033,"418":1320,"419":700,"420":84,"421":83,"422":816,"423":1089,"424":1059,"425":343,"426":1553,"427":78,"428":133,"429":308,"430":327,"431":336,"432":648,"433":76,"434":546,"435":3607,"436":1004,"437":839,"438":80,"439":2014,"440":140,"441":1722,"442":347,"443":933,"444":90,"445":737,"446":7263,"447":482,"448":76,"449":104,"450":142,"451":594,"452":1361,"453":1981,"454":1653,"455":408,"456":734,"457":1364,"458":345,"459":588,"460":98,"461":588,"462":1165,"463":721,"464":244,"465":1428,"466":816,"467":85,"468":683,"469":870,"470":1717,"471":652,"472":308,"473":135,"474":1569,"475":84,"476":345,"477":737,"478":752,"479":288,"480":485,"481":488,"482":395,"483":333,"484":569,"485":221,"486":155,"487":102,"488":1323,"489":778,"490":1629,"491":2547,"492":74,"493":1154,"494":1977,"495":347,"496":348,"497":172,"498":613,"499":90,"500":406,"501":688,"502":1388,"503":749,"504":2557,"505":1014,"506":80,"507":769,"508":1326,"509":513,"510":955,"511":580,"512":256,"513":332,"514":350,"515":640,"516":555,"517":1418,"518":2608,"519":1501,"520":345,"521":841,"522":269,"523":98,"524":580,"525":361,"526":383,"527"